            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-action_adventure-04",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Action / Adventure · 04/05",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == action_adventure}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Action / Adventure. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 4 of 5. }}{{// Source group: recipes-action_adventure; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPaaaqao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Lyrical Prose\n\nKeep the objective and its physical obstacles clear in plain clauses. Keep where the characters are, what they try, and what conditions permit recoverable through the movement into and out of association. A lyrical passage should not blur the causal line of the scene; it should make the cost or significance of a clear action felt.\n\nDevelop a sensory relation from the present action—a sound, a repeated movement, a texture, a change in breathing—through the focal character's attention. It may lead toward an established remembered encounter when that association belongs to this experience. Let the syntax follow that movement: a short plain clause can anchor the present, and a longer unfolding sentence can move from the current sensation into the memory. Give a recollection a particular connection through perception or the teller's associations, while keeping its time distinct from the current action.\n\nLet the remembered encounter develop the felt significance of the present movement. It may affect a grip, a route, speech, or purpose, but it can also remain meaningful private feeling or attention while the action continues unchanged. Give that experience its needed extent without requiring a behavioral demonstration, complete explanation, or symbolic resolution.\n\nKeep the remembered encounter's original knowledge and the present participants' separate access intact. A private association does not become shared information, and a spoken line does not have to match the inward image. Speech may be plain while the narration remains associative; the contrast can make the character's restraint or focus part of the scene.\n\nA later occurrence of the image or sound can deepen, complicate, or sustain its significance. Keep recurrence traceable to the scene or the teller's associations rather than manufacturing an event to repeat the motif. Do not force the image to appear at every difficulty or to supply a predetermined emotional conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nStage the present undertaking as a working scene. Show the route, the task, the obstacle, or the attempt clearly enough that the reader knows what is being done and what depends on it. Keep the task's state recoverable when attention turns inward; the length of reflection does not itself make fictional time pass or require physical delay.\n\nLet a concrete current detail bring an actual earlier occasion into attention. The detail may be a familiar movement, a place, an object, a phrase, or the way someone now performs a shared task. The memory should belong to the focal character's actual past and should arise from what the present scene makes perceptible, not from a general announcement that time has passed.\n\nDistinguish what is now available from what has changed. The character may still be competent, useful, and capable of pleasure. The remembered occasion may concern a relationship, a practice, a place, or a shared possibility that is no longer present in the same form. Let the difference appear through specific observation: who is absent, what is no longer said, what the character now does alone, what still works.\n\nAllow the memory to affect the present action without requiring it to revise the plan. The character may continue the task, accept help, make a practical adjustment, or notice the change and keep moving. The remembered feeling is part of the present experience, not a detour that must end in confession or collapse.\n\nDo not invent bereavement, a failed return, or a concluding statement of impermanence to create emotional weight. If the supplied story already contains loss or change, write it accurately. Where no loss is established, attend to remembered occasions and the present texture of participation without inventing a disused route, broken habit, or absence to create the mood. Warmth and unfinished attachment can coexist with the present work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqaq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling. Establish the present occasion first: who is speaking, who is listening, where they are, and what practical or relational situation has made the telling possible. Keep the outer occasion situated within the cast's journey, preparation, or recovery; listening and companionship may have value without advancing the objective.\n\nLet the teller recount a remembered act of help, restraint, shared effort, or failed cooperation. Stage the embedded account with the same clear physical action the genre requires: positions, attempts, obstacles, and consequences. Distinguish what the teller participated in, heard from others, or learned later; preserve the source of the account and the actual narrator's involvement rather than inventing firsthand participation. Keep the earlier participants' knowledge separate from hindsight.\n\nMake the selection and delivery of the account matter to the present encounter. The teller may be persuading, warning, entertaining, delaying, explaining, or offering a way to understand the current task. Let the listeners respond as the particular people they are. One may accept the account, another may question a detail, and a third may remain silent. The telling can change the present situation without requiring a bargain, a loyalty judgment, or a revised plan.\n\nPreserve what listeners receive, what they already know, and what remains private. The embedded account is composed speech with an audience and a purpose. It is not the teller's complete live interiority. Preserve actual authorized nonspoken experience in its selected presentation beyond both performed speech and observable listener reactions. A listener may understand the story differently from the teller, and the narration should keep those separate kinds of knowledge clear.\n\nReturn attention to the outer occasion when the account pauses or ends. The undertaking can resume on its existing terms, remain pending, or change through a supported response; the telling need not enable a new step. The frame need not close with a moral or a completed lesson. The account may remain under consideration, and the next practical step may be the actual continuation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Minimalist Prose\n\nAnchor each movement in exact visual staging. Give the reader the positions, direction, and physical consequence needed to follow the attempt. Select the details that make the action legible rather than describing every available surface. A single clear gesture can carry more than a paragraph of general motion.\n\nChoose the contextual details that explain the attempt's personal significance. A familiar object, a repeated movement, or a plain line of dialogue may carry established shared history without a recap. The detail should be exact enough to make the relationship inferable: who notices, who responds, who does not.\n\nKeep causality explicit. The reader should understand why a move succeeds, fails, or changes the next opportunity. Omission must not delete the causal connection or make the scene cryptic. A precise observable detail can carry inferable relationship context, while actual private motives remain available through authorized nonspoken experience rather than being replaced by behavior.\n\nRetain authorized private experience at its needed extent. Select precise inward particulars as carefully as physical ones, reducing redundant explanation without narrowing the character's emotional range. A developed feeling, memory, uncertainty, or movement of attention may need a long passage without explaining a choice or changing outward action; keep it as actual nonspoken experience, never a slogan or performed fragment.\n\nLet dialogue remain individual and plain. A familiar line may recall an earlier occasion without explaining it. Do not force every efficient act to become an emotional revelation. The scene can advance through practical work, and the personal meaning can remain present but unannounced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqas::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the undertaking as a continuous scene. Keep the reader oriented through readable positions, attempts, and consequences. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, and authorized inner experience. Use paragraph transitions to retain the scene's continuity when recollection or reflection takes a sustained passage, and summary to bridge actual intervals where needed.\n\nLet a memory enter when the present encounter makes it matter. Give the connection a particular basis in perception or association: a familiar movement, a phrase, a place, an object, or a privately recurring concern may bring it forward. The memory should belong to the focal character's actual past and should preserve what that person knew at the time. Do not let the narration grant later knowledge to the earlier recollection.\n\nReturn from the memory to the present action. The character may act, speak, or notice something that shows the memory's effect. The effect need not be a completed emotional arc. It may be a small adjustment, a renewed attempt, a hesitation, or continuing private feeling with no visible consequence. Give inward attention its meaningful extent without demanding tactical usefulness.\n\nKeep the participants' separate access distinct. A private recollection does not become shared information. Another character may see a change in expression or movement, but they cannot know the memory unless it is spoken or otherwise made available through the scene's actual channels.\n\nAllow practical work to continue without a new memory, changed bond, or complete local arc. The scene may advance the objective through ordinary competence and cooperation. The personal significance can remain present in the prose without being resolved or announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual supplied relationship premise. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. If the premise is rivalry, care, partnership, estrangement, or another established bond, select the scene's participation, coordination, distance, or attention from that specific premise and give it a clear physical form in the undertaking.\n\nMake the relationship legible through shared movement. Show who moves first, who covers whom, who waits, who reaches, who steps back. A practical action can express the relationship without a character announcing its meaning. The reader should be able to see the bond in the way the characters work together or fail to.\n\nUse callbacks that belong to the current encounter. A repeated gesture, phrase, or earlier attempt may carry private meaning. The callback should arise from the scene's actual material and should not guarantee mutual understanding. One character may recognize the reference while the other does not, or both may understand it differently.\n\nLet the relationship and the practical aim coexist through what the people actually choose. Aligned intentions and ordinary help are permitted. The scene does not require rivalry, romance, confession, or repaired attachment. A character may cooperate because the task matters, because the other person matters, or for both reasons at once. Keep the particular motive available without forcing it into speech.\n\nPreserve the relationship's unresolved elements. The scene may fulfill part of the premise's promise while leaving other parts unsettled. The next step in the adventure can be the actual continuation, not a relationship checkpoint.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the undertaking a retold shape through deliberate openings, named acts, and recurring language. Establish the account as remembered speech within the narrator's access. A compact declaration may open a passage, but it should not replace the actual scene.\n\nExpand selected declarations through the preparation, assistance, or restraint that gave a participant's move its basis. After naming an act, show the concrete work behind it: who prepared the route, who held position, who refused a rash move, who helped at a cost. The expansion should make the declaration more specific, not merely repeat it in longer sentences.\n\nKeep the account's public shape distinct from private experience. The narration may relate what a participant did and what others saw, but it must not grant the teller access to excluded minds. A remembered act may be accurately reported while the participant's private reason remains known only to that person or to the authorized focal narration.\n\nGive dialogue its particular speaker's register beside the chronicle's declarations, whether it confirms, questions, or simply continues the account. A participant may object to a detail, answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern, or correct the teller's emphasis. This need not become parody. The exchange can show that the remembered deed had a personal and practical basis rather than a single heroic meaning.\n\nAllow the account to remain unfinished. The undertaking may still be underway, and the teller need not declare a legacy on the community's behalf. A recurring phrase can retain or change its application through an actual later occurrence. The account may pause with the next step pending, without a completed legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social/domestic realism route. Keep the adventure's objective and obstacles concrete. Where sustaining work bears on the current occasion, show the actual people preparing food, repairing equipment, caring for someone, keeping a schedule, or handling a message alongside the undertaking. An interval may follow the attempt directly without a new domestic task or supporting-work cutaway. Present these sustaining activities as actual work, including duties and private concerns that matter to their owners beyond the expedition's usefulness. A person can help by holding a route open, remembering a supply, or taking over a duty so someone else can act.\n\nStage each significant action clearly. Establish where the people are, what they can reach, and what the immediate move changes. When a remembered interaction matters, let it surface through a present practical detail: a familiar way of packing a bag, a repeated instruction, an old injury that affects how someone lifts or climbs, or a shared task done differently after an absence. Do not turn the memory into a full flashback unless the scene needs it.\n\nKeep the emotional significance of an action attached to the relationship that gives it meaning. A successful maneuver can be tense because it depends on someone whose trust is damaged; a small act of care can advance the journey because it restores a working arrangement. Let cooperation and refusal both remain possible. Preserve each person's private concerns and their choice to help or decline. The scene may end with the practical work still unfinished, but the next step should be intelligible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqaw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Screenplay\n\nWrite the current part of the undertaking in concise action lines. Each block should make the physical situation readable: who is present, what they can see or reach, what they attempt, and what the attempt changes. Use scene headings when location or time changes. Keep dialogue attributed and grounded in the participants' current purposes; they may coordinate, explain a plan, or speak from a relationship concern without narrating the emotional meaning of every action.\n\nUse a cut or a spoken reference to connect the present action with an earlier encounter when it develops the current attempt's personal significance, including meaning that remains private and leaves behavior unchanged. A character can recognize a familiar stance, repeat an old instruction, or hesitate at a place they last saw under different circumstances. The cut should supply the particular remembered image or line, not a general emotional summary. Preserve causal time: the present action continues from the conditions already established, and a memory cannot alter what happened earlier.\n\nKeep private experience in the active nonspoken presentation rather than forcing it into voice-over. If a character's thought or feeling matters, render it as clearly attributed nonspoken material; do not make another character react to it. Visible effort can reveal strain, competence, or hesitation, but it does not prove a moral motive. The scene may close after a completed move, a failed attempt, or a practical interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Stage Play\n\nStage the current part of the undertaking through clear entrances, exits, reach, objects, and coordinated or independent work. Make the physical business playable: who enters carrying what, who can reach a tool or block a path, who works beside whom, and who leaves before a task is finished. Let dialogue arise from that business. A practical exchange can carry the relationship without requiring a confession or a monologue.\n\nWhen an earlier encounter matters, bring it back through its actual use in the present scene. A character may repeat a familiar instruction, return an object, take up an old position, or avoid a gesture they used to make. The recalled moment may affect current work or speech, or develop actual nonspoken feeling without an outward sign. A separately staged reenactment is available only when the supplied sequence calls for it; memory does not require one.\n\nKeep offstage events available through supported reports and effects: a sound, an entrance, a carried object, or a line that reports what happened. Preserve private experience separately from performed speech. A pause or an unfinished arrangement can be a valid scene boundary. The play can stop after a result, a departure, or a practical standoff without an obligatory reconciliation or revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqay::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet an authorized mind carry the current attempt through its actual movement of attention. Braid immediate perception, bodily sensation, private words, remembered gestures, and imagined possibilities as they arise, but keep concrete anchors for what is being attempted: the texture of a surface, the weight of an object, the sound of a step, the position of another person. The reader should always be able to distinguish present event from recollection or fantasy.\n\nWhen a remembered encounter enters the mind, let it arrive through a present trigger: a familiar grip, a repeated phrase, a pain in an old injury, or a place that looks the way it did before. The memory may change how the character reads the current action, but it does not create new capacity or grant access to another person's mind. Familiarity and conviction remain private conditions, not proof of what someone else intends.\n\nFictional time follows events, not prose length. A long inward passage can occupy a brief pause; a quick decision can follow a single sharp perception. The scene may end with the attempt incomplete or the character still uncertain. Preserve the active narrator and viewpoint contract throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqaz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the current part of the undertaking through attributed messages that carry its practical and relational exchanges, including coordination: who is going where, what they need, what they can see, what they are carrying, and what they are waiting for. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the present exchange. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may send short practical updates, another may hesitate, edit, or leave a message unsent.\n\nWhen a familiar phrase or earlier exchange matters, distinguish a message or attachment actually available now from a character's private recollection of it. Do not invent a retransmission to give the memory a place in the transcript. The reference may carry personal significance, but it does not prove receipt, comprehension, or agreed intention now. Distinguish a composed draft from a transmitted message, and keep live private experience separate from both. If a character dictates a message, show the difference between what they say aloud and what is actually sent.\n\nPreserve knowledge from other established channels. A character may know something from a prior call, a shared location, or an earlier conversation without the transcript explaining it. No acknowledgment, misunderstanding, or changed relationship is owed each interval. The exchange can end with a task still pending or a practical arrangement confirmed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Visual Novel\n\nOrient each scene with a relevant position, object, or gesture that makes the current objective and obstacle legible. Use concise scene-setting, then move among attributed dialogue, focal narration, and actual private experience according to the scene's needs. A background or expression cue can orient the moment, but it is not an automatic truth indicator or a required label on every line.\n\nWhen an earlier cue returns, let it return through the present physical situation: the same landmark seen from a new approach, a tool taken up again, a gesture repeated under different pressure. The return may hold or change its felt significance for the focal character, but it does not grant shared knowledge to others or dictate a visual reaction. Keep spatial causality and ownership clear: who can see the cue, who remembers it, and what the current attempt actually changes.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely from this style selection; preserve any separately authorized features and actual relationship premise. Preserve the active narrator and viewpoint contract. The scene can end after a completed move, a failed attempt, or a practical interruption without a required breakthrough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaqbb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Masashi Kishimoto / Web Serial\n\nAt the start of a new interval, restore only the current objective, conditions, or prior contribution needed to follow the resumed work. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the undertaking that first established it. Carry forward what earlier attempts really spent, enabled, or left pending: a broken tool, a cleared route, a promise made, a debt owed, or a person now absent.\n\nUse an established callback when it helps the present action. A character may repeat an old instruction, return to a place they last saw under different circumstances, or recognize a familiar gesture. The callback may clarify participation, sustain a private feeling, or affect current work or relationship; it need not produce a change or become a required recap or flashback. A practical advance needs its actual personal and material basis: show the relevant capacity, knowledge, preparation, or cooperation, and any cost the actual attempt incurs without inventing expenditure or sacrifice.\n\nNo episode owes a power increase, sacrifice, new relationship state, or cliffhanger. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Keep the through-line available for the next reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nKeep the supplied objective and the particular request or return before the characters. When a name, object, or promise recalls another story for the focal person, let that association affect attention, hesitation, or the wording of an offer, but keep it distinct from what the other person has actually agreed to do. The remembered story may explain a private fear or hope; it does not settle the present task.\n\nRender private feeling at its actual extent during the attempt. Show the cost of admitting a need, withholding a reason, returning after absence, or accepting help through concrete behavior: a delayed answer, a task done with more care than necessary, a practical question asked instead of the harder one, or a moment of warmth that does not cancel the hurt. Let strain enter the conversation and the work itself. A person may cooperate fully while still angry, or refuse a request without becoming cruel.\n\nDo not require a confession, sacrifice, or repaired attachment. Help may be given, refused, or left pending, and the scene may end with work and feeling changed, unchanged, settled, or still open. Preserve the active narrator's access and the causal sequence. A realization can matter as knowledge or private experience without a new act, but it does not perform an unperformed movement. A tale's resemblance alone does not prove another person's motive; a sourced account may inform an interpretation within its actual limits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Chat Roleplay\n\nEnter the supplied move through clear positions, available means, and the objective that gives the interval its shape. Introduce a name, object, or offered condition through practical use: someone recognizes a path by an old local name, asks for a tool by the title its previous owner used, or offers help on terms that sound like a remembered arrangement. Let the association remain a detail of the encounter rather than a preface that explains the whole undertaking.\n\nKeep each participant's knowledge separate. The focal character may recognize the older story behind a phrase or place while another person uses the same words without that history. Show what each speaker actually knows through what they say and do, not through narration that hands one person's memory to everyone present.\n\nLet other characters act, speak plainly, or leave a request pending according to the active ownership contract. They may offer practical help, ask a clarifying question, continue their own work, or refuse without becoming obstacles. End the interval where the scene naturally rests: the next move may be available, but no revelation, dramatic choice, or cliffhanger is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse classical or vernacular episodic telling. Give the current stage of travel, work, pursuit, or recovery a clear narrated occasion: where the characters are, what they are trying to accomplish, and what concrete obstacle or opportunity this episode presents. End the interval when that stage reaches a natural pause, not when the whole undertaking is complete.\n\nCarry an established object, request, or agreement into the next episode when the undertaking reaches it. A token may change hands, a promise may be repeated under new circumstances, or a place name may be recognized by someone who knows its older use. Let the carried thing keep its practical function; its significance comes from the history the characters actually have with it, not from a symbolic meaning imposed by the narration.\n\nWhen a short account behind the carried thing would deepen the present episode, tell it plainly as a story someone knows or a past event the narration can supply within its access. The account may explain why a route is chosen, why a request matters, or why a person hesitates. It does not need to deliver a moral, reveal a hidden power, or make the object magical. Keep the adventure's causal sequence intact: the remembered story informs the present attempt without replacing the work of travel, discovery, or recovery.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSelect the actual grip, surface, tool state, or bodily cost of a move and describe it plainly. Name the texture that matters: a rope's frayed section, a door that sticks at the hinge, the weight of a pack after a long climb, the ache in a hand after holding position. Keep transitions compressed; move from one concrete condition to the next without restating what the reader has already seen.\n\nLet a meaningful object retain weight and practical use even if it also recalls a tale. A knife with a name may still need sharpening. A path with an old story may still be blocked by weather or debris. The remembered name or bargain can sit beside the material fact without explaining it away or making the object more effective than its condition allows.\n\nPreserve sound equipment and real assistance. A tool in good repair can work when its actual capacity and the conditions permit; a person who knows the route can help; a plan that accounts for the actual terrain can succeed. Compress redundant explanation rather than private experience. Give actual feeling, attention, uncertainty, and reasoning the space they need, without requiring an immediate practical payoff. A worry can recur when it genuinely continues in the character's experience. Injury and failure are possible, not compulsory, and a hard closing maxim is never required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the actual letter, instruction, log, report, or other supported document the scene requires. Give each document a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the document's form govern what it can say: a field note records what the writer observed at that moment, a letter asks or explains for a particular reader, a log entry may be terse because the writer was tired or hurried.\n\nDistinguish what a document's words direct someone to do from any tale, reputation, or private association they recall. A letter may mention a place by an old name without explaining it. A reader may recognize that name from a story their family told and act on the recognition. Keep the document's actual instruction, attributed history, and any established properties distinct from a reader's or writer's private resonance. Evocative wording does not itself give the paper a new power, but the style does not erase properties or authority the supplied world establishes.\n\nPreserve original wording and later annotations as separate events. A note added in the margin is a second act of writing with its own time and purpose, not a correction that erases the first. Documents may be accurate, limited, or contested. A record can omit what its author did not know, and a later reader can misunderstand it. None of this replaces actual nonspoken experience: the private thought of a character reading a letter is still written as private experience under the active contract, not inferred solely from the document's text.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLocate a real preparation, shared task, or rest within the adventure and give particular care concrete form through the supplied preferences or resources. Someone may mend a strap before a climb, pack an extra portion of a food the other person likes, clear a dry place to sleep, or remember which side of the fire a companion prefers. Let the care be useful or pleasurable beyond utility: the mended strap is also a small relief, the extra portion is also a familiar taste.\n\nWhen a remembered custom or simple story enriches the kindness, keep it attached to the actual people and place. A family way of wrapping bread, a name for a particular stretch of road, or an old saying about resting before a river crossing can make the moment feel storied without turning it into a magical bargain. The story may explain why the care is given; its resemblance to a tale does not by itself introduce a hidden price. Preserve any actual terms the supplied scene establishes rather than inventing a cost or erasing one.\n\nLet warmth be sincere and accepted or declined without a mandatory consequence. A person may accept help with quiet gratitude, refuse it out of pride or preference, or accept only part of it. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. The undertaking continues, but this interval may be hospitable, and the scene may end with the characters rested, fed, or better prepared rather than with a confession or a threat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin from concrete activity: the current task, the physical condition of the route or shelter, the practical exchange between people. Then widen, where the chosen narrator permits, to relevant social history or a remembered account. A place may have been named for an earlier journey, an agreement may echo an older arrangement between families or trades, or a person may carry a story about the object now in use. Keep the widening deliberate and sustained, not a brief decorative aside.\n\nAttribute the larger story rather than treating it as a verdict about destiny. Say who told it, who recorded it, or how the focal character came to know it. The remembered account may explain why a name matters, why a request is delicate, or why a person hesitates. It does not prove what will happen next, and it does not bind the other characters to a pattern they have not accepted.\n\nReorient through the ongoing request or material condition. Return from the historical or remembered passage to the present task: the tool still needs to be carried, the route still needs to be chosen, the agreement still has terms. Let formal transitions sustain both practical causality and intimate resonance. The prose may be composed without becoming ornate, and the scene may end with the undertaking advanced while the larger story remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the scene inside a present undertaking at a point where someone has reason to tell a story: a halt for rest, a request for help, a question about a route or a name, or work that permits talk. Establish the teller, listeners, and occasion before the embedded account begins. Listeners may have needs related to the task or simply their own interest in hearing the story. The telling itself is an action in the scene; it can buy time, offer warning, build trust, or simply fill a shared interval, but it does not have to change anyone's plan.\n\nTell the embedded attempt as a concrete journey or encounter with its own objective, obstacles, and practical choices. Keep the teller's sources visible: what they did, what they saw, what someone else reported, and what they only understood later. A repeated phrase, object, or place name may echo between the tale and the outer task, but the echo is a resemblance the listeners can notice or miss. It does not certify that the same outcome, danger, or promise applies to them.\n\nReturn to the actual outer situation, which may include a listener response, continuing work, or an unanswered account. Someone may ask a practical question about timing, access, or sequence; someone may offer a correction or a competing memory; someone may simply return to the work. A tale may leave a name carrying weight, a route feeling familiar, or a caution available, but no changed understanding or new practical use is required. Preserve actual private experience separately from what the teller performs for the audience. The adventure continues from the present conditions, not from the story's pattern. Use the direct, familiar telling of the Gaiman profile to keep both levels readable, and let the frame's occasion, not a fixed quota, determine how much of the tale appears in any given reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the Romantic and uncanny route for this undertaking. When the cast reaches a threshold—a door, a pass, a boundary, a familiar room, a place someone has only heard named—hold one exact physical feature in view: the worn step, the locked gate, the particular light, the sound the structure makes. Where the feature recalls a story or a felt recognition for the character, place that experience beside the physical fact without imposing a pause to produce it. The recognition belongs to that character's mind and history; it does not alter the lock, the weather, or the route.\n\nKeep the three layers distinct in the prose. The physical feature is observable and testable. The remembered story is sourced—someone told it, a record preserved it, or the character half-recalls it. The felt recognition is private and may be wrong. The passage can be welcoming, practical, uncertain, or already understood. Do not require a sinister motive, an impossible opening, compelled fascination, or a changed feeling. The uncanny effect comes from the exactness of the ordinary detail and the unproven sense that it belongs to another account.\n\nLet the undertaking proceed by its own means. An opening or continued passage follows the supplied world's actual conditions, whether or not the focal person yet understands them. A remembered story may contain accurate practical knowledge that helps the undertaking, but resemblance alone does not prove that its method or warning applies here. Use direct, familiar narration for the practical sequence so the threshold's resonance stays intimate rather than ornamental. When the scene moves on, the place or name may carry the association forward without being explained again.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere supplied authority bears on the objective, stage the encounter as a practical exchange. Show what is actually offered—a resource, a permission, an invitation, a passage—and what accepting, declining, or negotiating would cost or change. Make the terms specific enough that the cast can act on them: a key that opens one door and not another, a favor that can be called in, a route that belongs to someone else. The authority's power is real because of these arrangements, not because a title or reputation makes it inevitable.\n\nLet an established tale or name give the exchange intimate texture. A character may know the story attached to the place or the person offering help; that story can shape how the room feels, what they expect, and what they dare ask. Keep the story sourced and distinct from enforceable control. A frightening reputation does not prove a threat, and a beloved name does not guarantee safety. The cast can be drawn to the offer, wary of it, grateful for it, or all of these without the story deciding the outcome.\n\nPreserve the world's actual mechanics. No imported vampire rules, secret powers, or omnipotent masters may close a gap in the adventure. Sincere help remains possible; attraction and concern remain real beyond utility. Let atmosphere change how the encounter is experienced—through material detail, private response, and the weight of the room—while the objective continues through choices, competence, and cooperation. Use the Gaiman profile's direct telling to keep the layered significance readable rather than murky.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the testimonial route and ground the account in an established participant, whether or not that person is the active narrator. Give the testimony an actual occasion and audience: someone asks what happened, a report must be made, a companion needs to know what lies ahead, or the participant offers the account as part of a bargain. The telling has a situated purpose; it may occur in a present interaction or belong to a recorded account without requiring a newly invented framing scene.\n\nWithin the account, keep four kinds of material distinct through phrasing and attribution. What the participant attempted is their own action and choice. What they witnessed is what they saw or heard directly. What they were told is another person's report, with its source named where the participant would name it. What they later learned may be added as a correction or a clarification, but it must not be written as if they knew it at the time. Use plain markers—\"I saw,\" \"he told me,\" \"we learned afterward\"—rather than a clinical case-summary voice.\n\nLet an associated name or story matter personally to the teller. It may explain why they chose a route, trusted someone, or hesitated, but it does not establish supernatural truth or settle another person's motives. The testimony need not confess, correct, or persuade; it may leave questions open. Preserve actual private experience under the narrator permissions, distinct from what the participant chooses to say aloud. The adventure continues from the facts the account makes available, not from the story the teller wishes were true.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaral::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the diary and reflective miscellany route. Let a situated recorder preserve the undertaking through entries composed at identifiable times: an evening after a crossing, a note made while waiting, a recollection set down when the day's work is done. Keep the practical objective recoverable across the record, recovering location or task state only when the reader needs it. A particular entry can linger on a view, an exchange, an unfinished association, or ordinary pleasure without reciting progress or a changed condition.\n\nTreat the entry as a composed artifact, not a live transcript of the recorder's mind. The recorder chooses what to set down, and the prose should show that selection through what is included, omitted, or noted as uncertain. A later annotation may add or correct context—\"I understood this only after we reached the river\"—but it must not silently rewrite the original entry's knowledge. Keep the original wording intact and let the addition sit beside it.\n\nAn ordinary detail may carry the sense of another story behind it: a name heard once, a mark on a wall, a tune someone hummed. Give that impression room without requiring a payoff. The detail need not become a clue, a prophecy, or a completed pattern. Distinguish the selected record from actual private experience under the narrator permissions; what the recorder does not write down may still be available to the narration if the contract allows it. Use direct, familiar telling so the reflective passages remain intimate rather than ornamental, and let the journey continue through its own practical sequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaram::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the polyphonic and nonlinear route. Juxtapose supported participant accounts of a shared act or place at different times, using a repeated name, object, or promise as the anchor the reader can hold across the shifts. Keep each account's source and temporal position clear. It may be permitted focal narration, an attributed report, or a composed record; only a performed or addressed account needs an actual audience, and no new listener or telling scene must be invented for each shift.\n\nKeep who knew what and when distinct from the order in which the reader encounters the accounts. A later account may reveal something an earlier participant could not have known, but the earlier account must not be written as if it already contained that knowledge. Use reports where direct mind access is unavailable: a character may describe what another did or said without the narration entering the other's private experience. When the active contract permits interiority for a focal character, keep it clearly separate from what that character tells others.\n\nThe accounts may agree, differ, or remain partial. A shared path can look like a rescue to one participant and a failure to another; a promise can be remembered differently without one memory being declared false. Do not require a new reveal or a magical explanation to close the gaps. Let the nonlinear arrangement create meaning through juxtaposition—what one account emphasizes, another omits—while the actual conditions of the undertaking stay stable. Use the Gaiman profile's direct telling to keep each account readable, and let the adventure's facts remain recoverable beneath the differing stories told about them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaran::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Light Novel\n\nEnter each scene by locating the relevant people, positions, and means before an attempt relies on them. Show where the characters are in relation to one another and to the objective, and make any spatial change easy to follow: a door, a turn, a climb, a change in footing or weather. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response so the reader can move through the scene without rereading.\n\nIntroduce an unfamiliar term or mechanic through actual use or an ordinary explanation when the scene needs it. If a tool matters, show what it does in someone's hands or have a character explain it in a way that fits the moment; do not pause for a lore inventory. Let focal thought and individually voiced dialogue take the space they need. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner, and no clue, joke, age group, or revelation quota applies.\n\nA particular name or object may suggest a larger story behind the present task. Let that association sit beside the practical work: a character may notice it, half-remember it, or ask about it, without the undertaking having to pause to resolve it. A character may also choose to ask, wait, or investigate when that follows their actual purpose and circumstances. The larger association can continue unanswered across scenes while the immediate objective advances through competence, cooperation, and improvisation. Use direct, familiar narration so the layered significance remains an invitation rather than a burden, and keep the world's actual conditions stable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose one concrete detail the focal character actually notices during the undertaking: a sound, an object, a repeated motion, a place name, or a piece of equipment. Let it first appear in plain use so the reader can follow the practical sequence. When the detail returns, shape the sentence around it with deliberate rhythm: a longer clause that lingers in narrated attention without itself advancing or stopping fictional time, a repeated sound or syntactical pattern, or a comparison drawn from the character's own memory or the supplied world. The association may deepen, change, or remain uncertain. Do not require the detail to become a symbol, reveal a hidden truth, or pay off at the end.\n\nDevelop the detail's resonance without losing the adventure's material logic. A rope remains a rope; a repeated name remains a name. If the world is ordinary, the detail cannot acquire supernatural power. If the world permits the strange, the detail's unusual properties must be established facts, not metaphors. When orientation matters, return to plain action and direct clauses so the reader can see what was attempted, what changed, and what remains to be done.\n\nLet the focal character's private experience carry the association. A remembered story, a fragment of speech, or a half-recognized pattern may attach to the detail because the character has lived with it. Other characters need not share the association. Dialogue and private feeling retain their actual range: a character may speak plainly while thinking lyrically, or may find the detail unremarkable. The undertaking continues through travel, pursuit, rescue, confrontation, or recovery, and the shaped detail accompanies it without replacing the work of the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a concrete present use bring a particular remembered occasion into view. The focal character handles a familiar object, follows a known path, or repeats a task that once belonged to another time or person. Write the present action first so the reader can follow the current undertaking. Then let the association arrive through a specific detail: the weight of the thing, the sound it makes, the way the route has changed, or the absence of someone who used to share the work.\n\nDistinguish what the character knew then from what they know now. A memory may be incomplete, a later interpretation may be wrong, and a story attached to the object may come from someone else rather than from the character's own experience. Do not let later knowledge rewrite the earlier motive. The current task can matter on its own terms: a rescue can succeed, a route can be found, a piece of work can be finished. The remembered occasion may remain unfinished without requiring the present action to complete its pattern.\n\nPreserve genuine pleasure, practical assistance, and actual remaining options. A character may enjoy using the old tool, accept help, or choose a different route. Attachment does not require death, loss, or a decision. The scene can end with the task still underway, the memory still present, and the character still able to act. No announcement of impermanence or concluding lesson is required; direct reflection can name a particular change when that belongs to the narrator or character. Let the coexistence of past and present remain a condition of the telling, not a conclusion the prose must deliver.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a modern memoir structure through an authorized participant account. The narrator may be the active focal character looking back, or the narration may hold a participant's retrospective understanding beside the scene's present action. Do not force an autobiographical first-person voice where the active contract uses another person or perspective. Link the earlier and later moments through one concrete detail: a name, a path, a promise, a carried object, or a repeated task.\n\nWrite the earlier undertaking as it was actually experienced. The character's original motive, knowledge, and means remain those of that moment. Later evidence or attributed stories may confirm, qualify, or leave the earlier meaning unchanged. A later account that makes the act seem fated, heroic, or ominous is a story told afterward, not a fact that governed the original choice. Do not let the memoir's later knowledge retroactively supply foresight.\n\nKeep the adventure's practical sequence recoverable. Travel, discovery, pursuit, rescue, confrontation, or recovery can each carry a scene. The retrospective voice may pause to note what the character did not know then, while keeping the relevant causal work recoverable. Uneventful or already established activity may be summarized without erasing a condition needed to follow the attempt. Preserve full nonspoken private experience beyond the memoir's explicit account: the character's live thought during the scene remains distinct from what the memoir later says about it. The scene can end with the undertaking still pending, the later story still uncertain, and no required confession, current emergency, or changed conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that carry the undertaking forward: a contact, an object, an offered term, a route, or a spoken name. Use direct clauses to show what was attempted and what changed. Keep the causal sequence recoverable: the reader must be able to see why the next action follows. Omit redundant lore, repeated interpretation, and restatement of what the scene has already established. Do not omit a needed fact, a meaningful private passage, or the step that connects one action to the next.\n\nLet a familiar word or name suggest another story, supplying the particular context the active narrator needs to give without a repeated lore recital. The character may use a name that carries a history, touch an object that belonged to someone else, or repeat a phrase from an earlier occasion. The reader may infer a larger account from the character's attention, while an authorized summary can supply context that attention alone would not make intelligible. The suggestion may remain unresolved. Do not require the detail to acquire a new meaning, reveal a hidden truth, or pay off before the scene ends.\n\nPreserve full emotional range and individual speech. A character may speak at length or briefly, think extensively or not at all, according to their actual state. Economy applies to the prose's selection, not to a quota of short sentences or suppressed feeling. The task or its significance may remain pending. End when the scene's natural boundary arrives, with the present state clear even when the next action is undecided or unavailable, without announcing what it must mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaras::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among work, observation, conversation, and authorized private experience. Keep the practical objective legible: the reader should always know what the character is trying to do and what stands in the way. Travel, discovery, pursuit, rescue, confrontation, or recovery can each carry a scene. Let paragraphing and transitions orient the reader without recap machinery.\n\nIntroduce a meaningful name, bargain, path, or object through use. The character hears the name in an instruction, follows the path because it is the available route, or handles the object because the task requires it. Keep the actual terms separate from memory, reputation, and hypothesis. A character may remember a story about the name, another may treat it as ordinary, and a third may distrust it. None of these responses automatically makes the story true or the term binding. Do not add gods, ghosts, a missing person, a child protagonist, a hidden price, or a preordained outcome solely from the author profile; preserve the characters and world facts actually supplied.\n\nLet the encounter carry another story behind it without replacing the present action. The older story may be suggested by a phrase, a local account, or the character's own recollection. Its attachment to the present event does not by itself make it a governing fact. Accurate knowledge or an established rule within that account may still matter through its actual conditions. Preserve authorized inner experience as actual nonspoken fiction. The scene can end with work still underway, a question still open, and the older story still only partially known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose a callback from the supplied relationship's actual history: a familiar phrase, a shared object, a private joke, a promise, or a repeated gesture. Place it beside the undertaking, giving only the supported context needed to make its shared history intelligible rather than requiring a pause or forbidding explanation. The character may use the phrase while packing, pass the object during a pursuit, or remember the promise while deciding whether to accept help. Ground the callback in the relationship's established past and whatever further context the active authoring contract permits; do not invent a backstory simply to certify the bond.\n\nLet the participants interpret the callback according to their actual knowledge and feeling; their readings may align or differ. One may remember the story behind it while another uses it plainly. Neither interpretation is automatically truer or shared. The difference may surface in dialogue, in private thought, or in a small hesitation. Do not require the callback to resolve the relationship, prove attachment, or become a world binding. A promise gains no magical force from the style alone; preserve any binding mechanism or ordinary obligation the supplied world actually establishes.\n\nKeep the adventure's practical sequence legible. The callback accompanies travel, discovery, pursuit, rescue, confrontation, or recovery; it does not replace the work. Warmth, disagreement, direct feeling, and private doubt can coexist with actual help or a pending reply. Preserve authorized inner experience as actual nonspoken fiction. The scene can end with the task still underway, the callback still carrying its private weight, and no required confession or sacrifice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the supplied undertaking a sense of retelling through deliberate openings and compact declarations at its actual scale. An errand remains an errand; a rescue remains a rescue. Use a recurring phrase or name because the character or community has lived with the deed, not as an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance. Return to the phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application or when familiar wording sustains the remembered act without changed meaning.\n\nKeep the practical act recoverable beneath the retelling's larger resonance. Keep relevant preparation, attempted action, and any actual consequence legible across the telling; no reply must complete all three. The reader must be able to see what was actually done, not only how it will be remembered. Later reputation never becomes earlier knowledge or fate. A teller may know a different account of the deed, or may simply affirm what happened. The difference between the actor's private experience and the public story remains available to the narration.\n\nPreserve full nonspoken experience and unfinished action. The actor may be uncertain, tired, amused, or indifferent to the reputation forming around them. Dialogue can interrupt the chronicle's authority: the subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. Do not require a heroic turn, a moral, or a completed legend. The account can end with the work still underway, leaving another modest act available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nTell the undertaking directly through its practical occasions: the route taken, the work done, the obstacle met, the help given, the thing recovered or left behind. Keep the narration plain enough that a reader can follow what was attempted and what changed. When a remembered condition belongs to the story—a road that floods in spring, a door that must be opened before dark, a kindness owed to someone who once sheltered travelers—state it first in ordinary words and let it return only when an actual encounter or repeated task makes it relevant.\n\nGive the recurrence a folktale shape without making the world obey the tale. If the cast crosses the same water twice, the second crossing may recall the first because the same bank, boat, or warning is present, not because a pattern demands completion. A precaution can prove useful, a kindness can be genuine, and a remembered saying can fit the occasion without revealing a hidden price. Let the characters notice the resemblance in their own terms, and keep the certainty of their interpretation proportionate to what they know. Incomplete knowledge does not force a guess to be wrong or prevent an ordinary account from being accurate.\n\nWhen an encounter seems to carry another story behind it, attribute that sense to a source: a person who told it, a sign that named it, a custom that preserved it. The larger story does not steer the outcome merely through resemblance. Any actual force or rule within it must belong to the supplied world, and the style does not erase one already established. Preserve the actual terms of the adventure—what depends on weather, strength, knowledge, permission, or another person's choice—and let the remembered condition affect the attempt only through those terms. A tale can make a risk legible without making the risk magical. Keep required private experience available as the characters weigh what they remember against what they can see, without requiring that comparison to produce a decision or action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings and concise action lines to show the undertaking as a sequence of observable attempts. Make preparation, access, movement, and transfer legible: who carries what, how they reach the next place, what they can see or hear when they arrive. Keep the action blocks concrete enough that a reader can track the objective without needing an explanatory voice-over. Let dialogue carry practical exchange—directions, warnings, requests, refusals, agreements—in the speaker's own register.\n\nWhen a name, object, or phrase recurs across supported scenes, let the recurrence work through the visible world. A character recognizes the same mark on a crate, repeats a direction they were given, or asks about a place another scene has already shown. The resonance belongs to what the characters know and do, not to a shot that insists on significance. Cinematic emphasis does not establish hidden meaning or motive. A permitted shot may still reveal an actual visible fact; distinguish the shown fact from what its framing invites the viewer to infer. If a private thought or feeling matters, keep it in the active required interiority mode, clearly attributed and separate from spoken lines; voice-over is additional performed material, never a replacement for that experience.\n\nLet a cut join locations that the story has actually connected. A scene may end with a departure and the next begin at the destination, but the intervening travel must remain inferable from the supplied conditions rather than assumed. Do not owe a completed result, new force, or supernatural explanation at a cut. A scene can close on a task still underway, a question left unanswered, or a person continuing ordinary work. The adventure advances through what the characters attempt and what the attempt changes, not through editing that implies a hidden pattern.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Stage Play\n\nMake the adventure playable in the shared stage space. Use entrances, exits, blocking, and props to show what the characters can actually do: who can reach the case, hold the rope, block the door, pass the letter, or leave by the visible way. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. When an offstage event matters, bring it in through a supported report, sound, or effect that the scene has established, not through a cut that shows another location.\n\nGive a phrase or offered condition its practical consequence when it actually has one. If a character repeats a name, a warning, or a promise, preserve any effect the actual response produces; the repetition may also remain personally meaningful with no following action or changed state. The remembered story behind that phrase belongs to the person who remembers it, and another character does not know it merely because the audience has heard it. Keep required private experience in the active nonspoken mode, clearly attributed; an aside or monologue is performed speech with its own audience and cannot replace that interiority.\n\nA request or attempt may remain unanswered. The scene can end with a character still waiting, still working, or choosing to leave, without a theatrical revelation, a sudden binding, or an invented confrontation. Let the adventure's stakes come from the actual task—what must be carried, reached, repaired, or returned—rather than from a hidden story that the staging must now prove. If a tale is told, give it a teller and a reason for the telling, and let the listeners respond as themselves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaray::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow the authorized mind's actual associative order through the undertaking. A heard name may open into a remembered telling; a familiar object may bring back an earlier journey; a bodily sensation may interrupt a plan with present discomfort or relief. Let the movement be particular to this person, not a generic sequence of symbol, memory, and fear. Braid perception, memory, private words, and recurring concern without merging minds or granting access to what this character cannot know.\n\nUse concrete returns to the present scene to distinguish what was done from what the mind rehearses or recalls. The character may imagine opening the door, remember someone else opening it, and then actually try the handle. The prose should make those differences legible through sensation, action, or a change in the external world, using plain attribution or explanation when needed without a redundant summary of every association. A memory may be meaningful without producing insight or action. The character can think of a story attached to a place and still act on the visible conditions—the wet stone, the loose latch, the person waiting—rather than on the tale's pattern.\n\nKeep fictional elapsed events independent of prose length. A long inward passage does not stop the journey or delay another character's arrival unless the supplied scene says it does. Let direct feeling have its actual extent: fear may fragment attention, calm may let a long association unroll, and neither is required to end in a decision. The adventure continues through what the character attempts and what the attempt changes, while the mind's movement remains one part of that attempt.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaraz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the undertaking through messages with actual senders, audiences, and practical purposes. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, another waits before replying. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the coordination. A message can request a route, confirm a meeting, report a delay, ask for help, or refuse a plan, and the next message or the surrounding scene can show what the recipient actually understood and did.\n\nWhen a repeated name or phrase recalls an older promise or story, keep that resonance private to the person who remembers it. The sent wording remains what it is: a request for a map, a question about a door, a familiar greeting. Its private resonance does not automatically create a code or secret signal, or prove that the sender shares the receiver's memory. Preserve any shared meaning the characters actually established. Distinguish a composed draft from a sent message and from private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact not yet transmitted; it may be unfinished or later sent and is not the character's live mind. If required interiority belongs to the scene, keep it clearly separated from the transcript and attributed to its owner.\n\nReceipt, comprehension, and action remain separate. A message may be delivered and still misunderstood; a request may be read and still refused; a promise may be sent and still depend on later conditions. No delivery failure, confirmation, or revelatory last message is owed. Let the adventure advance through what was actually transmitted and what the characters then did, while the older story behind a phrase stays a private layer rather than a hidden mechanism in the interface.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Visual Novel\n\nLocate the objective through real positions and objects: the case on the table, the path beyond the gate, the key still in the lock, the person standing between the character and the door. Use concise scene-setting to make the space legible, then move among cue, dialogue, and full focal response as the scene requires. A background or expression cue can orient the moment—the character looks toward the sound, the light changes, someone's hand stops moving—without becoming a truth indicator. An expression may draw attention, but it does not prove a motive, a lie, or a hidden story.\n\nWhen a repeated cue recalls a tale or an earlier occasion, let the recall belong to the focal character. The same mark on a wall, the same name spoken, or the same object waiting in a new place can orient unchanged work or open a private association. The association is not evidence that the tale is true or that the world has begun to follow it. Keep actual inner experience available in the active required mode, clearly attributed and distinct from what other characters can see. Another character does not know the focal character's memory merely because the scene holds on the cue.\n\nPreserve active ownership, ordinary help, and pending results. A character may assist without a hidden price, refuse without a betrayal, or continue working without a completed outcome. The style supplies no route menu, portrait command, romance route, or magical revelation. Let the adventure advance through what the characters attempt, what they learn from the visible conditions, and what the next scene can actually show.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaarbb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Neil Gaiman / Web Serial\n\nReenter through the actual task state. Open with what is underway now—the route already taken, the object still missing, the person still waiting, the work still incomplete—and recover only the earlier detail needed for orientation or lived continuity. If a locked door matters again, say that it is the same door and that it is still locked; do not retell the first discovery. Keep resources, access, and known terms stable until actual events change them. A later account may qualify an interpretation without rewriting an earlier act.\n\nLet a recurring path, phrase, or practical promise link installments. The characters may pass the same landmark, repeat a direction they were given, or return to an agreement made earlier. The recurrence can deepen its story-like resonance or stay simply familiar. It does not require a new mystery, a hidden price, or a supernatural obligation. If the repetition recalls an older tale, keep that layer attributed to the person who remembers it, and let the current installment proceed on its own terms.\n\nLet work, pleasure, recovery, or private consideration span replies. An installment can close on completed work, a pending request, a person resting after a hard stretch, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. No threshold, reveal, or completed result is owed at the end. The through-line is the undertaking itself: what the characters are trying to do, what has changed locally, and what remains available to continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLocate emotional strain in an actual part of the undertaking: a request, shared decision, preparation, travel or recovery. Keep the practical need intelligible while allowing the permitted account to notice and develop what the moment means to the person. A memory, direct feeling or qualified comparison can accompany the work without requiring a conflict over independence, a mistaken belief or a turning point.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's role, person and access. The narrator need not be the participant doing or feeling the work. Where private experience is authorized, give the character's actual concern, affection, reluctance or reasoning its needed extent in the selected Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation. Another person's knowledge follows their own sources and permitted access rather than what the reader has seen of that experience.\n\nLet spoken language and conduct fit the actual character and relationship. A reply can match the thought, simplify it or leave part unspoken; strain does not require a prescribed pause, hurried task or flat answer. Feasibility and willingness remain distinct, and a comparison can stand without correction. The objective can advance, conclude or remain pending at its active boundary without a confession, catharsis, forced repair or polished emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by placing the submitted move within the current objective: what the character is trying to do, what means are already in hand, and what the submitted action actually does or leaves underway. Use readable paragraphs with clear dialogue attribution so each speaker remains unmistakable. Let the narrator's account develop around the move—noticing a detail, speculating about a consequence, or asking a practical question—without claiming access to a participant's private mind beyond what the active contract permits.\n\nKeep other characters active and independent. They may continue their own work, answer partially, misunderstand, or act on knowledge the narrator does not have. An authored person's private experience can be fuller or simpler than what they say, according to their actual attention and relationship. Preserve the narrator's own permitted role and form rather than making the narrator the participant by default. A comparison may be offered and then qualified, or left sound if the narrator has no reason to doubt it.\n\nPreserve the active ownership and narrative person. If the scene uses hidden interiority, render the character's actual nonspoken experience in the required wrapper; if it uses visible interiority, keep it in the ordinary prose. Do not turn the reply into a forced observation, question, or event. The task may remain pending, a request may go unanswered, or the work may advance immediately. End at the natural boundary of the submitted interval, with the next step available but not artificially demanded.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the classical or vernacular episodic method: each scene is a distinct local encounter with its own location, occasion, and relation to the larger journey. A scene may cover travel, discovery, preparation, rest, or a side encounter that does not advance the main objective. Open each episode by establishing where the characters are and what practical business is underway, then let the encounter unfold through action and attributed speech.\n\nLet the narrator carry forward a remembered practical detail or personal impression from an earlier episode. The narrator may explain a local comparison—how this place resembles or differs from a previous one—and qualify it when the present evidence does not support the memory. Keep the source clear: a participant's actual observation, a report, a memory or other knowledge available to the permitted narrator. Do not invent narrator participation to supply a recollection. A side encounter may remain ordinary or unfinished; it does not owe a lesson, a changed option, or a secret connection to the main objective.\n\nPreserve private experience and actual character voices. The narrator's explanatory account is not a stock storyteller identity imposed on every speaker. Other characters speak in their own registers, and their knowledge remains separate from the narrator's. Use clear transitions between episodes, which may themselves span replies. Keep the journey continuous without a new location, new encounter or solved problem required in each interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSelect the material details that matter to the attempt: grip, load, reach, wear, distance, or the condition of a tool. Introduce a relevant detail where use, preparation or explanation needs it; a real preparatory inventory is available without becoming a compulsory checklist. Use direct clauses and compressed transitions to move from one practical action to the next. Let the narrator's explanation arise inside the work—a tentative comparison, an inward feeling, or a qualification of an earlier impression—while preserving the actual work and experience; the length of their representation does not itself advance fictional time.\n\nKeep the authorized person's actual developed thought and feeling intact, through the narrator and person the active contract supplies. The compressed style trims repeated setup and redundant gloss, not the actual private extent or a recurring concern. A longer thought is permitted when the character's reasoning needs it. Material damage and abrasive speech must follow from the scene: a worn strap, a failed grip, a sharp reply under strain. Do not impose hardship or an ending slogan. An attempt can succeed, fail, or remain underway.\n\nVary the rhythm. Short declarative sentences can carry immediate physical action; a longer sentence can hold an authorized owned reflection or a practical explanation. Ground rough speech in its speaker and situation. The style makes effort and limits immediate, while the account preserves actual narrator, person and ownership permissions, including owned judgment and direct feeling without inventing an autobiographical participant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the document form the undertaking actually calls for: a field note, a letter requesting help, a report on conditions, a receipt, a log entry, or a message arranging the next stage. Give each document a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the document carry the practical sequence: what was attempted, what was observed, what remains to be done, and what the writer asks or reports.\n\nLet the writer explain a method, qualify an impression, or state affection plainly. The document is a chosen communication, not a complete live mind. The writer may omit something they did not understand, or phrase a concern for a particular reader. Preserve the difference between what the writer knew at the time of composition and what later information reveals. Earlier wording remains fixed as later documents arrive; do not retroactively correct it.\n\nKeep actual private feeling and reasoning separate from the document. If the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, render it in the required form outside the document's text. The document may hint at a private concern, but it does not replace the character's actual experience. Pending records and ordinary competent administration can sustain the objective. Let the sequence move through the documents' delivery and reception, with gaps and delays as part of the record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nChoose an actual accommodation within the undertaking: a dry place to rest, a meal prepared with a familiar preference, a pack adjusted, a route chosen to spare someone a harder climb. Follow what the accommodation feels like to an authorized person, retaining the active narrator's role rather than making them its recipient automatically. The account may notice a small pleasure, explain why a preference matters, or remember a previous time the same care was given. Let the affection be sincere and direct; do not require a joke, a confession, or an emotionally guarded recipient.\n\nKeep the undertaking available to continue while care or rest occupies the current scene; the moment need not improve the plan or immediately return to work. Shared work and ordinary dialogue may continue where the scene supports them, while silence or unhurried care can also occupy the interval. A person may accept help without discussing it, or say something plain that matches the private feeling. Preserve actual material conditions without requiring short rest, simple food or imperfect shelter. A person's private experience extends beyond its effect on the plan and remains in its active nonspoken presentation.\n\nDo not impose an earned-comfort test or a forced repair. The kindness can be simply enjoyable, and the scene can end with enjoyed company, ongoing rest or work resumed according to the active boundary. Personality, privacy, and disagreement remain intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin with the work or arrangement already underway: a route being prepared, a task being divided, a plan being explained. Use deliberate transitions to widen the scene through the narrator's permitted knowledge—a relevant history, a commitment, a practical explanation of why this attempt matters. Keep the widening within actual narrator access: observed or reported conditions, a participant's memory, inference or broader knowledge where authorized. Do not invent narrator participation or remove permitted knowledge to create the frame. Distinguish observed conditions, recollection, and inference as the paragraph develops.\n\nLet the narrator's judgment remain owned and revisable. A comparison may be offered and then corrected as new evidence appears. The narrator may explain a social or personal context without delivering a verdict about the undertaking. Developed private experience and direct feeling remain in the selected form; the formal paragraph movement does not flatten them into a lecture.\n\nReturn to the current activity. The widened context can deepen or sustain the account of the work, its stakes or history without requiring a new interpretation. Actual conditions determine whether the task concludes or remains pending. Use sustained paragraph movement rather than short reaction beats. Formal architecture is the style; archaic diction, an ornate setting, and uniformly elaborate speech are not automatically supplied. Preserve the narrator's conversational movement and actual role rather than polishing every paragraph into an impersonal institutional or moral pronouncement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nPlace the telling within a current activity: a halt during travel, a meal, a wait for weather, or a pause in work. Give the listeners a concrete reason to hear the account that belongs to the scene, such as deciding whether to trust a route, understanding a companion's skill, or passing time before a task resumes. Let the teller select and arrange the remembered attempt according to that present purpose, not as a complete history.\n\nRecount the earlier attempt through the teller's developed understanding. Show what the plan depended on, what went wrong or held, and what the teller noticed at the time. The teller may qualify a detail, admit uncertainty, or remain confident without revealing everything. A listener may interrupt with a practical question about timing, access or sequence; an answer or deflection follows the actual relationship rather than a required exchange.\n\nKeep the listeners' purposes independent. One may extract a usable method, another may enjoy the story, and a third may remain unconvinced. The telling can change what they decide to do next, but it need not resolve their disagreement. Return to the actual outer task and listener state, including an unfinished tale, unchanged understanding or pending action. Actual performance and intervening events set duration; narrated length does not itself spend the interval or require a changed option. Preserve the teller's actual private experience during the telling as distinct from the narrated past; the story is a composed performance, not a transparent record of the teller's live mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism to connect actual attempts through remembered preparation, help or an earlier explanation of a method. When a participant uses a skill or resource and its origin matters, preserve the actual source of their preparation. Skill may be self-taught or already established; do not invent a teacher or require its history before every use. The remembered lesson need not be confirmed by the present outcome; it may hold, fail, or simply remain one way of doing the work.\n\nFollow the practical learning across attempts. When experience changes the person's approach, make the connection specific, such as a different knot or a revised route check. Their method may also remain sound and unchanged, without a new learning event. Do not require every attempt to produce a new lesson. Some work is simply repeated, and some learning is already settled.\n\nLet the narrator explain how a person approaches work while affection, uncertainty, and direct feeling remain independently meaningful. A companion's help can be remembered with warmth without becoming a moral lesson. Preserve real skills and resources across the trajectory; competence may grow, plateau, or be tested without a required failure or disillusionment. Respect active access and earlier knowledge: later understanding may include knowledge actually acquired since, but that knowledge must not be inserted into the earlier actor's mind or motive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere inherited authority, protection, or obligation bears on the undertaking, show what is offered and who can actually provide it. A gate, a pass, a debt, or a name may open a route; let the narrator explain the arrangement's terms as they are encountered, not as an abstract system. The person offering help has their own history and limits, and the narrator may notice the place's material detail—the worn threshold, the locked cabinet, the light through a barred window—without turning atmosphere into evidence.\n\nLet the permitted personal account notice the place, ask about a term, remember earlier experience, or consider accepting. An authorized participant may feel drawn to an arrangement or wary of it, but that feeling is distinct from its actual power; preserve the narrator's role rather than assigning them the participant's position. A beautiful room can still be a practical dead end; a decaying building can still hold a working route. Render an authorized person's actual private response through the permitted narrator and selected nonspoken presentation: attraction, distrust, gratitude, and uncertainty can coexist without a required confession or moral test.\n\nSupport can work or stay pending. The old authority may provide exactly what was promised, or its help may come with conditions the participant can consider according to the actual arrangement. No new institution, debt, or hidden corruption follows from the style. The undertaking remains the story's spine; inherited power is one of the obstacles or resources along the way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric and philosophical inwardness to develop a personal question prompted by an actual sensation, a companion's remark or an ordinary task: what the work means or what the person hopes for. The question need not be announced; it can grow from the feel of a rope in the hand, a pause at a crossroads, or a phrase someone says without knowing its weight. Develop the question through memory, comparison, and direct feeling in the authorized mind.\n\nKeep the practical activity distinct from the thought's conclusions. The participant may continue tying a load, walking a path or checking a map while the question unfolds; keep the active narrator distinct when they are not that participant. A decision to act does not prove an answer; the person may act without certainty, or act in a way that leaves the question open. The question may stay clear, uncertain, or unspoken, and no practical outcome is required to resolve it.\n\nPreserve supplied beliefs. If the authorized person holds a faith, a loyalty or a conviction, the inward question may test it, rest within it, or leave it untouched, but the style does not require a belief crisis or a correction. Keep actual time and private extent independent of prose length: a long inward passage may occupy a short pause, and a brief thought may carry a long day's weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasal::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nPresent the story through actual recording occasions: a participant pauses at the end of a day, during a wait, or after a conversation to write down a practical observation, a remembered exchange, a pleasure, or an unresolved comparison. Each entry is a selection, not a complete transcript of the day. The writer may record the state of the route, the condition of equipment, or a companion's words without explaining everything they privately thought or felt at the time.\n\nLet later entries revisit earlier wording without rewriting earlier knowledge. The writer may notice that a recorded hope now seems distant, or that a remembered method proved useful, but the earlier entry remains what it was. Do not require a new insight at each return; some entries simply continue the record of travel and work.\n\nKeep travel and material state recoverable across fragments. A reader should be able to follow where the party is, what they carry, and what remains to be done. Convey entries through the active narrator/person frame; if the active narrator is not the diarist, keep the entries attributed within the permitted narrative frame rather than changing narrator identity or inventing a reading action for them. Actual private experience stays separate from recorded selections, with no fixed quietness, seasonal image, or concluding lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasam::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social chronicle to follow how preparation, help, use or recovery enters a shared account. Distinguish witnessed work from spoken reports and actual records: a person may see a bridge repaired, hear that a guide has arrived, or read a note left at a waystation. Each source carries different certainty and different gaps. The narrator can explain a contribution without requiring praise, exposure, or unanimous interpretation.\n\nLet the community remain independently occupied. The people who help have their own work, errands and reasons; they may wait for the travelers when an actual arrangement calls for it, without existing solely as available helpers. A contribution may be given freely, traded, or offered with conditions. The narrator may understand only part of what a helper did, and the chronicle can record that partial understanding without inventing an omniscient explanation.\n\nKeep private experience distinct from what others have heard. An authorized person may privately doubt a report, feel grateful or worry about a debt while the public account records what was communicated. Keep that actual private experience in its selected nonspoken form without making the narrator the traveler by default. Preserve active access, chronology, and ordinary lives outside the objective. No political allegory or new viewpoint is required each interval; the chronicle simply makes the undertaking's social texture legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasan::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Light Novel\n\nOrient the current objective, relevant positions, and what is underway at the start of each scene. A reader should know where the party is, what they are trying to do, and what stands in the way without a lengthy recap. Introduce a needed method through preparation, explanation, or use: a tool can be shown before it is needed, a route can be explained as it is chosen, or a skill can be demonstrated when it matters.\n\nParagraph where attention, speech, or action actually shifts. A change of speaker, a move from one place to another, or a turn from outward action to private thought can begin a new paragraph. The personal account may qualify an assumption, understand plainly, or think at length without changing the plan. A narrator's developed explanation is not a pacing failure; it is part of the scene's information flow.\n\nKeep ordinary voices and actual nonspoken experience. A companion may speak plainly, joke, or stay quiet according to their own character, not a required reaction pattern. Fictional time is governed by events rather than text length: a long rendering of inward experience may occupy a short interval, while an actual spoken explanation takes its own fictional duration; a quick action may be described in a single sentence. No youth, joke, demonstration, or completed result is owed at the boundary; the scene ends at its active boundary, which may leave the physical work underway or a response pending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Lyrical Prose\n\nAnchor the lyrical movement in an actual material relation from the undertaking: the sound a rope makes under load, the repeated weight of a carried object, the rhythm of walking over changing ground, or the way a tool answers the hand. Let the narrator or focal person develop, qualify, or retain a comparison through their own attention. A comparison may become useful, prove incomplete, or be set aside when the work demands it; it is not evidence and it does not alter the world's facts.\n\nKeep the task itself legible in plain clauses. When the figurative movement has gathered force, return to the concrete position, means, or next action in the permitted voice, with an explicit transition or an unobtrusive one as the account calls for. The plain account and the shaped image may sit close together, and the same passage may move from one to the other as the person's attention does. Do not require an insight, a completed task, or a practical result after an inward passage. Rest, waiting, and enjoyment can have duration too.\n\nLet direct feeling and individually ordinary speech remain available alongside the lyrical telling. Another character may answer from their own practical concern, miss the comparison, or simply continue the work. Recurrence should come from the undertaking's actual sensory material rather than from an ornamental phrase repeated at every difficulty. Preserve the active narrator's access and ownership: the lyrical development belongs to the person whose attention is authorized, not to every participant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nUse an established earlier occasion tied to the present route, task, object, or relationship. Let the person notice a small change or a point of continuity: the same door now opens differently, a remembered instruction no longer fits the present conditions, or someone's way of helping has altered. Keep what the person knew then distinct from what they understand now. A memory may explain a present hesitation or a present confidence without becoming a corrected lesson.\n\nLet the reflection stay in the person's own terms. Private affection, regret, uncertainty, and present pleasure can remain meaningful without altering the task. The work may finish, continue, or pause without redeeming the past or announcing impermanence. Do not force a revelation of loss or foolishness, and do not require the remembered person to become a moral lesson.\n\nKeep the undertaking's material chronology clear. The remembered occasion can inform a choice, but the present attempt still depends on its actual means, conditions, and cooperation. A companion may share the memory, recall it differently, or be concerned with the immediate problem. Preserve the active narrator's access and ownership; the remembered feeling belongs to the person whose interiority is authorized, not to every participant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the undertaking through a modern memoir: a participant account located in an established later telling. Keep the active narrator distinct where person and ownership require it. The later voice may explain, confirm, or qualify an earlier interpretation using actual sources, but it cannot replace what the actor then knew, intended, or privately experienced. Mark the difference between the earlier lived attempt and the later account's understanding.\n\nPreserve the work's material chronology. The memoir may select and arrange remembered events, but the underlying sequence of means, conditions, contributions, and effects remains intelligible. When the later voice corrects or qualifies an earlier reading, ground the correction in a specific detail the actor noticed, a record they kept, or a consequence they could not then foresee. Do not add a migration, confession, lesson, or corrected memory that the supplied world does not already permit.\n\nLet the memoir's occasion and audience shape what the teller includes. The account may be candid, partial, or shaped by the relationship to its intended reader, but the actual private experience of the earlier self remains distinct from the memoir's recorded selections. Preserve the active narrator's access and ownership. The later voice may reflect on the earlier undertaking without becoming omniscient, and the earlier self may have been wrong, uncertain, or simply occupied with the immediate task.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the relevant object, position, or received words that explain what someone is trying to do. A single exact detail can carry the plan's dependence: the angle of a brace, the state of a rope, the distance left to cover, or the instruction someone gave. Keep causal information at the extent needed. The reader should understand what the attempt depends on and what the next action changes.\n\nKeep actual private feeling or reasoning at the extent needed as well. A recurring concern may return without requiring a new decision, and a person may explain a hesitation in one direct clause. Cut redundant qualification after the meaning is clear, not genuine conversational movement or useful explanation. Direct dialogue and an unfinished task remain possible without a fragment quota, cryptic omission, or final aphorism.\n\nLet the narrator or focal person own the inward connection. Another character may speak from their own practical concern, and the narration need not interpret every reply. Preserve the active narrator's access and ownership. The style's economy comes from selecting the detail that matters, not from making every character terse or every thought a confident tactical line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasas::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that continues the activity already underway. Move naturally among current work, dialogue, observation, memory, and authorized private experience. A comparison may be useful, incomplete, or qualified later. Another character can answer from their own practical concern instead of supplying the narrator's intended lesson.\n\nPreserve actual means, contributions, and effects alongside ordinary pleasure or affection. Show what the plan depends on and what the attempt changes through the scene's concrete movement: who carries what, who notices a problem, who improvises, and how the next opportunity opens or narrows. Thought may be developed or brief according to the person. Do not impose a fixed ratio between private experience and spoken exchange, a required antagonist, a new insight, or a completed task each reply.\n\nKeep the narrator's access and ownership consistent. The account belongs to the person whose perspective is active; other participants remain independently occupied and may know more, less or different facts according to their actual sources. Paragraphing and transitions should orient the reader without recap machinery. The scene may end with the work resumed, paused, or altered, with the actual status clear; the undertaking may be finished or still open without withholding a genuine completion or inventing a new opportunity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual bond and established history to select preparation, reliance, or recovery. A familiar phrase, task division, or returning gesture can remain welcome, become complicated, or mean little in the current moment. Let speech fit this relationship without requiring it to reveal or hide a prescribed amount of thought. The same person may ask one companion for help directly, explain a plan differently to another, or accept assistance without naming what it means.\n\nPreserve practical capacity, direct affection, and separate memories. Cooperation may work without confession, mutual understanding, or a repaired bond. The undertaking's obstacles remain real: the plan depends on actual means, timing, and competence, and the relationship shapes how the participants coordinate rather than replacing the work with emotional resolution.\n\nKeep private experience individually owned. The narration may reveal one person's reasoning, hesitation, or pleasure while the other participant retains what their actual sources and permitted access make available, without gaining the private experience merely because the reader has followed it. Recurrence should come from the established history: a shared joke, a remembered failure, a division of labor that once worked. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes. The scene may end with the bond unchanged, tested, or quietly supported, without a required payoff or crisis.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurrent phrasing grounded in actual contributions. A person may be introduced by a deed they performed or a way they helped, then expanded through the particulars behind that reputation, whether they confirm or complicate the public description. Recount the real means and conditions of a deed without replacing them with reputation: who carried what, who repaired it, who refused, and what the attempt changed.\n\nKeep earlier knowledge and owned private experience distinct from later telling. A participant can agree with the public account or answer it through ordinary practical detail. The chronicle may say someone was dependable; the focal account can show that helping was tiring, uncertain, or done for a reason the public story does not record. Preserve the active narrator's access and ownership. The remembered shape belongs to the telling, while the private experience belongs to the person whose interiority is authorized.\n\nReturn to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application, not as an ornamental epithet at every entrance. The account can end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered. No heroism, debunking, moral lesson, or finished feat per interval is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nFollow the undertaking through the actual social and domestic arrangements it enters: shared space, transport, schedules, household work, or care. Let a preparation, recovery, or request occupy the scene alongside the lives already running there. Show what the plan depends on by making the practical conditions concrete—who is available, what must be done first, what cannot be moved—and let the narrator notice when another person's ordinary obligations shape the answer.\n\nUse the manuscript voice to explain how a request is received without claiming access beyond the active narrator and ownership permissions; an actual known motive need not be made uncertain. The narrator may speculate about a delay, remember an earlier arrangement, or qualify a comparison between what was expected and what is actually happening. Keep that reasoning owned and revisable. Assistance may be reliable or limited for actual reasons: a schedule, a prior promise, a household task, a preference. Do not make every helper either perfectly available or secretly resentful.\n\nLet private feeling, practical learning, and pleasure exist beyond task efficiency. A meal, a familiar route, a repaired object, or a moment of ease can matter without advancing the objective. The scene may end with the work still pending, a partial arrangement accepted, or an ordinary continuation underway. No changed arrangement or lesson is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes to show the current attempt. Make positions, objects, and actual effects visible or audible: what the character reaches, moves, repairs, or fails to hold. Establish the relevant condition and the actual state of the attempt where the scene needs them, without requiring the next block to deliver a changed result.\n\nA practical explanation can be spoken or privately considered according to character and circumstance. Do not force a lecture or a silent-motive diagnosis. When a character explains something to another person, keep the dialogue fitted to the relationship and the immediate physical task. When the explanation is private, preserve it as actual nonspoken experience in the active visible or hidden interiority, separate from any separately authorized voice-over; performed speech does not replace actual nonspoken private experience.\n\nEstablish relevant offscreen work through actual reports or effects: a character returns with a result, a sound arrives from elsewhere, a condition has changed between scenes. Keep those conditions consistent across cuts. A pending attempt may remain pending, and an ordinary pause may end the scene without a manufactured cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stage Play\n\nPlace the objective's current operation in coherent blocking, entrances, and props. Let the physical business carry the scene: who holds the object, who can reach the door, what must be moved before the next step. Dialogue should fit real coordination, uncertainty, or affection while the work continues. A speaker may ask for help, warn of a limit, or answer a practical question without turning the exchange into an explanatory speech.\n\nAn authorized private comparison or feeling needs no aside, monologue, or immediate change in action. Preserve it as actual nonspoken experience in the active visible or hidden interiority. Public knowledge follows actual audibility and other established access; another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nOffstage help can be conveyed through credible reports or effects: a character enters with what was fetched, a sound indicates work elsewhere, a condition has changed between scenes. End with the actual arrangements clear, including continued work or an unanswered request. The scene may stop after a result or an interruption without an obligatory gathering or theatrical revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasay::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow an authorized mind through perception, memory, private speech, and interruption as they actually arise during the undertaking. A sensory detail may prompt a comparison, an imagined reply, or an unrelated pleasure; none must produce a corrected plan. Let the syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations while enough orientation remains to distinguish current events from recollection and imagined trials.\n\nKeep the actual undertaking recoverable. The reader should know what the character is doing, what the plan depends on, and what the attempt changes even when the mind moves away from the task. A remembered instruction, a past failure, or an imagined obstacle can enter the flow without replacing the present action. Source other voices to memory or communication rather than granting their minds.\n\nPreserve needed private extent and event-grounded time. An unfinished chain of thought is permitted at the active boundary; the scene may stop with the work still underway, a question unresolved, or an association left open. Do not force a neatly completed explanation or a lesson from every interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasaz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages for an actual coordination need in the undertaking. Keep wording, delivery, comprehension, and action separate. A message may be sent, delayed, read, misunderstood, or answered according to the actual digital habits of each person. Information received through other established channels remains distinct from the transcript.\n\nA long explanation, brief request, or plain confirmation can fit the relationship. Thought and sent text may agree or differ for actual reasons: embarrassment, attention, utility, uncertainty, or habit. Preserve actual nonspoken feeling and reasoning separately in the active visible or hidden interiority. An unsent draft requires supported composition rather than imagined wording; show actual composition, editing or an unsent text only when it belongs to the scene, including supported dictation or other input rather than requiring typing.\n\nLet ordinary or pending exchanges continue without an acknowledgment, misunderstanding, or result quota. The transcript may end with a message unanswered, a task still underway, or a plain confirmation that changes nothing dramatic. Do not fabricate interface events merely to add subtext.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration or inner response where the current work needs them, without a fixed rotation. A background, object, or expression can prompt a remembered comparison or orient the moment, but it does not certify motive or an unseen condition. Let a changed expression attract attention without making it a truth indicator.\n\nKeep the method or practical effect understandable through actual use. Show what the character reaches, moves, repairs, or fails to hold, and let the focal passage explain what that means to the person doing it. Private experience can develop without changing action; a thought may qualify a comparison, recall an earlier attempt, or feel the strain of a pending decision without producing an immediate result.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and person. Do not add assets, menus, romance routes, or a short-thought rule. An interval can remain unfinished, with the scene ending on a clear arrangement, a pending attempt, or an ordinary pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaasbb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nemo / Manuscript Voice / Web Serial\n\nReenter through the actual task, relevant prior details, and what remains available. Preserve resources, learned limits, help, and earlier knowledge rather than resetting them to supply tension. A person can revisit a comparison, retain a feeling, or explain something later without rewriting the earlier motive. Briefly recover the necessary particular rather than retelling the whole journey that established it.\n\nLet ordinary work, affection, discovery, and recovery share space alongside harder passages. The narrator may notice a familiar detail, qualify an earlier expectation, or continue a practical explanation across installments. Keep the through-line legible: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue.\n\nNo new obstacle, insight, joke, or completed local task is an installment fee. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts and relationships room to acquire meaning, not make a fair resolution perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nAnchor the scene in a concrete objective, then let the cost of a particular word surface through the actual work of asking for, offering, or accepting help. When a character uses a term like \"help,\" \"lead,\" \"wait,\" or \"trust,\" keep the exact wording in play. Make any actual personal risk in using that word concrete without requiring a safer alternative to exist: a need admitted, a debt accepted, a fear exposed, or a relationship redefined. Another character may respond to the word's scope, its tone, or the immediate practical request according to what they actually notice. One may answer the literal request while the other hears a larger claim. The strain may influence a practical choice or remain privately meaningful while work continues unchanged, without requiring confession, reconciliation, or verbal defeat. Private thought may revise the word's meaning after it is spoken, but the spoken version remains the actual wording; who heard, understood, or acted on it follows from the scene's events. Feeling may resolve or persist through the person's actual reflection, speech, or experience. A changed interpretation does not retroactively erase the earlier words, cost, or events.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen from the submitted contribution, keeping the current objective, positions, and available means legible where the current interval needs orientation. When a spoken line changes what someone is willing to do, present the actual wording in the active dialogue format with unmistakable attribution. Render any authorized private interpretation at its causal time, or show the listener's actual public response without granting unprovided access. The distinction may affect cooperation, clarify an existing understanding, or remain unresolved without an immediate change: one character may act on a literal reading, another may ask for clarification, and a third may continue the physical work without commenting. Keep each paragraph scannable, with dialogue attributed and action separated from speech. End the interval at a natural point in the shared activity—a task completed, a route chosen, a question left open—without forcing a clever twist, a cliffhanger, or a changed condition. Respect the active ownership contract for all choices and private experience; no character acts beyond their established means or knowledge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and satirical realism as the route. Introduce an ordinary designation or courtesy—\"volunteer,\" \"guide,\" \"guest,\" \"local,\" \"friend of the family\"—that carries weight in the current undertaking. Show the phrase through the people actually present and their own motives. Someone may rely on its social meaning, question it, use it accurately, or continue without examining it; no speaker count or fixed division of roles is required. Place the wording beside what accepting it would permit, request, or concede. A character may agree to a task because the term flatters them, or refuse because the term obligates them. Let the actual conduct and context clarify the relation between the label and the work, whether aligned or different; cleverness alone does not settle it. A speaker can be sincere and the term accurate; the satire comes from the social machinery around it, not from unmasking every user as a hypocrite. The undertaking continues through practical consequences—a door opened, a load carried, a route accepted—rather than through a new linguistic controversy.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the physical work legible through relevant weight, reach, material condition, footing, and actual time, while private experience follows its proper causal place rather than a mandatory public-first order. When a disputed instruction or promise matters, place the exact spoken line beside the labor and its bodily conditions without requiring the work itself to stop. A character may object to the word \"secure\" while still tying the knot, or argue about \"ahead\" while already moving. Make any effect of a correction on the expected task concrete, while allowing clarification, unchanged work, or a pending interpretation without replacing the material situation with debate. Trim redundant verbal performance while preserving purposeful repetition, hesitation, or a private question that genuinely persists. Give actual private thought, feeling, memory, and attention the development the character's experience needs; it need not produce a tactic or immediately hand the scene back to action. Speakers can be blunt or expansive as their own character permits, but no threat, insult, or semantic victory is required in every exchange. End at the active scene boundary with the actual work or pending response clear, without requiring an advance, a stall, or a winning argument.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through setting-supported documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. When a revision affects a promise or intended task, reproduce the exact wording of both versions rather than summarizing the change. Distinguish an unsent draft from a received instruction: the draft may show what the writer feared or considered, while the received text establishes what reached its recipients; what they read, understood, or acted on follows from the actual subsequent events. A later annotation must not be mistaken for what the original writer knew at the time. Let the documents carry the undertaking forward—a route described, a supply list corrected, a permission granted or refused—without requiring every record to contain a hidden contradiction. Writers may be plain or elaborate according to their own habits. Actual nonspoken experience exceeds the record; a document can be accurate, unanswered, or useful without resolving the private cost of writing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nLet the undertaking include room for ordinary help and small pleasures: a familiar phrase during preparation, a patient answer to a question about an instruction, a shared meal during rest, a remembered preference honored on the road. A verbal distinction may remove friction, accompany shared pleasure, or remain unresolved; it should not become a compulsory test others must pass to receive care. One character may ask what \"ready\" means and receive a plain answer; another may enjoy a pun without being required to perform one back. Keep differing tastes and unresolved concerns available—someone can decline the wordplay and still be cared for, someone can accept help without proving anything. Care need not arrive through a clever answer or an emotional confession. Keep the practical objective legible, whether work advances, continues, or pauses, while warmth stays in the chosen details: who remembers the tea, who checks the straps, who saves the last portion for someone else.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose in deliberate, sustained paragraphs that move from an actual statement into its context and back to the person acting on it. When a recurring public term—\"expedition,\" \"escort,\" \"claim,\" \"settlement\"—appears, keep three layers distinct: what the words state, what a listener or institution infers from them, and what the character acting on them privately understands. Do not demand an analytical speech from the cast; the narrator may frame the relation between layers, aligned or different, where the chosen perspective permits. Let a designation retain or change significance as events support it: a \"guide\" may become a \"hostage,\" a \"volunteer\" may become a \"witness,\" but a narrated shift must follow what actually happens and what the narrator may know; naming it does not create a new role or condition by itself. A paragraph or scene may stop before a linguistic resolution, leaving the term still contested while the practical work continues. Use the formal architecture to hold the social context, history, or judgment around the undertaking without turning every speaker into a philosopher.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nSituate the embedded tale within the actual undertaking, whether during rest, travel, ongoing work, or waiting. Make the teller, audience, and reason for the occasion clear, including enjoyment or passing time without a required practical gain. The listener may want a route, a debt, an ally, a warning, or permission to trust the teller; the teller may want cooperation, delay, justification, or a chance to be believed. Let those purposes shape what the tale includes.\n\nMake the tale's central material an earlier instruction, promise, or disputed description whose exact wording mattered then and is being interpreted again now. Preserve the earlier scene's actual words and the knowledge available to its participants. When the teller repeats or revises a phrase in the outer scene, show whether the change comes from memory, later information, or present purpose. The listener may catch the difference, ask a literal question about it, or remain unconvinced without the tale becoming false.\n\nDo not let the outer scene's final interpretation become an automatic lesson. The teller's account can be sincere, partial, self-protective, or mistaken in specific ways. The listener can accept a useful part of it while rejecting the teller's conclusion. A phrase repeated across the two levels should help the reader compare what was said then with what is being claimed now, not certify the tale as true.\n\nKeep the present adventure's physical work and private experience independent of the performed explanation. A character may think one thing while telling another, or may think through the tale's implications only after speaking. When attention returns to the outer scene, keep the actual work and response clear. The listener may use the account, continue listening, remain undecided, or leave the practical situation unchanged without a required next action. No changed plan, riddle, or cliffhanger is required at the return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist absurdist construction as the formal route. Establish one finite classification or rule that the actual setting supports and that bears on the undertaking's objective: what counts as arrival, delivery, permission, completion, ownership, or a valid attempt. State its terms precisely enough that a character can act on them, object to them, or discover that their ordinary wording does not match the work they have already done.\n\nLet the absurdity reside in the construction, not in random malice or universal confusion. A gate may require a permit that can only be issued by the person seeking entry; a route may be officially closed because it was never officially opened; a task may be counted complete only when repeated by someone who did not begin it. Keep the rule's power finite and its consequences observable. The cast can respond with practical improvisation, legal argument, physical work, or refusal, and a response may succeed without overturning the entire arrangement.\n\nUse Nisio's verbal encounters to make the rule's wording an active obstacle. A character may propose a definition, another may object to its scope, and a third may revise the wording until it permits a different action. Show what each speaker wants from the definition and what they can actually do with it. A clever reinterpretation has effects only through the authority, means, and choices present in the scene; it does not rewrite the world by itself.\n\nKeep the undertaking's physical dimension legible. Keep the state of travel, carrying, building, search, or other work clear; it may continue, pause, or not yet have begun during a discussion. Private reasoning may be elaborate while spoken replies remain plain, or a character may argue at length without reaching a usable conclusion. When an attempt occurs, ground it in the actual circumstances and the interpretation the actor uses, while the exchange may remain unresolved rather than delivering surviving wording or a general lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Gothic-Punk\n\nUse established authority only where it bears on the undertaking. An inherited title, family promise, old permit, ceremonial obligation, or institutional privilege can govern access to a place, a person, a resource, or a course of action. Put the ceremonial or familiar term beside what the arrangement really lets a person receive, refuse, or be asked to do. The collision between old authority and present life should be felt in the scene's material detail: a modern door under an old inscription, a working key attached to a dead condition, a current debt expressed in inherited language.\n\nMake the wording of the authority an active Nisio-style encounter. A character may claim a right under one phrase while another reads the same phrase as a restriction. Show the exact terms each participant thinks they accepted and what each stands to gain or lose by the interpretation. A verbal loophole has effects only through actual authority, means, and choices; it cannot open a locked route or cancel an obligation unless the established arrangement permits that consequence.\n\nWhere a personal relationship bears on the public terms, give its actual attraction, ease, or tension room without requiring a complication. A trusted companion may speak plainly while the focal character privately rehearses a more elaborate interpretation; a mask may fall away in private without changing what the person can publicly do. Preserve sincere help and negotiated boundaries. One character may genuinely assist another while still bound by an old condition, and a refusal may be practical rather than cruel. Do not import vampire physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules.\n\nKeep the adventure's physical work concrete. The cast should still travel, search, carry, negotiate, or confront while the old authority shapes their options. A revised or accepted term may inform an action through the actual access or obligation it governs, or leave work and a response pending without a new act at that moment. No compulsory coercion, deception, or tragic collapse is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse situated participant testimony as the formal route. Let an authorized participant in the undertaking recount their contribution in their own verbal habits: a guide explaining a route, a carrier describing a delivery, a companion accounting for a delay, or a survivor explaining what they understood an instruction to mean. Give the account an occasion and an audience with a practical stake in the present adventure.\n\nMake the testimony's central material a promise, instruction, or disputed description whose wording mattered during the earlier action. Let the speaker repeat or revise the phrase now, and attribute any revision to actual memory, later information, or the speaker's present communicative purpose, which need not be self-protective. The account may be candid or self-protective without being automatically false. A listener may accept the useful part of the testimony while noticing what it omits or rewords.\n\nSeparate three layers of knowledge: what the speaker directly experienced, what they received from others, and what they now explain after the fact. A later explanation cannot become the earlier motive. Keep the active narrator and person independent of the testifying voice; the narration may reveal private experience that the speaker does not say aloud, and another character does not automatically know it.\n\nUse Nisio's verbal precision to make the testimony an active encounter. A listener may ask what a word covered, object that the speaker's present wording differs from the original instruction, or propose a narrower reading. The speaker may defend, correct, or accept the revision. Listeners may use what the account supports alongside their actual means and authority, or leave a judgment or response pending; the testimony does not itself grant permission or require a next action. No required deception, confession, or semantic reveal is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatal::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose modern experimental inward fiction as the formal route. Let the syntax or paragraph shape follow an authorized character's actual self-correction, repetition, or uncertainty about what to call a request, action, or obstacle. A sentence may begin with one word for the task, interrupt itself, and settle on another; a paragraph may circle a phrase the character cannot yet say aloud. Keep the movement of thought specific to that mind rather than making every character a hyperverbal philosopher.\n\nPreserve the external undertaking beneath the inward experiment. The words actually heard, the physical positions, and the next attempted action should remain identifiable even when the narration's surface is associative or self-revising. A reader should be able to recover what happened and what was said, not only how the focal character experienced it.\n\nUse Nisio's verbal encounters in both inward and spoken forms. A character may privately rename an action before speaking, or may hear another person's definition and spend a paragraph testing its scope without answering. The mind need not revise every claim or become brilliantly self-aware. Private language may persist without influencing speech or the next move; a character can think through a precise objection and then say something ordinary, or can speak before the inward revision is complete.\n\nLet the adventure's physical work continue through the experimental prose. Travel, search, repair, pursuit, or confrontation should remain legible as events, not dissolve into pure reflection. A change in naming may alter an attempt, clarify an existing view, or register private feeling without a new understanding or changed action; keep its actual relation to the undertaking legible. No required semantic breakthrough or end-of-scene resolution is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatam::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic and nonlinear telling as the formal route. Organize the account of a shared instruction, promise, or encounter through several attributed sources placed at different telling times and event times. A companion may recall the instruction during the journey, another may describe it after the failure, and a third may quote it in a later dispute. Make each source and its permitted access clear; do not let the narration slide into an unattributed chorus.\n\nPreserve the exact wording of the shared statement where its interpretation matters. When different accounts quote it differently, show what each speaker knew at the original moment and what they have learned since. A later explanation cannot rewrite the original context or become the earlier motive. The versions may agree or differ without a required deception; a speaker can misremember, paraphrase for a purpose, or quote accurately while misunderstanding the scope.\n\nUse Nisio's verbal precision to make the polyphony active. One account may define a term, another may object to that definition, and a third may revise it in light of a different part of the journey. Let the reader compare the accounts through the recurring phrase rather than through an omniscient narrator's final correction. The actual undertaking remains the common object: travel, delivery, rescue, pursuit, or confrontation.\n\nKeep private experience available under the active contract even when the surface is polyphonic. A character's nonspoken thought may differ from their attributed account, and another character does not automatically know it. When accounts converge or diverge, keep any actual decision and its grounds clear while allowing participants to continue work or leave the interpretation and response pending. No new voice, pun, or twist is owed per reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatan::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish the positions, objective, and relevant task where the current scene needs orientation, without a fixed first-line recital. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when the group moves, climbs, crosses, or enters a new area, state the change plainly before the next exchange.\n\nUse attributed dialogue for every spoken line, and let a Nisio-style verbal encounter arise from the practical situation. A character may define the task, object to a companion's wording, or correct a term just before the group acts on it. When interpretation concerns words already spoken, keep their actual wording clear; prospective wording and the thought before a chosen line remain private experience at their actual causal point. A person may reason at length privately, answer plainly, or ignore the verbal play entirely; no fixed reaction cycle is required.\n\nLet paragraphs follow actual changes of attention rather than a required action-dialogue-thought sequence. Private experience may precede a chosen act, accompany it, or follow an event at its actual causal time, with the development it needs and no automatic transfer into another person's knowledge.\n\nWhere an exchange affects what the group tries, make the relation concrete through the route, object, position, or accepted condition involved. A clarification may also confirm an existing understanding or remain pending without an immediate practical change or semantic reveal. Clear presentation should not require a young cast, a joke, an argument, or a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the practical objective remain legible beneath the prose's rhythm. Establish the immediate task, route, or obstacle in plain terms before allowing a word or image to gather associative weight. When a character speaks, preserve the exact wording and its function in the undertaking; a metaphor may echo that wording later without changing what was actually said or proving that a listener knew an intention that was not communicated.\n\nDevelop recurrence through the authorized narrator's attention rather than through universal ornament. A sound, object, or phrase may return when the viewpoint character notices it under changed physical conditions: the same word heard across wind, machinery, or distance; the same landmark seen from a different approach. Let syntax and repeated sounds shape the paragraph's movement, but keep the recurring term attached to the scene's material facts. A phrase can remain personally significant without requiring a new interpretation or a confession.\n\nUse verbal encounters as part of the journey's pressure. A definition, objection, or revised wording may alter what participants believe they are permitted to do, what help they can request, or what a promise obliges them to attempt. Make any actual consequence of that change legible, including an interpretation that remains private or a response that is still pending without a new decision. The lyrical development may slow a moment for attention, but it should not suspend the objective; return to plain conditions, physical effort, or the next necessary step when orientation is needed. Private feeling may deepen without proving a hidden truth or making every speaker adopt the narrator's figurative register.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nAnchor reflection in a present task beside an actual remembered occasion, distinguishing what has changed from what remains available or familiar. Begin with the work, route, or obstacle now underway, then let a returning word, request, or promise bring the earlier moment close. Keep the two times distinct: what the character knew then, what they can verify now, and what they merely remember as the sense of what was said. A revised understanding belongs to the present, not to the earlier motive.\n\nWhen a remembered phrase returns, preserve its original wording where the character can recall it exactly, and mark uncertainty where they cannot. The melancholy may reside in the gap between the phrase's former use and its current application: the same promise made under easier conditions, the same request now answered by a different person or with different resources. Let the character retain affection, regret, or uncertainty without making the old attachment a mistake or the present task a tragedy.\n\nKeep the adventure objective operative. Reflection may accompany preparation, travel, recovery, or an actual pause. A character may act on it or continue with the same task and understanding; no immediate act is required to justify the private experience. A verbal encounter in the present may echo or contradict the remembered wording, and its practical effect should be shown. The scene can end with work succeeding, continuing, or pausing without requiring a confession, a betrayal, or an announcement that everything has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route. Let the account be composed by a participant with a clear occasion for writing or recollection, and keep the adventure's objective, route, and obstacles recoverable through that account. When the memoir records an earlier practical choice or spoken exchange, preserve the original wording as the teller can attest to it. If the teller later corrects, qualifies, or confirms a reading, mark that commentary as belonging to the time of composition, not to the earlier moment.\n\nMake verbal self-presentation concrete. A definition, objection, or revised phrase from the remembered undertaking may be quoted and then examined: what the speaker meant then, what the teller understood then, and what the teller now thinks the wording permitted or excluded. Do not let the later self-description become the earlier motive. The memoir's language can be precise and self-aware without requiring an obligatory confession or a complete reinterpretation of the past.\n\nKeep the active narrator, narrative person, and access independent of the memoir's author. Preserve the memoirist's or other authorized focal person's actual nonspoken experience separately from what the composed record contains. If the active contract authorizes private interiority, render it separately from the composed record; the memoir is an authored artifact with its own audience and purpose, not a transparent window into the writer's present mind. The remembered undertaking may remain unresolved, and the present need not produce a new act or revised belief. End at the active scene boundary with the account and undertaking in their actual states, without requiring the record's occasion to be satisfied or a new practical question to emerge.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few details that carry the present objective: the route, the obstacle, the tool, the person whose help is needed. Use direct clauses to move between material conditions, omitting redundant restatement while allowing a meaningful distinction or private question to recur. When a character speaks, give the exact wording and the circumstance that makes its scope matter. A definition, objection, or correction may change what someone will attempt, permit, or refuse; keep the effect or unresolved status of that reading clear without requiring a new action or explaining the same connection twice.\n\nOmit redundant verbal framing, not causality. The reader should still understand why a step follows from the previous one, what was communicated, how the speaker is understood, and what the viewpoint character actually knows or does not know, without forcing access to an excluded intention. A longer thought may remain when it materially contributes to the account, but do not pad it with performed fragments or repeated self-questioning. A plain answer can be enough; economy does not require a new objection, a clever closing line, or an immediate behavioral payoff.\n\nKeep private experience available under the active contract. Sparse prose may render a character's uncertainty, relief, or suspicion in one exact clause, but it does not delete authorized interiority to save words. Let each speaker's verbal habits remain individual. The scene can end when the immediate task is understood, attempted, or interrupted, leaving the next step inferable rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatas::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nBegin with the undertaking already in motion: travel, preparation, repair, pursuit, or an exchange about the next step. Let the scene move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized private experience without pausing for a summary of the objective. The reader should understand what the characters are trying to do and what currently obstructs them from the way they work and speak.\n\nLet a verbal distinction arise from the task, the speakers' actual interests, or a meaningful private association without making every exchange into an argument. A definition, objection, contradiction, or revised phrase may enter during a practical exchange and change what participants ask, promise, or understand. Preserve the actual statement and the circumstances in which it was made. Track any supported consequence without requiring the next act or decision to change; a correction can clarify existing understanding, remain private, or leave the plan untouched. Different speakers may pursue different interests without all of them becoming hyperverbal philosophers.\n\nKeep private thought distinct from spoken language and other characters' knowledge. The narration may reveal a focal character's reasoning, uncertainty, or attention while a companion remains unaware of it. A recollection may surface when the present scene calls it up, but it should not replace the current action. End where the scene naturally pauses: a task completed, a route chosen, a request answered or left open, with the next movement available rather than forced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the supplied relationship premise as the source of the scene's particular terms. Select a callback, private phrase, or repeated moment that belongs to the established history of these people, and preserve what was actually said or done when the callback matters. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes; the relationship's promise should emerge from the actual supplied bond and its consequences.\n\nLet the callback enter the adventure task through use rather than announcement. One character may invoke a shared phrase to request help, assign a role, or justify a risk; the other may accept it, misread its scope, or apply it differently. A definition, objection, or revised wording can change what the participants think they owe each other in the present undertaking. Show any practical effect that actually follows while allowing an unchanged act or pending response; neither mutual understanding nor a solved emotional question is required. Cooperation can continue while the shared language carries different private meanings.\n\nKeep private intent distinct from received language. The narration may reveal what one character means by a callback without the other knowing it. Give the relationship room for warmth, friction, or ordinary competence without forcing a confession, a changed label, or a relationship crisis. The scene can end with the task advanced, paused, or complicated by the relationship's terms, leaving the next exchange available rather than resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account the shape of a retelling without losing the present undertaking. Open with a compact declaration that establishes the deed, route, or obstacle, then expand into the particulars that make the reputation incomplete. Use recurring phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application, not as an ornamental epithet attached to every entrance. The adventure remains a sequence of practical choices: travel, discovery, rescue, confrontation, or recovery.\n\nWhen an oath, title, or remembered statement appears, preserve its original wording and the circumstances in which it was made. Distinguish what the original participants understood from what later tellers claim the words meant. A definition, objection, or revised wording may have changed the undertaking at the time; its later repetition may be accurate, disputed, or applied to a different situation. Where public memory affects present conduct, make that effect concrete; its significance may also remain emotional, social, or unresolved without another act. Reputation does not rewrite the actual past.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt the chronicle's authority. A participant may object to one detail, answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern, or correct a companion's version of what was promised. This need not become parody. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and keep private thought distinct from shared reputation. The account can end with the deed concluded or resumed, leaving another act available to be remembered rather than declaring its legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the undertaking in essayistic narrative: a permitted narrator or focal person states a limited claim about the scope of a word, promise, instruction, or label, then sets that claim beside a concrete case drawn from the established action. The case may be an event the cast has already experienced or an explicitly marked hypothetical. Keep the claim recognizable when the passage returns to it, and let the return be confirmed, qualified, or left provisional.\n\nDo not require the comparison to produce a rhetorical victory or a new decision. The argument can pause while the journey, rescue, pursuit, or recovery continues. A hypothetical is a tool for testing the claim, not an event that happened; keep its status clear. The objective and obstacles remain the through-line, and the essayistic movement serves them rather than replacing them with commentary.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and knowledge permissions. A focal person may test their own wording privately or aloud, but another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. When a later scene returns to the claim, recover the exact prior wording rather than paraphrasing it into a different assertion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Screenplay\n\nEstablish the actual dialogue and the visible condition it concerns before a reinterpretation matters. When a speaker defines a task, gives an instruction, or objects to a plan, show the object, location, or action that gives the phrase its practical scope. A later gesture, use of an object, or performed answer can clarify what the wording permitted or failed to change without adding a hidden translation or a revised replay of the line.\n\nUse concise readable action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Keep cuts within actual event and knowledge permissions: a cut can connect permitted locations or times, but it cannot grant a character knowledge of an offscreen event. Preserve required nonspoken private experience in whichever Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation is active, using a wrapper only where that selected presentation calls for it rather than turning verbal reasoning into automatic voice-over. A voice-over is performed speech when separately authorized, not the default destination for thought.\n\nDifferent speakers may be literal, eloquent, or uninterested. The scene can end after a completed action, a pending request, or a visible consequence of the spoken distinction, without an obligatory final line or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Stage Play\n\nLocate the object, entrance, or ongoing work that gives an actual phrase its stakes. When a speaker defines a task, objects to a plan, or revises an instruction, make the shared stage space show what that wording permits or refuses: who can reach the object, hold the line, open the entrance, or continue the work. A speaker may challenge or accept the phrase's scope while others respond through ordinary action instead of equal verbal brilliance.\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Keep required private experience separate from performed asides and cast knowledge. An aside or monologue has its own audience and permissions; it does not replace nonspoken interiority, and another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nThe arrangement may remain unchanged or a response pending at the active boundary. No decisive last line is required. The scene can stop after a completed task, a refused request, or a visible consequence of the spoken distinction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatay::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet a heard term, physical task, or private concern lead into a self-correction, recurring phrase, or imagined answer according to this person's attention. Braid perception, memory, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern through the authorized mind, but keep the actual undertaking recoverable: the reader can still distinguish what happened, what was said, what was remembered, and what was only imagined.\n\nMark actual words heard, memories, and hypothetical speech clearly. A remembered exchange is not a new event; an imagined reply is not a spoken line. Private experience may be long, brief, or unresolved without producing a clever interpretation. Actual event time is independent of the amount of prose rendering it: a moment of physical effort may be rendered briefly or at length, and a long inward passage need not advance the clock.\n\nDo not require the mind to reach a polished conclusion. The self-correction may remain unfinished while the journey, rescue, pursuit, or recovery continues. Preserve the active narrator and knowledge permissions; another character does not know a private revision merely because the reader has followed its formation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaataz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse actual sender-attributed messages around the undertaking, keeping a quoted phrase or known edit unchanged when its meaning matters. When a later message interprets an earlier instruction, promise, or objection, the exact sent wording remains available to the reader and to the participants who received it. A recipient-visible edit is part of the transmitted record; an unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought.\n\nDistinguish private interpretation from transmitted text. A character may read a message and privately revise its meaning, but thinking the interpretation does not transmit it to the sender; any knowledge or inference follows actual communication, observation, or other access the established contract permits. When a recipient acts, keep that action distinct from the received wording: it may confirm, complicate, or fail to match the message without proving intent. Receipt can also leave a response or action pending. Different digital habits can be plain or elaborate; no typing cue, delayed reply, or correct semantic reading proves intent or completed help by itself.\n\nUse only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the undertaking. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The scene can end after a sent confirmation, a pending request, or a visible consequence of the coordination, without an obligatory cliffhanger or reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the current setting and task, present the relevant actual wording, and give permitted focal self-argument the development it needs. Use concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. When a speaker defines a task, objects to a plan, or revises an instruction, preserve the actual phrase when interpreting words already spoken, while private formulation before a chosen line remains at its own causal time.\n\nScene and expression cues can locate a reaction but do not certify a lie or an unseen intention. A changed expression may attract attention; judge the phrase's practical scope from its actual wording, context, established authority, and available means; later conduct may clarify it without certifying unseen intention. Different speakers may be literal, eloquent, or uninterested. Keep current work and private knowledge clear without automatically adding branching story routes, menus, compulsory puns, or a changed ending.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene can end after a completed action, a pending request, or a visible consequence of the spoken distinction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaatbb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Nisio Isin / Web Serial\n\nReenter through the objective and the exact prior wording needed to understand the current exchange. When a later source or changed circumstance qualifies an interpretation, recover the earlier statement as it was actually made rather than paraphrasing it into a different assertion. The qualification applies to the interpretation, not to the original event or wording.\n\nLet tasks, questions, and private self-description span replies. No new pun, corrected definition, completed check, or reversal is owed to each installment. An ordinary agreement can keep working, and a request can remain unanswered while the undertaking continues. Briefly recover context only when it helps the current scene; do not retell the investigation or journey that first established the wording.\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, without an artificial cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nKeep the current objective and the available means legible in the scene: what the characters are trying to reach, carry, repair, or get past, and what the next practical step depends on. When a character's hurt, need, or difficult admission surfaces, write its particular shape through the established narrator's diction and distance. Show how that exposure actually affects the exchange: it may change what the character says or does next, or it may remain private while the work continues. Do not require the feeling to resolve, improve, or produce a confession.\n\nLet strain alter the conversation and subsequent behavior only when the character would act on it. A request may be made around the hurt rather than about it; an apology may be partial or refused; warmth and pain can occupy the same pause. Keep other characters' knowledge separate from the focal private experience. The adventure remains the organizing through-line: emotional exposure accompanies the attempt and may complicate cooperation, but it does not replace the objective or guarantee a particular outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the shared interval by grounding the submitted action in position, purpose, and the capacities that matter: where the characters are, what they are trying to accomplish, and what they can actually do next. Resolve the submitted contribution through the active contract's ownership and permissions; do not decide another participant's character or add a result the scene has not earned. Keep dialogue attribution unmistakable and keep NPC activity independent, including practical help, resistance, or ordinary business that continues off the immediate exchange.\n\nWrite private experience in the selected presentation and keep it owned by the focal character; another character does not hear it unless the active contract permits. End the interval where completed or pending work naturally leaves it. The continuation may be a task still underway, a changed condition, or an unresolved practical question, but do not force a question, cliffhanger, player choice, or new obstacle merely to close the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic construction to link arrivals, passages, preparation, and recovery through actual encounters and the means available there. Orient a new occasion through its immediate practical situation; an episode can span replies, and an encounter may change the next step or leave existing work underway without a test or result at the boundary. Carry established supplies, arrangements, injuries, debts, and knowledge forward across transitions. People met earlier remain independent and may reappear with their own unfinished business, but do not require every encounter to complete or repay something.\n\nLink episodes through concrete continuity rather than a repeated symbol or moral. A stored resource, a known route, a message, or a prior agreement can orient later work when it matters. Do not import a martial school, social satire, or new debt merely because the style is selected. Keep the journey's objective intelligible across episodes while allowing an episode to end with the work still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the physical work through exact, relevant particulars: load, resistance, footing, grip, material condition, and the immediate cause of a success or failure. Use forceful verbs and compressed transitions, but keep the causal sequence complete enough to follow. When equipment, terrain, or bodies show wear, render only the wear the established situation supports; do not damage sound objects or exhaust a character merely to darken the tone.\n\nLet private reasoning and feeling take the length the character's actual state requires beside the physical effort. A developed feeling, uncertainty, or movement of attention can take space without solving a problem; a short reaction may suit a character whose action outruns deliberation. Keep each speaker's diction individual rather than uniformly hard-edged. The scene may end with capacity intact, a problem unsolved, or an injury avoided; no injury, collapse, or worsened ending is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the adventure through the document form the actual task supports: a field report, letter, log, manifest, note, or record with a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Keep a document within what its writer knew or believed at composition, distinguishing observation, report, and speculation where relevant. Separately track what was actually transmitted and received: a private or partly delivered record still contains its composed text. A record may be accurate, incomplete, mistaken, or self-serving without immediate narrative correction.\n\nPreserve independent work outside the documents and render actual authorized nonspoken experience in its selected presentation when required; a document is a composed artifact, not a complete live mind. The records can carry an interval without a compulsory cutaway to unrecorded activity. Gaps may reveal later events, but do not require a missing page, changed instruction, confession, or next opportunity from every record. Preserve continuity so the reader can follow what happened, what was reported, and what remains to be done.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake each offered comfort or accommodation particular to the actual people and the means at hand: a shared meal, a dry place to rest, a repaired strap, a remembered preference, or an ordinary kindness that fits the journey. Let the care be useful, enjoyable, or both, and let it occur between new acquaintances without inventing deep familiarity. Use the established narrator's language for warmth rather than borrowed sentimental cues.\n\nKeep the adventure's practical limits and ongoing tasks present. A character may refuse comfort, keep a private pleasure unshared, or accept help without becoming agreeable. Comfort can make the next leg easier without healing an injury, resolving a conflict, or guaranteeing success. Do not require gratitude, agreement, or a successful finish as the price of the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to move between the concrete undertaking and the relevant social or historical conditions around it. Open with the present attempt, widen to the arrangements, customs, or pressures that bear on it, and return to the immediate choice or obstacle. Make that relation understandable through the established narrator's actual knowledge; do not add knowledge the narrator would not have or force a character into an explanatory speech.\n\nKeep dialogue individual and private experience in the selected presentation. The formal architecture may frame a scene through public and private contrast, but it does not require costume, a remembered personal wound, or a moral conclusion at the boundary. The adventure remains the organizing sequence: the wider context explains what the attempt costs or depends on, while the next practical step stays open to the actual conditions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nTreat the framed account as a present event inside the larger objective. When a character tells a story, show who is listening, where they are in the journey or task, and what practical or social need makes the telling happen now. The telling may pass time during a wait, steady someone during a difficult stretch, explain a route or tool, or keep a companion engaged while work continues.\n\nKeep the outer scene alive around the embedded account. Let actual activity continue around the telling when present: a listener may attend to a task, ask a question, or simply listen without interruption. Let elapsed time follow the performed telling, listening, and other actual events rather than a fixed narrative rhythm or the length of narrated private thought. Distinguish the teller's source and knowledge at the telling from what the embedded participants knew during their attempts. A vivid account does not by itself make every interpretation certain, but ordinary reliable information may be used without a compulsory independent investigation.\n\nUse the embedded account to make the present undertaking more intelligible without making it a required lesson. A told experience can explain why a route is risky, why a person distrusts a plan, or what a previous attempt cost. It can also simply occupy a difficult hour. Let reception follow the actual listeners: they may attend together, miss different portions, or respond to different details. Their responses should arise from their own positions in the task, not from a demand that the tale change their minds or produce a callback.\n\nPreserve actual private experience outside the performance within the active narrator and mind-ownership contract. What the teller withholds, fears, or privately remembers is not automatically spoken in the tale. What a listener privately understands or doubts remains separate from what they say. When the outer action resumes, carry forward only the concrete effects the telling actually had: a question raised, a pause used, a task completed during the account, or a decision made afterward. The telling may leave no visible change at all, and the undertaking continues on its own terms.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism to follow the undertaking through a participant's actual position. Ground each stage in the concrete conditions of work, travel, or recovery: who is responsible for what, what the task costs in time and effort, and how the participants' social roles shape their access to resources, information, and decisions. Let the objective remain intelligible even when the characters understand it differently.\n\nPlace expectation beside actual participation without turning the scene into a test. When a character expects a task to confirm a capacity, a social standing, or a relationship, place what the work actually requires beside the expectation and render the particular response, whether the two match or differ. The response may be confirmation, uncertainty, frustration, or no change. Do not force a maturation milestone, a humiliation, or a corrected self-image. Let the character's experience inform later choices only when the narration shows that connection.\n\nKeep the narrator's scope governing reflection. If the narrator is a participant, distinguish what they knew and felt during the event from any later knowledge available at the telling. If the narrator has a wider social view, it may frame the undertaking through the institutions, histories, or working arrangements that bear on it. In either case, move between outward action and inward response through the particular pressures of the scene, not through a general commentary on society.\n\nLet the undertaking develop across occasions. A later scene may show how an earlier effort changed a character's practical judgment, but it need not announce that change. The story may end with the objective incomplete, a plan adjusted, or a participant simply continuing under altered conditions. The developmental method lies in the accumulating relation between expectation, work, and response, not in a required arc of growth.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere established power touches the undertaking, make the contact concrete through people and place. Show what an old authority actually controls in the present: a building, a permit, a route, a debt, a piece of equipment, or access to someone who matters. Make that control concrete through the actual means of contact, access, or enforcement, locating the participants and relevant material conditions as needed. The material surroundings should carry the weight of that authority: worn fixtures, locked doors, visible security, inherited furnishings, or the contrast between an old claim and present use.\n\nLet atmosphere and private desire color the encounter without deciding its outcome. A character may want something from the person who holds access, may resent the deference the situation requires, or may feel the pull of an old loyalty. Write that private experience as part of the scene, but do not let it prove hidden motive, determine consent, or turn every offer into a trap. Sincere help remains possible, and a refusal may be practical rather than sinister.\n\nKeep the undertaking's known mechanisms intact. The old authority can obstruct, enable, or complicate the objective through the world's established rules. Preserve any supplied powers or bonds, while refusing to invent a supernatural mechanism or omnipotent institution merely to close a gap. When a character negotiates, works around, or accepts a restriction, show the actual terms and any cost those terms entail. Let different characters understand different parts of the arrangement and protect different interests; disagreement need not be ignorance or corruption.\n\nWhere people manage an outward presentation, distinguish it from their actual private experience; expression and feeling can also align sincerely. A character may present deference, competence, or indifference while privately experiencing something else. A mask may fall through this participant's actual response; trust can follow shared history or be freely offered, tentative, or incautious according to their disposition and the scene. Do not require the other person to earn it first. Carry any actual effects into the undertaking: access may be granted, refused, or still pending. An interval can leave resources, relationships, and arrangements unchanged while its intimate experience remains meaningful; the encounter owes no new debt or practical result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nOrganize the relevant action through social and domestic realism. That means the undertaking remains legible inside an ongoing network of provisions, care, transport, bargains, kept promises, and ordinary duties. Where relevant to the occasion, follow the actual work of people who travel, remain, help, or decline. Make the undertaking's dependence on provisions or commitments specific through these activities and the permitted narrator's account, without inventing a household role or requiring a cutaway around every attempt. A route can succeed because a neighbor remembers the correct landing place, fail because a cart is owed elsewhere, or change when someone refuses to abandon a household task.\n\nLet the objective remain an actual objective. Obstacles should arise from weather, distance, the state of the road, available skill, competing claims, and the consequences of taking resources away from those who need them. Do not turn the departure into a lesson that domestic life was always the real adventure. The undertaking may extend beyond the domestic setting; the present interval locates it among the relevant work, commitments, and concerns of the people involved, without requiring a dispute or domestic interlude.\n\nKeep distinct private experiences. A person can help efficiently while resenting the traveler, or refuse without being selfish or cowardly. A child can understand the route but not the reason for a delay. A traveler can notice how much effort the departure costs someone else and still leave. Where conduct and private feeling differ, render the actual distinction; they may also align sincerely. Give private experience room beyond productivity without requiring reconciliation. Comfort or cooperation, when it appears, can be partial, practical, or accompanied by continued disagreement.\n\nGround any direct reflection in this particular location and its ordinary work. Instead of announcing a social theme, let a specific provision, a repeated chore, a crowded ride, a postponed ceremony, or an unfinished meal carry the situation. The protagonist is one participant among others, with a plan to pursue; other people have their own reasons to help, delay, come along, or stay behind, and those reasons remain active even when they do not change the route.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaual::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany as the organizing method. Place the action in relation to a situated recorder whose entries, notes, remembered details, and brief reflections follow the active narrator and person supplied by the story. The recorder does not have to be the protagonist; it may be a traveling companion, a local observer, or someone who hears an account later. Keep the form intelligible through the occasion of each entry: a stop after a difficult crossing, a note made while waiting for a companion to return, an observation recorded because it interested the writer and not because it served the plan.\n\nSelect observations for their actual interest as well as their practical use. A detail about the road, the quality of light, a companion's manner, or a strange local custom may be recorded because it stayed in the writer's attention. It need not later become a clue or payoff. The undertaking continues around and sometimes beside the record; a day can be remembered for an overheard argument, a ruined meal, or the way a boatman handled a current, even when the objective itself moved forward unchanged.\n\nDistinguish composition from live private experience. A diary entry records what its writer chose to set down at a particular time; it is not the whole mind. Later annotations may disagree with the original note, correct it, or point to what the writer did not understand then. Reveal those layers explicitly when they matter, for example by saying that the writer added a line days later or that a later reader saw a different implication in the same words. Keep required nonspoken interiority in its actual present form, not restated as if the diary already contained it.\n\nLet the adventure remain present in the writing. An entry can describe an obstacle, a failed attempt, a decision to wait, or the feel of carrying an injured person downhill. The reflective method should not smooth away effort into a composed recollection. Use concrete physical movement and practical problem-solving inside and around the recorded observations. The final effect is a readable miscellany moving along an actual undertaking, not an exercise in restrained atmosphere or a list of seasonal impressions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauam::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as social chronicle and testimony. Keep a clear temporal spine through the actual proposal, attempts, continuing conditions, and what remained to be done; discovery and failure enter only when they occur. The chronicler can be a participant, an observer, or a later compiler using records, but the reader should always be able to follow the actual sequence of the undertaking. Keep events recoverable through reflection without making inward prose length determine elapsed fictional time. A journey, rescue, confrontation, or recovery should remain a series of concrete attempts and consequences, not a memory that has already completed itself.\n\nIncorporate testimony where the active narrator and situation permit. A participant may describe what they saw, what they were told, or what they decided under pressure. Mark each account by its time and position. Show whether the person was present at the event, heard about it later, or is repeating a version that served a particular purpose. Accounts may agree, differ, or leave questions open without changing the established action. Do not use a disagreement as permission to make the event itself indeterminate; the story keeps its own factual spine while the testimonies reveal how people understood, feared, defended, or regretted what happened.\n\nKeep the material conditions of the undertaking visible. Chronicle the route, the supplies, the damage, the labor, and the local arrangements that made movement possible or impossible. A chronicle is not merely a list of notable speeches. It records who carried what, how long a crossing took, which road was chosen and why, and what state the participants were in when they arrived. These practical details carry the adventure and ground any broader social observation.\n\nDistinguish chronicle testimony from private life. A witness can state only what they know or claim to know; their inward experience remains separate fiction and must be rendered in whatever form the active contract requires. A character may testify firmly to something they privately doubt, or report an event accurately while misunderstanding its cause. Render any such difference in actual authorized nonspoken experience, distinguishing it from the attributed account. It may affect behavior or remain private without a subsequent choice or an omniscient gloss after every testimony. The result is a readable, attributed account of an unfolding undertaking, not an abstract treatment of history or community.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauan::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Light Novel\n\nOpen the scene by naming what the cast is currently trying to do, where they are in that attempt, and what they have available or still need. Introduce unfamiliar tools, routes, terms, or mechanisms through relevant preparation, explanation, observation, or use. Make their practical significance clear when needed without requiring a demonstration, test, or discovered limit.\n\nKeep the writing in continuous readable prose with clean spatial transitions. When the group moves, make the change of place and position easy to follow. Break paragraphs around action, dialogue, and the focal character's private response, but vary that arrangement by what the scene needs; do not impose a fixed pattern of one action, one line, one thought.\n\nLet the focal character's inner experience be actual and specific: reasoning through a choice, measuring distance or time, noticing what a companion's silence or movement might mean, or holding back a spoken fear. Give actual feeling, sensation, uncertainty, and reasoning their needed extent, including developed inward passages that produce no decision or visible change. Keep the outer situation recoverable and let fictional time follow actual events rather than prose length.\n\nMake the relation between an attempt and current conditions understandable. Preserve what was actually learned, used, changed, or left pending so a later move has a real basis. Failure need not produce injury, expenditure, an argument, or a useful clue; a task can remain underway without a new result.\n\nKeep the established narrator's language and individual cast voices. Comedy, a young cast, a sudden power reveal, or a triumphant changed ending is not automatically provided. Any such element occurs only when the supplied story establishes it.\n\nContinue through the current objective for as long as it remains active. The reply may stop at an unresolved obstacle, a completed stage, or a changed condition, but the next continuation should be inferable from the conditions just written, not forced through a compulsory cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the undertaking through the narrator's actual attention. When a surface, sound, or movement draws attention during the undertaking, let syntax develop its felt relation to this experience without requiring practical usefulness. Use plain clauses where needed to orient what is being attempted or remains underway. Keep the physical sequence recoverable: an image may open an association, but the reader should still know where the characters are, what they are doing, and what obstacle or opportunity now applies.\n\nLet recurring sounds or images develop through the narrator's attention and actual associations as well as later events. Their meaning may deepen, remain familiar, or stay unsettled without a new occurrence or outward result. A comparison can change what the narrator understands without granting knowledge or altering world facts. Do not make every image resolve into a symbol; an association may remain open or be revised by the next practical result.\n\nPreserve ordinary speech and actual private experience. A character may speak plainly while perceiving something more textured, or may notice little while working. The lyrical movement belongs to the selected narrator's attention, not to every voice, and it must not bury the objective under ornamental density.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present detail open an established memory while the work continues. When a tool, place, phrase, or routine recalls an earlier occasion, state what was actually available then and what has changed, without supplying a loss the world has not established. Keep the memory attached to the current undertaking: the character can continue preparing, traveling, repairing, or negotiating while the recollection runs.\n\nDistinguish earlier knowledge from present knowledge. What the character understood then may have been partial; what they understand now may include later facts, but that later knowledge cannot become the earlier motive. Let continuing attachment, pleasure, or uncertainty remain present without forcing it to change the next action.\n\nDo not require a death, failed return, restored past, or final statement of impermanence. Actual changes can matter through small irreversible differences: a route no longer available, a person no longer present, a skill no longer reliable. The undertaking and other people's lives remain active, and ordinary pleasure can remain pleasurable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse picaresque construction: follow the mobile participant through a sequence of encounters, each with its own social situation and practical terms. When the undertaking requires passage, supplies, assistance, or information, make the counterpart's position clear—what they can offer, what they want, what they will accept, and what they will not. Let the traveler's acceptance, refusal, or partial agreement carry forward as a real condition.\n\nTreat each encounter as a distinct occasion rather than a repeated obstacle. One person may help straightforwardly; another may bargain; another may refuse for reasons that have nothing to do with the traveler. Keep the established setting, language, and community specific, and let the speakers' voices come from their actual situations.\n\nCarry accepted, refused, or pending arrangements into later scenes. A debt, an open request, or a closed road can shape the next opportunity without requiring tricks, incompetence, or a new bargain at every turn. The journey remains intelligible through these concrete social terms, not through generic local color.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the attempt with direct clauses and a small number of exact details. Name the positions, available means, and causal connections the reader needs to follow the objective; select the concrete particulars that carry the physical situation without a prescribed number, and let omission leave a supported inference rather than hide a necessary fact.\n\nKeep only the explanation or inward connection that materially contributes to the present account. Reasoning, feeling, uncertainty, and attention can each require a developed passage, whether or not they explain a decision. Remove redundant explanation while preserving the actual inward movement, including meaningful recurrence. Brevity is not a quota, and short sentences are not a substitute for missing causality.\n\nPreserve individual speech and actual private experience. A character may speak at length when the situation calls for it, or answer with a single word. Do not force cryptic dialogue or withheld emotional disclosure as a style; the selected details should make the attempt understandable, not obscure it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauas::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that follows the current objective through actual attempts and ongoing conditions. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without announcing each shift; use paragraphing and transitions to keep the reader oriented in place, time, and consequence.\n\nLet the narrator's voice and the immediate scene set the register. A quiet preparation, a failed attempt, a practical conversation, and an inward hesitation can all occupy the scene as needed. Preserve resources, knowledge, and consequences across the passage: what is used, learned, or broken remains part of the next opportunity.\n\nDo not impose a fixed channel cycle, a compulsory antagonist, a memory device, or a lesson at the end. The undertaking may remain unfinished, and a scene can end with work resumed or a new condition understood rather than with a completed stage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose the scene's relational content from the actual relationship premise and the undertaking it accompanies. Coordination, care, rivalry, distance, or discovery can organize the interaction because the supplied relationship makes that particular form of attention legible. Let the practical work remain real: a task may be the vehicle for the relationship without requiring the relationship to change.\n\nUse callbacks only when established. If the characters share a private phrase, repeated moment, or known history, let it return when it belongs to the current encounter, with familiar or changed significance; new acquaintances need no invented routine. Preserve each person's private meaning, boundaries, and knowledge, and do not let one character's inference become another character's knowledge.\n\nAllow agreement and uncertainty to persist. A scene can fulfill its relational promise through continued cooperation, an honest refusal, or an unresolved distance without confession, romance, repair, synchronized recognition, or a changed relationship at the end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, names, and recurring phrasing. Identify the undertaking or a person's part in it with a compact declaration, then unfold the actual preparation, route, or effort that gives the declaration its particular meaning. A recurring phrase can hold a familiar relation or gain a new application through a supported occurrence; an entrance does not require an epithet.\n\nGround remembered deeds in what was attempted and actually known. Separate later reputation from contemporary motives and private experience: being remembered as dependable does not mean the work was easy, cheerful, or automatic. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and let dialogue interrupt the chronicle when a participant objects to a detail or answers an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern.\n\nOrdinary contributions and unfinished deeds remain available. The account can pause with business concluded, resumed, or still pending, without declaring a legacy on anyone's behalf. No oath, heroic doom, public verdict, or changed meaning at each recurrence is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the essayistic route of Scandinavian Literary Traditions to let a concrete detail of preparation, travel, or recovery open an explicit consideration within the active narration's permitted voice and knowledge. Anchor the reflection in the present task: a piece of equipment, a route condition, a delay, or a small repair can carry the thought, with its attribution kept clear. A narrator who reports rather than opines can situate the consideration in an authorized character's thought or attributed account. Return attention to the work when the scene requires. Place a provisional claim beside the established particulars of the attempt. Let the particulars confirm, qualify, or leave the claim uncertain; do not force the reflection to produce a lesson, a changed plan, or a personal creed.\n\nKeep the undertaking intelligible as action/adventure. The reflective passage can develop an idea at length while the objective and conditions remain recoverable. Narrative attention does not by itself make fictional time pass or require a delayed attempt. When attention returns to the activity, preserve what was actually known and what still needs doing. Keep setting and individual voices as supplied; the essayistic movement requires neither reserve nor bleakness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings to establish a scene or mark changes of time or place, and select the positions, objects, actions, and sounds that make the current objective and obstacle legible. Keep action lines concise and causal: show what a character can reach, move, hear, or observe, and let each cut preserve actual movement and information. Offscreen sound or reported action may continue the wider attempt when the scene supports it, but do not use a cut to skip a required causal step or to fake a discovery.\n\nKeep required private experience in the active nonspoken presentation, clearly attributed to its character, rather than converting it into voice-over or dialogue another character can hear. A visible cue may support an inference in context, but it cannot certify motive or hidden intent. End on completed work, a pending task, or a clear change in conditions; do not require a camera inventory or a concluding spectacle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Stage Play\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, and pauses to stage the current part of the supplied undertaking. Make reach and relevant objects playable without redesigning the objective to fit a single room; when the wider journey or offstage action matters, bring it in through credible reports, supported sounds, or observable effects that the scene can actually stage.\n\nKeep required private experience as nonspoken material in the active presentation, distinct from asides and monologues. A monologue or aside may be used only when its dramatic purpose is established; it does not replace thought or give other characters access to it. Let the scene end with work underway, a response pending, or a practical result, without forcing a revelation or a rearranged stage picture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauay::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet a particular authorized mind's associative succession organize the telling through the active narrator and person: braid bodily sensation, private words, memory, interruption, and continuing concern as that mind actually attends. The narrator need not be the experiencing participant. Keep the action/adventure objective and immediate physical situation recoverable through concrete orientation, even when syntax and paragraph shape follow the mind's movement. Distinguish events from recollection, anticipation, or fantasy, and preserve real fictional time independently of how long the passage spends inside the thought.\n\nDo not require each return to the outer task to produce an insight, decision, or plan. An association may matter personally without becoming a clue, a shared fact, or a solution. Keep other minds separate from the authorized center; their motives remain unavailable unless the active contract permits access.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauaz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages as the story's transmitted record, and include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that the scene actually supports. Distinguish a composed draft or actual dictation from imagined wording and from transmission: a draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought, and a sent message is not proof of reading, comprehension, or completed action.\n\nKeep receipt, reading, comprehension, and subsequent work separate, while preserving knowledge the characters have from other channels. Required private experience remains outside sent text and must appear only in the active nonspoken presentation when the contract calls for it. Let the exchange coordinate the undertaking without owing an acknowledgment, outage, misunderstanding, recollection, or completed task at every interval.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaauba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and focal narration or actual private response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Let background and expression cues orient position, condition, or lived experience without requiring a payoff or certifying hidden motive; a changed expression may accompany a discovery, but the discovery must come from the scene's causal and informational movement.\n\nPreserve knowledge, ownership, and the action/adventure objective across scene changes. Keep private response inside the active focal presentation and outside other characters' knowledge unless the contract permits access. Do not introduce images, menus, romance routes, a fixed alternation of scene and reaction, or a changed ending merely because the format is selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaubb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / No Named Influence / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: recover only the prior state needed to follow the resumed work, and preserve actual resources, agreements, knowledge, and unresolved tasks. Let genuine developments continue across installments while preparation, rest, or ordinary acknowledgment remain valid material. A context reminder or hook appears when it helps re-entry, not on a schedule.\n\nKeep the action/adventure objective and its obstacles available without turning each reply into a complete miniature chapter. Keep required private experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or a clear change in conditions; do not require a new level, enemy, recap, reversal, or cliffhanger as an installment fee.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene around a concrete contribution the viewpoint character needs from someone else: a route, a supply, a repair, a promise to wait, or another practical element of the current undertaking. Keep that objective legible. The request may come directly, or an ordinary shared interest and practical business may accompany the difficult conversation. Do not require a small-talk prelude or postpone a request the actual contribution has already made.\n\nLet the sustained exchange carry the emotional cost through what each speaker chooses to raise, avoid, or answer only partially. The viewpoint character may rehearse the request privately, notice the other person's patience or impatience, or wonder whether asking would affect the relationship, without that fear guaranteeing a change. Show the strain in the particular relationship: an old debt, a recent failure, a promise already broken, or the simple difficulty of admitting need. Do not require either character to name the full feeling or to become an adversary. A plain refusal, a postponed answer, or practical help given with visible reluctance can all advance the scene.\n\nUse the adventure frame to give the conversation physical stakes. The request should be connected to a real plan: what depends on it, what the characters can attempt without it, and what changes once it is made or refused. Let the exchange occupy genuine time, with the speakers' attention divided between the topic and the task around them. A pause to check equipment, watch a route, or finish a small job can make the emotional exposure feel situated rather than performed.\n\nKeep private experience in the active authorized mode. The viewpoint character's actual feeling may be more specific than what they say, and the other character's private reasons remain unavailable unless the contract grants access. Do not make the scene end in confession, reconciliation, or rupture. The request can remain pending, the help can be given without warmth, or the characters can separate with the practical matter settled and the emotional one still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the reply by placing the supplied move in the current scene: where the characters are, what they are carrying or working on, and what the next step of the undertaking requires. Keep the spatial situation easy to follow so that a reader can see who is where and what each person is doing while they talk.\n\nLet the characters sustain an ordinary conversation that runs alongside the practical work. Give each speaker a distinct investment in the surface topic: one may care about the food, the route, a piece of equipment, or a remembered place, while another is only half-listening or occupied with a task. Use clear dialogue attribution so the exchange remains readable without forcing every line to advance the plot. The conversation can be enjoyable, distracting, or uneven; it does not need to be clever or hostile.\n\nWhen practical activity changes the situation, make the effect legible; ordinary talk, waiting, or continuing work may also leave conditions unchanged. Someone checks a map, secures a load, tests a rope, or finishes a repair. These actions can alter access, preparation, or what the group knows without requiring a completed result. Let the conversation and the work interrupt each other naturally: a question can wait while a task is finished, or a task can be paused when a speaker needs attention.\n\nKeep private experience in the active authorized mode and separate it from what other characters can hear. A participant may be thinking about something unrelated to the talk, or may be privately weighing what the other person wants. Do not give other characters access to that thought unless the contract permits it. End the reply at a natural continuation point: the work may be partly done, the conversation may be unfinished, and no question, reversal, or cliffhanger is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavac::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Situate the scene in the actual arrangement of work, passage, supplies, or cooperation that the current undertaking requires. Let the reader see who is in a position to wait, ask, interrupt, or help, and let those positions come from the characters' practical circumstances rather than from a fixed hierarchy of cleverness or corruption.\n\nBuild the sustained exchange around a familiar interest or routine that reveals the social assumptions in the room. The speakers may discuss the price of a service, the proper way to pack a load, a local custom, or a remembered transaction. Let their particular investments show what each person takes for granted about the other: who is expected to yield, who is owed patience, who can afford to be careless. Do not require the conversation to expose a hidden scheme or produce a social victory. An ordinary answer can be accurate and useful.\n\nKeep the practical request legible within the talk. The viewpoint character may need directions, a favor, a fair price, or cooperation from someone whose interests do not align with theirs. Show what the request depends on and what changes when it is made or refused. The satirical edge should come from the gap between what people say and what their positions reveal, not from making every speaker witty or every exchange a contest.\n\nPreserve private interpretation as fallible. The viewpoint character may misread another person's politeness, impatience, or reluctance, and the narration can state or leave that uncertainty inferable according to the active voice and actual private experience. Do not let the scene end in a completed transaction or a social triumph unless the actual circumstances support one. The request can remain pending, the misunderstanding can persist, and the undertaking can continue with its practical state changed only in part.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavad::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSelect the actual room, load, object, or movement relevant to the undertaking and frame the scene with forceful direct facts. Name the material conditions that matter: the weight being carried, the condition of the road or equipment, the distance remaining, the state of a person's hands or clothing. Use concrete nouns and active verbs, and compress transitions so that the reader moves from one physical fact to the next without repeated narration about tension or atmosphere.\n\nLet the sustained conversation take its actual space. Do not shorten the exchange merely because the surrounding prose is compressed. The characters can talk at length about a particular interest, a shared memory, or a practical disagreement while the material situation remains present around them. Keep the dialogue attributed and let each speaker's diction stay individual; abrasive or rough speech should belong to the person using it, not to every voice in the scene.\n\nUse the compression on repeated framing, not on meaningful private experience. Actual feeling, uncertainty, attention, and reasoning can take the extent the person experiences, without an immediate-action criterion. Show what the plan depends on and what the attempt changes through small physical developments: a strap is tightened, a route is ruled out, a tool is handed over, a person's grip fails. These changes should be legible without a paragraph of explanation after each one.\n\nAllow the rhythm to vary. A burst of short clauses can mark a physical interruption or a sudden change of access, while a slower passage can hold a moment of actual reasoning or private response. Do not require profanity, damage, or a hostile standoff. Sound equipment, ordinary talk, helpful people, and an unresolved attempt all remain possible within the style.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavae::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Epistolary / Documents\n\nChoose the supported document form for the current scene: a letter, journal entry, report, note, or another record with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let that form govern what the document preserves and what it omits. A letter to a superior may summarize a conversation differently from a private journal entry written the same evening, and a report may record only the practical result.\n\nCarry the undertaking through the documents. Keep the plan and actual work recoverable across records without making every entry a progress report or request. An entry may preserve ordinary conversation, interest, or an unfinished concern. Preserve enough conversational particularity to make the exchange feel lived: a specific phrase, a moment of hesitation, a digression about food or equipment, a question that was never answered. Do not require the document to reproduce every line of the conversation.\n\nDistinguish contemporaneous wording, reconstruction, quotation, and later annotation. If the writer quotes someone, show whether the words are remembered or copied. If the writer adds a later note, keep the later knowledge separate from what was known at the time. Let gaps and omissions be legible: a document may conceal something deliberately, or it may simply not record what its author did not notice.\n\nDo not treat the document as the writer's live mind. A diary entry or unsent draft is a composed artifact with its own audience and purpose. Keep actual private experience in the active authorized mode, separate from what the document says. The delivery of a message, the response to a request, or the next step of the undertaking can remain pending at the end of the entry.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaf::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the supplied goal legible during preparation, passage, or rest. The scene can take place while the characters pack, wait out weather, share a meal, or recover from a completed leg of the journey. Let the reader see what the next step of the undertaking requires, but do not let that requirement dominate a moment that is genuinely about companionship.\n\nGive a specific enjoyed topic room to develop. The characters may discuss a favorite food, a piece of music, a comfortable place, a shared memory, or a small preference that matters to one of them. Let the conversation be sustained and particular rather than a quick exchange of pleasantries. A listener may be talkative, quiet, distracted, or uninterested without becoming a foil, and the warmth can be uneven without turning into conflict.\n\nInclude an ordinary helpful act that fits the people involved: someone shares a portion, mends a strap, offers a dry coat, remembers the way, or simply keeps company while the other works. Let the act be sincere and practical. It does not need to improve the task, earn gratitude, or signal a hidden agenda. Preserve personality, privacy, and disagreement; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.\n\nKeep private pleasure and uncertainty available in the active authorized mode. A character may enjoy the moment while still worrying about the route, or may accept help while privately unsure how to return it. Do not require a joke, a confession, a task improvement, or a sudden reversal. The scene can end with the practical state partly changed and the companionship simply present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavag::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen the scene with deliberate framing. Use the narrator's permitted distance to place the current task inside a relevant social context, history, or arrangement: the route has a reputation, the work carries an obligation, the people involved have a past that bears on what they can ask of each other. Keep the framing specific to the undertaking rather than a general meditation, and let it establish why this particular conversation matters now.\n\nLet the sustained exchange occupy the scene. The characters can talk at length about an ordinary interest, a shared memory, or a practical disagreement, with each speaker's diction remaining distinct from the narrator's composed prose. Do not make the dialogue ornate merely because the narration is formal. The contrast between composed paragraphs and plain speech can itself carry the scene's social meaning.\n\nKeep physical business legible within the formal movement. Show what the plan depends on and what the attempt changes through concrete details: a load is shifted, a letter is produced, a route is reconsidered, a person leaves and returns. Let the narration move deliberately from the conversation to the physical situation and back, without treating every topic as a tactic or every pause as a hidden meaning.\n\nShow why delay or a detour matters when it does. If the conversation postpones the task, make the cost or the relief of that postponement available. If the talk changes what the characters decide to do, let the consequence appear in the practical state of the undertaking. Return orientation to the actual work or the pending request at the end. Formality does not require a closing verdict, and a scene may conclude with the practical matter advanced and the social or emotional question still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavah::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nPut the telling inside the present undertaking: someone recounts a past journey, mistake, deal, or rescue while work, travel, waiting, repair, or recovery continues around them. Give the listeners a current stake in hearing it, but keep their reasons ordinary—they may want to pass the time, judge a companion, learn a route, avoid an argument, or decide whether to trust a request.\n\nLet the embedded story stay connected to the outer objective without making it proof. Its details can explain why a tool is handled a particular way, what a person once promised, where a hazard was seen, or why a place has a rule. A return to the outer scene may bring a route question, timing issue, or division of labor into view, but can also leave listeners considering, enjoying the account, or continuing unchanged work. The tale may influence conduct before anyone spells out its lesson.\n\nPreserve the Tarantino contribution as particular interests inside the telling, not ornamental speech. A long digression about a minor preference can become relevant when it reveals what the teller noticed, valued, or ignored. Let the listener interrupt, dispute a wording, supply a missing part, or keep working without responding. No listener is required to change plans, confess, or accept the tale's version.\n\nKeep levels distinct. The embedded story is an account delivered at a specific moment. Attribute experience to the correct narrative level; actual nonspoken experience in either level follows the active narrator and ownership permissions and is not replaced by the performed tale. A later retelling may change emphasis or preserve the same meaning without a required new effect. Recurrence can mark a phrase, object, or promise that accrues different weight in different tellings without making repetition a certification that the story is true. Close where the telling or the surrounding task reaches a natural pause, not with a forced revelation or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavai::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist and absurdist construction through a supplied procedure: a permit, schedule, inspection, packaging rule, route restriction, communication window, or another regulation that shapes what the cast may do next. Introduce its actual terms early enough for the reader to follow them. The undertaking then becomes an attempt to work through that procedure's ordinary consequences under pressure.\n\nLet sustained dialogue expose how differently people live inside the rule. One speaker may treat the procedure as common sense, another as a convenient delay, another as a personal insult, another as an opportunity to help. Keep their talk particular, with its duration following the actual speakers and circumstances. They may discuss a signature, measurement, or remembered exception while work continues or waits; no delay or uniformly unhurried pace is compulsory. The procedure does not need explaining every time; show it through what people ask for, refuse, check, or ignore.\n\nAllow the absurdity to arise from internally consistent application rather than random chaos. A small requirement may bind movement. A sensible exception may be unavailable at the wrong hour or to the wrong person. A sincere attempt to comply can fail because the rule was not designed for the present case. Preserve real practical costs: time, distance, material, safety, or the chance that another party proceeds without them. The world does not turn into pure joke; a violation may create genuine difficulty and sincere help may untangle it.\n\nDo not give every conversation the same outcome. A matter can be resolved by a person who actually has the authority, halted by an ordinary inconvenience, left pending for the next opportunity, or accepted without moral victory. Let private thought remain distinct from the performed coolness or irritation a speaker shows. The procedural machinery should remain finite, established, and open to specific consequences; new exceptions cannot appear only to rescue the plot.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaj::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhen the objective brings the cast within reach of inherited authority, let that authority live in the room: a building's age, a controlled entrance, a waiting area, a private office, a landscape someone's family has administered for generations, or an object that only a particular person may carry. Make these details part of what the cast can see, touch, or be denied, not backdrop.\n\nRun a sustained exchange beside the request for passage, support, privacy, equipment, or recognition. The speakers may talk around the favor through a shared interest, an old obligation, a local matter, or an item on the desk. Track status or control when it actually shifts inside the talk: one person may steer the topic, another may interrupt, and practical control can move with a signature, a refusal to answer, a phone call, or an unlocked barrier. Keep these shifts staged so the reader understands who now has access or permission.\n\nSeparate what the cast infers from what they know. A speaker can appear sympathetic while planning refusal, or brusque while preparing real help. Another participant may read the room wrongly without the scene confirming that suspicion. Render actual authorized private experience, including motives that are known and uncertainty or feeling that is not fully understood. It does not grant everyone insight into the person with authority. Sincere help and ordinary refusal both remain possible.\n\nUse the atmosphere to modulate the encounter rather than supply new powers. Old fixtures, narrow light, worn authority, or inherited wealth can raise pressure, but they do not compel a betrayal, impose an irresistible patron, or create a new crisis after every success. Ground any abrupt physical consequence in the setting: a door that locks, a road that is closed, a record that is missing, a debt that becomes due. The adventure may move through a difficult meeting to leave with less than it wanted, more than it expected, or nothing changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavak::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern urban and popular-fiction narration to follow the objective through supplied linked encounters: a delivery, route search, repair run, transfer, market errand, transport connection, or a series of places where the cast must obtain something from particular people. Let the reader track what has been accomplished, what is still missing, where the cast is going, and which objects or information they carry.\n\nKeep a familiar topic alive across those stops. A concern about a missing payment, a person's bad habit, a disputed map, a cheap choice, or an earlier promise can resume with a new listener or during a new task. Each return changes or preserves its significance according to what actually happened in between: an item may be found, money may be spent, a third party may already know the story, or a destination may make the topic urgent. The repetition need not pay off as a clue at every stop.\n\nLet each exchange remain a real conversation rather than a transaction for plot information. People argue about the detail according to their own interests, interrupt work to talk, keep talking while they move, or listen only partly. Different audiences may hear different parts of the topic or share its full context. Track what each actually received, including an altered report when one occurred, without forcing a later listener to know less or misunderstand. Use actual gaps where they affect cooperation or difficulty; shared knowledge and straightforward coordination can also remain intact.\n\nMaintain the style's accessible urban texture through concrete place details and brisk scene changes, but do not transplant a setting or community the supplied material does not establish. Close the installment when the errand reaches an intelligible pause: the cast may board later than planned, pay more than intended, find a promised item absent, gain help from an unexpected worker, or arrive in time but without a missing resource. Keep the world active beyond the cast, and allow a later reply to pick up the same topic from a changed position.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaaval::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse early-modern comic/theatrical telling as a prose method rather than a script: the scene gains shape from entrances, exits, props, roles, and timing. An arrival may interrupt, alter, or simply join an ongoing exchange according to the actual scene. Track handled objects to orient practical business, with a change of attention or control only when one occurs.\n\nKeep the surface topic or request running through physical business. Two people may continue talking while one measures a rope, waits for a kettle, guards a road, or searches a pack. The talk need not halt for the action, and the action need not produce a joke. Comic timing can come from a pause, a missed cue, a repeated phrase, a plain person quietly completing useful work while others perform concern or authority. Serious timing works the same way through a delay, an entrance, or a prop that is no longer where it should be.\n\nDistinguish performance from private experience. A speaker may take a role, exaggerate, flatter, or affect ignorance while actually thinking about the objective, the risk, or the next step. The narration may show that contrast when access permits, but another character might only see the performance. Do not make every speaker witty or theatrical; one participant may simply continue working and tell the truth in an unperformed way.\n\nLet the objective remain practical. The theatrical surface organizes the scene but does not replace the adventure: a package may be found because someone left it beside a bench, a road may open because the right person happened to arrive, or a plan may fail because the crowd misread a familiar gesture. Do not impose a historic setting, a required loss of control, or a closing reversal. End when the scene's performance and work reach a natural beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavam::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nPick one concrete conversation or practical event and let it recur as an anchor: a disputed order, an arrangement at a doorway, a handoff, a decision to take one road, or an explanation of why a bag is missing. Revisit that moment from different attributed accounts, each tied to a particular time, speaker, and source. The actual words and actions remain stable even though the reader meets them out of order.\n\nUse the nonlinear return to show different access to the shared undertaking. One account may report what a person did but not why; another may contain the private calculation behind the same act; a third may remember a phrase incorrectly or hear it secondhand. Keep each teller's knowledge limited to what that person could have known at that time. Agreement is allowed: several voices may simply confirm an ordinary fact without a new revelation. Reports supply plurality when direct private access to other characters is unavailable.\n\nPreserve Tarantino's conversational texture across the accounts. A speaker's interest in a minor detail—food, road condition, a debt, a familiar phrase—may appear in one version and disappear from another, not because it is hidden evidence but because different people notice and value different things. Let them return to the topic in different situations, with one voice prolonging an issue another has already forgotten. These differences shape cooperation, blame, or the next attempt without making every omission significant.\n\nKeep the adventure causal and material. The nonlinear arrangement reorganizes reader discovery; it does not change event order for the cast or add supernatural influence. Show how a decision made at the anchor event affects later travel, recovery, pursuit, or repair. Close when a return to the scene has yielded enough to understand the present position, even if that understanding is partial and no one has solved the whole undertaking.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavan::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Light Novel\n\nOpen by establishing where the cast is in relation to the objective: one or two concrete sentences can name the place, what is being attempted, and what practical condition bears on the next action. Do not pause for a long introductory tour; begin the scene close enough to the prior work that the reader knows what the conversation may affect.\n\nUse paragraph breaks to follow actual shifts. A new speaker, a new action, a movement through a door, a handled item, or a change from dialogue to private response can each begin a new block. Let a sustained exchange use readable paragraphing at its actual length rather than requiring short paragraphs or a dense block of speech. Attribute each line clearly, and keep the speakers' differing investments visible: one may be focused on the plan, another on an unrelated preference, a third on a person who is not present.\n\nAllow a long conversational detour or a private passage to take its actual space. The readability comes from orientation, not from shortening every exchange. When the scene moves from one location or stage of the undertaking to another, mark that change plainly so the reader can follow who is present, what has changed in the terrain, and how much time has passed.\n\nKeep reaction beats tied to their owner and to the active scene. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to the character. It does not need to be a tactical payoff or a clue. Access to every private experience follows the active narrator and ownership contract; the style neither adds minds nor excludes access explicitly granted. Narrated private material does not automatically become another character's knowledge. Maintain the adventure's forward logic: the conversation may help, delay, or alter the plan, but the next opportunity remains continuous with what came before. End at a readable pause rather than forcing a cliffhanger, a confession, or a completed plan.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavao::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet one actual sensory detail from the occupied scene recur as the conversation continues: a loose shutter, a cooling engine, a rope shifting against a rail, the particular sound of someone's boots on the floor. Return to it when the talk pauses, when a speaker's attention changes, or when the next practical step begins. The recurrence gives the exchange a felt rhythm without by itself proving a motive or predicting a decision. Any practical significance of the actual sound or object follows its established role in the scene.\n\nKeep the dialogue itself in the speakers' own register. They can discuss a tool, a route, a debt, a meal, or a person they both know without becoming figurative or sharing the narrator's imagery. Let ordinary interests and particulars occupy real time. A comparison may develop feeling or attention, including an unchanged concern, without requiring new understanding. Its rhetoric does not establish lying or a reversal.\n\nUse plain action when spatial or causal orientation is needed. Show what the plan depends on, what the attempt changes, and how cooperation or improvisation affects the next opportunity. The image may retain its meaning, change it, or remain unresolved. End with the task still underway, the conversation unfinished, or the next action available rather than requiring the recurring detail to close the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavap::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present gesture, topic, or practical routine recall a particular earlier conversation. The trigger can be ordinary: someone repeats a phrase, reaches for the same tool, or asks the same question in a different place. Write the memory as an occasion the focal character actually lived through, not as a summary of what it must have meant.\n\nDistinguish three things: the remembered tone, the words the character can actually recall, and the interpretation available now. A remembered exchange may have been funny, easy, or useful without becoming a hidden explanation for the present task. Later knowledge can qualify the memory, but it cannot retroactively change what the character knew then.\n\nKeep the present undertaking moving. The old conversation may accompany preparation, travel, recovery, or a pause without deciding the next action. Direct help and genuine cooperation remain possible. Let the scene end with the task continued, a question unanswered, or an ordinary pleasure still pleasurable rather than requiring loss or regret to certify the reflection.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaq::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse a modern memoir account for the scene. The memoir belongs to an established participant, while the active narrator may present that person's account without becoming its protagonist. Preserve the narrator's person, ownership, and actual access. Reconstruct the earlier conversation as an actual occasion: where people stood, what they handled, what they said, and what each of them could know at the time.\n\nKeep ordinary interests genuine. The speakers can discuss a route, a price, a favor, or a shared acquaintance without every line becoming evidence of a later outcome. Let the sustained exchange occupy its own time. The memoir's later perspective may confirm, qualify, or simply revisit what happened, but it cannot give the earlier participants knowledge they did not have.\n\nSeparate private experience from memoir self-presentation. The teller may choose what to record, omit, or emphasize, but the underlying fictional interiority remains distinct from that performance. Show what the undertaking depended on, what the attempt changed, and how cooperation or improvisation affected the next opportunity. No confession, revelation, or present framing crisis is required to close the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavar::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Minimalist Prose\n\nState the objective-bearing position, object, or actual interruption directly. Use the physical anchors needed for this scene, such as a door, gauge, or map. Avoid redundant restatement, while returning to an anchor when it helps orientation or belongs to actual continuing attention. Let the dialogue carry the scene's rhythm.\n\nGive individual speakers their actual space. A sustained exchange about a tool, a route, a debt, or a shared interest can continue without narration explaining that each line matters. Trim repeated descriptions of tension or intent, not the private experience that belongs to the focal character. Actual private experience may be long enough to make sense and may align with spoken content. Trim a redundant explanatory summary without deleting the feeling or thought merely because it concerns what was said.\n\nKeep causality inferable. Show what the plan depends on, what the attempt changes, and how cooperation or improvisation affects the next opportunity. Ordinary unquotable lines and enjoyment can remain. End with a pending exchange, an unchanged task, or the next available action rather than requiring a maxim, new insight, or physical outburst.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavas::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nKeep the supplied objective and actual physical situation clear as the scene moves among dialogue, work, observation, recollection, and authorized private experience. The reader should always know where the characters are and what the plan depends on, even when the talk turns to something else.\n\nLet distinct people discuss particular interests. A topic can be enjoyed, used tactically, or left unfinished according to its speaker. One person may be passing time while another is negotiating; one may care about the subject and another may only need the answer. Do not make every mundane detail a plot device or every exchange a coded negotiation.\n\nPreserve genuine coordination and real consequences. Show what the attempt changes and how competence, cooperation, or improvisation affects the next opportunity. The scene may end with conditions unchanged, ordinary continuation, or a task still underway. No violent reversal is required to close the passage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavat::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose actual shared language or a recurring topic from the supplied relationship premise: an old argument, a private joke, a familiar question, a particular way of asking for help. Let it accompany a current task or request. The surface interest remains alive; the characters can talk about the thing itself, not only about what the thing signals.\n\nParticipants may mean the words alike or differently. One may hear affection where the other hears routine, or one may be negotiating while the other is simply enjoying the conversation. Keep private thought separate from reception. The focal character's understanding does not become shared knowledge, and another person's response does not prove what they felt.\n\nLet useful action, affection, disagreement, or a pending conversation carry the bond without requiring betrayal, rescue, or declaration to certify it. Show what the plan depends on, what the attempt changes, and how cooperation or improvisation affects the next opportunity. The scene may end with the relationship unchanged, the task continued, or a question still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavau::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the undertaking remembered shape through clear actors, compact declarations, and established recurring words. Open with a deliberate statement of who set out, what they meant to do, or what stood in the way. Return to established wording for a new application or continued remembered meaning, without requiring novelty or an ornamental epithet at every entrance.\n\nKeep ordinary topics and small interruptions around the act when they belong. The sustained exchange can include a complaint about the weather, a question about a tool, a joke that only one person finds funny, or a disagreement about the route. These particulars are part of the retelling, not noise to be stripped away. Distinguish actual words, recollection, and later embellishment; the teller's hindsight is not the actor's earlier knowledge.\n\nShow what the plan depended on, what the attempt changed, and how competence, cooperation, or improvisation affected the next opportunity. A deed may remain underway. Neither heroic language, completed consequence, nor a changed bond is required to close the passage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavav::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse psychological and essayistic narration in the supplied tense, person, and voice. Examine a situated claim arising from the encounter: a statement about the task, another person's reliability, or what a familiar topic now means within the plan. Keep the claim provisional and within the narrator's access. Return to its exact words, to observed physical business, or to a memory the narrator already holds in order to test its scope. A repeated phrase from the conversation may become an object of examination without being proven or exposed.\n\nLet the characters speak at natural length, with ordinary interests and particulars sustaining the exchange. Their talk is not the narrator's commentary, and a speaker may drop a topic, contradict a practical assumption, or continue with an irrelevant preference without announcing a theme. Track what the plan depends on through concrete changes: a tool must be reached, a route confirmed, a repair attempted, a message delivered. Weigh the claim through actual observations, supported recollection, or reported events within narrator access, including success and ordinary continuity as well as failure. The claim may stand, be qualified, or remain unresolved without a new event in this scene.\n\nDo not force the essayistic method to conclude with a thesis, a universal skepticism, or a rewritten version of the claim. The examination may simply pause when the task requires attention, when another speaker interrupts, or when the available particulars can support only a narrower version of the statement. Preserve the active narrator's boundaries and each character's separate knowledge: private access follows the actual contract rather than a new style restriction. Observation alone is not automatic access to intent, and any permitted inward knowledge remains separately attributed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaw::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Screenplay\n\nOpen a scene heading that names the location and its time, then use concise action lines to establish the occupied physical space and the current relation to the objective: where the characters are, what they can reach, and what task they are actively working. Keep each speaker cue distinct. Let dialogue carry genuine ordinary interests and particulars at length, so the talk has its own momentum instead of existing only to deliver information about the plan.\n\nWrite visible and audible events that make the plan's dependencies legible. Keep actual movement, inspection, and transfer legible. Show effects on the undertaking when they occur without making every physical action create a new opportunity. Use supported scene transitions when the undertaking moves to a connected location; a cut can follow dialogue or action without supplying a new plot result, while its represented location and time remain supported. A scene heading can mark a physical change of place or time, not a stylistic flourish. When voice-over or offscreen sound is used, keep it as a performed or externally audible element, and do not turn it into a substitute for the full nonspoken private experience required by the active contract.\n\nPreserve each character's actual agency and ownership of their own actions. Cooperation may work, improvisation may alter the plan, and a closing line can leave the next step underway. Do not require a confrontation, an explosion, or a discovery at the end of every scene. A cut may follow a completed task, a changed position, or an unanswered request. A reaction shot or expression cue is a visible event, not a disclosure of hidden intent.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavax::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Stage Play\n\nPresent the current interval of the supplied undertaking through a coherent playable arrangement. The form does not confine the whole journey to one room or redesign the objective. Use clear speaker labels, entrances, exits, and stage directions that name the objects, positions, and physical actions available to the cast. Establish what the objective requires from this room: an item that must be found or prepared, a person who must be persuaded or met, a route that must be confirmed, or work that can continue here while help is awaited. Make permissions a visible part of the blocking—who can open a door, handle an object, leave the room, or be overheard—so the audience can follow the task's practical state.\n\nLet the dialogue carry sustained exchanges with ordinary interests and particulars. The characters may discuss a familiar topic at length while engaged in physical work, so the scene does not become a static information transfer. A prop can change hands during a line, a pause can be covered by an action, and an entrance can redirect the conversation without ending it. Offstage activity may enter through credible reports, sounds, or consequences, but do not import what the stage cannot support.\n\nKeep full private experience in the selected nonspoken presentation; a monologue or aside is an additional performed element with its own audience and purpose rather than the default home for thought. The scene may end with the task still underway, the speakers in unchanged positions, or a request left unanswered. Do not force a revelation, a violent punctuation, or a gathered confrontation at the close. A practical result, a failed attempt, or a decision to wait are all usable exits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavay::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow one authorized mind at a time, with any perspective transition explicit and governed by the active contract. Let perception, memory, private wording, bodily sensation, boredom, enjoyment, and practical worry arrive in that person's actual associative order, including interruptions and reversion to an earlier topic. Keep the external conversation recoverable within the active perspective. Quote only wording the narration can actually supply; a portion the focal person did not hear may remain unknown until a supported repetition, report, or other authorized source provides it. Keep real dialogue distinct from the speaker's imagined reply, a remembered version of a line, or a feared outcome replayed as if it had happened.\n\nUse a plain external cue—a door closing, a hand on a tool, a name spoken, a sudden silence—to reorient the prose when the private thread becomes too diffuse. Reorientation need not resolve the association; it may simply return the narration to the present room and the next spoken line. Track actual elapsed events independently of the length of the thought passage so that the plan's state remains legible: the group has not moved farther, the task has not advanced, or a concrete action has occurred while the mind was elsewhere.\n\nThe style adds no new access to another mind and does not remove access explicitly allowed by the active contract. A participant may understand or misread another through actual speech, shared history, observation, or inference; intuition is not automatic proof, and ordinary correct understanding remains possible. The stream may contain uncertainty, wrong guesses, and repeated worries without producing a tactic, an insight, or a visible action at the end of every passage. Keep the adventure's practical dependencies visible in the surrounding events, so the mental drift accompanies an ongoing attempt rather than replacing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavaz::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCompose the scene as an exchange of readable, sender-attributed messages. Give each participant a recognizable digital habit: length, punctuation, response speed, use of images or voice notes, and willingness to continue a side topic while coordinating the plan. Let ordinary interests and particulars occupy genuine conversational space. A long typed message or voice note may be rambling, affectionate, speculative, or argumentative without being strategically significant, and it remains what its sender chose to transmit.\n\nKeep the undertaking's practical state legible through what is sent, received, and acted upon. A message about a route, a tool, a meeting place, or a completed errand should make clear what the plan now depends on. Record only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. When a message is edited, keep the earlier event distinct from the edited version and show an original trace only where the actual interface and viewpoint support access to it; an unsent draft and private thought are separate material and must appear outside the transmitted text where the active contract permits.\n\nLet receipt remain distinct from comprehension. A read receipt does not mean the receiver has agreed, understood, or changed their plan. A delayed answer may mean distraction, disagreement, or ordinary life rather than concealment, unless the scene establishes otherwise. The interval can close on an ongoing exchange, a pending reply, or a completed coordination. Do not require a new plan or a sinister notification to end the scene, and do not grant a speaker private knowledge merely because the narration includes it; any actual access still follows the supplied world and authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavba::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Visual Novel\n\nEnter the scene by naming the current location and one relevant object, position, or room feature that ties the cast to the objective: the map spread on the table, the locked cabinet, the cart being loaded, the rope tied at the top of the path. Give concise scene-setting passages and unmistakable dialogue attribution so the reader always knows who is speaking and where the speakers stand. Move among scene description, spoken exchange, and the focal character's private response according to the encounter's actual material, not a fixed rotation.\n\nLet dialogue sustain ordinary interests and particulars at length. Characters may speak about food, a remembered place, a disliked piece of equipment, or a shared joke while doing practical work. Recurring cues or topics may orient later moments or simply remain enjoyable; a repeated gesture, object, or phrase does not need to have changed significance by the scene's end. Focal narration and inner response belong to the active narrator or viewpoint character. Access to other minds follows the active narrator and ownership contract. An expression cue is an observable detail rather than automatic proof of hidden intent.\n\nPreserve actual cooperation and active ownership. A character can hand over a tool, refuse a request for their own reason, repair something, or make a mistake that changes the plan. Preserve actual consequences of discovery or completed work without requiring a clear next action. The style supplies no branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable interface elements and neither grants nor cancels the active contract's character-decision permissions. The scene may close on continuing work, a changed position, or an unanswered question without requiring a climactic final line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaavbb::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Quentin Tarantino / Web Serial\n\nOpen the installment by recovering only the current task state and the exact prior wording or shared knowledge needed for the scene: where the group is, what the plan now depends on, and which earlier topic or promise this exchange returns to. Do not retell the whole investigation, journey, or preparation that produced the moment. Keep earlier events and the private experience that actually occurred intact, while current concerns may develop according to the person and circumstances; a later installment may show that new evidence qualifies an old interpretation, but it cannot rewrite the physical event or make a character deliberately forget a supported conclusion.\n\nLet the conversation contain ordinary interests and particulars that persist across installments. A recurring topic may return for enjoyment, orientation, or companionship without producing a new result each time. A familiar reference can hold continuity, while a repeated phrase may acquire meaning only after several installments. Preserve actual receipt, knowledge, and agency: a character can answer later, refuse for their own reason, or remain unaware of what the narration has established.\n\nAdvance the through-line through travel, work, rest, preparation, discovery, or quiet recovery. Allow tasks to span replies, and let an installment close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration gives selected facts room to accrue meaning rather than making a fair resolution perpetually recede. Do not require an escalating confrontation, a revelation, or a cliffhanger at the end of each reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaawaa::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Salman Rushdie / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the strain through the practical decisions of the undertaking: accepting help, changing a route, making a request, or dividing work. Where a practical choice carries emotional strain, develop the specific private reaction to this person or task. A character can accept help with relief or mixed feeling, or apologize while hurt remains; the response follows the actual relationship rather than a required cost of every choice.\n\nUse the Rushdie profile's associative movement to carry that strain. A familiar phrase, a remembered account, or a public story can surface in the authorized mind during the work, but keep its source and ownership clear. Distinguish a character's association from broader commentary available to the active narrator; keep both within their actual sources and permissions. It may explain why a request feels loaded or why a route change stings, without becoming the factual cause of the event. The practical terms—what must be done, who can do it, what depends on it—remain legible even when the emotional register is turbulent.\n\nLet other people answer plainly or mean something else. A helper may notice accurately and respond well, miss the strain, or attend to another concern. The owned private experience can remain unresolved or develop while the task advances, concludes or stays pending. Do not force a confession, a new historical revelation, or a repaired bond. Keep the current opportunity legible while allowing feeling to persist, ease or change at its own pace.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPaaawab::♢ * [STYLE] Action / Adventure / Salman Rushdie / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by orienting the exact contribution: what the submitted move does, what the current objective is, and what means are actually available. Keep the shared interval coherent in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Each speaker's words stay their own; do not let the narrator's associative range leak into what other participants know or say.\n\nLet the active account associate a name, object, or remark with a sourced recollection or public story when the moment permits. Give the association its needed extent in the permitted narrative or nonspoken channel, identifying its owner and source; concrete scene anchors keep the shared interval recoverable. Another participant may recognize the reference, misunderstand it, or have a different history with it. That difference can surface in their response—a pause, a correction, a flat reply—without requiring a digression or a shared revelation.\n\nPreserve active ownership and actual private experience. A reply can continue the work, adjust the plan, or leave a question pending. The larger story does not need to advance in every turn; what matters is that the shared attempt remains intelligible and the next opportunity follows from what was actually done or said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
