            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-character_drama-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Character Drama · 02/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == character_drama}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Character Drama. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-character_drama; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPabafag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nMove from a specific exchange into relevant history or social context within the narrator's access. A request, refusal, or small act can open into family history, work, custom, or public expectation. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement to make the wider account part of the scene rather than an interruption.\n\nReconnect the context to the particular exchange. The wider account should make the concrete act more legible, not replace it with a moral or a summary. Use exact activity and direct language: the object handled, the door closed, the letter read, the money counted.\n\nKeep speakers in their ordinary registers and their own amount of speech. A composed narrative may frame an unresolved feeling without resolving it. The paragraph can end on the particular person or object rather than a lesson or a changed judgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen with the occasion of the telling: who is present, what practical or relational purpose the account serves, and why this teller chooses to speak now. Establish the teller's access to the embedded events. The story may recount what the teller saw, did, heard from a named source, or was told later, but the narration should not quietly grant knowledge the teller could not have.\n\nBuild the embedded account from exact objects, actions, and spoken lines. Use direct language and let the teller's selection become part of the characterization: a remembered phrase may be repeated because it pleased the teller, because it still troubles them, or because they believe the listener needs to hear it. Do not require the teller to confess, conceal, or explain the selection. The omission can be ordinary: the teller may leave out what seemed unimportant, what they did not understand, or what belongs to someone else.\n\nReturn to the outer scene through an actual response or continuing attention. A listener may ask a question, repeat a phrase, look away, offer something, continue a task, or simply listen. Let that participation belong to the present relationship without requiring a changed understanding or a correct interpretation. The teller may accept the response, misunderstand it, or leave it alone.\n\nKeep the narrative levels distinct. Use a repeated phrase or object to connect the embedded account to the outer occasion, but do not make recurrence certify the story's truth or force a change in the relationship. The telling is an action in the present; the told events remain particular and limited to what the teller can know.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose social or developmental realism and organize the passage around a return: an earlier act, exchange, or expectation becomes relevant to a later occasion. The return need not be announced as memory. It may appear through a repeated task, a familiar phrase, a similar room, or a decision the character now makes differently or in the same way.\n\nRender both occasions with exact particulars and direct speech. Show what the person asked for, accepted, withheld, or misunderstood in the earlier scene, then show the later conduct that carries that history. The connection may be causal, partial, or contradictory. A person can remember an old instruction and still act against it, follow it without naming it, or discover that it no longer explains the present.\n\nLet the later scene remain a present encounter rather than a summary of development. The character may be more competent, more tired, more honest, or more evasive; none of these is automatic proof of moral improvement. Keep the narration close to what the character does and says, and let private understanding, when authorized, include uncertainty or refusal to settle the meaning of the past.\n\nA closing lesson is not required; a character may reach an understanding when that is what actually occurs. The earlier experience may matter because it shaped a habit, because it now seems false, or because it simply returns without being resolved. The story's interest lies in the particular way the past bears on the present choice, not in a completed arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nEnter the scene through an actual point where old authority meets present life: a room, key, desk, title, account, permission, or object that carries an inherited claim. Describe it in exact material terms and show how a character uses it, avoids it, asks for it, or must work around it. The authority should be concrete enough to affect the exchange, not merely atmospheric.\n\nKeep the dialogue and action direct. A person may speak plainly about what the authority permits, costs, or protects. Let the relationship carry the intimate weight: one character may trust the holder of the authority, resent the obligation, need the access, or misunderstand what it requires. Private desire or discomfort, where authorized, can be extensive and need not be reduced to a single visible reaction.\n\nUse the material detail to make the social history present without turning it into evidence of corruption. A worn object may indicate long use, not moral decay. A formal permission may be honored, resented, or questioned for practical reasons. Atmosphere can heighten the encounter, but it does not prove what the authority means or what the characters will do.\n\nLet a social mask fall only when the particular relationship supports it. Familiarity may produce genuine ease, partial trust, or a careful lowering of formality. The scene does not require a confession, a betrayal, or a revelation of hidden power. The drama lies in how the person meets the inherited arrangement and what they choose to ask, accept, or change.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose testimonial accounting and establish an actual participant who speaks or records for a particular audience and purpose. The account may be given to a family member, an official, a friend, a community gathering, or a later reader, but the occasion should shape what the teller includes. Open with enough of that occasion to make the telling an action in the present.\n\nUse exact acts and direct phrases to distinguish what the person lived from what they heard or learned afterward. When the teller reports another person's words or actions, keep the source visible: the teller saw it, was told it, read it, or inferred it. Do not let the testimony quietly become omniscient.\n\nLet selection carry personal meaning. The teller may state feeling plainly, repeat a phrase that still matters, or omit something for a supported reason such as shame, loyalty, uncertainty, or the belief that the listener would not understand. Omission need not be concealment with a hidden truth behind it; it may simply be what the teller cannot or will not put into the account.\n\nKeep required private experience outside the performed or recorded testimony. The testimony is a composed artifact with an audience; the teller's live unspoken thought, where authorized, remains separate and does not become part of what the audience hears. Return to the accounting occasion through a concrete response or continuation, without requiring the listener to resolve or judge the account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect psychological realism and let a particular authorized mind organize the passage. Begin with a concrete detail from the encounter: an object handled, a phrase spoken, a sound, a gesture, or a change in the room. Keep the literal event clear while the focal character's attention moves from the detail to memory, feeling, or inward words.\n\nUse direct language for both the outer scene and the inner experience. The character may name an emotion plainly, recognize it only through a physical sensation, or understand the detail differently from the other person. Let sustained psychological narration follow the character's actual attention, including associations when they occur, without requiring associative syntax to organize every sentence. A memory may arrive because the detail resembles an earlier occasion, because the character has been waiting for this moment, or because the present exchange revises what they thought they knew.\n\nDo not require the character to interpret correctly. They may misread the other person's intention, notice only part of what is happening, or live with a feeling they cannot settle. The inner experience can be extensive and need not be cut short by speech or action.\n\nReconnect with the literal encounter where actual attention or events take the passage there; private experience can also continue across the active boundary without a choice or response. The character may speak, remain silent, perform a task, or leave something unsaid. The scene's meaning lies in the particular relation between the precise perception and the person's private understanding, not in a lesson extracted from the moment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect social realism and chronicle. Follow a shared place, recurring activity, or relationship through specific ordered encounters. The order may be chronological, seasonal, or tied to a recurring event such as a market day, a visit, a meal, a delivery, or a public occasion. Let each encounter be a distinct scene with its own exact particulars and direct exchanges.\n\nShow continuities through repeated objects, tasks, phrases, or arrangements. A person may always bring the same thing, ask the same question, or sit in the same place. Show actual changes through what someone now provides, refuses, accepts, or no longer mentions. The chronicle should make the relation between continuity and change legible without announcing it as progress or decline.\n\nAttribute remembered or reported material to its source. One person may recall an earlier occasion, repeat what another said, or describe what the community believes. Do not turn a shared account into everyone's private mind. Authorized interiority belongs to the particular focal character, not to the group.\n\nKeep the prose direct and the social context present through practical activity. Work, money, food, transport, housing, and obligation can carry the relationships. The passage need not end with a judgment about the community or a lesson about the characters. Its interest lies in the particular ways people ask, provide, stay, or change across the ordered occasions.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter through the actual activity. Begin with a concrete action, object, or exchange that places the reader in the immediate situation, then establish who is speaking through clear attribution and selective detail. Do not open with a summary of the relationship or a block of background.\n\nKeep spatial and temporal changes easy to follow. When the scene moves from one room to another, from action to speech, or from speech to private response, use a clean paragraph break or a direct transition. Explain an unfamiliar term, name, or practical arrangement only as much as the reader needs to follow the present exchange; do not pause the scene for a glossary.\n\nLet paragraph movement pass among action, dialogue, and authorized inward response according to the focal character's attention. A reaction may be serious, uncertain, angry, or quiet. Straightforward language does not require every reaction to be short, understated, or comic. The character may think at length about what the other person asked or about what they themselves are deciding.\n\nUse exact objects and direct lines to carry what remains unsaid. A person may withhold an answer, repeat a practical instruction, or continue a task instead of speaking. The personal significance of the moment should be accessible through the concrete exchange and the focal character's actual experience, not through a closing explanation. End at the active scene boundary, including within continuing private experience or an exchange whose next response is not yet determined.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nBegin with an exact object, sound, or activity from the immediate scene and let the narrator's attention develop it into a comparison that remains traceable to that source. The image may unfold across a sentence or a short paragraph, but it must not detach from the literal encounter: a wet glass, a door left open, a repeated knock, or a task done slowly can carry the feeling without becoming a symbol that replaces the action. Use direct statements to interrupt or support the lyrical movement when the exchange needs clarity. A character may say plainly what they want or refuse, and the prose can then return to the image with that knowledge in place.\n\nRepeat the image or a sound related to it when a later moment gives it a new application. The recurrence may preserve meaning as readily as alter it; the same object can mean the same thing on a second occasion and still matter because the person has returned to it. Do not require the image to resolve into a decision or a completed insight. Let the shaped motif guide the inward movement while the outward activity continues in concrete terms. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the active perspective, and do not let another character's mind enter the lyrical passage unless that access is authorized. The paragraph may end with the image still present, the exchange unfinished, or the feeling named directly, without a compulsory explanation of the image. The narrator may express their own interpretation where authorized, rather than imposing a universal meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWhen memory belongs, place a specific past act, phrase, or object beside its present occasion without letting the past replace the present. The remembered particular should be exact: the way a person once held a tool, the words they used when refusing an invitation, the sound of a door closing on a particular afternoon. Let the present scene continue in concrete terms while the recollection sits alongside it. The character may still enjoy the present moment, and that pleasure does not have to be undercut by the memory. A familiar meal can taste good; a task can go well; a person can be glad to see someone and still remember what is no longer available.\n\nAllow direct feeling or an omission to follow the person's voice rather than a resigned conclusion. The character may name what they miss, or they may say nothing and return to the work at hand. Do not require the scene to end by announcing impermanence or by making the remembered person newly unavailable. The reflection can simply coexist with the ongoing exchange, and the interval may close with the present activity still underway. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the active perspective, and do not let another character's private memory enter unless that access is authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the account as modern memoir: a composed retrospective in which a later teller selects and presents an earlier encounter. Identify whose account is being presented under the active narrator contract, and keep the participant's earlier knowledge distinct from the later teller's understanding. The person in the remembered scene may not know what the teller now knows. The teller can recount remembered feelings of their own earlier self or another participant's experience supported by actual sources and narrator access, without inventing unprovided knowledge. Render the earlier encounter in direct language with precise objects and activities: what was said, what was done, what was left unsaid. Then let the later teller's reading appear as a separate movement, marked by a shift in tense, a comment on the memory, or a return to the present occasion of writing.\n\nLink the two occasions with an exact phrase or activity that recurs across the gap. The same words may be confirmed, reconsidered, or left uncertain in the later account. Every uncertainty need not be resolved; preserve an actual resolution when supported, and do not require the teller to arrive at a lesson. Composed memoir and actual live private experience remain separate channels: the memoir records what its author chooses to record, while required interiority still renders the present experience of the composing mind or the authorized focal character under the active contract. Keep the language, period, and community specific to the supplied setting rather than importing a generic regional atmosphere.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse a small number of exact details and direct clauses to establish what was said, done, or left open. Select the particulars that make the personal choice intelligible: the object someone set down, the word they repeated, the question they did not answer. Do not delete the inward connection that explains why the act matters. A thought or feeling may be rendered in a single direct sentence, and an essential longer thought may remain long enough to make sense. Omit redundant explanation where the context already supports the inference, but do not omit the person's actual feeling or motive merely because it would lengthen the passage.\n\nLet silence be ordinary. A pause need not conceal guilt, and a withheld answer need not be a refusal. Direct emotional speech is available; a character may say exactly what they want or what they cannot accept. The interval may end before a choice is completed, with the work still underway or the question still open. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the active perspective, and do not let another character's private experience enter unless that access is authorized. The prose should remain economical without turning into a sequence of performed fragments; a precise extended sentence may serve the method better than several short ones.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the encounter in sustained scene-first prose, moving naturally among the acts, words, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience of the focal character. Choose particulars that matter to this person without making each object symbolic. A cup, a tool, a coat, or a repeated gesture can appear because it belongs to the practical life of the scene, not because it must stand for something else. Let dialogue carry its own weight in direct language, and let a character's speech remain individual rather than uniformly terse or eloquent.\n\nAllow an omission to carry meaning only where the relationship supports it. A person may not say what they feel because they are tired, because they do not yet know, or because the moment does not require it; the narration can still make the feeling available under the active contract. Feeling may be named directly, and inner experience can continue without materially changing the next action. A character may think through a memory or a doubt and then return to the task at hand. Do not force the scene to close with a completed insight or a changed decision. The paragraph may end with the exchange still underway, the work resumed, or the question left open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose moments from the actual relationship premise and history: an old phrase, a known preference, a permission, or a recurring activity that belongs to these particular people. Let a practical exchange matter because of who they are to each other, whether the meaning is spoken plainly or partly understood through omission. A character may hand over a familiar object, repeat a private phrase, or perform a small task in the way the other person recognizes. Do not insert a roster of familiar tropes; use only the supplied premise and its established history.\n\nPreserve full private feeling and legitimate differences in what each person knows or wants. One character's recognition need not be the other's, and a repeated act may mean different things to each of them. The scene may include rivalry, difficult attachment, comfort, or discovery according to the actual premise, but it need not resolve into a milestone. Recognition can simply be present, and the interval may end with the relationship still unfinished. Keep the narrator's access consistent with the active perspective, and do not let one character's private experience enter another's mind unless that access is authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings and compact declarations around an established act, promise, or shared history. Begin with a phrase that names what is remembered: who kept the promise, who refused the request, who returned the thing. Then expand the declaration into exact activity and attributed speech. Show the practical particulars: what was carried, what was said, what was left undone. Keep the diction plain; do not inflate an ordinary errand into mock-heroic combat or ceremonial doom.\n\nUse recurrent phrasing when a later occurrence gives it a new application. The same compact phrase may return with a different weight because the situation has changed or because another person now speaks it. Keep actual scale and access intact. A present undertaking may still be unfinished, and the account may end with the work resumed or the question still open. A community's description of a person does not dictate that person's private feeling or the next outcome. The participant may object to one detail of the remembered story, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective, and do not let shared reputation replace the focal character's authorized interiority.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narrative as the actual movement of the scene. Open from a concrete position the authorized narrator can presently hold: a plausible claim about what an exchange asks for, what a person withholds, or what an offer would mean. Keep the claim stated in direct, practical words, not as an abstract maxim.\n\nThen place beside it an exact case from the available scene or recollection: the specific movement, object, or spoken phrase that the claim ought to explain. Give the case its actual personal conditions. An untouched dish or unfinished line may bear on a claim that someone avoids help, but does not prove that interpretation. Establish acceptance through an actual answer or supported context before treating a later act as its consequence.\n\nReturn to the claim where the essayistic movement calls for it, whether the case supports it, qualifies it, or leaves it unsettled. Neither strain nor altered meaning is required. A lesson or refutation may occur when earned, but neither is owed. The narrator may stop before the thought is settled, holding the case and the claim apart. Keep the essayistic frame in the narration; dialogue itself should remain individual and direct. Preserve the active narrator's actual access to private experience and do not give another character a conclusion automatically through reader access; preserve any understanding they independently possess or acquire.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the public scene in concise action blocks and clear dialogue. Use action blocks for particular visible or audible conditions and events, with movement where it actually occurs: a cup placed on a table, a door held open, a pause before a direct answer, a task continued during speech. Let the exact movement accompany what is said, not explain it. A person can put down a tool and still refuse; another can say yes without looking up. The action shows the exchange, not the motive.\n\nKeep required nonspoken interiority outside the scene description and character lines. Use the active hidden or visible interiority convention for the authorized person's actual feeling or reasoning. Do not turn that private prose into a voice-over, parenthetical acting note, or a character line another person can hear. If voice-over is separately authorized, keep it as performed speech with its own audience and purpose, distinct from live private thought.\n\nUse scene headings when a genuine change of place or time occurs. A cut may set another room beside the one just shown: one person leaves the request hanging while the other resumes ordinary work. The contrast does not need to explain itself. Let the last image or line follow from the immediate exchange and stop there, without requiring a reveal, confession, or completed answer. Preserve the active narrator and point of view; a screenplay action block cannot grant access to a mind not authorized by the active contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nPlace the exchange in a continuous shared stage space. Keep entrances, exits, and the positions of people and objects clear enough to be performed. Where the scene supplies physical business, make it exact: counting cash, folding a cloth, setting a chair upright, opening a letter. Connect a line to that business where the relation matters; a scene may instead use position, stillness, and speech without assigning an invented task or prop. A pause can be an action performed more slowly, not an instruction to feel strongly.\n\nLet dialogue carry the request, refusal, acceptance, or misunderstanding in plain words. A character may speak at length when the relationship and immediate need support it; directness is a matter of exact wording, not a short-line quota. Do not let stage directions explain what the characters feel. Directions show movement, timing, and visible use of objects.\n\nKeep required nonspoken private experience in the active hidden or visible interiority form, not as a parenthetical performance cue or an aside. An aside is performed speech when separately authorized, with a dramatic audience and purpose; private reasoning is not automatically audible to the house. A request may remain pending, and the scene may end on ordinary business without an emotional summary. Preserve authorial access according to the active contract; neither the audience nor another character acquires a mind that is not authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nEnter the authorized mind through a specific sensory particular in the present scene: the weight of a cup, a wet sleeve, the sound of a spoon against a sink, the give of a door. Let the mind move from that particular by association rather than by chronological report. A word heard now may join a phrase remembered from another morning, a bodily coldness may bring back an old confidence, or a question may keep turning under later perceptions. Let syntax and paragraph break follow the actual movement of thought when it runs or stalls.\n\nKeep each association traceable to the person's concrete experience. If the mind leaves the present into memory, let a remembered object, voice, or act establish the memory's ground. External time and other people's behavior continue according to actual events: someone may keep speaking during the thought, finish a task, or change the room. A perception can return the mind to the present, but no fixed number of associations before return is required.\n\nThe mind need not orbit a practical task or the immediate conversation, though it may return to either where that is the actual concern. The concern may be an unanswered request, a withheld apology, or what the person is trying not to say, and it may arrive through plain words inside the thought rather than figurative decoration. Preserve access only to the active narrator or authorized focal mind. Do not let another character perceive what has not been spoken or otherwise established. Close where the mental movement has brought the person, without requiring the thought to resolve, confess, or become visible to others.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the scene through sender-attributed messages and only the channel details that affect the exchange: a delay that leaves a message unread, a typing indicator that vanishes, a read receipt followed by silence, an edit or deleted message where the active context establishes it. Let each person's practical digital habits stay individual; one may write and send quickly, another may leave incomplete sentences, another may answer the second message but not the first.\n\nKeep transmitted words plain and exact. A repeated question may mean pressure, worry, or a practical need that the first answer did not satisfy; do not make repetition automatically prove concealment. An unanswered request may be felt privately by the recipient without another message being sent. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text, available to the reader only through supported narrator and interface access; it is not the sender's live private thought. Keep it distinct from the actual transcript and distinct from authorized hidden or visible interiority. Mark its unsent state clearly when it appears; formatting never makes an unsent draft a transmitted message.\n\nLet the recipient interpret later, revise an impression, or leave a concern alone. If the scene includes required nonspoken private experience, keep it separate from transmitted text; a sent message contains what the sender chose to send, not necessarily the whole private state. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. Do not add message features merely to create subtext. Stop when the actual exchange or silence has established the present state, without requiring a response, question, or completed decision to close the transcript.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nPlace the encounter through an exact visual particular: where someone sits, what their hand stays beside, how a coat is left across a chair, which part of the room receives the light. Use concise scene-setting and clear speaker attribution. Let dialogue continue at its own pace; a cue need not appear before every line and a reaction need not follow every cue.\n\nWhen the authorized focal person's attention goes toward the cue, follow it into their actual interpretation or feeling. The movement may be short or extended: a person may see an object and think what it was used for, remember who gave it, or feel that the other is waiting. Keep the observation distinct from what it might mean. A smile, a long pause, or a hand kept in a pocket is fact; the focal meaning attached to it is interpretation and may be wrong.\n\nKeep reader access distinct from cast knowledge. The focal person's reading is not automatically public when they speak or act; others know only what their established access and the actual communication support. Do not present a background or expression cue as proof of a hidden motive. The scene may end on an unresolved request, continuing private experience, or a silence with understanding changed, unchanged, or unsettled. No branching menu, romance route, image, or executable engine command is activated by this style. Preserve the active narrator and persona; first person is not imposed, and the focal inner response belongs only to the mind the active contract authorizes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nKeep the personal through-line alive by returning to a selected earlier particular only when the present scene needs it: the same question rephrased, the same object now in another place, the same task taken up again. Recover that particular briefly and accurately. Do not retell the entire prior installment or replace what actually happened with a convenient altered version. Preserve stable conditions as well as changes; a relationship may remain suspended, an obligation may continue unchanged, and a repeated phrase may carry the same feeling it did before.\n\nLet the returned particular enter the current exchange in an ordinary way. The person may ask the same question because the answer still matters, touch the same coat without deciding to give it back, or perform the same chore with a different relationship to it. Repetition can deepen understanding without requiring a new interpretation at every appearance. A present use of an old detail may change what it suggests, but the earlier event remains part of the history.\n\nGive the present installment enough immediate scene to be readable without a full recap: who is present, what is being done, and what remains at issue should emerge through direct action and dialogue. Do not isolate the installment from its history. Preserve required actual inward experience under the active contract. Private feeling or thought may remain unresolved without a new decision or a change to the serial through-line. Close on completed work, ordinary activity, a pending request, or private uncertainty. Serial duration lets particular concerns accrue meaning over time; it does not require an emotional revelation or resolved decision before the installment can stop.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nChoose an actual request, admission, refusal, or return that matters to a particular person or relationship, even when no immediate outward consequence follows. Let the person's private reasoning and the spoken exchange belong to the same situation but not necessarily carry the same terms: someone may say less than they know, say one truthful part of a larger worry, or say exactly what they mean while still feeling the exposure of saying it. Render reasoning and feeling at their actual place before, during or after speech and at their needed extent, without a fixed placement or requirement to appear at all three times.\n\nMake visible how ideology and self-image give the difficulty its personal shape. A character may frame asking for help as a verdict on their competence, treat accepting an offer as proof of debt, or refuse because a longer-held principle makes the practical matter feel indecent. Allow another person to answer from a different concern, including genuine relief, ordinary generosity, or an accurate but differently ordered sense of the same principle. Do not convert being helped into humiliation, and do not require the speaker to denounce their prior view before accepting assistance.\n\nLet the strain alter the texture of the interaction rather than simply supplying an emotional label. One exchange may pause before a needed word; a later gesture may arrive before the words that explain it; a third may move too quickly from a hard admission into a smaller practical task. Follow any actual effect on subsequent behavior while allowing conduct to continue unchanged: a person who asked may now be attentive in a way they could not manage earlier, or may pull back precisely because the request has been granted. Comfort may be partial, awkward or genuinely settling; the underlying question can persist, ease or be resolved according to the actual people and exchange. The scene may end in the middle of that unresolved condition; it should not be driven toward mandatory confession, full repair, or an announced lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nStart from the submitted contribution and resolve it through the action, speech and experience the active authorship contract permits within the present shared scene. Let an NPC respond to what was actually said while remaining occupied with their own actual concern, whether already stated, shared with the speaker or different. Their agreement, hesitation, question, or practical adjustment should not require them to adopt the player's reasoning or make a public speech about their own condition.\n\nGive the selected character's actual private reasons their needed extent in the active nonspoken presentation; the consideration can continue beyond a reply boundary without being reduced to an unwritten plan. They may catch one part of another person's meaning, decide to address only the practical request, or recognize that a larger argument touches a principle they have been carrying and still choose not to finish it. Where thought and speech differ, ground that difference in the person's actual situation; they can also align. Preserve their separate channels: the narration can contain a fuller perception, doubt, or remembered obligation without transferring that knowledge to the other party. Do not force the character to announce every inference or turn each exchange into a debate.\n\nThe shared interval can continue through recognizable ordinary behavior: a task that gets resumed, a question that gets answered in terms the speaker can manage, an offer that changes what the other character now knows or can do. Let NPCs pursue their own interests and cooperate when their actual concerns permit it. If the exchange does not resolve the larger matter, leave the continuation rooted in the last available state of the relationship and the scene: someone may leave, accept the current arrangement, begin a practical errand, or wait for an answer already requested. Do not punctuate the reply with an artificial question, changed option, cliffhanger, or complete confession. Keep paragraphing scannable around action, dialogue, and private response, with unmistakable speaker attribution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and satirical realism by placing a stated moral description of an arrangement beside its practical operations. Choose a claim about fairness, duty, or deservingness that belongs to a particular person: a worker may say the schedule is equitable, a relative may say the household helps everyone in turn, an employer may say that opportunity is open. Then show the actual terms through whom the arrangement costs time, money, room, or responsibility, and how the participating people experience those terms, whether equal, different or not yet tested.\n\nAttribute every position explicitly. One speaker may believe the generous description sincerely while implementing the arrangement in a way that favors their own ease; another may repeat the description because it is expected, without examining whether it matches the receipts, the hours, or the repeated tasks. A third may state the discrepancy plainly and still benefit from leaving the present arrangement in place. The satirical pressure develops from that alignment between speech and practice rather than from a narrator's contempt or a sudden unmasking. Do not assign hypocrisy automatically; allow the comparison to show a claim untested, fully or partially supported, qualified by a particular circumstance, or contradicted on an actual practical point.\n\nLet Dostoevskian self-argument arise through the difficulty of seeing one's own position. A character may defend the arrangement, notice an exception that touches someone they depend on, defend it again with a different reason, and then act in a small way that does not resolve the conflict. Preserve private experience as their actual response to the comparison, while other characters' knowledge follows their actual sources and permitted access rather than reader exposure to that private response. The scene may expose a contradiction without changing the arrangement; reform, reversal, and public confrontation are not required results.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nKeep the prose hard-edged and bodily by selecting particular conditions that bear on the present request or refusal: the weight of a load, the cold arriving through a coat, the worn stairs, the exact cost of a meal or medicine, the movement of hands when someone helps or avoids helping. Use direct clauses and short transitions so each physical particular stays connected to the exchange instead of decorating it. Vary the rhythm when one concrete action interrupts a longer consideration or when a task resumes before the argument is over.\n\nMake the material situation a meeting point for a person's convictions. Someone may value exactness because they count what the work costs; another may hold a principle about independence while their body already shows fatigue; a third may help with practical competence without adopting the other person's moral language. Show what each party can actually do under the circumstances, including genuine cooperation and ordinary care. Do not treat physical difficulty as proof of virtue, blame, or bad character, and do not require an injury, a confrontation, or a hardened refusal whenever the situation is uncomfortable.\n\nExtend a thought when the character's reasoning genuinely needs room. Let plain sensory information and private argument sit together: a person can notice the temperature, the unpaid bill, or another person's careful hands, and then move into a longer internal dispute about what accepting help would allow or what the needed action means. Compress redundant framing without deleting the lived reasoning, sensation or feeling. Private experience does not need to change an action or add a new inference to retain its needed extent. Keep the ending on the live emotional or practical state of the characters; it does not need to resolve the request, convert the stubborn speaker, or announce that the city, the work, or the relationship has broken them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the story through a sequence of letters, notes, reports, accounts, or other records, each composed by a named author for a definite recipient or purpose at a particular time. Let the selection within each document be governed by that situation. A letter asking for help may present the request as a matter of household duty; a reply may address only the practical arrangement; a note to a third person may describe the same event as an imposition. Let justification and emphasis follow the actual audience and purpose; they can change or remain stable without making consistency suspicious.\n\nLet Dostoevskian self-argument operate within the composition of records. A writer may begin with a general claim about fairness or gratitude, qualify it when the concrete person comes into view, cross out or speak around a reason they cannot send, and finish with a smaller request that contradicts the earlier principle only in practice. If a draft or unsent passage is included, show it as an artifact with its own material condition and keep its composed content distinct from the writer's actual live nonspoken experience in the selected channel, even when their wording overlaps; a finished letter does not supply everything its author felt while writing.\n\nPreserve readable chronology and the actual place of each later record, including confirmation, continued discussion or an unchanged practical need without a required new fact or delay. A reply can fail to answer the moral argument because love, time, or administrative necessity pulls the exchange elsewhere; the earlier writer's concern may remain active underneath later records without being restated. Use retrieval, enclosure, or a dated margin note to connect entries when relevant, but do not append an omniscient paragraph after every document that tells the reader what it really means. End on the last available record's state of the relationship or the task, not on a forced reconciliation or a finished verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nMake care a selected and concrete part of the story. Pick an accommodation, remembered preference, or shared pleasure that already fits the supplied relationship: the correct tea, a quiet errand taken over without being discussed, an open place by the window, the return of a borrowed object, or a meal that the other person can eat without effort. Show the care being carried out in ordinary terms instead of turning it into a demonstration the recipient must interpret or repay. Let personality and privacy remain intact; not everyone becomes agreeable or verbally affectionate after being helped.\n\nAllow the recipient's actual inward state to include direct enjoyment. A person can accept the accommodation because it is easier, feel relieved without listing the reasons, or notice the other person's competence and remain silent. Dostoevskian elements need not produce a confession: a long-held question about deservingness, independence, or what is owed may still occupy the character's mind, but this scene can let it persist beside the comfort rather than requiring the comfort to untangle it. Let the thought reach whatever extent the character actually needs, neither abbreviated artificially nor expanded into a philosophical dispute merely because the style is present.\n\nThe care may return the scene to ordinary life. After receiving the help, someone may become able to work, eat, sleep, or stay in the same room with less guardedness, and the next moments can follow that small change. If a larger unresolved matter remains, let it remain without introducing a painful reminder, sudden embarrassment, or a moral lesson as the price of enjoying the care. The ending should rest in the warmth or usefulness of the present moment, not in announced emotional repair or a transformed self-image.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with an actual present exchange or act: a particular request, refusal, agreement, or small choice that belongs to the current characters. Establish their speech and immediate conduct without making every utterance ornate or patterned after the narration. Then widen deliberately. Let the narrator provide social context only through the selection actually permitted to the active point of view: the history of the house or workplace, the practical conditions that make the request costly, a public expectation about family or duty, or the recent occasion that changed what someone could ask. Give the widened material enough deliberate sentence movement and formal transition that the reader feels the frame shift away from the immediate scene and back toward it.\n\nKeep the person's claim distinct from the narrator's frame. What a character says their act means—obedience, generosity, fairness, shame, necessity—retains attribution to that person rather than becoming established merely because it was stated. The active narrator can confirm the account where established facts, known motives or supported judgment actually permit it. If the character's inward experience is authorized, let it supply another level: the person may privately understand their public position as false or incomplete, or they may be fully sincere and informed, or lack a material fact available elsewhere in the narration. Other characters retain their actual prior knowledge and permitted access, and can learn through their own sources. Composed context does not itself transmit a fact merely because the reader encounters it.\n\nUse ceremony, correspondence, and public/private contrast when the actual situation supports them. A formal occasion may give a person words they cannot otherwise produce; a letter may preserve a version they later face in person; a private room may show the cost of a public promise. Each level should let the practical relationship continue rather than turning the scene into a trial of stated beliefs. Return to the current people with their actual state clear, whether changed or continuing: an answer still open, a task now possible, a refusal better understood, or an obligation accepted under narrower terms. Do not require final moral judgment or a symmetry between the public history and the private exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside a present occasion where someone is telling another person or group about a past incident that matters to the current relationship. Establish who is listening, why the teller has chosen this moment, and what actual interest the listeners have, including ordinary company or enjoyment. Let the teller's purpose be concrete: to justify a decision, to explain a refusal, to secure help, to defend someone, or to make a companion understand what the teller once did.\n\nRecount the embedded incident through specific acts and material circumstances. Show what the earlier participants did, asked, accepted or withheld through the teller's actual sources. The teller need not be the earlier participant or the active narrator; preserve what each earlier actor could actually know. If the teller now understands the event differently, identify its actual later source and let it appear as a separate layer: a qualification, a correction, or a moment of hesitation in the present telling. Do not retroactively insert the later understanding as the earlier motive.\n\nGive the teller's moral interpretation room to develop, but do not make it the only thing the listeners receive. A listener may accept the account, question a practical detail, remain unconvinced, or respond to a different concern entirely. Agreement is not required, and interruption is not a compulsory beat. Keep the present relationship available during or after the embedded account. The telling may change what someone asks or understands, or simply continue without such a result; the active boundary may fall within an unfinished account, without confession, lesson or completed resolution.\n\nUse the frame structure to make the narrative levels legible: when the teller speaks, keep the embedded incident clearly attributed; when the outer scene resumes, let the listeners' responses and the teller's present manner carry the emotional weight. Preserve actual private experience in the selected nonspoken channel, distinct from both performed telling and listening responses. Repeated phrases or objects may recur between the embedded account and the outer occasion, but they should connect the levels rather than certify that the teller's version is true.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the present scene as the current occasion of a responsibility or role that the focal character has acquired through particular earlier experiences. Establish what the person is now being asked to do, accept, or decide, and let the narration move between the present encounter and the specific earlier occasions when this responsibility was learned, imposed, or taken up. Show the acquisition concretely: a parent's instruction, a workplace demand, a failure, a promise, or a period of self-directed effort.\n\nKeep the comparison between earlier and present handling exact. When the character acts or speaks now, let the narration register how this resembles or departs from what the character did before. The character may defend the old way, qualify it, apply it with difficulty, or discover that the present situation does not fit the earlier lesson. Do not require the character to grow, repent, or revise the view. The change may be partial, uncertain, or absent.\n\nLet the character's moral understanding develop as a personal problem rather than a thesis. The narration may follow the character's reasoning as it defends, accuses, or entangles itself, but it should remain tied to the present practical situation and the other people involved. Other participants are not assigned as teachers by the form; they may have their own independent concerns, and their responses need not confirm the character's self-account.\n\nPreserve the developmental frame without making every exchange a lesson. The present occasion can be ordinary: a favor, a refusal, a shared task, a request for help. Let the weight come from the accumulated history of the role and the character's present effort to act within it, not from a forced crisis or a completed moral verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere an established authority, dependency, or old permission matters to the scene, make its actual terms concrete. Show what the arrangement permits in practice: who can enter, what must be asked, what debt or obligation remains, what material condition makes the old authority present now. Let the atmosphere of the encounter—the room, the worn object, the formal gesture, the unevenness of need—carry the weight of that arrangement without becoming a substitute for its actual function.\n\nDistinguish three things clearly in the narration: what a character is able to do under the arrangement, what the character agrees to do, and what the character privately judges of it. A person may accept useful help without corruption, guilt, or rebellion. A person may comply outwardly while privately questioning the arrangement, or may agree sincerely while another person reads the agreement as submission. Keep these layers separate so that atmospheric intensity does not establish motive or add powers.\n\nLet the inherited obligation become a personal moral problem through the present encounter. The focal character may defend the arrangement, resent it, depend on it, or try to use it for a particular person's benefit. Let the reasoning develop through the actual exchange: what is owed, what can be refused, what the other person's need makes possible. Do not force a crisis of loyalty or a rejection of the old authority; the character may remain inside the arrangement and still experience its cost.\n\nUse the Gothic-Punk treatment to make the collision of old authority and present life felt in the scene's texture: present practical concerns meeting inherited forms, with any actual private desire, doubt or obligation alongside public conduct. A trusted moment may allow candor without requiring a concealed self or shame. But keep the world's established facts stable. Let atmosphere develop the actual personal relation to authority; it does not add political conflict, supernatural rules or an omnipotent institution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nEnter the focal character's inward consideration through a concrete occasion: an actual request, a remembered exchange, a bodily sensation, or a practical difficulty that has just arisen. Let the occasion open a question about what the character values, owes, or believes, and follow the movement of that consideration as it develops. The character may defend a conviction, meet a plain inward objection, feel direct affection pull against a principle, or find two legitimate concerns in conflict.\n\nKeep the private movement developed at the length the character's reasoning needs. Do not compress it into a single inference or a performed fragment. A defense, accusation, reversal or entanglement can arise where it belongs to this person, while a settled conviction, plain reason or direct feeling remains available. Keep the actual occasion and people recognizable without imposing a sequence of moral voices. A plain inward objection can unsettle an elaborate self-justification; a simple memory can complicate a moral claim.\n\nDo not require the inward passage to produce a finished argument, a reversal, a spiritual insight, or a visible decision. The character may end the passage still uncertain, still holding the original conviction, or changed only in a small way. The significance lies in the lived movement among actual reasons, not in a conclusion the form demands.\n\nLet the outer world remain present, with interruption or uninterrupted consideration following actual events and attention. Other people may speak, act, or continue their practical lives around the character, and the narration may return to them briefly. But the primary method is the sustained private consideration, and the scene need not resolve it through an external exchange. If the character acts afterward, preserve the actual reasons and circumstances, including habit, an external request or a choice informed by thought; inward development need not cause a new act or lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene in event order, keeping what is said and done clear as the focal character interprets a request, favor, refusal, or ordinary exchange. Keep who is present and what has actually been asked or offered recoverable while rendering private and public events in their actual order; interpretation can precede speech or action. Let the narration move between the visible exchange and the focal character's private reading of it, so the reader can distinguish what happened from what the character makes of it.\n\nLet the focal character's self-image become a live problem in the encounter. The character may read a request as a test, a favor as an obligation, a refusal as a judgment, or an ordinary remark as a confirmation of something feared. Develop the private reasoning at the length it needs, but keep it anchored to the present exchange and the other person's actual words and actions. Ground an interpretation in actual context, sources and permitted access; do not invent an insult or hidden meaning merely to complicate the scene. A supported insight need not have been explicitly supplied by the other person.\n\nAllow the other person's actual response to support, qualify, or leave the focal interpretation alone. The other participant has independent concerns and need not confirm the focal character's self-account. A response may be more generous, more indifferent, or more practical than the focal character expected, and the narration should register that difference without forcing a reconciliation or a completed understanding.\n\nPreserve direct private experience as the focal character's own. The narration may reveal what the character feels and reasons, while other characters retain what their actual sources and permitted access allow, rather than learning the thought merely from reader exposure. Keep the psychological realism in the sequence of the present encounter: the interpretation develops as the exchange proceeds, and a later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nOrganize the passage around a shared personal event: an established act, request, material contribution, or moment of care or refusal that more than one permitted mind or attributed account can address. Move among these accounts in a deliberate nonlinear order, placing them beside one another so that each voice's concerns, justifications, and self-understanding become visible. Mark time and source clearly at each shift so the reader can distinguish the actual event from each account's interpretation of it.\n\nLet each voice develop its actual concerns, with moral reasoning where it belongs and ordinary practical attention where it does not. One person may defend the act, another may feel it as an obligation, a third may be concerned with something practical that the others do not notice. Convictions can agree, differ, or remain irrelevant to another speaker's concern. Do not force a contradiction between accounts, and do not appoint a final correct voice. The juxtaposition itself carries the meaning, not a verdict the narration supplies.\n\nKeep the actual event stable across the accounts. A later account may add information or reinterpret an earlier moment, but it cannot change what the established facts were. If a character now understands the event differently, let that later knowledge appear as a later layer in that character's account, not as a retroactive alteration of the shared event.\n\nUse the nonlinear placement to reveal how each person's self-image and material conditions shape what they emphasize, omit, or defend. A plain speaker may unsettle an elaborate justification; a practical concern may expose the cost that a moral account leaves out. No voice gains truthfulness merely through its placement or plainness; preserve any facts and reliability actually established by the sources. The scene's drama lies in the coexistence of these owned accounts, not in a forced convergence or a collective mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nOrient the scene immediately: who is present, where they are, and what has actually been asked, offered, or left unresolved. Keep the practical situation legible while private experience unfolds in its actual order, including before speech or action. If an unfamiliar name, relationship, or arrangement matters, present it through actual observation or discussion as well as use, using whatever explanation the actual account needs rather than requiring a demonstration or an immediately dependent action.\n\nBreak paragraphs when speech, action, or focal response needs separation. Let dialogue sit in clear attribution, let physical action or spatial change have its own readable unit, and let the focal character's private reasoning open into a longer passage when the character's thought needs it. Do not force a fixed cycle of action, dialogue, and reaction. A concise reply may follow a long private development, or a long private development may follow a single spoken line.\n\nLet the focal character's self-image and convictions become personal problems inside the exchange. The character may defend a decision, accuse themselves, reverse a judgment, or entangle two legitimate concerns. Keep the reasoning tied to the present situation and the other person's actual words, not a philosophical debate detached from the scene. A plain speaker may unsettle an elaborate self-justification; a practical detail may expose what a moral account leaves out.\n\nDo not require an emotional payoff or a completed decision at the end of the passage. The exchange may remain unresolved, the character may still be uncertain, or the practical situation may continue into the next scene. The readability comes from clear orientation and clean transitions, not from forcing a confession, a reversal, or a lesson into every installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a moral or emotional phrase belonging to the active account develop through syntax beside concrete sensation. A conviction can be stated, qualified or held beside a plain acknowledgment; the movement follows the person's actual reasons rather than a fixed sequence of justification and reversal. Return to a word or image where attention or the narration warrants it, allowing its resonance to persist as well as change.\n\nGive the sound a source in the scene or the permitted narrator's associations. A remembered phrase can meet the handling of an object, the sound of a reply or an actual bodily sensation without making the narrator an embodied participant by default. The comparison can sustain feeling or thought without changing understanding. A developed cadence does not certify a moral claim, and ordinary speakers retain their own plain or expansive language.\n\nPreserve actual private reasoning, affection, uncertainty and sensation at their needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel. The rhythm can hold a conviction and another legitimate concern together without requiring accusation, lament or a rhetorical winner. Let the active boundary fall within continuing thought, an unanswered exchange or ordinary conduct. No final poetic turn, newly bearable memory or resolved moral verdict is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nPlace a remembered act, phrase, or responsibility within the present figure's attention as something still capable of affecting ordinary practical life. Allow the reflection to move between what the person knew at the earlier time and what they know now by marking that difference clearly in the narration: 'she had understood then' or 'he could not yet see' are ways to keep the two times distinct without making later knowledge retroactively explain the earlier decision.\n\nLet the remembered detail remain individual and material: the cracked handle on the cup, the specific wording of a refusal, or the sound of someone putting down a tool. Return to it because it now means something, not because the present scene requires a moral monument. The style is willing to dwell in an unresolved tie sustained by affection, pride, shame, or loyalty. An old act may continue to compel respect even when the character no longer wants the same thing.\n\nDo not push every remembered occasion into a lesson or a confession. A pleasure can be remembered simply as pleasure, even when the person is no longer inside the circumstances that made it possible. The passage may close with the recollection changing in significance without undoing what actually happened, leaving the present conversation or task open rather than forcing a final resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route. Locate a later composition recounting an earlier personal encounter, keeping the memoirist, earlier participant and active narrator distinct where the selected contract does. The earlier participant may have understood accurately, incompletely or not at all according to the actual situation. Reconstruct the earlier scene through the acts and statements that were actually available then: what was asked, refused, accused, promised, or left unoffered. The narrator may report the earlier participant's reasoning as that participant actually experienced it, even when that reasoning later becomes difficult to accept.\n\nWhen later knowledge qualifies the memory, make the turn explicit. 'I learned afterward' or 'the letter came months later' can introduce what the present memoirist now understands, but that later knowledge must not contaminate the earlier sentence or make the past self predictive. Let the later account examine or retain the earlier self-understanding through the material record: the room, the actual reasons and the practical need that shaped what could be said. A former conviction can remain sound without a required self-accusation. Preserve actual private experience in its selected nonspoken channel beyond anything the composed account records.\n\nDo not require confession, exile, self-condemnation, or redemption. The memoirist may retain the former decision as necessary, or may find no resolution, or may remember helping someone without drama. The present account is a composition with a purpose; the past scene keeps its own separate quality. The active boundary can remain inside remembered events, later reflection or current composition. A return to the memoirist's present relation is available without a mandatory concluding judgment or moral ceremony.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nUse exact words and acts to make the personal exchange intelligible. A direct clause can state what happened; another can preserve what the person says or privately understands about it. Distinguish the act from its claimed meaning without requiring a discrepancy. A request can be sincere, an answer can be plain, and a material particular can locate why the occasion matters to this person.\n\nOmit redundant explanation while retaining actual private experience. A belief, fear, intention or memory can develop at its needed extent even when conduct already suggests it or the person has named it before. Repeated inward wording can sustain concern or self-argument; it is not disposable merely because it supplies no new inference. Keep the active narrator's access and the selected Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation intact.\n\nA plain reason or actual objection can sit beside an elaborate inward case without making either win, inventing opposition or forcing thought into short fragments. Defending, accusing or reversing a self-image are possibilities of this particular person, not a required cycle. Direct feeling and an unchanged conviction remain available. End at the active boundary with the actual situation recoverable, including continued thought or an unfinished exchange without a new decision, changed conduct or final moral tag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as a continuous present in which a particular conversation, errand, or household task keeps its ordinary rhythm while a person's reasons work underneath it. Move naturally among an object, memory, private reason, direct feeling and response as the actual experience calls for them, without a fixed order or required self-questioning. Let the prose indicate whose mind is being entered at each moment, while other characters actually present continue from their own lives and concerns: they answer a question, turn away to a task, repeat a misunderstanding, or bring in a separate worry that does not arise from the focal person's conscience.\n\nMake the Dostoevsky pressure a function of the particular character's self-image. A person who believes they have been patient may notice their hands moving too quickly; someone who has decided to ask for help may defend the decision to themselves before saying one plain sentence. The inner voice should arise in relation to actual material conditions and relationships, not become a universal argument about dignity separated from the scene. It may be unjust to the other person, or too generous to themselves, without being corrected immediately.\n\nDo not require a challenger, a disclosure, or a return from thought to action at a preset moment. The exchange may remain incomplete; the other character may respond only to the practical surface; the focal person may keep the larger concern until later. Close at the active boundary, including within ordinary business or ongoing thought without requiring a pause, resumption or new event. The relationship and self-account can remain open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose from the supplied relationship premise a supported exchange or remembered moment that releases its particular significance. The bond can become legible through details actually available in the shared occasion, without requiring everyone to recognize them: the kind of care they anticipate, the wording they remember having used before, the habit they privately interpret as affection or as strain, or the practical help that means more than the occasion appears to contain. Develop the scene from inside the established relation rather than using a generic confession or reconciliation formula.\n\nLet each authorized private account belong to its actual person. One character may experience the same act as generosity while the other experiences it as an unspoken obligation; render each account the active contract authorizes as real and particular, without opening an excluded mind for symmetry or making one a moral correction of the other. A plain statement of affection may be enough. Ambivalence, pride, or uncertainty may coexist with love without requiring jealousy, humiliation, or an invented past debt. Let the valued bond meet the person's actual convictions and self-estimate. These may change, remain sound or coexist with another private concern; love does not automatically erase a scruple or create one.\n\nUse callbacks as consequential memory, not decoration: a repeated phrase, object or gesture can sustain an established meaning or acquire a supported new one without requiring changed understanding. Preserve supporting people and first-time meaningful acts without making every sweet moment a plot point. End at the active boundary with the bond's actual position available, including unchanged feeling or a pending exchange; possible developments include: a request now easier or harder to make, a small shared plan beginning, or one person's changed understanding not yet spoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nBegin the passage as if the present action already has a remembered shape: a compact declaration that establishes who asked, who refused, who kept faith, or who made a small repair. Let the declaration be sourced in an actual deed or reputation, then move closer to the concrete exchange so the reader sees the particular person and the ordinary conditions beneath the public statement. The cadence should give a feeling of retelling without turning household work into martial heroism.\n\nUse recurrence deliberately. A phrase such as 'he said he would come' or 'the debt was settled' may return in a later part of the account and acquire a different weight because the original act has now produced consequences. Keep the public memory and the actor's private reason distinct: the community may remember the help as reliable, while the actor privately remembers reluctance, shame, affection, or something they never explained. Do not require the private feeling to debunk the public meaning or to confirm it as a lesson.\n\nThe Dostoevsky element enters through the person's relation to the remembered meaning: a speaker may defend, question, or no longer recognize the version of themselves the account preserves. Where an ordinary conversation actually enters, it can interrupt or coexist with the elevated cadence as a living response rather than compulsory mockery; no added exchange is required. The passage may close with the deed unfinished or the memory still contested; it need not announce what will ultimately be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration as a situated inquiry rather than a neutral treatise. Let the narrator state a moral claim that arises from the present relationship or material conditions: a person may declare what they owe someone, what they cannot forgive, what they believe about their own need, or what they insist another person should understand. Keep the claim in the narrator's own terms and make its source visible in the scene.\n\nThen examine a concrete case through the actual people involved. The case may be an established event in the current story, a remembered occasion, or an explicitly hypothetical situation one person proposes to test the claim. Show the circumstances that make the case particular: who asked, who answered, what practical task or obligation surrounded them, and what each person stood to lose or preserve. Do not turn the case into a detached illustration; let any actual stake in the claim shape attention without inventing an autobiographical role or interest for the active narrator.\n\nReturn to the original terms and state what the case has done to them. The claim may be confirmed, qualified, or left provisional. A person can keep the same words while their confidence in them changes, or revise the claim because a particular relationship no longer fits it. Let several convictions bear on the same case without requiring an opponent or a refutation. The return should not force a completed moral verdict, a confession, or a decisive action; the narrator may end with the claim still under pressure and the relationship still open.\n\nPreserve actual private experience beyond what the formulation selects, without treating the stated claim as the character's whole mind or imposing a length ratio. The narrator may think, feel, or remember more than the claim can hold, and another character's unstated reasons remain their own. A plain speaker can interrupt an elaborate argument with a practical reply. Render its actual reception, which may address, interrupt or leave the argument unchanged without making the other person a mouthpiece for it. Preserve the active narrator's access and the selected presentation's rules for nonspoken interiority. The construction can remain unfinished across replies; actual circumstances determine whether action precedes, accompanies or follows the examination.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite the personal exchange in readable action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the action lines establish what is visible and audible: who enters, what object they handle, where they stand, and what practical task continues around the conversation. Keep the physical business specific enough that a stated conviction meets actual conduct rather than floating above the scene.\n\nLet a plain answer or material act respond to a developed spoken reason. A character may explain what they believe they owe, what they fear about themselves, or what they want another person to accept, and the other may answer with a short refusal, a practical question, or an action that changes the immediate situation. Do not make gesture proof of motive. A character can turn away, set down a cup, or continue working without the screenplay certifying what that movement means. The reader may infer, but the inference must come from supported details and the active narrator's access, not from a stage direction that labels the feeling.\n\nPreserve full private experience in the selected nonspoken channel. Use the active presentation's clearly attributed nonspoken note for the focal character's actual thought, reasoning, or feeling, and keep it separate from authorized voice-over. Voice-over is performed speech with its own audience and purpose; it does not replace the private note. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Conversely, do not conceal an authorized focal conclusion until the rest of the cast catches up.\n\nDo not require a confession scene, a completed decision, or a fixed emotional outcome. The scene may end after a request remains unanswered, a task continues, or one person leaves with the spoken reason still in the air. Use cuts between occasions only when the selected sequence needs them, and keep each cut's source and destination clear.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nKeep the shared stage space coherent. Mark entrances and exits, establish where each person is in relation to the others, and give objects or blocking that can actually be staged. A request, explanation, or reply should sit beside what the participants do: one person may speak while folding a cloth, checking a door, or moving a chair, and the physical business should remain playable rather than decorative.\n\nLet an actual spoken reason meet a practical reply without requiring speechifying or a contradictory gesture. A character can state a conviction about what they owe, what they have become, or what another person should understand, and the other can answer with a short line, a question about the immediate task, or silence while continuing to work. Do not use blocking to certify hidden guilt or sincerity. A pause, a turned back, or a held object may mean several things, and the audience should not be told which one is true unless the active presentation authorizes that knowledge.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when they have a dramatic purpose in the performed scene. They are spoken or performed material with an audience, not the default destination for private thought. Preserve required nonspoken interiority in the active presentation's clearly attributed nonspoken channel, keeping it separate from anything another character can hear. A character's actual private argument may be more developed or more uncertain than their lines, and that difference belongs to them rather than to the audience's inference.\n\nAllow pending responses and continued work at the active boundary. The scene may stop after a request remains open, a task resumes, or one person exits without a completed decision. Do not force a confession, a reversal, or a moral resolution merely because the exchange has reached a pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the actual associations of the authorized mind set the order of the passage. Begin with a perception, a bodily sensation, a remembered phrase, or an ordinary task, and let it lead to the next item through the character's own connection: a smell recalls a room, a repeated word recalls an accusation, a physical discomfort recalls an obligation. Do not impose a debate structure on every thought. A remembered accusation, a defended reason, a direct affection, or a practical phrase may enter only as it belongs to this particular person at this moment.\n\nKeep the interpersonal event recoverable. Even when the mind branches away from the present, anchor enough orientation that the reader can distinguish what is happening now from what is remembered, imagined, or feared. A line of dialogue may interrupt a private association, or a private association may interrupt attention to the dialogue. When attention returns, make the actual state recoverable, including an unchanged room or continuing exchange. Actual attention and events determine elapsed time; the length of rendered thought does not itself advance the clock.\n\nPreserve developed thought without compulsory fragmentation. A character may pursue a sustained self-argument for several sentences, then break off at a sound or a physical need. Let the syntax and paragraph shape follow that movement: longer clauses for a reasoning stretch, shorter fragments for interruption or sensation. Do not make every thought a reversal, a guilt confession, or a sudden insight. The mind can circle a concern, set it down, and return to it later without resolving it.\n\nKeep access and knowledge separate. Only the authorized mind's private experience appears in this associative movement. Another character's thoughts remain outside the narration unless the active contract grants access. Preserve the selected presentation's rules for visible or hidden interiority, and do not let the stream technique authorize minds that the active authorship contract excludes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages to carry the personal exchange. Each message should read as a selection made by its sender for this channel and this recipient: a request, an explanation, a refusal, an ordinary reply, or a developed written argument. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution. One may write long paragraphs, another may answer with a single word, and a third may revise a message before sending or leave a draft unsent.\n\nInclude only the channel events that matter. Timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes may appear when they affect what the participants know or how the exchange proceeds. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext. A delay may mean hesitation, interruption, or ordinary life, and the transcript should not certify which one unless the active presentation authorizes that knowledge.\n\nKeep sent content distinct from unsent drafts and private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact with its own wording, not automatically the sender's live private experience. If the active presentation requires nonspoken interiority, place it in the selected clearly attributed channel separate from the transcript. A sender's actual reason for writing may be more developed, more uncertain, or more affectionate than the message they send, and that difference belongs to them rather than to the recipient's inference.\n\nPreserve each participant's actual knowledge. Failure of delivery does not communicate a message to its intended recipient. Preserve any knowledge they already have or gain through another actual source, while keeping those routes distinct from delivery. A message that was delivered may be read, ignored, misunderstood, or answered in a way that addresses only part of it. Let a developed written argument receive a plain answer without compulsory evasion or a debate. Do not require a delay, a changed relation, or a completed decision at the end of the exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and owned inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Establish the location and the people present in a few lines, then let the exchange proceed through clear speech and the focal character's private reaction. Do not impose a fixed sequence of cue, line, and response; let the scene's actual needs set the order.\n\nPut a stated reason or practical reply beside a contextual cue. A character may explain what they believe they owe, what they fear about themselves, or what they want another person to accept, and the cue may show the room, an object, or a change in expression. Keep the cue purposeful: it should orient the moment or give the focal character something to notice, not function as an automatic truth indicator. A changed expression can attract attention, but an inference about what it means must follow actual support and the active narrator's access.\n\nPreserve full private extent in the owned inner response. The focal character may think more than they say, reason through a self-accusation, or feel an affection they do not express. Keep that private experience separate from other characters' knowledge. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Conversely, do not conceal an authorized focal conclusion until the rest of the cast catches up.\n\nLet plain speakers remain plain. A practical reply can meet an elaborate stated reason without the cast becoming uniformly eloquent. Do not require portrait-coded guilt, branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. The scene may end after a request remains unanswered, a task continues, or one person leaves without a completed decision or mandatory realization.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nRecover only the context needed to follow the current exchange. If the scene turns on an earlier request, bring back the actual wording or the material arrangement that made it matter. If a person has already learned something, let that knowledge shape what they say or withhold now. Do not retell the investigation or relationship history that first established the fact; include the particular that the present moment needs.\n\nKeep past motives and events stable. A later understanding may confirm or qualify an earlier one, but it cannot replace the earlier event or make a character deliberately forget a supported conclusion. If a person once asked for help, that request remains part of the history even if they now describe it differently. If a promise was made under a specific condition, the condition remains relevant when the promise is recalled.\n\nLet private consideration, ordinary care, and continued conversation sustain a reply without a fresh accusation, confession, or completed decision. A character may spend the installment thinking through what an earlier exchange meant, performing a practical task that keeps a relationship alive, or returning to a question that remains open. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a moral resolution perpetually recede.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's access and the selected presentation's rules for nonspoken interiority. A later realization cannot be retroactively inserted as an earlier motive. Keep the through-line readable for reentry without turning every installment into a complete miniature chapter or an artificial cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the present request, admission, apology, or refusal as an action with a cost the character can feel before, during, or after speaking. Develop that cost through actual private experience and any effects on conduct, which may include hesitation, rephrasing or restlessness without requiring an outward display. Keep the other person's response capable of understanding, partial help, ordinary continuation, or refusal; strain does not require a quarrel.\n\nWhen a remembered phrase, family account, or public story enters the scene, anchor it to its actual source and to why it matters now. A sentence may gather years of repetition while staying attached to a human consequence: what someone once said, who now recalls it, and what the speaker hopes or fears the words will do in this exchange. Do not let the remembered version prove another person's motive or require the present relationship to repeat the past.\n\nDistinguish what a character says from their actual private experience, allowing the two to align or differ. Dialogue may be abrupt, careful, or indirect; private experience can contain the larger need the words avoid. If comfort occurs, let its actual quality and effect follow the people involved: it may be awkward, partial, ordinary or deeply helpful without requiring emotional repair. End the scene where the existing role and action boundary permit, whether the relationship's immediate condition is clear, unchanged or still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nContinue the submitted shared interval in coherent paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution and a clear sense of who is present, where the exchange occurs, and what practical activity surrounds it. Resolve the existing material under the active narrator, person, and ownership contract rather than introducing a separate storytelling frame.\n\nWhen a name, object, repeated phrase, or continuing private thought calls up memory, let it enter through the participant whose experience it belongs to. Keep the remembered voice distinct from present speech and let each person respond only to what they can actually know. A recollection may deepen the present exchange, be interrupted, or remain private without requiring the conversation to resolve it.\n\nAdvance the scene through present decisions: a person asks, withholds, accepts, misunderstands, or chooses to continue as before. The interval may end at a natural pause without a new anecdote, revelation, or closing question; leave ongoing work, NPC behavior, and the relationship's next step available for continuation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling. Open an occasion with a compact declaration that situates the visit, request, refusal, reunion, or shared errand in particular lives. Then develop the actual contact: what is said, who waits, what practical task accompanies it, and how the participants understand their actual obligations, including shared or compatible ones. Keep the telling direct and grounded in the character's community and present material circumstances rather than framing every event as a symbolic turn.\n\nLet a recurring name, phrase, or object connect episodes where it belongs. When such a repetition appears, mark whose account or habit it comes from, and let its meaning gather through successive occasions without requiring each recurrence to reveal hypocrisy or complete a decision. A family saying may mean different things to different hearers.\n\nContinuity can rest on an unchanged arrangement: a debt still unpaid, a visit still expected, a house visited again, a promise remembered unevenly. An episode may end with the next practical step clear rather than with a new moral or a completed stage of feeling. Preserve the active narrator and world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse strong verbs, direct clauses, and concrete physical contact to locate a request, boundary, or admission in present activity. Show what a person handles, sets down, avoids, or relies on: a worn tool, a held door, a crowded table, a hand given or withdrawn. Compress redundant transitional explanation while preserving the actual reasoning, feeling or association between noticing and acting, whether brief or developed.\n\nWhen a familiar object, phrase, or place opens the past, let the sentence gather that history around a particular person. The recollection can stretch the prose while remaining grounded in the thing that prompted it; memory need not soften the present or alter the next act. Keep the remembered voice separate from present speech and the source of the recollection clear.\n\nAbrasive or rough language belongs to its owner, not the whole cast. Other speakers may be plain, careful, or quiet. Let changes of rhythm serve the exchange: a short refusal can follow a longer account, or a cramped practical task can interrupt feeling. Preserve the active narrator, interiority permissions, and the scene's actual limits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the relationship through the document form the actual scene supports: a letter, note, entry, receipt, will, notice, or other record. Make obvious who composed it, for whom, and when. Let the document contain only what its author would actually record in that situation, including what they omit, soften, repeat, or misunderstand.\n\nLet repeated wording link records across time by appearing in different writers' hands or through someone quoting an old phrase. Distinguish the original record from a copy, a later annotation, or a recollection of what it said. A recurring name or phrase may confirm a habit, alter its meaning through use, or remain unexamined without uncovering a secret.\n\nTreat delivery, reading, and response as separate events: a letter may arrive late, be read aloud, be answered differently than intended, or remain unopened. Required actual private experience remains in its selected Hidden or Visible nonspoken presentation, independently of the document's composed text. Keep the sequence readable, and follow what each record actually communicates about a person's request, offer, refusal or understanding, allowing a continuing or pending matter without a new development.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive the scene a particular act of kindness or shared pleasure: food remembered from home, a task taken over before it is asked, a place arranged for rest, an ordinary joke repeated at the right moment, or another small care that belongs to these people. Show the act in its practical detail so the warmth is an event rather than an atmosphere.\n\nWhen a remembered preference, phrase, or custom connects the care to earlier occasions, name whose habit or memory it is. The familiar thing may be welcomed, altered, or declined; another person can enjoy the gesture without understanding its history. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or cancel a difficulty already present in the relationship.\n\nLet ordinary time continue during or after an offer of care, with the active scene boundary free to fall while the offer or its reception remains unfinished. The recipient may speak, return to work, sit quietly, or make a different choice. Personality and privacy remain intact. No confession, inclusion, or emotional repair is required, and the interval may end with the next practical moment still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nShape the reply in sustained paragraphs with deliberate transitions between the present exchange and the family, social, or public context that surrounds it. Allow the narrative enough distance to frame an obligation or choice through history and judgment where the chosen narrator permits, while keeping spoken language in each person's actual register and the present situation concrete.\n\nGather contextual detail around a specific human exchange: a request, refusal, promise, misunderstanding, or decision to change. Move between times and accounts with clear orientation, but do not force a fixed scene-history-return pattern. A paragraph may begin with a present gesture, move through what the household remembers about that gesture, and return to the next practical word without announcing each shift.\n\nKeep every source distinguishable: what happened earlier, what someone said later, what the public account claims, and what the narrator now judges. A contextual judgment may confirm established understanding, remain limited to one person's view, or stay private without changing visible conduct. Preserve the active narrator, interiority permissions, and world facts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nBegin with a definite telling situation: who is present, why this account is being offered now, and what practical or emotional need brings it into the room. The teller may be passing time, preparing someone for a request, explaining a refusal, or simply keeping company. Establish the listeners as particular people with their own immediate concerns, not as a passive audience awaiting a lesson.\n\nMove between narrated past and present performance by marking the teller's selection and emphasis. Let the teller choose what to include, what to compress, and what to repeat. A remembered phrase, a name, or a detail from the past may return in the telling because it matters to the teller now, not because it proves a point. Distinguish what the teller knew at the time from what the teller has learned since, and let that difference remain visible in the account.\n\nGive the listeners private reception under the active contract. A listener may attend closely, drift toward a personal worry, compare the tale with another version, or offer no response at all. Their silence or distraction is part of the scene, not a failure of the telling. The boundary of the scene may fall naturally at a pause, a shift in attention, or an unfinished sentence; do not require persuasion, interruption, or a completed story to end the interval.\n\nWhen the telling pauses, follow the outer scene's actual activity and private attention without requiring a new question or outward change: someone may ask for something, withhold an answer, accept a small change, or simply resume an ordinary task. The tale and the present exchange may illuminate each other without one explaining the other. Keep recurring language and layered time anchored in human consequence, and do not import a royal court, magical bargain, or compulsory nested tale.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose. Place an actual available record beside an authorized recollection of a relational occasion. The record may be a letter, a ledger entry, a notice, a photograph caption, or another artifact that exists in the supplied world. Mark its author, date, and intended purpose, and keep its knowledge limited to what that author could have known at that time.\n\nIntroduce the recollection through a permitted mind. The remembered version may agree with the record in its facts while preserving another aspect of experience: the heat in the room, the tone of a voice, the reason someone stayed late, the unease no one wrote down. Let the two sources sit together without forcing them to contradict each other. A shared phrase may appear in both, but the record's official wording and the memory's private weight remain distinct.\n\nMove between record and recollection through deliberate transitions that keep the reader oriented. The narration may quote the record, then return to the present person holding it or remembering the day it was made. The comparison may illuminate a relationship without resolving it: someone may understand a parent's choice more clearly, recognize what a friend omitted, or simply notice that the record leaves out what mattered most.\n\nDo not turn the comparison into an obligatory investigation, a newly discovered secret, or a practical lesson. The record and the memory can remain meaningful without deciding a request or changing a relationship. Let the scene close at a natural point of reflection or resumed practical life, preserving the active narrator and the actual private experience beyond the document.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround inherited authority in the supplied arrangements and lived places of the story. A family name, an old building, a public designation, or a remembered obligation may color a permission, intimacy, or refusal. Show how that authority operates through actual access, expectation, or material consequence: who may enter, who is owed an answer, what must be maintained. Do not grant authority supernatural properties or guaranteed effects merely because of the style; preserve any powers and limits the supplied world establishes.\n\nDistinguish the circulation of a name or reputation from the person's actual desire. A character may be publicly known as an heir, a caretaker, a disappointment, or a keeper of some old arrangement, while privately wanting something else. Render the actual relation between public role and private desire, which may align or differ and may be expressed directly or remain unspoken: a hesitation before accepting an invitation, a task performed with resentment or relief, a private thought that does not match the public role.\n\nRender any supported change of social manner in its real context. A mask may fall away in trusted company, but show what trust consists of here: a particular person, a shared history, a room where the usual audience is absent. The change need not be dramatic. Someone may speak more plainly, accept help, admit fatigue, or stop performing competence for a moment. The atmosphere and memory remain personally significant without unmasking a secret, producing distrust, or making old power mystical.\n\nLet urban or domestic material detail carry the collision of old authority and present life. A worn stair, a locked drawer, a renovated room, a name on a buzzer, or a routine that once belonged to someone else can hold the relationship's history. Keep the focus on particular people deciding what to ask, withhold, accept, or change, and do not import Kindred physiology, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose social and domestic realism. Follow the ordinary distribution of shared responsibilities among particular people: who cooks, who waits, who arranges transport, who answers a letter, who remembers an anniversary, who asks for help, who declines. Let the arrangement be visible through actual tasks and exchanges rather than announced as a theme.\n\nAllow an inherited saying, an earlier occasion, or a remembered habit to accompany present work. A phrase from a parent may surface while someone folds clothes or prepares a meal; an old grievance may return during a routine errand. Render custom as the particular living practice established here; it may be ordinary, sacred, coercive or freely valued according to the actual people and arrangements, without an automatic label. The same habit may feel like care to one person and burden to another, and both responses remain legitimate.\n\nDevelop private responses independently of spoken agreement. Someone may agree to help while privately resenting the assumption, or refuse while privately regretting the refusal. The narration may reveal that difference under the active contract, but another character need not know it. Let existing care work smoothly when it does; do not require a contested duty or a completed negotiation.\n\nKeep language, region, community, and period specific when supplied. Do not force English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene. The story remains about particular people and their actual self-understanding, whether continuing or changing, with remembered language and public events gathered into the present exchange without overwhelming it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose diary and reflective miscellany. Present selected observations or entries with a situated time and owner. Mark when the entry was written, who wrote it, and what that person could have known then. A later annotation or recollection may follow, but keep it distinct from the original record: the writer may now understand something differently, or may simply notice what the entry left out.\n\nLet a phrase or object remain plain noticing or gather personal and public memory. A particular sound, a seasonal detail, a household object, or a remembered remark may appear in the entry without needing to finish or gain a new symbolic meaning. The association may deepen the reader's sense of the relationship without resolving it.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and actual private experience beyond the record. The diary is a composed artifact, not the writer's complete live mind. Under the active contract, the narration may show what the writer felt but did not record, or what the writer recorded while privately thinking something else. Distinguish the entry's chosen wording from the private experience surrounding its composition.\n\nMove between the record and the present scene through clear transitions. The present scene may follow a person rereading an entry, recalling its occasion or setting it aside; preserve the active narrator and person independently of the diary's writer or reader. Let the scene close at a natural pause in reflection or resumed practical life, without requiring a lesson, a completed interpretation, or a uniform quietness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaham::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nChoose polyphonic nonlinear telling. Arrange permitted voices, memories, or records from distinguishable positions and times around an actual relational moment: a departure, a return, a request, a refusal, a shared meal, a public event that touched a household. Each account belongs to a particular person with limited access, not to a collective memory.\n\nLet a shared saying or recurring phrase carry different, aligned, or simply separate significance for each speaker. One person may remember it as a promise, another as a joke, another as something overheard. Keep each speaker's original access intact: what they saw, heard, knew, or guessed at the time. Use attributed testimony when private access is unavailable, so the narration does not claim knowledge it has not been granted.\n\nMove between accounts through deliberate shifts in time and speaker. The sequence need not be chronological, but each shift should be legible: a name, a place, or a repeated phrase may mark the return to an earlier occasion. Let an account remain unfinished. A voice may break off, a memory may stop at the moment that still matters, a record may end without explanation.\n\nPreserve established events. The accounts may differ in emphasis, feeling, or interpretation, but they cannot alter what the supplied world has already established. Let the juxtaposition illuminate the relationship without requiring every return to reveal a contradiction or a hidden truth.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish who is present, where they are, and what exchange is underway. Introduce unfamiliar names or social arrangements through needed explanation or encounter: a brief identification when a name first appears, a concrete detail that shows a relationship's standing, or a short exchange that makes an obligation legible. Avoid redundant genealogical explanation while giving family history or private recollection the development needed for the actual account, including passages that do not change the exchange.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around speech, movement, and private response. A line of dialogue may be followed by a small physical action, then by the focal character's private thought or feeling. Vary the rhythm according to the actual exchange; do not impose a fixed rotation of speech, action, and interiority. Let a meaningful thought take the space it needs without a brevity cap.\n\nAllow a remembered saying or public story to enter with enough context to matter. A name from the past, a phrase a parent used, or a rumor about a family may surface during the present exchange. Keep it attached to its actual source: who said it, when, and what it meant then. The memory may illuminate the present request or refusal without becoming a lesson or a prophecy.\n\nLet humor and emotional reaction follow the actual person. A character may be dry, warm, evasive, or plain-spoken according to their established voice. Keep spatial changes easy to follow, and close at a natural pause in the exchange rather than a forced cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the scene so that a present word, sound, or image gathers connected details across time through the narrator's authorized associations. Let the syntax follow that gathering: a phrase spoken now can open into the earlier occasion when it was first heard, the person who used it differently, or the public story that later attached to it. Keep the literal exchange legible beneath the cadence. Keep legible what the active perspective can establish about present words and actions, even when the sentence carries a remembered voice alongside it.\n\nDistinguish recalled voices from current speech. When a character remembers something someone said, attribute the memory to its source and time rather than letting it blur into the present conversation. A remembered phrase may return with its original speaker's tone, or it may arrive altered by later retellings; either way, the narrator's access to that memory remains distinct from what the other characters now know. Do not let a resonant image make a memory true or grant knowledge of another person's motive. The cadence can suggest connection, but the connection follows the active narrator's authorized knowledge and associations, with personal experience, reports, records and imagined possibilities kept distinguishable.\n\nLet recurrence preserve familiar meaning or remain unfinished. A phrase may return because it still means the same thing to the person who uses it, or it may return precisely because its meaning has become uncertain. Do not force every recurrence to resolve into a new symbolic meaning. Plain statements and individual dialogue registers provide contrast. When a character speaks in an ordinary, unlyrical way, let that speech stand as itself rather than absorbing it into the narrator's figurative register. The lyrical movement belongs to the narration, not to every voice in the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWrite the scene so that a particular established change, attachment, or continuing memory enters current experience without displacing it. Let a familiar phrase, object, or habitual act carry the remembered occasion into the present moment. The present relationship remains the scene's center: what someone asks for, accepts, withholds, or chooses to change now. The remembered material accompanies that present exchange rather than replacing it with a summary of the past.\n\nPreserve the actual clarity or limits of recollection. When the narrator remembers something, preserve its actual clarity and any uncertainty without manufacturing a gap. A person may remember exactly what was said but not the expression that accompanied it, or remember the occasion vividly but not the sequence of events. Do not let reflection fill in what the narrator could not have known. Distinguish the act of private remembering from what has been communicated, allowing their content to coincide or differ. Another character does not automatically share the experience or its content; preserve any knowledge actually gained through shared events, communication or other established access.\n\nLet warmth and pleasure stand. A remembered occasion may be pleasurable even when it is no longer available, and present comfort may coexist with the awareness of what has changed. Do not require a confession, regret, altered decision, or concluding announcement of impermanence. The scene may end with the relationship continuing in its present form, with an ordinary task resumed, or with a question left open. The reflective treatment shapes how the narrator attends to time and attachment; it does not dictate that the relationship must be mourned or repaired.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene through lyric, reflective, and philosophical prose. Choose a word that belongs to the particular relationship and its history—belonging, owing, returning, or another term the characters themselves would use—and let that word gather the person's actual memories, sensations, and questions. The word should emerge from the scene's concrete material: what someone said, what was owed or returned, what the authorized focal person felt when a door opened or a debt was acknowledged. Do not begin with an abstract definition. Let the inward connections develop through sustained syntax and concrete remembered voices.\n\nKeep the meditation anchored in particular people and their actual exchange. When the narrator or an authorized focal person reflects on what belonging has meant, ground the reflection in a specific occasion: the first time the word was spoken, the person who refused it, the room where it became a question. A remembered voice should arrive with its source and time. Distinguish the reflecting person's private understanding from what other characters have said or know, preserving the active narrator and person independently of that focal role. The philosophical movement belongs to the authorized inward experience being represented, not to a collective voice or an omniscient cultural pronouncement.\n\nLet the saying remain personally meaningful whether the person affirms it, questions it or leaves it open; neither a verdict nor a resulting action is required. The narrator may turn the word over without reaching a conclusion. The scene may end with the word still open, with an ordinary practical act continuing alongside the reflection, or with a question that the relationship has not answered. Do not impose a creed, wisdom verdict, or universal cultural value. The tradition supplies the reflective and philosophical route; it does not require the scene to settle a moral argument or to speak for an entire community.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nWrite the scene with a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Choose the particular request, preference, or ordinary act that carries the relationship's history, and let a familiar expression or object do the work of recall without retelling the past redundantly. If a character says the same phrase they said years ago, let present use carry the history where the available context supports inference, retaining any account of the earlier occasion needed to make the connection intelligible. The detail must be exact enough to carry that inference: the specific word, the specific object, the specific gesture that belongs to these people.\n\nKeep attribution clear where a saying is remembered or reported. When the narrator recalls something someone said, identify the source and time directly with enough context for the actual account. Do not let a remembered phrase float as if it were spoken in the present scene. A reported saying from a third party needs its own attribution. The minimalist treatment does not mean omitting the source; it favors direct attribution while retaining whatever development makes a source or its personal significance intelligible.\n\nOmit repeated explanation, not meaningful reasoning or feeling. A character may need a longer thought to make sense of what they are deciding, and that thought should remain intact. Cut restatements and explanatory gloss only where they are redundant; retain recurrence that develops the actual thought, feeling or account even when it returns to the same point. Do not impose counts for objects, thoughts, or recurrences. The style is selective attention, not a quota. Personal significance can remain present without changing the exchange: a preference may be stated, accepted, or refused, and the scene may end without the relationship having been altered by it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience in their causal order. Let the present scene carry the story: what someone asks for, withholds, accepts, misunderstands, or chooses to change now. When a name, phrase, or continuing concern gathers earlier occasions or public stories, mark the source and time as the memory or report enters the narration. The reader should know whether the material comes from an authorized person's direct experience, a family account, public rumor or another supported source, and when it belongs; the active narrator need not be a participant.\n\nDo not require a flashback or a return to the past on every turn. A remembered occasion may enter as a brief association, a single sentence that carries the earlier event into the present exchange, or a longer recollection when the person's actual private experience calls for development, even without explaining or changing a decision. The movement should follow the scene's actual pressure: a character may think of the past because the present conversation makes it relevant, not because the narration has a quota of memory to fill. Keep others' reasons and knowledge distinct. The narrator may understand something privately that another character does not know, and another character's motive may remain unavailable.\n\nLet a preference, acceptance, or unexpressed feeling sustain the scene without an obstacle plot or relationship lesson. A character may agree to something without the agreement resolving the relationship. A character may feel something and choose not to say it, and that unexpressed feeling can remain part of the scene's private texture rather than becoming a secret that must be revealed. The scene may end with an ordinary task resumed, a conversation concluded, or a decision made without announcing what it means for everyone involved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect moments that fulfill the established relationship premise through present choices, private language, and the history of ordinary acts. The scene should make the premise legible without announcing it: what these particular people do for each other, say to each other, or refrain from saying reveals the relationship's terms. Use the supplied premise as the selection principle. If the relationship is built on a specific kind of care, rivalry, dependency, or discovery, choose the present exchange that shows that particular promise in operation rather than importing a roster of familiar tropes.\n\nLet a shared phrase or repeated occasion carry the relationship's history. The phrase may be used and understood identically by both people, or it may mean something different to each of them. Its emotional force does not establish intent. One character may say the phrase with full knowledge of its history while the other uses it casually, or both may feel its weight without either being certain what the other feels. Keep inherited accounts and private memories with their owners. What a family account says may align with, differ from or address another aspect of either participant's memory, and what one participant remembers is not automatically known to the other.\n\nAllow the relationship to continue unchanged or to develop through actual events. The scene may show a moment of comfort, friction, or ordinary cooperation that leaves the relationship exactly where it was. It may also show a small change: an acceptance, a refusal, a new piece of knowledge, or a shift in what one person is willing to ask. Do not prescribe jealousy, confession, or increasing intimacy. The relationship's promise is fulfilled by the particularity of the scene, not by a required emotional escalation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nWrite the scene as an account of a particular relational act that has acquired or is acquiring a retold form: a visit, promise, refusal, or act of care. Open with a deliberate statement that places the act in time and names the people involved, then expand into the human circumstances and sources behind that reputation. Where circulating language connects the act to a family account or public occasion, show who carries that formulation into the present telling and what it means to that person. The retold form should not invent a communal verdict. Identify actual witnesses, reports and later additions where they exist, without inventing a chain of tellers for every act. A reputation may be accurate, partial, or contested, but the narration should distinguish the act itself from the accounts that circulate about it.\n\nUse compact declarations and grounded recurrence. A recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase when a later occurrence gives it a new application or confirms its continuity. The recurrence may simply confirm that the relationship has remained what it was, without requiring the phrase to gain a new symbolic meaning. Keep the present scene's scale. A promise or act of care remains a particular human exchange even when the telling gives it a public form.\n\nPreserve private experience beyond any public account. The person remembered as dependable may have helped reluctantly, or the person remembered as having refused may have done so with private grief that no one saw. The narrator's access to that private experience follows the selected perspective. Allow ordinary speech, uncompleted action, or ongoing recollection at the boundary. The scene may end with business concluded or resumed, with a promise still being kept, or with the account itself unfinished, with the actual relationship and the attributed account still available to continue, without deciding on the community's behalf what the act must mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the essayistic narrative route. Let the authorized narrator state a situated proposition in their own terms: a claim about what a phrase meant, why someone belonged somewhere, or what a family or community came to believe. The proposition should arise from the narrator's particular position and available knowledge, not from an omniscient summary of the cast.\n\nSet that proposition beside a specific interpersonal case. The case can be an established exchange available under the narrator's actual access, whether firsthand, reported or recorded, an explicitly hypothetical situation the narrator constructs to test the claim, or a remembered occasion whose details the narrator can still distinguish from later retellings. Give the case a concrete decision or relation appropriate to its actual or hypothetical status: someone asked, withheld, accepted, misunderstood, or chose to change. Draw on the personal experience, family memory, public account or recurring language actually relevant to the occasion, keeping each source attributable instead of requiring every layer or reducing the case to an abstract illustration.\n\nReturn to the proposition through confirmation, qualification, or continued uncertainty. The return need not correct the belief, disclose a motive, or alter anyone's next response. Distinguish the emotional authority of a circulating account from what its source actually supports. A phrase repeated by many people may carry weight without becoming evidence of what the original speaker felt. A private memory may qualify the public version without being announced to anyone else.\n\nKeep the essayistic movement available across replies. One installment may state the claim and open the case; a later one may return to it after new experience or recollection. Do not force the return to arrive within a single scene, and do not require the narrator to resolve the proposition before the story can continue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings when location or time changes, readable action blocks, and clear dialogue cues to locate an actual interpersonal act. Let the visible scene carry what the cast can see and hear: who enters, what they handle, what they say, and what they leave unsaid.\n\nWhen a memory sequence belongs to the actual experience, distinguish its source, time and place from the current scene. It may arise from a spoken phrase, an object or an ongoing private concern without a fresh visible trigger. Use a separate heading when presenting the remembered occasion as a scene, with readable action and dialogue; private recollection may instead remain in its active nonspoken presentation. Keep the relation to current events legible without requiring a cut or a completed return before the scene boundary.\n\nPreserve authorized nonspoken experience independently of voice-over or performed narration. Private thought, feeling, and association appear in the active private presentation, clearly attributed to the character, and are not converted into dialogue another character can hear or into a voice-over that the scene itself performs. A character may speak a memory aloud when the relationship and occasion support it, but the spoken version is a chosen communication, not the automatic destination of private recollection.\n\nA cut or expression does not certify motive. A character may look away, repeat a phrase, or leave a room without the screenplay declaring what it means. The reader's access to private experience follows the active contract, not the visible expression. No memory sequence, revelation, or changed result is required for each scene. A scene may end on a pending request, an ordinary continuation, or the simple fact that someone has not yet answered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nMake positions, entrances, exits, objects, and ongoing business playable around the actual request or exchange. Let the stage directions establish who can see whom, who is near the door, who is holding or avoiding an object, and what practical task continues while people speak. Let live choices and any actual nonspoken experience carry the relationship, keeping stageable business distinct from the private presentation rather than replacing either with a generic explanation.\n\nLet remembered accounts enter through speech only when someone actually tells them. A character may repeat a family phrase, describe a past occasion, or report what the community says, but the telling is a present action with a present audience and purpose. Another character may hear it, interrupt it, misunderstand it, or decline to respond. The spoken version does not automatically include everything the teller privately associates with the memory.\n\nKeep a prop's personal history from broadcasting itself. An ordinary object on the table or in someone's hands may carry private meaning for one character, but the stage directions do not announce that meaning. Other characters' knowledge of the object follows actual observation, memory, communication or other established access. Preserve authorized nonspoken experience in the active private presentation, attributed to the character, rather than disguising it as an aside or monologue that other characters can hear.\n\nKeep offstage lives and actual hearing distinct from narrator access. An exit does not automatically grant or remove knowledge: what the person can hear offstage, receive through communication or otherwise access follows the actual circumstances. A character who is present may still not know what another character privately feels. Permit a pending response or quiet continuation without a compulsory entrance, revelation, or resolution. The scene can end with someone still waiting, still working, or still holding an unanswered question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet current sensation, private wording, personal memory, and received public accounts connect through the authorized person's actual associations. Begin from what the character perceives or feels now, then let the mind move to a remembered voice, a family phrase, a rumor, or a public event when the association makes that movement intelligible. The braid should belong to this particular mind: its concerns, its vocabulary, its bodily experience, and its actual knowledge and any limits.\n\nMark enough temporal and source anchors to distinguish heard rumor, remembered speech, and present event. A remembered sentence may appear in the flow, but the reader should know whether the character is hearing it now, recalling who said it, or repeating what the community claims. Use small anchors such as a name, a place, a time marker, or a sensory detail to keep the levels separate. Do not let a public account become the character's own memory merely because both appear in the same paragraph.\n\nThe stream can settle or continue inward without changing conduct. A character may follow an association to a painful or comforting place and then return to the present task, or remain absorbed without acting. Give meaningful experience its needed development without charging fictional time for prose length. The length of the inward passage does not mean the scene has advanced, and a short passage does not mean the feeling was trivial.\n\nDo not merge other minds into the flow. Another character's remembered words enter only as the authorized mind recalls them, with the limits of that recollection. Another character's present thought remains outside the stream unless separately authorized. The communal story is material the character carries, not a shared consciousness the narration can enter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages and supported transmission events. Each message belongs to a particular sender and appears in the order supported by the represented record, with any relevant differences between sending and delivery made clear. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may write in fragments, another in full sentences, another may leave a message unread or type and delete.\n\nWhen a forwarded saying, old thread, or familiar phrase enters the exchange, make its source, time, and recipient clear. A character may forward a family phrase, quote an old message, or paste a public announcement, but the transcript shows what was actually transmitted and to whom. A copy is a copy, not the original occasion. An old thread is a record, not the live memory of everyone who participated in it.\n\nDistinguish copies, unsent drafts, reading, and response from actual private experience outside the transcript. An unsent draft is a composed artifact that was not transmitted; it does not automatically become the character's live private thought, and another character's access depends on actual visibility, communication or other established circumstances rather than a transmission that never occurred. A read receipt records what the supported interface actually confirms; it does not by itself establish comprehension or feeling. A delay need not imply resentment, and a quick reply need not imply ease.\n\nKeep private memory and association outside the transcript in the active private presentation, clearly separated from the transmitted exchange. A shared text does not make every associated memory shared knowledge. One character may read a familiar phrase and privately recall its original occasion, but sending the phrase alone does not disclose what it stirred; any knowledge of that response follows actual communication or other established access.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the shared scene and actual dialogue with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and a flexible rhythm of movement and inward prose. Use focal narration for an authorized person's attention while preserving the active narrator, narrative person and any additional permitted access; a focal passage does not authorize new minds or erase access the contract already supplies.\n\nUse purposeful background or expression cues as references suited to an illustrated scene. A changed expression, a familiar object in the background, or a shift in posture can orient the moment, but it does not certify the relationship's state. The focal narration may notice the cue and interpret it, miss its significance, or connect it to a personal or public account whose source remains intact. A repeated cue can gather history without requiring a new meaning at every revisit.\n\nWhen a familiar object or phrase recalls a personal or public account, let the association arise from the focal character's actual memory or concern. The narration may move from the present scene to the remembered occasion and back, keeping the time and source distinguishable. A family phrase repeated in dialogue is a present utterance; the focal character's private association with it is separate and need not be spoken aloud.\n\nPreserve all authorized private experience without requiring a visible reaction, concise thought, or new meaning at a revisit. The focal character may feel something without the narration assigning an expression cue to it, and a memory may remain private without becoming dialogue. Features such as menus and images follow their separate authorization; do not add branching choices, romance routes, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nCarry forward established words, decisions, and pending matters without replacing them with a full recap. Where orientation is needed, recover the particular context that matters now: the request still unanswered, the phrase someone used, the task left unfinished, or the person who has not yet returned. Continue from the actual current situation, recalling earlier history when useful without a compulsory or redundant full recap.\n\nWhen a remembered phrase or account recurs, let it arise from continuing private concern or present circumstance. A character may repeat the phrase aloud, hear it from someone else, or privately return to it because the present situation makes it matter again. Its meaning may remain unchanged or develop as warranted. Do not force a new significance onto every recurrence, and do not pretend the phrase was never said before.\n\nPreserve each person's access across the interval. Knowledge of an earlier exchange follows actual perception, reports, records or other established access; absence from the scene does not itself settle what someone could know. A character who left with a misunderstanding may continue to act on that misunderstanding until actual reconsideration or new information changes their interpretation, without requiring a fresh external fact. The installment may show separate activity in different places without merging what each person knows.\n\nAn installment can contain company, separate activity, or an unfinished decision without requiring progress, a revelation, or a changed relationship. The through-line is the continuing life of these particular people: what they are doing, what they remember, what they are waiting for, and what remains available to continue. Stop according to the existing role and scene boundary, not a quota of completed turns.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nFocus the scene on one exchange in which a character asks, admits, refuses, or apologizes. Choose the particular word, gesture, or distance that carries the cost for this relationship. Write with brief declarative movement: state what is said, what is not answered, and what the viewpoint character feels or understands. Where an actual pause, unfinished sentence, or shift of attention belongs to the encounter, let it carry its particular meaning without hiding private experience. A direct continuous exchange is also possible; do not manufacture a gap. Render the authorized interiority fully: the character's need, hurt, resolve, or hope remains available even when not spoken. A later response may alter, sustain, or leave unchanged the significance of what was said, and a response can remain pending. Do not demand betrayal, cryptic wit, interrupted confession, visible distress, or a revealed motive; the scene's power lies in the precise exposure already present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen by orienting the submitted contribution: identify the ongoing occasion, the active speakers, and any ownership or control boundaries. Choose one direct answer, noticed particular, or supported pause that gives the interval its focus for this reply. Write in clear, readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Use brief narrative movement while preserving each speaker's actual diction and needed extent: state actions and speech directly, leave meaningful gaps where a character truly does not respond or has no access, and select a distilled image that carries the relational weight. Preserve all NPC choices and the active world. Render the focal character's private experience in full—thoughts, feelings, uncertainties—without turning it into dialogue or knowledge available to others. End at a natural stopping point that follows from the scene; do not fabricate a question, cliffhanger, or prepared next move.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the intimate urban or domestic narrative tradition. Locate the relationship through how characters actually use a room, possession, visit, or shared arrangement: a teapot left out, a chair turned, a coat hung by the door. Follow their real exchange with brief, declarative sentences and a few telling particulars. Let a space between utterances or actions preserve ease, uncertainty, or attention as the scene supplies; do not force every pause to hide a feeling. Keep the focal character's private experience fully rendered—what they notice, remember, or decide—even when not voiced. Allow a later occasion to confirm or qualify an interpretation without obligatory conflict, domestic duty, cultural stereotype, or sudden revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nChoose the actual physical condition bearing on the exchange: comfort, distance, the state of an object, or effort freely undertaken. Write with forceful, concrete clauses and compressed transitions that make that condition felt. Use exact details—a worn handrail, a borrowed jacket, cold air between seats—and let their significance be supported by actual context, avoiding redundant gloss without withholding a known meaning or the character's direct private understanding. Keep needed causes explicit: show why a character acts or refrains. Use concise outward narration without shortening a speaker's actual utterance or deleting inner life merely to maintain brevity. Develop the focal character's reasoning, feeling, and uncertainty as fully as the scene requires, even when their outward actions are brief. Speech remains individual; roughness or injury is supplied by the world, not the style. The scene may end without a decision or return to labor; the relational condition itself is the point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the document form this relationship supports: a letter, journal entry, report, or note with a clear writer, intended reader or purpose, and time of composition. Let a precise request, object, or phrase make the record particular—an old address, a borrowed book, a half-finished sentence. Select precise recorded words and details while keeping the document's actual writer, purpose, register, and needed extent intact. Concise wording may fit, but an expansive letter or report remains available; any gaps follow that writer's actual knowledge and choices of disclosure. A writer may deliberately omit something known from the record, while the authorized live private experience remains available outside it. Keep the actual circulation or lack of delivery as a factual matter. A gap between records may be ordinary time, not a withheld clue. Later wording may sustain or change how an earlier passage is read, but it must not retroactively change what the writer knew then. Outside the documents, render the character's live private experience fully—their present thoughts and feelings—clearly separated from the composed artifact. Do not require omission, confession, or an answering record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSelect the actual kindness, preference, or comfortable presence this person notices: a favorite tea, a remembered joke, a warm room, an unhurried offer. Write with Gu Long's directness—brief declarative sentences and a distilled image that holds the warmth. Let dialogue include openly named affection if the character would say it, and render the focal character's private pleasure or gratitude fully. Quietness may be ease rather than withheld feeling. A repeated gesture can stay familiar without gaining new meaning. Keep each person's personality and boundaries intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable. The scene owes no gratitude, healing, confession, improved bond, or reveal after a pause. Follow the active boundary within care, continued company, or an actual conclusion. A promise can be spoken when the person makes one, without requiring a future emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nCompose with deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open from a particular word, act, or relation in the present scene, then widen into the social or personal history the active narrator may know—an arrangement, a past occasion, a public fact. Use this context to develop real inward understanding, not to delay it. Return to the present with a direct, brief sentence or image typical of Gu Long, concentrating attention on the exchange. Preserve the distinction between interpretation and another person's actual motives: the narration may understand one character's perspective without knowing what others truly think. Keep speech individual. A qualification or recognition follows supported context and may be unnecessary; the scene can end without a revelation, social verdict, or complete explanatory arc. Render the focal character's private experience fully within the authorized scope.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nSelect the actual occasion, teller, and listeners for the told episode. Situate listener attention through actual speech, conduct, and authorized private access. Wants can remain unknown; the occasion may involve company, enjoyment, persuasion, or instruction without requiring a changed response or proof. Use brief declarative movement in the outer scene and inside the embedded account: choose where a speaker stops, what they omit because they do not know it or choose not to share, and which concrete detail carries the listener's reception.\n\nKeep the teller's present knowledge separate from the earlier participants' knowledge. A statement the teller now understands may not have been understood then, and a current silence need not conceal a usable fact. When the embedded account includes direct exchange, let it stand as the participants' actual spoken practice rather than a clue planted for the outer listener. Let pauses and returns remain ordinary ways of choosing emphasis or collecting memory; do not require each to become interruption, revelation, or a hidden message.\n\nUse the selected particulars needed to keep the episode and its relation to the listeners intelligible, such as a repeated word, pictured object, or recognized request. No fixed count or resolved episode is required before the active boundary. These particulars orient the return to the outer scene without certifying the account as true or making it a lesson. A response may be simple acknowledgment, changed attention, renewed patience, or an unanswered question; do not force a lesson or a final interpretation. Keep private experience beyond performance: the teller's own unspoken experience remains distinct from what the account selects, and the listeners' private reception remains their own rather than being announced as a reaction beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and developmental realism through a participant's established expectation about capacity, belonging, or obligation. Select the concrete encounter or recurring interval through which that expectation is lived or directly discussed: a routine request, a familiar task, a moment of help or refusal that occurs inside a known social arrangement. Use brief declarative presentation to keep the encounter specific, and use meaningful gaps and distilled images to let the person's reading of events remain distinct from what others actually do or say.\n\nAccumulate known details across occasions without requiring them to produce a changed self-image. A person may stick with the same understanding after seeing the same evidence, or may revise it slowly because a particular detail finally registers; the narrative needs no maturation test, humiliation, or concealed lesson. Let private interpretation differ from established fact: show what the viewpoint person notices, expects, and concludes, while keeping the actual conduct and speech of others available through their own actions and words.\n\nWhen the scene includes direct exchange, let the person's expectation color attention without making the other party's behavior proof of hidden motive. Keep authorized private experience available: what the viewpoint person feels, hopes, or fears about the encounter may be rendered, while other participants' experience follows the active permissions. Keep a viewpoint inference from words or conduct distinct from any separately authorized direct access to another mind. Use intervals to preserve understanding as much as to change it; the reply may end with the person still carrying the same expectation into the next practical task, without a lesson attached.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhen established authority bears on a scene, give that authority a concrete operation: an invitation extended or accepted, permission asked or granted, a condition stated, a small privilege used, an ordinary act done under the terms of a larger arrangement. Keep the operation specific rather than atmospheric. Use brief declarative movement to present the gesture or material detail, and use meaningful gaps to leave what the participants do not say open without treating silence as proof.\n\nLet a direct line or pause carry a participant's particular desire, uncertainty, or recognition, but do not let it certify corruption, concealed intent, or a mask about to fall. A request can be sincere, assistance can be genuine, and affection can be freely offered inside the inherited arrangement. Preserve the person's actual private experience through the visible or hidden interiority the authorship contract permits; what one character feels or suspects remains separate from what another knows or promises, and the narration may reveal the focal concern only if access exists.\n\nKeep the collision of old authority and present life concrete through selected urban material detail or intimate conditions the world already includes. A room, a key, a signature, a family expectation, or a practical obligation can carry the encounter without becoming a symbol of hidden price. Let private desire and ordinary need coexist with the arrangement; the scene may end with the same authority intact and the relationship unchanged, if that is what the people actually do. Do not require a mask to fall or a secret cost to surface.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nCompose the scene as situated testimony from a particular participant. Choose one person whose experience of an acceptance, refusal, stay, departure, or act of care the segment will carry, and establish that account's occasion: when it is told, to whom, and with what present purpose. The active narrator need not be that participant; reported speech or written record may stand when direct access is unavailable, but the account must clearly originate from a specific source.\n\nLet the participant present exact words and concrete details from the shared encounter. Their account can be accurate, incomplete, or contested as the world supports; do not invent contradiction for its own sake. Use brief declarative presentation for the key exchange, and leave meaningful gaps where the participant did not notice, did not understand, or chose not to say what happened. Distinguish what the participant could observe from what they now claim: outside report, later inference, and direct experience remain separate.\n\nPreserve any known private reason separately from the spoken or written selection. A character's unspoken motive may be rendered under the active interiority contract, but the testimony itself carries only what the participant chose to disclose. Do not require the account to conclude with confession, corroboration, or a withheld answer; the listener's response may be simple acceptance, further practical need, or silence. The account need not settle the relationship; follow its actual reception and consequences, including supported clarification or an unchanged understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaial::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse early-modern comic and theatrical telling as narrative construction within the active presentation and narrator contract. Select a direct exchange and arrange it for timing: place an attributed line beside the physical business it accompanies, then mark what follows—a stop, a continuation, a glance, a second line. Let the relation between words and circumstances create the effect. An incongruity can emerge when a plain statement meets an over-elaborate object, a formal arrangement, or an inconvenient practical task, but the scene may also run on accurate speech and straightforward affection without comic defeat.\n\nUse direct narrative transitions while keeping each speaker's actual register and needed extent; neither the whole exchange nor its private experience has a brevity quota. Let the selection of what a person says, what they do instead of saying, and what another person actually receives carry the meaning. Meaningful gaps are pauses in the telling, not omissions of necessary fact: a character may stop because the timing is wrong, because the task is easier than explanation, or because the other person has already acted. The narration may hold a private recognition beside the presented action if the active interiority contract permits it, but no mandatory gag, witty retort, or stage audience is required.\n\nUse a distilled image or physical particular to anchor the comic/theatrical arrangement: an object that must be handled, a doorway, a garment, a serving tray, a shared bench. Let that particular make the timing legible without becoming a symbol. The reply may end at an ordinary pause or a completed exchange; it need not deliver a punchline, a reversal, or a new understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nCompose the segment as nonlinear polyphony. Choose an established orienting element already present in the world—a word someone repeated, an object passed or refused, a request, a departure, a shared meal—and let its recurrence mark shifts between actual accounts. Keep event order and source time distinct: a later account may remember an earlier detail without being treated as knowledge its source did not have at the time.\n\nUse several voices or records only as the active narration and world support. Each account must belong to an identifiable source with particular access: what that person saw, heard, said, or later learned. Let accounts change the reading of an earlier detail, confirm it, or leave it ordinary; do not manufacture contradictory testimony, delayed disclosure, or a puzzle that must snap shut. The narration may include direct interiority for authorized minds, but for characters without a mind available, use attributed speech or written record rather than claiming their private thought.\n\nWithin each account, use direct declarative movement where it concentrates attention, preserving the source's individual voice and any needed extended account or private passage. Select the exact words or concrete actions that matter, and use meaningful gaps where a source did not know or did not say something, without turning silence into a withheld answer. A reply may pause within one account, return through the orienting element, or slide to another source only when that source's access justifies the shift. The passage need not converge on a final interpretation of the relationship; the sequence may remain multiple and open without a reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaian::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: name the actual occasion, the people present, and the relevant spatial detail that lets a reader picture who can speak, move, or touch what. Use direct sentences to concentrate an exchange while paragraphing keeps speech, activity, and private response readable without a fixed cycle. A dialogue beat may stand alone; a private passage may extend as far as the actual reasoning, feeling, sensation, uncertainty, or attention needs; no short-thought rule applies.\n\nExplain unfamiliar context only through the narration's permitted access, observation, or use. A term, object, relationship, or practical arrangement can become clear because someone uses it or recalls it, without a demonstration or a break to gloss it. Keep Gu Long's brief declarative movement: select the few actions and lines that carry the encounter, and let a distilled image—a cup put down, a door left open, a task resumed—orient the reader when the scene shifts.\n\nLet private interpretation differ from established fact without a changed relationship or a revelation. A noticed detail or pause can remain familiar and unresolved: someone may ask for something, may decide not to say more, may continue an ordinary routine. The narration may render the authorized person's private attention, but that private content need not predict a later turn or produce a joke. Follow the active boundary, including within continuing private attention or a completed exchange, without requiring a reaction line or a return to outward movement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nUse the active narrator's permitted attention to develop one scene-grounded image—a sound, a visual relation, a texture—across a paragraph. Let its cadence open and close the attention: begin with a direct clause that selects the image, allow a longer inward movement to trace its felt duration for the focal character, and let a direct return to the image concentrate attention when it belongs, whether understanding changes or remains familiar. A completed return is not required before the active boundary. Do not make the image a fixed symbol for the relationship; let its significance belong to this person's current interpretation, which may shift later.\n\nRecurring attention to the same image may appear later in the scene or in subsequent replies. When it recurs, preserve or alter its significance according to what the character has since experienced or decided. The private meaning of the image need not be announced to others, and no new action or concealed motive is required. Keep sensed experience, remembered occasion, imagined possibility, and known fact distinct in the prose. Actual feeling may be directly named or left under consideration, but it must remain accessible as this character's private experience when the active contract permits.\n\nLet the selected image anchor the character drama rather than replace it: the request, withheld truth, or ordinary exchange remains the engine of the scene. The lyrical development gives that exchange felt duration and emotional weight without making every pause symbolic or every reflection a turn in the plot. A conversation may be interrupted by the image, but the image only matters because it carries this person's attention, not because it predicts a later twist or stands for an abstract theme.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSelect a particular detail in the present scene—a word, a place, an ordinary act—that the focal character associates with an earlier occasion already established in the story's continuity. Present the detail first in direct terms as it appears now, then allow the association to draw the character's private attention to that former experience. Do not blur the boundary between then and now: keep original knowledge separate from what the character thinks or feels in the present moment. The earlier occasion should be signaled only as the character's permitted memory or private realization, never as new information the reader is expected to infer from silence.\n\nThe reflective movement need not issue in a visible reaction. Let the association develop through the actual occasion. Other participants may notice an outward sign or continue without noticing, and their knowledge follows actual channels rather than automatic access to the private recollection. Recurrence of the detail can sustain attachment or ordinary pleasure as well as register real alteration. Do not supply death, failed reunion, silence, or a newly poignant meaning merely to make the pause significant. The reflection may include unfinished longing, but it must not force the character to announce loss or to see the relationship as doomed to repeat absence.\n\nLet the active boundary of the reply leave the reflection continuing or conclude the exchange without a lesson. The melancholy treatment is a mode of attention, not an outcome; the character drama proceeds through the concrete request, acceptance, refusal, or act of care that anchors the scene. What is no longer available need not overshadow what is presently being done or said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern memoir through an actual participant's account conveyed by the active narrator, person, and perspective, without inventing the narrator's participation or authorship of the memoir. Reconstruct a particular relationship act—a request, acceptance, refusal, or act of care—using the knowledge available at that earlier time. Present the original exchange in direct terms with the same degree of uncertainty or clarity the character had then. Do not allow later understanding to color what the character knew or intended in the remembered moment.\n\nMove between the remembered occasion and the memoirist's later interpretation as actually recorded or recounted. When that later account comments, place that commentary at its proper retrospective time, clearly distinguished from the original event. A selected detail from the former occasion may become newly understood in the present or remain familiar without overturning the original reason. The memoir need not resolve into confession, regret, absolution, or a completed sense of the past.\n\nPreserve actual nonspoken experience beyond the composed account. The memoir is a composed account by its actual author, not a transparent window into all private thought or a document the active narrator must create. When the active contract authorizes hidden or visible interiority, write the character's live private experience separately from the memoir's composed record, and do not substitute the document for that inner life. Keep the relationship being recalled and any actual present occasion intelligible without requiring a new live writing scene, revelation, or closure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the relevant word, position, or act that constitutes the present exchange between characters. Use direct clauses to state what is said or done and to retain its causal and personal connection to the speaker's decision. Do not reduce the scene to the exchange alone: include the minimum context—who is present, what obligation or history is active, what physical circumstance matters—necessary for the reader to infer the terms of the relationship. Omit restatement, redundant interpretation, and explanations that do not materially contribute.\n\nA pause may indicate ease, uncertainty, or another supported state; it need not conceal a withheld answer. A plain answer may settle the matter without a follow-up twist. Speech and thought can align, and the focal character's private attention can be long even when the prose is short. Do not delete known motives, important context, or developed feeling merely to create brevity; minimalism is economy of presentation, not absence of inner life.\n\nLet the active boundary govern the pause, including within an unfinished exchange or private consideration as well as after a conclusion. The relationship's terms should remain inferable through precise context without a reveal, a prepared next move, or redundant gloss; needed explanation and actual private experience may take a paragraph. Keep each character's private knowledge separate from what they say, but do not artificially delay information already established in continuity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaias::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among exchange, ordinary activity, perception, recollection, and the focal character's authorized private experience. Use a direct line or selected detail to concentrate attention within a longer passage, but do not impose terseness on every speaker. Let the narrator's voice remain flexible: a paragraph may shift from observation to memory to unspoken thought as the scene requires, without breaking the continuity of action.\n\nWhen a later context qualifies or confirms an interpretation, ground that shift in material already present or later supplied, not in a new hidden fact introduced solely for effect. Ordinary particulars—a chair moved, a repeated phrase, a glass set down—may carry personal meaning, without having to become clues. A supported inference can follow actual context, while an ordinary detail may simply remain part of lived experience. Preserve independent NPC life: those not in focus continue their own choices and responses, visible or offstage.\n\nRespect the active narrator authority. The narrative may reveal the focal character's private knowledge while other characters do not share it. A pending or completed exchange can stand without a crisis, lesson, or concealed motive at the boundary. Let past and present choices carry their actual human context; a sudden turn can occur when the people and circumstances support it, without being required for dramatic interest. Close the passage at the natural pause dictated by the existing scene and role.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nAnchor the scene in the actual relationship premise from the supplied material. Choose a present request, offer, ordinary pleasure, or established callback that belongs to those particular people. Let the exchange proceed with its own terms: shared understanding and direct affection are possible, as are confusion, restraint, or disagreement. Do not make every selected word or gesture a test of devotion.\n\nUse a selected phrase, gesture, or recurring detail from the characters' shared history to locate the moment, but keep its significance local. A familiar expression may simply be familiar; a remembered habit may recall attachment without implying a hidden wound. Each person's known history, private reasons, and actual response remain distinct. New acquaintances need no invented past to create intimacy.\n\nThe relationship may continue, change, or conclude without a forced revelation or repair. Passionate language is allowed but not obligatory; genuine care can be quiet. When private feeling is authorized, render the actual experience, including directly named feeling or reasoning when appropriate, rather than replacing it with detached commentary about what the reader should think. The promise of the bond is fulfilled through how these people actually treat each other in the current scene, not through an imposed arc of discovery or reconciliation. Follow the active boundary within continued company, uncertain understanding, or an actual conclusion, without a newly legible relationship being a prerequisite.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nUse deliberate openings, named acts, and coherent recurring phrasing within the active narrator's authority to give the passage the sense of a retelling. Introduce a compact declaration that names the relationship act—a request, acceptance, refusal, or contribution—and then expand into the actual particulars bearing on its scope and accuracy. A public account may be accurate, limited, or mistaken without the narration rewriting events to vindicate it. The chronicle may state what was done while leaving private reasons unattributed or uncertain.\n\nLet recurring phrasing return when it belongs to the actual account, with familiar or changed application; repeated epithets are not required. A later detail may qualify the earlier retelling without a mandatory debunking or a new significance at every recurrence. Keep original knowledge and actual private experience distinct from what the public version records. The saga voice does not grant omniscient access to motives named in the declaration; the narrator's authority follows the selected person and contract.\n\nLet the account pause unfinished or conclude without heroic promotion, hidden sacrifice, or a community verdict. The relationship act remains the center: what was asked, given, withheld, or resolved. The retelling may elevate the act without falsifying its human scale. End where the current telling naturally rests, leaving the next installment available but not declaring the story's meaning for anyone beyond the narrator.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the psychological and essayistic mode. Select a direct claim that one person actually makes about a relationship—spoken aloud or held as an authorized private judgment—and place it beside a particular instance the narration can remember, perceive, or clearly imagine. Keep the claim's source and the instance's source distinct: a remembered act is not the same as the later sentence that interprets it, and a hypothetical case is not an event that occurred.\n\nLet the claim arrive as a brief declarative statement. It may be a single line of dialogue or a compressed inward formulation. Preserve needed context and the claimant's actual words or private reasoning, without adding redundant explanation around the declaration. Then develop the comparison through sustained inward consideration: what the person noticed at the time, what they now recall, what the claim would mean if applied to that particular, and whether the fit is exact, imperfect, or still uncertain. The comparison may confirm the claim, qualify it, or leave it open. It does not need to produce a new test, a refutation, or a completed argument.\n\nKeep feeling available beyond the reasoning. An authorized person may experience bodily discomfort, a recurring image, or reluctance to finish the comparison. Preserve the actual owner of that response; the narrator need not be the claimant or the person experiencing it. Another character's private motive remains separate from what the narrator infers.\n\nUse the Gu Long movement through selection and omission. Concentrate on the particulars that make this claim's scope intelligible without a fixed count. Where an actual interval or shift of attention falls between claim and instance, let it carry its meaning rather than manufacturing a gap, and let a distilled image—a room, an object, a gesture—carry part of the comparison without being decoded. The scene can end while the question remains open, without forcing a crisis or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nPresent the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use scene headings when location or time changes, and keep cuts to supported shifts in place, time, or attention. An action line should establish what is visible or audible: who enters, what object is handled, where a person stands, what sound interrupts. Do not crowd the page with camera commands or symbolic close-ups.\n\nLet a noticed object, gesture, or silence orient the exchange without becoming proof of private motive. A character may set down a cup, look toward a door, or leave a sentence unfinished. The action line records the observable fact. The meaning of that fact belongs to a particular person's interpretation, and another character may interpret it differently or not notice it at all.\n\nKeep direct speech brief and declarative where the speaker would be brief. A line may carry a request, a refusal, an ordinary question, or a partial answer. Do not require every exchange to end in a revelation or a completed emotional statement. Let physical time follow the events: a pause is a pause, not a coded confession.\n\nPreserve actual private experience in its selected presentation, clearly attributed and separate from voice-over. Voice-over is a performed or narrated layer with its own audience and permissions; it is not the default container for thought. A focal character's unspoken reasoning, memory, or sensation remains available to the reader under the active contract without being converted into a line another character can hear.\n\nThe scene may stop within consideration, ordinary company, or a completed exchange. A cut can move to a later moment without implying that the intervening silence resolved anything.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script. Give each speaker a clear name, and use entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be performed in a shared space. A stage direction should tell an actor or director what is physically present and what changes: who moves, what is picked up or set down, where a person stands in relation to another, what sound comes from offstage. Do not add film-camera instructions.\n\nLet the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character may speak while doing something ordinary—folding a cloth, checking a door, pouring water—or may stop moving before answering. The action does not need to illustrate the line. A pause is a supported silence, not a coded emotion. One character's stillness can mean attention, fatigue, refusal, or something else; the script does not certify which.\n\nUse direct declarative speech where it fits the speaker. A request, an admission, a practical question, or a refusal can arrive in a short line. Do not require every character to be witty, cryptic, or eloquent. Offstage events remain available through credible sounds, reports, or visible traces. A character may also fail to react, and that absence is itself a playable choice.\n\nPreserve actual nonspoken experience in its selected presentation, clearly attributed and separate from asides and monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech with a dramatic purpose and an audience; it is not the default container for private thought. A focal character's unspoken reasoning, memory, or sensation remains available to the reader under the active contract without being disguised as a line another character can hear.\n\nThe scene may end within consideration, ordinary company, or a completed exchange. The boundary need not wait for a disclosure or a rearranged stage picture.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nOrganize the telling through one authorized person's actual mental succession. Follow what the mind moves among: a present perception, a memory, an imagined reply, a bodily sensation, an interruption, a recurring concern. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations rather than a linear report of events. Keep enough orientation to distinguish what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is only imagined or wished.\n\nUse the Gu Long movement within this associative flow. A brief declarative return can concentrate attention: a short sentence that names what the person has just realized, or a single image that stops the drift. Do not shorten the inward movement itself or force a material interruption to create drama. The mind may circle back to the same phrase, object, or unanswered question without resolving it.\n\nPreserve what the person actually knows. A conclusion already reached remains available in thought even when unspoken to others. A later realization cannot be backdated as an earlier motive. Distinguish the person's private words from what they would say aloud; they may rehearse a sentence, reject it, or imagine another person's response without sending it.\n\nKeep access boundaries intact. The associative movement belongs to the authorized mind only. Another character's thoughts remain separate unless the active contract grants access. The active narrator's role and any additional permitted access stay in force. No reveal, decision, or return is required before the boundary; the passage may end while the mind is still turning something over.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the story through readable sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one sender writes in fragments, another in complete sentences, another leaves a message unsent. Do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nLet direct wording be brief or developed as its speaker needs. A request, an ordinary question, a shared reference, or a refusal can arrive in a short message. A familiar phrase may recur without becoming a coded signal. Do not require every exchange to produce an answer, a reveal, or a change in the relationship.\n\nDistinguish the stages of communication. A composed draft is not a sent message. An imagined reply is not a received one. Transmission is not receipt, receipt is not comprehension, and comprehension is not action. Keep other knowledge channels separate: what one person knows from a call, a shared room, or an earlier conversation does not automatically enter the transcript.\n\nPreserve actual nonspoken experience beyond sent text. A sender's private reasoning, hesitation, or feeling remains available in its selected presentation, clearly separated from the transcript. A short answer, silence, or familiar phrase need not conceal a motive, predict a disclosure, or change the bond. The exchange may end while the relationship remains exactly where it was.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the scene through a relevant place, object, or distance. Use concise scene-setting to establish where the characters are and what is visibly present. Then move among attributed dialogue, focal narration, and actual private attention according to the scene's rhythm. A background or expression cue is a purposeful reference, not an executable engine command or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nLet a familiar cue remain ordinary or acquire a supported new reading. A character may notice the same doorway, the same cup, the same posture as before. The narration may treat it as unchanged, or a later fact may give it new significance. Do not require fixed cue-and-reaction cycles, and do not force a changed significance where none is supported.\n\nKeep the focal person's interpretation separate from what another character knows. The narration may reveal what the focal person infers, doubts, or privately recalls. Another character does not know that inference merely because the reader has followed its formation. Conversely, do not conceal an authorized focal conclusion until the rest of the cast catches up.\n\nPreserve developed inward experience during quick exchanges. A short dialogue line may be surrounded by a longer private response: reasoning, memory, sensation, or an unspoken reply. The style does not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or a required final reveal. The scene may end while a question remains open, without forcing a choice screen or a completed emotional statement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies. At the start of a new installment, recover only the context needed for the continuing relation, ordinary activity, or private concern. Name the actual earlier request, response, commitment, or unresolved matter rather than retelling the whole history that established it.\n\nPreserve what people already know. A previously settled answer stays available. A later interpretation cannot be backdated into an earlier motive. If a character now understands something differently, the narration shows the new understanding arriving now; it does not pretend the earlier moment already contained it.\n\nLet a familiar particular recur without forcing it to change meaning. A character may notice the same object, phrase, or gesture as before. The installment may treat it as unchanged, or a supported new fact may give it new significance. Repetition may sustain or deepen understanding without restarting a settled suspicion or manufacturing a delayed disclosure.\n\nKeep inward experience and independent lives continuing without a per-installment reveal, conflict, new detail, or prepared next move. A character may spend the installment working, resting, talking, or thinking. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Preserve actual settled answers and continuing uncertainty alike; serial duration supplies no deadline for resolving a relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nSituate the difficult request, admission, apology, or return in the actual encounter. Where ordinary activity is already present, give it unhurried attention: clearing a table, waiting for water to boil, or walking to a station. The person may also simply speak, wait, or think without an invented task, and activity may continue or pause according to actual events. The speaker may pause, restart, or leave a sentence incomplete because the request costs something, not because the scene must become a confrontation.\n\nAttach the private concern to a concrete detail the viewpoint notices in the present: a stain, a repeated sound, the weight of a bag, the way a familiar object has been left in the wrong place. Let the detail recur where attention brings it back, without a fixed count or a requirement to explain its meaning. It should carry the person's association rather than signal that the other character understands it or that the relationship has already changed.\n\nGive the focal person's inward experience the room it actually needs. A direct thought, a sustained reflection, or a sharp physical reaction may interrupt the outward exchange. The other character responds from their own knowledge and purposes. They may answer the spoken request, address only part of it, notice the hesitation, or remain occupied with the task. Their response need not complete the focal person's feeling.\n\nKeep speech in each speaker's ordinary register. A plain sentence can carry more strain than an elaborate one. Do not require the exchange to end in reconciliation, rupture, confession, or a visible change in behavior. The difficult thing may remain partly unsaid, and the ordinary activity may simply reach its own small conclusion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nCompose the current interaction as a coherent shared interval under the active role contract. Use readable scene paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Orient the reader through ordinary spatial and practical details: where the characters are, what they are doing, and what remains unfinished around them.\n\nLet the focal person's attention move between the shared activity and a private association when that connection occurs. The association may arise from a familiar object, a repeated sound, or the particular way another person performs a routine action. It belongs to the focal person's experience and is not automatically visible to the other characters.\n\nKeep NPCs active from their own knowledge and purposes. They may continue a task, answer a question, change the subject, or pursue an independent concern. Their speech and behavior should follow from who they are in the scene, not from the focal person's private interpretation.\n\nAllow the interval to remain unhurried. The reply may stay in thought, waiting, or routine activity without an obligatory question, cliffhanger, or forced choice. End at a natural point in the shared continuity, leaving the next exchange able to continue from what has already been established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Write the scene through an actual routine of proximity: sharing a room, waiting for a visitor, working beside someone, or moving through a familiar neighborhood together. Keep the social arrangement particular to the supplied setting and community.\n\nLet a recurring ordinary detail connect present contact to earlier feeling. The detail may be a sound from the street, a household object, the timing of a meal, or the way a particular task is divided. It should return naturally within the routine, not as a symbolic announcement. The focal person's association with it may be private, while another character experiences the same detail differently or simply as part of the day.\n\nPreserve the unhurried Murakami surface. Describe practical activities evenly and allow conversational distance to remain. A moment of direct feeling can surface through the routine, but it need not break the shared activity or produce a visible confrontation.\n\nAllow ease to remain ease. Co-presence may be companionable, strained, or simply ordinary. Do not presume estrangement, hidden resentment, or a required emotional repair. The scene may end with the routine still in progress and the private association unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful diction and compressed transitions for the material conditions of the scene: worn surfaces, exact sounds, bodily effort, the weight or resistance of objects. Keep these details concrete and brief. Let their actual presence register through the outward exchange or focal attention, with interruption only when it occurs, without inventing a survival predicament for the style, while preserving any such conditions actually supplied.\n\nWhen a private association arises, allow the prose to slow. A sustained thought or memory may occupy a longer passage even though the surrounding action remains compressed. An association may arise from a present detail, bodily sensation, or the person's established memory and concern; it need not attach to a worn object or repeated sound. It brings the concern back without proving danger or forcing a physical response.\n\nKeep rough or abrasive speech in the mouth of its actual speaker. Another character may speak plainly or with care. Do not make every voice hard-edged, and do not require the focal person to answer inward pain with visible toughness.\n\nUse changes of rhythm deliberately. A compressed action can be followed by a longer inward passage, then by a short practical exchange. The inner movement need not be completed by the scene's end. Material pressure may continue while the private concern remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual documents. Give each document a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific form the scene requires: a letter, note, report, message, or record. Do not rotate through formats mechanically.\n\nLet an ordinary phrase or item recur across records where the history supports it. A familiar object, a repeated instruction, or a small practical detail may appear in different documents. Each writer may associate it differently, but the recurrence should not invent contradictions or force a hidden pattern.\n\nKeep the document's wording straightforward. A writer may record what happened, what they want, or what they believe the recipient needs to know. The record is a composed artifact, not the writer's complete live mind. Distinguish what the document says from what its writer or reader privately feels. The recorded meaning may be plain even when its resonance for the reader is larger.\n\nUse gaps between documents to carry time and change. A later record may answer an earlier request, avoid it, or continue a practical matter without addressing the private concern. The reader's private association with a recurring detail need not be confirmed by any document.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on a feasible gesture, shared routine, or remembered preference that belongs to these particular people. The care may be practical: preparing food, adjusting a room, walking together, or completing a small task the other person has been avoiding. Describe it unhurriedly, with enough sensory detail to make the comfort tangible.\n\nLet a sensory detail sustain warmth or an individual association without requiring an explanation. The smell of tea, the texture of a blanket, the sound of rain, or the familiar way a person arranges objects can carry the focal person's private feeling. The detail need not be named as significant by anyone else.\n\nKeep the recipient's response their own. They may enjoy the kindness directly, need different help, remain preoccupied, or accept the care while thinking of something else. Their reaction does not have to match the focal person's intention or resolve a private uncertainty.\n\nAllow the comfort to remain partial. Warmth can coexist with an unresolved concern. Do not require a confession, emotional repair, or a moment of complete understanding. The scene may end with the ordinary care still present and the private concern still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open the scene through the authorized narrator's social or historical access: the conditions of the meeting, the history of the place, the obligations that bring these people together, or the public meaning of the occasion. Let this framing give the exchange context without becoming a lecture.\n\nMove between the shared occasion and a sustained private concern through clear transitions. A paragraph may begin with the outward activity, turn toward the focal person's association, and return to the shared moment. Keep the association specific to the person who experiences it; it may remain unspoken or be communicated through an actual exchange, without automatic transfer of knowledge. Do not treat it as a factual explanation of another character's behavior or as the scene's official meaning.\n\nKeep speech in its ordinary individual register. The formal architecture belongs to the narration, not to every speaker. A character may speak plainly, awkwardly, or with care. Conversational distance can remain even when the narrator's prose is composed.\n\nAllow the formal arc to remain in progress. The scene need not reach a judgment, resolution, or completed interpretation. The private association may stay open, and the social context may continue to exert pressure without determining the outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the scene inside a specific telling situation: identify who is speaking, who is listening, and what immediate need or pleasure makes the story worth telling now. The teller may want company, to explain a decision, to fill a silence, or to avoid a question, but the occasion itself should remain concrete and social.\n\nWhen the embedded account begins, shift levels cleanly. Let the teller describe ordinary actions in an unhurried, even way: a person making tea, walking a familiar route, waiting for a call, returning an object. Give the account its own literal time and place, and keep the teller's selection and emphasis visible. A detail the teller returns to may carry private feeling without being announced as significant.\n\nInside the tale, let ordinary things associate with the teller's private concern where their attention makes that connection, without a fixed count. The association should arise from the teller's attention, not from an omniscient explanation: a repeated sound, a particular hour, a task done alone. Do not require the tale to become strange or to explain the association.\n\nReturn to the frame as an actual social event. Show an actual response or continuing attention: a small action, a practical question, a delay, or quiet listening may locate the ongoing occasion. The listener may connect the tale to their own situation, but need not decode it, confront the teller, or reach a new understanding. The telling may affect the room's atmosphere or sustain it unchanged; no atmospheric or relationship change is required.\n\nIf the scene continues, let the outer exchange continue according to the people's actual attention; the tale may remain a private concern, a shared pleasure, or something they have simply finished hearing. Recurrence can work through a phrase or object reappearing in the frame, but it should not certify a hidden meaning. Keep the teller's private experience and the listener's private experience distinct.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse Romantic and uncanny narration as a way of holding a familiar encounter at the edge of recognition. Where the encounter contains a relevant threshold, work with that actual occasion rather than inventing a crossing: a doorway, a street corner, a station entrance, the moment before a visitor speaks, the return to a room after absence. Keep the place literal and the person's access to it real.\n\nDescribe the threshold through unhurried practical activity and exact surfaces. Let attention settle on an actual familiar detail, whether unchanged or changed: the sound of a lock, the angle of light, a coat on a hook, the order of objects on a table. Let familiarity reside in the person's attention and even description; an explanation or remembered source may be present when the person actually knows it.\n\nAllow the impression to develop slowly. The character may pause, repeat a small action, or continue speaking while the sense of familiarity gathers. The threshold feeling can settle into comfort, persist as an unresolved strangeness, or fade back into the ordinary exchange. Do not force it to become dread, revelation, or a supernatural event.\n\nKeep the social encounter active. Another person may ask for something, withhold an answer, misunderstand, or offer help while the focal character remains partly absorbed in the threshold impression. Let conversation and inward attention coexist, pause, or interrupt one another according to actual events and this person's attention, without a fixed alternation or compulsory simultaneous continuation.\n\nIf the scene returns to the threshold later, let the repeated detail carry the accumulated feeling without announcing its meaning. Where an uncanny effect belongs to the experience, develop the relation between the known place and the person's actual sense of it. Familiarity may also remain comfortable or ordinary; no feeling of dislocation or invented cause is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet an established authority enter the scene through its practical use rather than as a backdrop. The character may work in an old building, carry out a task governed by an inherited rule, visit someone who holds a position, or handle an object that belongs to an institution. Show the authority through what it permits, requires, or makes awkward in the present encounter.\n\nDescribe the material setting with unhurried attention: worn surfaces, locked drawers, a particular chair, the way light falls on a nameplate or a schedule. Let these details create an intimate atmosphere, but keep them as actual features of the place. Do not let mood stand in for information about the institution or the people in it.\n\nGive the focal character a private desire or concern that the authority touches. The desire may be small and practical: to be believed, to keep a promise, to leave early, to be allowed a particular choice. Let it appear through actual attention, association, or direct speech; a confession is possible where supported but is not required. The character's feeling about the authority can be mixed without becoming paranoia or automatic rebellion.\n\nAllow trust and help to remain possible. A person inside the institution may listen, cooperate, or offer comfort without being secretly corrupt or about to unmask. Social masks may fall away in a trusted exchange, but they may also remain in place because the situation requires them. Keep the difference between what the character feels and what the institution actually does.\n\nIf the scene moves toward a decision, let it arise from the practical collision between old authority and present life: a request granted, a rule bent, an obligation named, a private desire acknowledged. Let atmosphere give the actual encounter and any personal cost concrete texture, not supply a hidden threat where none exists.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric and philosophical inwardness as a single reflective construction. Begin with an ordinary activity that the scene already contains: preparing food, waiting for someone, folding cloth, walking beside another person, clearing a table. Let a sensation or repeated act open a particular question about closeness, wanting, or obligation.\n\nFollow the association through the authorized person's actual language and feeling. The question need not be stated as a thesis; it can emerge through a comparison, a remembered phrase, or a shift in attention from the task to the other person. Keep the reflection tied to the scene so that the inward movement does not float away from the literal activity.\n\nReturn to the activity where attention returns. The person may complete the task, speak, or notice the other person watching. The question may remain open, settle plainly, or acquire a new meaning through actual experience. A maxim, revelation, or spiritual conclusion is neither required nor supplied by the style. The value of the passage lies in the person's effort to think through something that matters while continuing to live.\n\nIf another person is present, let them remain distinct. They may answer a practical question, misunderstand the silence, or offer something the focal character did not ask for. The inward question can coexist with ordinary exchange, and the other person need not know what is being thought.\n\nAt the active scene boundary, preserve the question's actual state, whether unfinished, settled, or no longer occupying attention; no lingering pressure or lesson is owed. The character may carry it into the next action or let it recede. The reflective construction should make the private concern legible without forcing the encounter to become a debate or a confession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse diary and reflective miscellany as the organizing form. Follow a situated recorder or an authorized reflective account through selected encounters, phrases, and observations. The recorder may be writing soon after the event or returning to it much later; keep those times distinct when they matter.\n\nLet the recorder choose what to include. Some details should be exact and unhurried: the order of a room, the sound of a kettle, a sentence someone said, the feel of a particular hour. Details may remain ordinary and uninterpreted according to the recorder's actual attention. Do not force every observation into a hidden pattern or a single emotional meaning.\n\nAllow an occasional detail to associate with private concern. The recorder may return to a phrase, a gesture, or a repeated task because it has begun to matter. The association should belong to the recorder's attention and language, not to an omniscient explanation. A printed diary entry is a composed artifact; it is not the same as the character's live nonspoken thought, and it may omit or arrange what was actually felt.\n\nKeep the social world active. The recorder may describe another person's request, silence, help, or misunderstanding while attributing any knowledge of that person's thought to actual sources and permitted narrator access. Let the entry preserve the difference between what happened, what was said, and what the recorder later understood.\n\nIf the passage includes more than one entry or reflective return, let returns follow the recorder's actual selections: some may develop a concern, while others can remain independent observations connected by the occasion or recording voice. A phrase or object may reappear with changed weight. The miscellany should feel like a person keeping a record over time, not like a sequence of forced epiphanies.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate psychological or urban fiction with psychological co-presence as the method. Follow an actual encounter in the supplied social setting: people together, a person awaiting someone, or a remembered exchange brought into the present setting. A street, workplace, home, bus, or public building can supply the scene when already established. Keep the public activity traceable even when private meaning becomes unsettled.\n\nLet the focal person's attention connect an ordinary feature of the setting to a private concern. The connection should arise from perception: a particular color, a familiar smell, the way someone waits, the sound of a gate, a phrase overheard. Describe the feature evenly and without forcing it to become symbolic for everyone present.\n\nKeep the other person's experience separate. They may notice something different, ask a practical question, or continue talking while the focal person drifts. If the narration alternates perspectives, do so only when authorized, and attribute each person's associations clearly; their content may differ or overlap without automatically becoming shared knowledge. Do not let the focal person's private meaning leak into the other person's speech or knowledge.\n\nLet the encounter develop through ordinary decisions: where to sit, whether to wait, what to buy, how to answer, whether to mention something. The psychological movement can run beside these decisions without needing to interrupt them. A moment of understanding may occur, but it need not be shared or resolved.\n\nIf the scene continues, preserve the actual setting and its established inhabitants, whether urban or otherwise. People who are present may pass, work, argue, help, or remain still according to their own lives. The private association should feel like one person's experience inside a shared world, not like a hidden truth the city is waiting to reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter the actual activity with clear orientation. Name the place and the people present, and make spatial changes easy to follow: who is standing, who is holding something, who turns toward the door. If an unfamiliar name, object, or practical term appears, present it in plain context when clarification is useful so the reader can continue without confusion.\n\nKeep dialogue attribution unmistakable. Let each speaker's words carry their own rhythm and diction, and do not flatten everyone into the same conversational distance. A pause, a practical action, or a direct reply can follow a line without requiring a fixed reaction beat.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around action, dialogue, and private response. When the focal person's attention drifts to an ordinary detail, let the association develop with the extent the actual experience needs, rather than reducing it to a labeled reaction. The detail should come from the scene: a cup left on a table, a repeated sound, the way someone checks a phone or adjusts a bag.\n\nLet the private resonance coexist with the conversation. The focal person may answer, ask, or remain quiet while the association continues. The other person need not know what is being felt, and the scene should not force a discovery, confession, or comic interruption to close the moment.\n\nIf the scene continues, keep any actual next action legible, while allowing waiting or private experience to continue without a new practical movement. The break can end on a practical movement, a spoken line, or a private shift in attention. Do not require a cliffhanger or a question. The light novel presentation should make the ordinary exchange readable while preserving its unhurried emotional weight.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nDevelop a concrete element from the scene or the narrator's established associations: a sound, object, activity, or phrase may recur where attention brings it back. Its meaning or felt weight can remain the same, change, or stay open. Keep its literal presence distinguishable from its private resonance without requiring a practical use or an advancing task. Use the active narrator's attention to develop the image, allowing sound or syntax to carry part of the feeling. When the character says aloud that they are tired or relieved or worried, let that plain statement sit beside the image rather than translating the image into a message.\n\nKeep the viewpoint's private associations distinct from the other person's response. A focal character may notice the way a kettle's whistle arrives exactly as an old friend asks about the same decision twice, but the friend need not know what the sound has come to mean. Develop the motif through the actual course of attention, without a fixed observation-association-change sequence. Physical alterations such as dimmer light or a slower movement follow actual causes and timing; a changed association alone does not produce them. The image can remain unresolved; it is enough for it to continue existing after the encounter without compelling the character to name what it meant.\n\nBalance unhurried practical activity with the character drama's request, acceptance, or withholding. Dialogue can stay calm and indirect, and another person's speech can be plainly useful while the viewpoint's attention keeps returning to the image at a different rhythm. Do not make everyone speak in the narrator's figurative register or require every phrase to be figuratively shaped. A single recurrence may be more effective than several, and the ordinary action that carries it may conclude or remain unfinished without becoming a symbol of that outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet reflection begin from a present particular rather than from a general statement about the past. A character filling an order, walking past a shut shop, or adjusting a curtain can be enough to bring back one specific earlier occasion: a phrase used then, a different weight of a familiar object, or a habit someone no longer performs. Keep the current scene physically legible while the remembered event crosses it. Do not let the memory replace the present action; the character can still answer a question, miss a turning, or finish the immediate task.\n\nDistinguish what the character knew then from what they understand now when the active narrator permits later knowledge. Avoid giving the remembered self a conclusion that belongs to the present teller. If the current person says a friendship did not last, the later outcome alone does not turn an earlier detail into proof of an inevitable ending. A supported connection may be considered from the later vantage without rewriting what was known then. Let warmth, relief, and ordinary pleasure stay available in both times. A character may recall laughing hard during an evening that later changed nothing and still find the laughter real.\n\nAllow unfinished attachment to surface through practical recurrence: the same meal is still made with too much salt, the same radio station still warns of rain, a name still sounds close when someone else says it. These recurrences do not need to be resolved or announced. When a character chooses to return to the relationship or let the matter lie, render the actual choice and any behavior it produces. The choice may remain private, with no visible change at the current boundary. The melancholy belongs to the active mind and need not drain the scene of other people's uncomplicated present lives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route as a situated later telling, not as a slide into essay. Render a particular earlier encounter through an identified participant account: what that person saw, handled, asked, or withheld. The participant may be the active narrator or someone whose account the narrator presents within actual source and perspective access. Keep the earlier experience immediate and concrete without making the active narrator an invented participant. If the narrator is also the later memoirist, clearly locate the later vantage through the composition itself—a calm qualifying sentence, a reference to what the person did not yet know, or the memory's placement beside a current activity. Do not invent a new memoir form; let the selected narrator and ownership decide whether the telling is spoken, written, or merely remembered.\n\nLink then and now through a specific object, phrase, or repeated practical activity. The later writer may recall the exact weight of a borrowed radio, the grain of a table, or how someone answered a question by first rinsing a glass. Let the later reflection confirm one element, correct another, or leave the earlier meaning open. The link should make the past event available, not prove that its outcome was inevitable. A relative's departure, an unanswered letter, or a childhood errand can retain its original confusion even when the teller now understands more.\n\nKeep required actual private experience in its authorized nonspoken channel at the narrative time in use. A composed or performed memoir is not the participant's or teller's complete live mind. The earlier participant's experience and the later teller's current feeling remain distinct where each is authorized. When the earlier self worries about a request while still performing an errand or listening to someone argue mildly across the room, give that inward experience its own time. Do not turn the whole scene into an explanation of the memoirist's theme. Other people in the remembered encounter may continue their own routines without sharing the narrator's later interpretation, and the present writing voice may be calm even when the remembered event was not.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details from the present scene and keep them in active use: a person's hand near a cup, the position of a chair, a particular way a door closes, the smell of rain on a jacket. Let one of these details carry the focal character's private association without repeating an explanation that adds nothing, while allowing later clarification when the experience calls for it. Write direct clauses that keep the action and speech plain, but do not shorten the inward experience merely to create brevity. If the character needs a longer thought to understand why they are reluctant to answer or relieved by a familiar voice, give that thought its necessary extent in the same economical register.\n\nLet omission leave something inferable rather than mysterious. When a permitted focal character leaves something unsaid, retain the actual inward connection that makes their experience legible. Another person's silence can remain partly unexplained where narrator access is limited; behavior or known context may support an interpretation without proving its source. A plain object should remain ordinary unless the scene has already given the character reason to register it differently. Do not make every held cup or unopened drawer into an unspoken message.\n\nKeep other people's speech and action sufficiently concrete that they are not reduced to prompts for the viewpoint's feeling. A friend can answer about money, a sister can say she cannot stay, and the narrative can record both without ornament. The refusal, acceptance, or misunderstanding remains causally legible through stated words and chosen actions. The scene may end with the errand finished or unfinished, without requiring every detail to be accounted for or every feeling named; an actual clear understanding may also be present.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the encounter in sustained scene-first paragraphs that move between visible action, attributed speech, perception, recollection, and the authorized character's private experience without previewing the passage's meaning. Start inside a concrete practical moment rather than with a thesis about the relationship. If a woman is repairing a window while her brother explains why he will not lend her the money, keep the repair, the argument, and her separate observations running in the same narrative movement. A direct emotional statement can appear when it fits the speaker; a character may say she is ashamed or say only that the train was late.\n\nLet ordinary details keep their practical identity. A cooler, a receipt, a broken umbrella, or the time a bus is due can matter because the character needs to use it, not because it must also stand for something else. Where a private association does form—the receipt reminds the viewpoint of the last shop she visited with an absent friend—give that association its own inward space while the world continues: someone else rings a bell, a child reads a label, the rain starts before the paragraph ends. Prevent action progress from becoming the justification for the scene; an errand can remain unfinished and still be dramatically significant.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's access and stay within it. Another person's decision is known only through the active narrator's actual access and established information; it need not remain partly unknown when a reliable source or authorized perspective makes it clear. The focal character may interpret their behavior, and that interpretation can be wrong. Use calm conversational distance where the characters have it, but do not require everyone to be reticent. The sentence rhythm can lengthen for reflection and tighten for practical activity, keeping the ordinary exchange legible while private concern develops underneath it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse only the actual relationship premise already supplied. Make the relationship legible through the details the characters share over time: a greeting, a place they meet, a task one does for the other, a phrase that turns out to belong to them rather than to everyone else. Let relevant details appear naturally and recur where the shared history brings them back, in unchanged or changed circumstances, such as the same order placed on a different day or the same key left on a different table. The recurrence is a consequence of shared history, not an announcement that the relationship is now closer or worse.\n\nSeparate the two people's experience of the familiar detail. One character may notice that the other always stirs the coffee before handing it over and privately read that as attention; the other may do it from ingrained habit or nervousness. The viewpoint's association can be strong, tender, jealous, or uncertain while the partner answers in a plain or preoccupied way. Allow recognition and comfort to be genuine, but do not require similarity in feeling as proof of connection. If the supplied premise includes rivalry or difficult attachment, render any actual distance or competition through the particular people's private experience and whatever behavior occurs, without requiring a visible expression or making it a universal attitude.\n\nKeep the report unhurried and even rather than emotionally inflating every callback. A familiar object can be described with its practical dimensions intact, and calm dialogue can remain calm. Let the supplied premise matter through actual feeling, private association, and any choices that occur, without requiring a new emotional consequence. A person may act in a particular way: one waits, one asks, one avoids a question, one returns the item, one accepts a smaller favor. The response may resolve the immediate beat or leave it open. Inward experience belongs to the authorized viewpoint; the other character does not automatically know what a recurring preference has come to mean.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen the scene with a deliberate compact declaration that positions an ordinary act in a remembered history: who repaired the door after the flood, which relative paid the taxi, who always phoned before the last bus. Then expand into the actual concrete activity without making it mock-heroic. Preserve the door's actual nature and function in the supplied world; an ordinary repair may involve buying a hinge or waiting for a neighbor's drill without requiring an error. Keep the declaration's weight in the recurring public phrase or remembered consequence, while the action retains its actual scale, difficulty, and available means rather than being made manageable for the retelling.\n\nDistinguish the community's remembered designation from the focal person's private experience. Others may call a woman dependable because she met the last train every night for a month, but her own association can include exhaustion, resentment, or the smell of the station after rain and have nothing to do with dependability. Let actual dialogue agree with the public account, qualify it, or address ordinary business; it need not interrupt the retelling. For example, the person named may say she only did it because no one else would, or answer an elevated introduction by asking for a receipt. This is not parody; it is the ordinary life the retelling carries.\n\nUse recurrence where a remembered phrase or deed returns through actual attention or shared history. Its application and meaning may stay the same, change, or remain unsettled. A phrase about arriving before the shops close can first describe a practical habit and later return when someone decides to accept or refuse a request. The new use need not resolve the first meaning. Keep the teller's access consistent with the active perspective. If the narration is not from an omniscient chronicler, the account should report only what the viewpoint would know or what the scene can show; private resonance remains private even when public remembrance continues to name the act.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narration by having the authorized narrator state a particular observation about closeness, attention, or obligation in their own terms, then move to a concrete actual, remembered, or explicitly imagined encounter with a person or practical task. Let the ordinary details of that encounter remain unhurried and exactly described: a shared meal, a repeated errand, the feel of a familiar object, or a sentence someone did not finish.\n\nDo not make the encounter illustrate the observation by turning strange, portentous, or decisive. The case may support the proposition, qualify it, leave it open, or return the narrator to a question they thought they had settled. After the encounter, return to the starting observation and show what the return to it now feels like: the proposition may be confirmed, revised, or held more tentatively than before. The return can be incomplete, and the reflection may stop while both meanings remain available. Where practical activity or another person is present, preserve their actual continuation, stillness, or response alongside the narrator's reasoning, so the essayistic movement stays grounded in particular people rather than becoming a detached meditation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise action blocks and attributed speech that establish the observable conditions of an encounter without letting a quiet gesture stand as proof of emotional distance, closeness, or concealment. Scene headings mark changes of location or time, and cuts may juxtapose actual occasions when the sequence needs them. Keep the practical details concrete and evenly presented: a person arriving with wet shoes, coffee left cooling, a door held open, a task finished slowly.\n\nLet the public scene remain unhurried even when nothing visible resolves. The characters may ask for something, withhold it, accept it, misunderstand it, or choose to change it through what they say and do, while the public exchange remains legible in the action and dialogue. Preserve required live private experience separately from performance or voice-over, clearly attributed as nonspoken interiority for the authorized viewpoint without automatically supplying it to other characters through reader access. An unchanged physical scene can accompany extensive inward movement, so the action blocks do not need to signal every private association. Do not add camera commands, probative close-ups, or a required final image.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared playable space to carry an ordinary exchange. Stage clear entrances, exits, blocking, objects, and practical business, and let the relationship between what characters say and what they do in that space move the public scene forward. The action need not become demonstrative: a person can fold a cloth, wait at a table, put a cup down, or remain seated while another speaks, and the supplied context gives such movement its weight.\n\nA pause or familiar movement may matter through what has already been established, but it does not prove concealment, longing, or emotional distance. Speakers may ask for something and be refused, accept it, misunderstand it, or change their minds without announcing the change. Preserve required nonspoken interiority separately from asides and monologues; use the permitted hidden or visible interiority convention for private experience, while an aside or monologue is reserved for performance with its own audience and purpose when sanctioned. Keep offstage information and pending exchanges available where supported, and let the active scene boundary govern the stop, including within an unanswered request, continuing private experience, or ordinary activity. A result or interruption may occur but is not owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow a permitted focal mind through live perception, bodily sensation, remembered words, private concern, imagined alternatives, and interruption as they arise. Let repeated particulars orient the associative movement: a familiar smell, the texture of a chair, a phrase from an earlier conversation, or the sound of another person moving in the next room can shift attention to a memory or an unresolved question. Keep the movement unhurried rather than forcing attention back to a task at fixed intervals or demanding a crisis.\n\nMark the boundary between live event, memory, and imagined alternative so the reader can distinguish them. An actual person may continue speaking or remain still according to the external scene, not according to how long the thought lasts. Intersperse the inward movement with enough practical continuity to keep the present encounter legible, but let the stream carry ordinary associations without explaining them as hidden meanings or symptoms. Required private experience belongs to its authorized mind; other people know it only through their actual established access or information, not automatically through narration. The scene may end while an external event remains unfinished or a private question remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the exchange through readable sender-attributed messages, using only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the current sequence. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write and send immediately, another may type, delete, and wait; one may answer a practical question directly while postponing another subject. Do not insert an interface event merely to add subtext.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text, distinct from live private experience. Its storage and visibility follow the actual interface and narrator access; it is not delivered to another participant merely because the reader sees it. A visible message may acquire a recipient's private resonance through its timing, phrasing, or relation to an earlier exchange, but the transcript does not certify that interpretation; the recipient may respond plainly, misread the message, or let it pass without addressing it. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, for the authorized narrator or scene grounding. Another message need not settle the recipient's feeling or make an ordinary conversational distance into proof of estrangement.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting passages and clear dialogue attribution to place the actual encounter. Give practical details plainly: where the characters are, what they are doing, what they notice or reach for. Keep background or expression cues as purposeful references for the illustrated rhythm, not as mandatory labels or automatic indicators of what someone feels or conceals.\n\nLet the focal narration or inner response share the rhythm with the public dialogue. When attention turns from a visible cue to a private association, render the actual inward experience fully for the authorized viewpoint: a memory, a repeated phrase, a bodily sensation, or a concern that remains unfinished. Keep the cue's literal status distinct from its felt significance: the other person's expression, a familiar object, or a pause in conversation may acquire a private weight for one character without becoming a confirmed message from another. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, romance, or images to the presentation. The scene may linger on an ordinary detail without forcing a changed interpretation or a fixed alternation between dialogue and response.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments by keeping what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover a relevant detail or previous exchange briefly only when it helps the current interval make sense to the reader: a repeated phrase, an unfinished errand, a familiar object, or a promise mentioned earlier. Preserve the actual events and stable conditions already established, so a later recurrence cannot rewrite them.\n\nLet a private concern persist, settle, or return unchanged across installments. An ordinary particular may carry that concern without acquiring a promised hidden explanation, a supernatural cause, an emotional milestone, or a fresh mystery. The interval can close after practical work, company, or inward reflection; a task may remain unfinished and a request unanswered without any new local change or advance in the character's situation. Use earned developments when they occur, but do not make every installment a complete miniature chapter or force a cliffhanger. Recurrence deepens the ongoing lived continuity rather than restarting the same suspicion each time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nCenter the scene on an actual relationship matter involving asking, acceptance, refusal, admission or omission. The person may raise it, privately consider it, withhold it or leave it unspoken. Where terms are actually offered or discussed, keep what is requested, acknowledged or declined clear along with its practical or social constraints; no public step is required to make private strain meaningful. Let the focal character's private reception of those terms develop in its actual relation to the offer, whether it complicates acceptance or coexists with a clear answer. Strain need not come from a misunderstanding; it can come from knowing the request is reasonable, wanting to grant it, and being unable to do so cleanly.\n\nFollow any actual after-effect of exposure while allowing ordinary conduct to continue unchanged. The omission of a reply, the retreat into a practical task, the particular comfort a person accepts, or the way a later topic reopens the original subject can carry emotional weight when those responses belong to the person. Do not require the admission to produce relief, confrontation, or a repaired bond. A character may remain in a state of wanting with no one at fault.\n\nUse the Austen profile's attention to received social meaning to give one exchange several active meanings: a debt may also be a claim, a courtesy may also be an accusation, an offer of help may also announce dependency. Keep the private interpretation owned and grounded in the person's actual sources and permitted access. Other motives can be known, partly known or uncertain according to the active contract; style does not reduce that access. Do not let the narrator's ironic distance cancel the felt urgency; where free indirect thought fits the selected person and perspective, let it render the actual feeling without making narrative polish an automatic verdict.\n\nKeep the turbulent treatment specific to the relationship and occasion. It may appear as repeated private rehearsal before speaking, abrupt practical action after a painful line, or a long thought broken by the effort of maintaining composure. The situation the story supplies determines how much speech the character can manage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{#setvar::NPabakab}}♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nResolve the submitted material as a piece of a shared scene rather than a complete transcript. Acknowledge what the player's character already said or did, and let the next section of narration show the NPC's conduct in answer: a reply may be polite, practical, evasive, or partial according to that character's situation and obligations. Give dialogue unmistakable attribution so the reader can follow who is claiming what.\n\nKeep the NPC's private interpretation distinct from other fictional participants' knowledge, including the player-controlled character; reader access does not itself communicate it within the scene. The character may privately understand an offer as a test, consider a request as a new obligation, or feel relief at what they infer to be a refusal without making that inference an established fact. The narration can show only what belongs to the active point of view and permission. Do not state the player's character's inner life without authorization, and do not assume that courtesy proves warmth or silence proves resentment.\n\nLet the NPC's actual behavior and private experience develop or continue the exchange without a required changed response. A changed arrangement, a half-finished task, a recollection returned to later, or a practical adjustment can accompany a received social meaning when such an effect actually occurs; continued ordinary activity can also stand. The continuation point should follow naturally from what has happened; it need not ask a question, force a decision, or freeze the scene on a cliffhanger.\n\nUse the Austen profile's attention to small social facts: who sits, who waits, who pays, who accepts an offer, and who is left with a changed obligation. Keep the social particulars and actual private experience distinguishable in their real order. Feeling may precede, accompany or follow observable conduct and receives its needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel. {{/setvar}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse intimate domestic narration to follow a personal exchange through the actual shared use of a place or object. A visit, meal, accommodation or ordinary preparation can make an offer concrete: room at a table, a borrowed item, time given to another person's preference. Preserve the supplied space, relationships and ownership rather than inventing a household or family hierarchy.\n\nPlace what someone offers, accepts or declines beside the meaning it has for each authorized account. A courtesy may be intended and received as simple consideration, read as special recognition or matter little to a person occupied elsewhere. Keep observed conduct, inferred intention and known motive distinct under the active narrator's permissions. Changes in an object's use can support a contextual reading but do not prove a feeling, and an unchanged arrangement can remain personally meaningful.\n\nLet the domestic activity and conversation keep their actual course. An accepted task may be completed, remain pending or be reconsidered for a supported reason; no physical result or corrected impression is owed. Give private feeling and judgment their needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel, independently of whether the person names them aloud. The active boundary can fall within ordinary use of the space, continued thought or an unanswered offer without a prescribed welcome, resentment, farewell or lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the material facts of the exchange in hard, concrete terms: the weight of a bag someone offers to carry, the cost of a service, the state of a room after a difficult visit, the physical fatigue of waiting, or the exact handling of money or goods. Use direct clauses and let the thing itself carry the first meaning. Do not describe the item only to catalog damage; its wear, price, or utility is relevant because it enters a decision between people.\n\nThen give the owned interpretation room to differ from those facts. A person may feel obliged by a small expense another person finds negligible. A worn surface may register as failure to one character and as familiarity to another. Let the reader see both the shared physical reality and the private construction placed upon it. Keep the hard style from hardening the character: direct feeling and a developed thought can remain, especially when the character needs time to reason from a concrete detail to a social conclusion.\n\nUse compressed transitions to move from one consequence to another without substituting abrasiveness for agency. A refusal may leave a practical arrangement unfinished; an acceptance may create a new task. Follow any actual response to what was handled or exchanged while allowing ordinary activity or another concern to continue without a new reaction. Ground abrasive or rough speech in the actual speaker; a person who is blunt may still be considerate, and a polite phrase may carry an unyielding practical demand.\n\nKeep Austen's sense of social position attached to material circumstance. Who owns or owes, who can afford to wait, who must work, and who may accept a convenience can form a concrete part of the relationship without exhausting its private or emotional meaning. Do not assume that rough conditions prove neglect, that expensive help proves motive, or that hardship requires a breakdown. The scene may leave a person with unresolved feeling and an unchanged situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nSelect the actual documents through which this exchange will be told: a note may be needed immediately, a letter may offer the space for formal consideration, and a record may be written later after the practical outcome is known. Give each document an author, a recipient or intended purpose, and a moment of composition. The form and timing affect what the writer includes. A request made verbally may be summarized in a letter; an offer may be accepted in person but recorded differently for a third party.\n\nLet the wording carry social meaning at the level of the document's form. Address, signature, length, repetition, and what a writer chooses to omit all belong to the relationship and the situation. A courteous sentence can be sincere or guarded. A document may be polished precisely because the writer feels the matter sharply. Do not use the document's composed tone to announce that its author is honest or dishonest. Keep the reader able to compare a later account with the earlier account without an omniscient narrator resolving each difference.\n\nPreserve private experience that no written record contains. A character may think more freely during or after composition than the document states; a letter may serve the writer's purpose while concealing the writer's most painful knowledge. Render any actual live response at its own place in events under the active narrator's permissions, without requiring one after every record. Preserve the selected Visible or Hidden nonspoken channel; this private experience is distinct from a letter, draft or note even when their wording overlaps.\n\nLet gaps and material traces work as narrative. A missing reply, a note found late, a phrase repeated from an earlier letter, or a record that now appears to have been written for a different reason can all change what the characters understand. Follow any actual effect on relationships or obligations while allowing confirmation or an unchanged arrangement; a document does not owe a reveal or alteration. The sequence may end with a consequence still being absorbed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nChoose particular acts of care that fit the characters and world: a meal prepared from a known preference, a practical accommodation made without being asked, a task removed from someone's morning, or a minor courtesy repeated often enough to show attention. Present the act in enough concrete specificity that the reader can enjoy it. Who made room, paid attention, remembered the detail, or adjusted a plan?\n\nLet the recipient's private experience include direct pleasure. This may appear as relief, appetite, the comfort of a familiar object, a slower breath, or the wish to prolong the interval. Allow the character to notice and enjoy the care without immediately converting it into suspicious analysis or an obligation to repay. Sincerity in the text is compatible with the character not understanding the giver's full intent.\n\nPreserve personality and privacy within the comfort. A person may accept help while still wanting something else, express thanks in an economical way, or remain quiet because the ease is enough. Do not require a confession, emotional repair, or a moment of stated gratitude. Disagreement can remain unfinished elsewhere in the relationship; the comfortable exchange need not resolve it or pretend it has vanished.\n\nLet the social detail carry the Austen profile without turning the scene into arbitration. A service performed in deference, an offer accepted as familiar privilege, or a small gift that marks an unspoken position can all coexist with genuine warmth. Keep the telling aligned with the active narrator's distance and diction, so that the pleasure remains the characters' rather than an imposed authorial coziness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with the actual act or exchange: a question put, a request made, a condition stated, a note delivered, or a practical arrangement proposed. Keep the opening close to the people involved and to the words they would use. Then widen outward through social context, history, or situation that the active narrator is legitimately able to supply: the material position of the household, the previous conduct of the family, the reputation attached to an offer, or the difference between a public account and a private one.\n\nReturn from the wider view to the people in the scene. The widened context should make the reader reread the opening exchange, not replace it. A person's self-description may now appear too generous, too modest, or at odds with established conduct, but the narrator should not state a final truth unless the active narration permits such judgment. Free indirect thought may carry the focal character's assessment; keep it owned and in that character's range. Other voices keep their own registers rather than speaking in the narrator's polished syntax.\n\nUse deliberate transitions and paragraph movement rather than a patchwork of commentary. A change to history, reputation, or practical circumstance should be motivated by what the scene needs the reader to see. Ceremony, correspondence, or the contrast between a public obligation and a private wish may organize a passage when the story supplies it. Do not import such apparatus merely to make the scene feel formal.\n\nKeep the emotional situation available during the widening. The social frame may explain why an offer matters, but the private response to that offer must still be registered. After returning to the characters, allow the exchange to continue or pause; the scene need not conclude with a judgment or a lesson from the wider context.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen by establishing who is telling, who is listening, and what present concern makes the telling worth attempting. The teller may be trying to persuade, delay, entertain, or explain a decision, but the story they tell should remain a specific social incident: an offer made, a request refused, a visit misunderstood, or a promise kept under strain. Let the teller's selection and emphasis show what they believe matters. A generous account of a rival can be sincere while omitting something the teller never knew.\n\nRecount the embedded incident through attributed acts and wording. Keep the teller's received social meaning distinct from the original participants' knowledge. If the teller was not present, show what they learned from others and what they merely infer. A courteous phrase can be reported accurately while its motive remains uncertain. When the teller adds a judgment, let it belong to them: they may call a silence prudent, an offer generous, or a refusal proud, without the statement alone certifying that reading; established facts, known motives or permitted narrator judgment can support it.\n\nKeep the outer occasion recoverable while the telling continues, without a required cutback or premature ending. A listener may ask about a concrete detail, agree without changing their plan, or continue a separate task. The telling does not need to produce a lesson, expose vanity, or force a change of mind. Let the repeated phrase or object connect the two levels only where it actually helps the reader see what the teller is doing. Preserve the active narrator's identity and access independently of the teller, and actual private experience in its selected nonspoken channel separately from performance. Keep what the teller understood then distinct from what they understand now, and let the scene end where the present relationship stands, not where the story resolves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse developmental realism to follow how a particular person's social understanding has been acquired across actual occasions. Establish an earlier way of reading or handling an exchange through concrete evidence: a remembered visit, a family saying, a past refusal, or the practical conditions that once made a certain courtesy necessary. Do not summarize the history as a lesson. Show it through the person's present attention to a comparable offer, request, or omission.\n\nLet the current occasion test that earlier understanding without requiring it to be wrong. A character may recognize that a once-feared silence now means something else, or that a familiar act of deference no longer fits the relationship. Where the selected person and perspective permit free indirect judgment, keep its owner clear: the character's evaluation, not the narrator's verdict. Preserve the material conditions that shaped the earlier understanding. Money, family obligation, reputation, and physical circumstance can explain why a person learned to read a gesture one way without making that reading foolish.\n\nLet actual words and conduct give the comparison substance; a supported stated realization or developed private reflection can accompany them without being required. The character may act on the older understanding, hesitate, or adjust without announcing the change. Learning through experience, observation, or instruction is enough; no teacher, humiliation, or lost certainty is required. End the scene where the current relationship stands, not where the character has completed a new understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere old authority bears on access, help, or intimacy, give its present terms material specificity. A person may be admitted to a room, granted an introduction, or offered assistance because of a family connection, an old obligation, or a reputation that no longer matches their present life. Show what the authority actually permits: who may enter, what may be asked, what must be returned. Show how the arrangement operates in this encounter; an established general rule can be stated when it helps make those actual terms clear.\n\nDistinguish what someone can offer from how it is received. A favor may be granted with courtesy and accepted with resentment, or offered with genuine care and received as a claim. The narration may use measured irony to qualify a flattering account, but it must not invent hypocrisy, seduction, or a corrupt bond. Let the atmosphere change how the encounter is experienced: a familiar room may feel like an inheritance, a polite phrase like a condition. Keep the private interpretation owned by the focal character, not broadcast to the cast.\n\nPreserve the difference between permitted knowledge and actual motive. A character may know that a door was opened for them without knowing why, or may suspect a motive without being able to state it. Let the scene end where the present relationship stands, with the inherited terms still concrete but not resolved into a moral verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-character_drama-03",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Character Drama · 03/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == character_drama}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Character Drama. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 3 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-character_drama; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPabakak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a source-marked participant account to carry the scene. Establish who is speaking or writing, to whom, and for what present purpose. The account should concern a personally meaningful exchange: an offer, a visit, a refusal, or a moment of help that the participant actually witnessed or received. Distinguish firsthand acts and words from later reports. If the participant learned something from another person, mark that source; if they infer a motive, let the inference belong to them.\n\nKeep the active narrator's person and permissions independent of the testifying participant; frame the account within the selected narration when they are different. Preserve the participant's identity beyond the testifying role. They are not only a witness; they have their own practical concerns, affections, and limits. The account may be composed for a friend, a family member, an official, or a private record, and its purpose should shape what is included. A courteous phrase may be reported accurately while its intention remains uncertain. The participant may state what was said and done without claiming to know what it meant.\n\nPreserve live private experience beyond the communicated account. The participant may feel more than they say, or understand something only after the exchange. No interrogation, confession, or corrected prejudice is required. Let the scene end where the account's purpose and the participant's present situation actually stand.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse psychological realism to follow an actual encounter in event order. Keep who is present and what has been said or offered recoverable while the focal person forms or retains an impression in the actual order of private and public events. Keep the exchange recoverable: the reader should know what was actually said and done, even when the focal person's interpretation is partial or mistaken. An offer, greeting, or omission may receive an owned interpretation that the next response supports, qualifies, or leaves alone.\n\nUse free indirect evaluative language only where the selected narrator and access permit it. The evaluative words belong to the focal person, not to an omniscient judge. A character may think a silence deliberate, a courtesy cold, or a refusal kind, without the thought alone certifying the reading; actual facts, known motives and supported narrator judgment can confirm it. Developed private feeling is welcome: attention, uncertainty, relief, resentment, or affection may occupy the mind while the exchange continues. No required shame, restraint, or sudden insight is imposed.\n\nLet the active boundary fall within the present encounter as well as after it, including continued thought or an unanswered exchange. The focal person may leave with an impression intact, altered, or unresolved. The narration does not need to summarize what the exchange meant or correct the character's reading.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic and nonlinear telling to place several source-owned readings of a shared occasion beside one another. Choose an actual offer, visit, refusal, or object that more than one permitted mind or attributed account can address. Mark time and source beneath the presentation order so the reader can reconstruct what happened even when the accounts are not chronological. A later account may recall an earlier moment, or an earlier account may record what a participant anticipated then, without treating a later outcome as already known.\n\nLet each reading belong to its owner. One person may call a silence respectful, another cold, another simply convenient. The narration does not need to choose among them or supply a final superior narrator. Meanings can agree or differ according to context, and a difference need not be a misunderstanding. Preserve each person's actual knowledge: what they witnessed, what they were told, and what they infer. A courteous phrase may be reported accurately by one account and interpreted differently by another.\n\nKeep the shared occasion concrete enough to hold the accounts together. The object, room, or exchange should remain recognizable across the different tellings. No shared mind, compulsory misunderstanding, or false account is required. Keep the occasion legible through juxtaposition while allowing an account to remain unfinished. Established facts can be confirmed without requiring a final voice to settle every social meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Light Novel\n\nOrient the reader to who is present and what has been said or offered before the exchange develops. Use clear spatial and temporal transitions so the reader can follow where the characters are and the actual temporal relation between occasions without inventing a precise duration. When an unfamiliar context, name, or practical arrangement matters, present it through actual observation, discussion, or narration rather than a detached explanation.\n\nUse paragraph breaks when action, speech, or owned response needs separation. The order of breaks should follow the actual pace of the participants' experience, not a fixed template. A long private thought may occupy its own paragraph when the character's reasoning needs it; a quick reply may follow a gesture without a break. Let ordinary speakers remain ordinary. No automatic joke, romance beat, or completed decision is required.\n\nKeep the social meaning of the exchange developing through what the characters actually say, do, and privately understand. A courteous offer may be received with relief, suspicion, or indifference, and that reception belongs to the focal character. End the scene where the present situation stands, with the exchange legible but not forced to a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet one source-available phrase, offered object, repeated sound, or remembered gesture organize an actual social exchange. Develop it through ordered syntax and sound rather than treating it as portable commentary: a courtesy may return as a half-echo, a material detail may gather weight because the focal person keeps noticing it, or a word may alter when a different speaker uses it. Each repetition must come from an actual occurrence or authorized association, not from imposed poetic decoration.\n\nHold social inference to its owner. A composed sentence may place the explanatory judgment close to free indirect speech, but the writer should make it clear which person interprets the past or another character's behavior. Narration can sustain sympathy and irony without converting it into proof of hidden motive. Where the perspective gives access, render the private experience behind a withheld or offered response at the extent the scene requires; another character receives what actual communication, prior knowledge and permitted access provide, and may form the same or a different reading; reader exposure to private prose is not itself communication.\n\nKeep plain clauses as structural contrast. Lyrical construction may shape attention to an offer, rejection, family expectation, or practical decision, but it does not require every speaker to use the narrator's figurative register. Dialogue keeps the specific roles, habits, and social reach of its speakers. The closing passage may let the developing image settle in a person's continuing thought, but it must not enforce a revelation, corrected vanity, or ornamental verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nAnchor reflection in a specific earlier personal exchange: an offer, greeting, apology, gift, promise, or familiar place already supplied by the story. Let a present perception recall it rather than introducing a generalized mood. When reporting the remembered occasion, distinguish what remained unsaid then from the present person's later knowledge and present feeling, and preserve what the past participant actually knew without revising that earlier state. Any broader context available to the active narrator remains distinct from the participant's earlier knowledge.\n\nAllow the current scene to sit beside the recollection. The remembered meaning may still hold, may have become unavailable, or may be touched by later events, but the writer need not produce a corrected interpretation, reveal a betrayal, or make the present conversation supply a neat closure. Affection, regret, embarrassment, continued trust, or ordinary pleasure are equally usable, and a warm current moment may remain warm while still carrying awareness of absence or altered circumstance.\n\nUse social precision rather than elegiac abstraction. A mannered offer or a refusal can matter because of the actual household, income, expectation, or family relationship around it, and each speaker's current language remains distinct. The narrator may let a familiar object or polite phrase carry the association, but the private account belongs to the authorized focal person. End where the encounter and recollection genuinely arrive; an unrevised attachment or unfinished question is an acceptable stopping point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite as a modern memoir already being composed. Preserve the active narrator's person, role and access independently of the composer and earlier participant. Establish the composer's later position and the material from which the remembered exchange is reconstructed: direct memory, another person's account, a letter, a practical result, or a surviving record. Keep each source's limits visible. When later evidence confirms, qualifies, or contradicts what the original participant believed, present that difference as a later acquisition rather than a correction performed inside the earlier scene.\n\nReconstruct the central personal exchange through the words, conduct, and knowledge then available. An offer, omission, polite claim, or family decision may carry several meanings depending on the hearer's social position, and the memoirist can note without concluding which meaning was intended. Preserve the difference between the later narrator's understanding and the earlier person's actual response; do not make the past participant prescient or inject retrospective motives into reported memory.\n\nInclude private experience beyond the evidentiary record. Even when the memoir uses documents, preserve authorized earlier lived experience and present remembering in their actual temporal positions. Both remain actual nonspoken experience in the selected channel beyond the memoir's composed selection, rather than being replaced by the record. Speech remains individual and attached to actual participants. The memoir may end with an open question, qualified account, or stable attachment, but it must not demand exile, corrected self-image, or a conclusive lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Minimalist Prose\n\nState the social transaction directly: what is asked or offered, what is said, what is withheld, and what the other person does with it. Use exact detail and direct clauses so the reader can see the arrangement, but omit secondary explanation once the act carries the relation. A refusal may be given through a small practical movement; a sincere courtesy may need no commentary. Do not select words merely to imply more than the scene supports.\n\nWhen the selected perspective allows it, render the character's private feeling and understanding at the length the matter requires. The fact that a meaning is already evident from outward behavior does not permit deleting lived interiority. A person may accept an offer and quietly dislike what accepting costs, or may speak politely and privately believe the compliment. Interiority need not be withheld to create an inference, and it must not be replaced by a prompt for the reader to notice.\n\nKeep clauses economical, but preserve causality. Omission may leave a detail inferable; it must not remove the connection between an earlier debt, proposal, or family pressure and the present response. Let dialogue remain individual. Irony belongs to a specific narrator or participant when used, and no character's private view becomes an automatic explanation of another person's motive.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove naturally among the events that make up a personal encounter: dialogue, ordinary handling of objects, perception of the room, recollection, and the authorized person's private thought. Let each movement arise from the scene rather than from narration that stops to explain a theme. An offer may be answered while someone signs a paper, pours tea, remembers an earlier request, or registers the other person's tone; these details can coexist without interrupting the conversation.\n\nUse social interpretation as an owned act. When free indirect style or measured irony appears, keep it attributable to the focal person or the narrator's permitted judgment rather than spreading it across every mind. A first reading of another person's behavior may persist, be qualified by the next exchange, or belong only to the observer while the observed person is concerned about something else. Simple kindness and unchanged relations remain valid endings, as do unresolved practical decisions.\n\nPreserve distinct private and public levels. The narration may reveal what a character wants, fears, or mistakes about the exchange while the other party acts on their actual knowledge and permitted access, whether complete, partial or different. The writer may pause for memory or association when the character's attention actually moves there, and may return to present conduct or remain within that experience at the active boundary without a lesson, reversal or emotional summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect one supported exchange or remembered occasion that belongs to the actual supplied relationship: a familiar phrase, an offer, a first-time act, a returned object, or a change in ordinary routine. Build the scene around that event's actual significance within this bond, including an established meaning that persists unchanged, not around a generic roster of recognized fanfiction conventions. Let the relationship be legible through particular history, shared language, or a repeated small obligation rather than announced through type labels.\n\nDistinguish what is done or said from each authorized private meaning. One person may intend simple care, the other may receive it as unusual attention, rightful expectation, or another feeling. Accurate recognition and uncomplicated affection are as valid as delayed understanding. Do not force a mutual realization, a debt, a confession, or a payoff when the supplied history does not require one. Preserve the involvement of people outside the relationship as independent actors with their own practical concerns rather than witnesses arranged to highlight the pair.\n\nLet recurrence do the emotional work. A familiar phrase may mean differently because of an earlier scene, or a repeated offer may become significant through the actual circumstances of its repetition. The writer should return to the relationship's established particulars when they become salient, without converting them into mandatory callbacks at every exchange. The scene may close in easier contact, continued need, silence, or ordinary practical resumption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nWrite ordinary personal life with the cadence of a chronicle. Open with a deliberate declaration that establishes a remembered favor, refusal, gift, marriage prospect, or contribution, then move into concrete particulars of the actual occasion. Keep the scale human: an offer of help is not a battle, a breached understanding is not an oath of doom, and family obligation retains its domestic weight. Use compact sentences where the retelling names an act before showing how it was carried out.\n\nBind recurrence to an actual established deed or phrase. A designation such as a nickname, quoted line, or remembered object can return for familiar remembrance, renewed emphasis or a supported new application within the community's actual history. Do not attach ornamental epithets to every entrance. Let the public retelling keep its own opinion; a character may be remembered as generous although the private account shows reluctance or an unspoken motive, and the writer may present that contrast without forcing debunking or heroic correction.\n\nAllow the scene's own speech and authorized private thought to interrupt and complicate the chronicle. Dialogue may answer an elevated formulation with the practical concern of someone still managing money, work, or family. A plain line can meet the retold words and make the recollection human. The account may end with business concluded, another small act available to be remembered, or a reputation left unsettled rather than proclaimed legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nOpen with a definite social estimate that the narrator can actually hold: a claim about what a person's consideration, reliability, or manner of receiving an offer means. The claim may be the narrator's own, a community judgment, or a provisional reading of the current relationship. State it in terms precise enough to be tested, not as a general observation about people.\n\nExamine one established interpersonal case against that estimate. Use the actual supplied scene or relationship history: a particular offer, omission, request, apology, or arrangement whose wording and circumstances are known. Let the case show what the estimate can explain, what it cannot reach, and where the person's private interpretation or later conduct qualifies it. Do not invent a counterexample merely to correct the claim. The case may support it, complicate it, or leave it unresolved.\n\nReturn to the original terms. The closing reflection should speak again of consideration, reliability, or the meaning of the exchange, now carrying the weight of the examined case. The narrator may retain the estimate, narrow its scope, or acknowledge what remains uncertain. Preserve the active narrator's role and permitted private extent; the reflection belongs to that narrator and does not grant access to other minds. Ordinary practical life may continue around the reflection. The claim, examination or return can remain unfinished across replies, and actual circumstances govern whether action precedes, accompanies or follows it. Preserve actual private experience beyond the stated estimate and any access already granted by the active contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in readable action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use scene headings when location or time changes, and keep each block to what can be seen or heard. Let a courtesy, request, or reply meet a visible or audible act: a cup set down, a coat taken, a door held, a pause before an answer. The act gives the exchange material weight but does not certify what the speaker means. A polite gesture can accompany genuine care, reluctance, or an unrelated private concern.\n\nLet dialogue carry the actual wording of offers, omissions, and requests. Do not require wit or polished speech. A plain line can be considerate; a formal line can be sincere. Context and prior knowledge govern what a character may infer from another's words or conduct. The audience may see more than any character knows, but the scene should not turn a gesture into a truth indicator.\n\nPreserve required private experience in its selected nonspoken channel, clearly attributed to the character and separate from authorized voice-over. A voice-over is performed speech with its own audience and purpose; it does not replace the character's live thought. Allow the scene to end on a pending request, an unanswered question, or ordinary continuation. No revelation, confession, or completed relationship turn is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Stage Play\n\nKeep entrances, exits, positions, props, and lines coherent so that who can see, hear, or reach what remains clear. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character may speak while folding a letter, setting a table, or moving toward a door. Select the business that belongs to the immediate practical situation; do not require a new gesture for every line.\n\nLet a spoken offer or judgment meet conduct that can be staged. A courteous act may accompany agreement, conceal disagreement, or express a concern the speaker does not name. Context and permitted knowledge govern what another character may infer. The audience may understand more than the characters, but no gesture independently certifies hidden motive.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, clearly attributed to the character. A monologue or aside is performed speech with its own dramatic purpose and audience; it does not replace the character's actual private thought. Allow the scene to end on a pending exchange, an ordinary exit, or a practical task resumed. No confession, reversal, or completed interpretation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet the actual associations of one authorized mind set the order. A phrase heard in the present exchange may lead to an expected reply, a remembered occasion, a bodily sensation, or a private word. Move among these without losing the distinction between what is happening, what is remembered, and what is imagined. Keep the present exchange recoverable through enough concrete detail: the other person's wording, the room, the practical task, or a line the focal person actually spoke.\n\nLet a social interpretation form through association. The mind may connect a courtesy to an earlier omission, a tone to a family obligation, or an offer to what it would cost. The interpretation may be accurate or uncertain. Do not require an interruption, a misreading, or a sudden insight. The mind may return to the same phrase several times without discovering a changed room or a hidden motive.\n\nPreserve developed private experience and individual minds. The associative movement belongs to the focal character only; it does not grant access to what another person thinks. Event-based time continues around the mental movement. Returning attention may find the exchange still underway, the other person waiting, or the practical moment unchanged. No completed judgment or relationship turn is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent the exchange through sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write quickly and plainly, another may revise, delay, or send a formally worded reply. A formal message can be sincere; a plain one can be considerate. Do not treat politeness or delay as proof of intent.\n\nKeep sent, delivered, read, and understood content separate. Sending a message does not itself give its recipient the content, and reading or other actual access must have a source. Preserve what they already know or learn through another route rather than treating an unread marker as proof of ignorance. A read receipt does not mean the reader has accepted the request or understood the offer. Existing knowledge and prior relationship history remain available to each character according to their own access. Preserve meaningful private experience beyond unsent drafts: the sender's reason for choosing those words, the recipient's reaction before replying, or the practical situation around the phone. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought.\n\nLet the exchange carry actual requests, replies, or ordinary updates. No delayed answer, apology, or changed relation is required. The transcript may end on a pending question, a plain acknowledgment, or a message left unanswered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and owned focal response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Do not alternate them mechanically. A background or expression cue may orient the moment: the state of a room, the position of a chair, a character's look at a letter or a clock. The cue supports a contextual reading but does not independently certify hidden motive. A changed expression can support a reading in its actual context, alongside relevant wording, arrangements or prior knowledge; neither the cue nor additional conduct automatically proves motive.\n\nLet the focal character's response carry their actual private interpretation. The response may be a short narrative passage, an inward reaction or a developed consideration at the person's needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel; its experience remains owned by that character. Preserve the active narrator's access and prior knowledge. Another character does not know what the focal character has inferred merely because the reader does.\n\nKeep ordinary speech individual. A character may speak plainly, formally, or awkwardly according to their situation. Allow the scene to end on a pending request, an unanswered question, or ordinary continuation. Do not add branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. No compelled revised judgment or relationship turn is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabakbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jane Austen / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what has changed locally, and what remains available to continue. When a later installment needs an earlier particular, recover only that particular. Bring back the actual wording of an offer, the arrangement of a room, a prior request, or a piece of knowledge the character already has. Do not retell the whole relationship or restate every earlier scene.\n\nKeep earlier events and received meanings intact. A later understanding may confirm, qualify, or remain open without rewriting what the character previously knew or felt. A person's interpretation of a courtesy or omission can remain stable across installments even when new information arrives. Do not invent a new misunderstanding, corrected prejudice, or relationship turn merely to create movement.\n\nLet ordinary care, conversation, and extended private experience sustain a reply. The installment may advance through a practical task, a partial answer, or a character's continued reflection. Recover context briefly only when it helps the reader re-enter. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. No cliffhanger, confession, or completed interpretation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLocate the request, refusal, apology, or act of dependence in the immediate scene: the speaker enters carrying a concrete need, a prior silence, or an obligation the other person has reason to recognize. Give that decision enough scenic room to unfold through approach, explanation, and response, but let the difficulty reside in what is actually at stake for these particular people rather than in a manufactured contest between loyalties.\n\nRender the cost of speaking from the focal character by tracking what admitting the need exposes: fear of being too much, uncertainty about being wanted, resentment at having to ask, or relief that the request is finally outside them. Let private thought differ from spoken words when the character chooses brevity, politeness, or deflection, and make any difference legible through the character's actual experience, without requiring it to create outward pressure. A long restrained interior passage can show the character rehearsing what they cannot say; a sudden interruption can show thought giving way to blurted speech.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile's movement and shared history without forcing a heroic confrontation. An old commitment can return through a specific memory, tone or claim the person actually makes, including a claim of superiority where supported; invoking it does not make that claim true. Let each participant act from enough of their own position that agreement, partial help, awkward comfort, or a request left unmet remain possible outcomes. The emotional turn does not have to arrive as a confession, a clean apology, or a completed repair. Warmth may appear imperfectly alongside pain.\n\nKeep the style's turbulence in the narration as well as the dialogue: allow syntax to quicken under strain, a sentence to break off, or a detail to become too present. Do not translate suffering into a fixed vocabulary of hearts, wounds, or trembling. The cost should belong to the active narrator's diction and the scene's actual conditions. Let any relationship change follow actual events; the scene may also end with a stable arrangement, an unasked question or a response still pending.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the scene as one coherent shared interval with the active participants clearly present from the first paragraph: who is speaking, who is being addressed, and what practical or personal matter brings them together. Use unmistakable dialogue attribution in every exchange, keeping paragraphs readable enough that a reader can follow position, tone, and physical movement without a narrator's overhead explanation.\n\nRespect the active narrator, person, and ownership settings exactly. When the narrator is focal, render their actual private experience in its selected Hidden or Visible nonspoken presentation: the recognition that an offer is needed, the hesitation before declining, the relief of being met without conditions. When the ownership contract excludes a participant's mind, keep that boundary throughout; show their position through speech, action, and what others can observe.\n\nLet a request or decision develop through actual conversation rather than as a pre-stated problem. Give the participants room for explanation, a pause in which an idea changes, or a practical demonstration of care. Use the Jin Yong profile by making technique and capacity matter through particular choices: someone knows how to fix the immediate trouble, remembers what the other dislikes, or chooses not to use an advantage. Do not import sects, martial lineages, or a historical Chinese setting.\n\nSmall obligations and shared history can surface within the interval as references the characters already understand. A reputation may be mentioned, but attribute which participant knows it and how it affects the present exchange. The scene may conclude with an agreement, continuing company, or a matter still underway. Do not end with a compulsory question, cliffhanger, or new loyalty test. Provide a continuation point that follows naturally from the current exchange, leaving the next step available without forcing it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose vernacular episodic telling. Open with a compact situational introduction that places the visit, request, shared meal, journey, or returned favor within an ongoing cascade of human occasions. The introduction may name who has come, what prior matter brings them, and what practical condition now exists. It should not recite a character's whole history or resolve the episode before it begins.\n\nLet each encounter proceed through ordinary explanation, conduct, refusal, acceptance, or change of intention. Use the Jin Yong profile by keeping movement generous: participants travel, wait, work, share a table, or take a longer way home while the matter between them remains live. Let technique or capacity appear as particular knowledge, not as spectacle. A person may know how to mend a tool, speak to an official, keep a promise under inconvenience, or choose restraint when action would be easier.\n\nWhen reputation or a remembered undertaking enters the scene, attribute it precisely: who knows the report, whether they believe it, and how it bears on the present exchange. Do not let a public name stand in for private motive. Show the focal participant's actual experience beneath the episode's social surface, including what they decide not to say. The narrator may move between participants as the active contract permits, giving each authorized experience the development it needs.\n\nEnd the episode when the immediate occasion reaches a natural pause. An arrangement may be confirmed, a burden may be accepted, or a question may remain open. No duel, revelation, new ally, coerced loyalty, or completed emotional transformation is required. The next episode will connect through the state this one leaves.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nGive the exchange hard edges by locating it in specific objects and physical tasks, with pressure from time or weather only where the actual conditions supply it. Write concise transitions: the door opens, the work is set down, the tea is poured, the rain starts against the window. Use forceful diction that comes from the scene's actual conditions rather than copied from a list of harsh adjectives. When a speaker is rough, make that roughness belong to their habits, fatigue, or history, not to the narrator's general tone.\n\nDo not compress thought as a reflex. When a character performs a careful technique, takes a risk, or restrains themselves, expand the actual reasoning and feeling that accompany the act. A long inner passage may follow a single gesture: the memory of an earlier failure, the calculation of cost, the private decision to trust. Let this interiority change the rhythm against the clipped external movement so the prose does not become a monotone of short sentences.\n\nUse the Jin Yong profile by making competence and relationship intelligible through actual use, explanation or established experience, without requiring a fresh demonstration. A practical offer or remembered method can carry shared history, but the story should also let the recipient handle the matter badly or well. Material wear may appear as a cracked cup, a mended strap, a blister, or a patched wall; use it to mark passage and care without making endurance itself the point.\n\nKeep the character drama central: the physical action should reveal what someone is willing to do, decline, accept, or hide from another person. Sincere help, competent ease, and unchanged affection remain possible. Do not require injury, betrayal, or a grim moral verdict to make the exchange significant.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual records: a letter requesting help, a note confirming an arrangement, an account of how a task was performed, a reply accepting or declining. For each document, establish its writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Begin where the correspondence has a reason to exist, not with a scene abstracted from the record itself.\n\nMake the wording of a promise or report perform the dramatic work. When a letter offers assistance, make its actual scope and conditions legible without requiring a later payoff: what exactly is offered, under what conditions, with what tone. A report may accurately describe an act while omitting the exhaustion, relief, second thoughts, or private meaning that accompanied it. Drafts, delays, and delivery belong to the documentary form: an unsent draft is an artifact, not a live mind.\n\nKeep required interiority outside the document rather than collapsing it into the record. Render the character's actual nonspoken experience in its selected Hidden or Visible presentation, clearly distinct from the artifact: what they felt before writing, what they did with the finished letter, what they imagined the recipient would think. Another person's receipt and interpretation of the document are distinct facts. A misunderstanding may develop because the record says less than its author knew.\n\nWhere actual travel, shared obligations or particular competence enter the correspondence, make their relation to the people specific: a long journey that delays a reply, an item sent along with a note, a name the recipient recognizes. Do not require a revelation, confession, acceptance, or documented resolution. The sequence may end while a matter remains undecided, with the next artifact still unwritten.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSelect a particular kindness that belongs to the people in the scene: a remembered preference, an offer to share a task, an unhurried explanation, a favorite food prepared at the right time, or a quiet hour given without strings. Spend attention on the small exact manner in which it is offered and received. Use the Jin Yong profile by letting the kindness follow from someone's actual competence or familiarity, without assigning a protector role merely from competence; preserve any role the actual relationship establishes.\n\nPreserve the recipient's actual choices and private experience, whether they actively contribute or simply receive the care. They may decline part of the care, add their own touch, return help later, or admit that a different approach would suit them better. This preserves personality and privacy inside the warmth. Render the focal person's actual private response, which may include enjoyment, awkwardness, gratitude or complicated relief without requiring any of those feelings.\n\nDo not make comfort contingent on a successful task or a fixed emotional outcome. The moment can remain small and easy: a meal finished in company, a knot untangled, a worry heard but not solved. Shared history can make the care familiar, but a newcomer may also offer sincere help without first passing a loyalty test. Let the scene end with room for the relationship to continue rather than with a forced confession or completed repair.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with deliberate narrative movement that locates the decision in a wider field: who is visiting, what social condition makes the invitation or request delicate, and what history surrounds the moment. Use enough distance to frame the scene, but keep the narrator's own view distinct from public rumor. The paragraph may move from the room to the street, from the present exchange to the way such matters are usually handled, without treating convention as the final truth.\n\nDevelop the actual reasons behind a participant's choice through sustained interior paragraphs. Let the authorized narrator or focal person consider what is owed, desired, feared or hoped, and let the prose build through subordinate clauses and ordered reflection rather than through rapid cuts. Distinguish reputation from motive: a person may be known as dependable, difficult, generous, or proud, while the actual private reasoning may align fully, align partly or differ from that public name.\n\nKeep Jin Yong's generosity of movement and consequence in the formal architecture. Practical matters such as an invitation, a debt, a piece of work, or a family obligation may carry the incident across a longer span. When characters speak, preserve their own registers instead of raising every speaker into the narrator's formal diction. Let a plain reply stand against an elaborate surrounding paragraph when that contrast belongs to the people present.\n\nClose without requiring ceremony, a pronouncement, or a completed change of obligation. The contextual paragraph may remain reflective or end on a small observable fact: a letter accepted, a coat returned, a gate closed, a decision not yet spoken.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen the scene around a particular telling: who is speaking, who is listening, where they are, and what the speaker wants the account to do in the present. The story may seek help, offer an explanation, defend a choice, or keep company, but its purpose should be visible in what the teller chooses to include and in how the listeners receive it.\n\nLet the teller select from shared history and private knowledge. Show which details of a remembered act are offered, which are summarized, and which are shaped by affection, obligation, or uncertainty. Represent an omission only through what the active narrator can actually know, whether the surrounding scene, authorized private experience or a later question makes it apparent; an omission may also remain unnoticed, without a compulsory exposure or interrogative return. Keep what the teller knew at the time separate from what they have learned since; a later understanding may color the telling without replacing the original experience.\n\nUse the listeners to keep the story anchored in the current relationship. They may interrupt with a practical question, return to a particular phrase, or respond by recalling their own part in the event. Their attention need not produce agreement or a completed exchange. A listener can remain silent, change the subject, or accept only part of the account, and the scene can end with that unresolved reception.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's access and private experience independently of the teller's performance. The narration may show what a listener privately thinks of the story or what the teller withholds, but it must not grant access to minds outside the active contract. A performed tale is not a substitute for required nonspoken interiority; the teller's actual feeling during the telling and a listener's unspoken response remain available where authorized.\n\nLet the embedded story and the outer scene inform each other through concrete recurrence. A repeated name, object, or phrase can return in the present exchange with a changed meaning, but it need not resolve into a lesson. The telling may alter the relationship, leave it exactly as it was, or reveal something the teller did not intend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and developmental realist mode. When a character offers help, accepts a task, refuses a request, or relies on someone else, show the particular skill, habit, or expectation they bring to that choice. Where the history bears on this experience, relate the present act to how the capacity was learned: a remembered failure, a period of work, a family practice, or a long familiarity with the other person. Make the connection intelligible, giving it the development its practical or personal significance needs whether or not it changes or explains the decision.\n\nDistinguish what a character can do from what they privately think of that ability and from how others estimate them. A person may perform a task competently while doubting it, or accept a reputation they no longer believe they deserve. Let the narration render the actual private appraisal under the active access contract, and keep another character's estimate separate from the fact of the performance.\n\nAllow experience to confirm an existing view as readily as it revises one. A character may discover that an old method still works, that a familiar person has changed, or that a remembered lesson no longer applies. Do not force a moment of growth, a mentor's correction, or a newly articulate self-understanding. The scene may end with the character holding the same view but acting on it under new conditions.\n\nGive the exchange enough room to move through practical activity, explanation, and relationship. Follow the actual relation between attachment and obligation, allowing alignment or tension where established: what the character owes, what they want, and what their training or history tells them to do. Keep the developmental material particular to these people and their experience, including meanings that do not produce a new choice, rather than substituting a general biography.\n\nKeep the narration's syntax and distance in the social-realist register: direct, observant, and attentive to the conditions that formed the character, without turning every exchange into a case study or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the scene in the supplied institutions, spaces, and reputations that make an encounter's terms concrete. Show what a person may enter, use, or expect because of an old arrangement, a family position, or a long-maintained public role. Let the material details of the place carry that history: a room kept for formal visitors, a door someone no longer has the right to open, a title that still changes how a request is heard.\n\nLet private attachment and public role align or diverge according to the particular people involved. A character may want the same thing their position requires, or may accept an obligation while privately resenting it. Show the actual reasons through action, speech, and authorized interiority. Do not make every inherited authority a burden or every offer a manipulation; a person can find genuine ease, loyalty, or relief inside an old structure.\n\nUse social presentation as a changeable surface with real consequences. A character may maintain a careful manner before others and let it fall away in trusted company. When that happens, make the shift visible through posture, address, or what the person finally says. The lapse need not be total or dramatic; it may be a single honest sentence or a moment of unguarded attention. Preserve the active narrator's access so that a private thought is not automatically legible to everyone in the room.\n\nLet morally complicated intimacy develop through particular choices rather than through atmosphere alone. A character may help someone while knowing the help is incomplete, or accept comfort while aware of what it costs. Show the practical and emotional texture of that exchange without requiring a confession, a betrayal, or a resolution. The scene can leave the relationship changed in a small, specific way.\n\nGive the encounter room for actual explanation, practical business and private experience. Follow how attachment, obligation and inherited authority relate in what these people do, including compatible commitments and arrangements that continue without a collision.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimonial and autobiographical narration. Establish the occasion on which a character gives an account of something they did, witnessed, or decided, and make the addressee's interest concrete. The listener may be a family member, a colleague, a friend, or someone with a practical claim on the story. Show why the account is being given now and what the speaker hopes or fears it will do.\n\nLet the speaker select from what they did, heard, intended, and later learned. Show the emphasis through the order and fullness of the telling: a detail expanded because it matters to the listener, a moment compressed because it is painful, a fact offered as proof of loyalty. The selection need not involve deceit. A person can tell the truth while choosing which truths to place in the room.\n\nKeep the testimony distinct from the active narrator and from actual private experience. The speaker's performed account may be sincere, partial, or mistaken. Where the active contract permits, show what the speaker privately thinks during the telling or what the listener does not say. Do not let the testimony replace required nonspoken interiority, and do not grant the listener knowledge of a private thought simply because the speaker has chosen to talk.\n\nLet the receiving person respond according to their own situation. They may agree, ask a practical question, remain silent, or leave the account unanswered. No confession, credibility verdict, or completed reconciliation is owed at the scene boundary. The exchange may end with the listener holding a changed understanding that they do not yet voice.\n\nLet the speaker's actual attachment and obligations shape the selection where relevant, whether those commitments align, compete or leave the account straightforward. A promise, a refusal, or a shared history can be the center of the testimony, and the telling itself can become an act of loyalty or distance.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the social and psychological realist mode. Follow the focal person's actual noticing, recollection, reasoning, and feeling around a specific exchange: what they see in the other person's face or manner, what memory the moment calls up, what they conclude, and what they decide to say or withhold. Preserve the causal order of that experience. A later realization must not be inserted as an earlier motive.\n\nLet a reputation or prior act inform the focal person's reading without settling it automatically. A character may expect someone to refuse because they refused before, or may hear a promise as sincere because of a long familiarity. Show the inference as the character's own, and let it be confirmed, revised, or left uncertain by what actually follows.\n\nGive direct feeling, calm understanding, and extended thought room to develop. A character may reason carefully before speaking, or may feel something plainly and act on it without a long inward passage. Do not require reserve, misreading, or a changed response. The scene may show a person understanding another correctly and still choosing distance, or understanding only part of a situation and acting on that part.\n\nKeep the narration's access within the active contract. Private experience follows the active contract for each person; a focal passage neither grants excluded access to another mind nor cancels access already authorized. A visible action or spoken line may be interpreted by the focal person, but that interpretation remains distinct from the other person's actual motive.\n\nWhere attachment, obligation or reputation matters to the exchange, follow how the focal person actually understands it, allowing aligned commitments and no new interpretive problem. A promise, a reliance, or a restraint can be the specific thing they notice, remember, and interpret, and the scene can move through that understanding without forcing a confrontation or a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling. Arrange the permitted accounts around a single relational occasion—an offer, a refusal, a departure, a return, or a promise—so that different participants and times illuminate it from different positions. The passage need not include every viewpoint; select the accounts that make the occasion's personal weight legible.\n\nDistinguish participation, report, and actual private access. A character who was present may know what happened but not what another person felt. A character who heard about the event later may know only what they were told. Where the active contract permits, show the actual private experience of an authorized focal person; do not grant that access to a character who merely reports the event.\n\nLet the same offer or refusal matter differently, identically, or not at all to another participant. One person may remember a promise as a turning point while another remembers it as a small courtesy. The accounts may agree without becoming redundant, or may differ without producing a contradiction. Keep established events intact and attribute knowledge before it affects a response.\n\nUse time as an ordering device with a clear purpose. A later account may explain what an earlier participant could not see, or an earlier moment may return to qualify a later judgment. Make the movement between times legible through concrete markers—a place, an object, a phrase, or a stated date—so the reader can follow which position is speaking.\n\nLet actual attachments and obligations appear across the accounts where relevant, whether they align or compete. Different participants may weigh the same obligation differently, or the same person may understand it differently before and after. No final consensus, contradiction, or revelation is required; the scene may end with the accounts simply held together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Identify the actual participants, the place, and what is underway in the first few sentences, so the reader can follow the exchange without a long preamble. If a social arrangement, technical term, or personal history matters, give it enough context through explanation, observation, or use to make the present choice intelligible.\n\nKeep spatial changes and paragraph breaks easy to follow. When the scene moves from one room to another, from speech to action, or from action to private response, mark the change with a clear transition. Use readable paragraphs around dialogue, movement, and interiority so that the reader can track who is speaking and what the focal person notices.\n\nLet a useful technique or remembered promise matter through its actual role in the scene. A character may know how to repair something, keep a commitment, or read a situation because of past experience. Show the capacity's actual relevance through use, explanation, observation or private experience, including instruction before handling where needed. Do not add a tutorial, power display or loyalty proof merely to exhibit the profile; supported explanation can itself be part of the relationship.\n\nPreserve individual humor and meaningful extended thought. A character may make a dry remark, take a situation seriously, or reason at length before acting. Do not impose a young cast, a comic tone, or a closing cliffhanger. The scene can end at a natural pause in the exchange, with the relationship continuing in an ordinary way.\n\nKeep the actual relationship at the scene's center, including attachment and obligation where relevant without requiring them to conflict. Let the accessible orientation clarify what the characters owe, want, and choose, while the private response remains available under the active access contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Lyrical Prose\n\nWrite the scene through a focal character's attention to a concrete word, motion, or sensory detail that belongs to the present exchange. Let that detail develop the character's actual association with care, restraint or obligation. Plain statements and direct names for a feeling can stand beside the image in the person's own diction; avoid generic labeling that substitutes for their actual experience. Develop the association through sentence rhythm and recurring sounds or images, so the syntax itself moves between the literal event and what the character privately understands it to mean. Keep the actual exchange clear: who spoke, what was asked or refused, and any practical action that followed or response that remains pending.\n\nUse recurrence as an accumulating trace, not an emblem. A gesture, object, or phrase may return with a fuller meaning when the character's situation has changed, retain a familiar meaning, or remain unfinished because the character has not resolved what it signifies. Do not make an image prove another person's virtue or hidden desire; it renders the focal person's association without by itself establishing another mind.\n\nFollow how actual obligation and attachment enter the same concrete particulars, whether they align, create pressure or remain ordinary concerns. A formal duty may be rendered as a repeated motion the character performs while thinking of someone else; a private attachment may surface in the rhythm of an explanation the character gives in public. Preserve the active narrator and person around authorized focal lyric passages, while each speaker retains their own register. An ordinary act need not become a moral emblem or a completed emotional verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present exchange recall an earlier relationship, capacity, or occasion only when the memory actually enters the character's current attention. Anchor the recollection in a specific detail from the scene: a familiar method of doing something, a promise repeated in altered circumstances, a gesture that once belonged to someone else. Show what the character notices now and what the memory makes available, without letting the past overwrite the present action.\n\nPreserve the distinction between shared content and private remembering. Other characters may know the same history without sharing the focal character's associations, and they may respond to the present request or obligation on its own terms. Keep the memory's actual clarity intact: what the character knew then, what has since changed, and what remains continuous. Do not make the earlier self ignorant or the later self uniformly wiser.\n\nAllow pleasure, care, and ordinary competence to remain sincere. A present commitment can continue alongside the memory of what is no longer possible, and reflection need not end in regret, renewed duty, or a pronouncement about time. Let the scene move through the practical exchange and the character's private attention together, giving remembered change its actual personal significance without requiring a deeper relationship, an irreversible loss or a completed emotional resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route. Establish an authorized participant's later recollecting occasion, but do not let that later voice replace the active narrator or the scene's immediate perspective. Move between the participant's experience while acting and the later understanding that now frames it, keeping the two positions distinct. What the character knew then, what they forgot, and what they learned afterward remain separate; the later self is not automatically wiser or the earlier self merely mistaken.\n\nFollow a particular offer, reliance, or restraint through its actual human circumstances. Render the attachments and obligations actually experienced at the time, whether aligned or competing, with the practical details of travel, work, explanation, or relationship given room to unfold. The memoir frame may comment on what the participant could not then see, but it must not retroactively insert that knowledge as the earlier motive.\n\nPreserve actual private experience beyond the composed account. The memoir records what its author chooses to tell; the narration may still render the focal character's nonspoken thought, sensation, or uncertainty as it occurred. Let the later recollection deepen the present telling without turning every remembered choice into a lesson, a completed self-justification, or a fixed judgment on the other people involved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact words, objects, or gestures that carry the particular offer, acceptance, or refusal in the scene. Let each selected detail do real work: a tool set down, a name spoken in a particular way, a step taken toward or away from someone. Use direct clauses to connect observable events and authorized private experience in their actual causal order, including thought before action or feeling that does not explain or change conduct.\n\nA known method, prior promise, or shared history may remain implicit where the scene already supports it, but explain what would otherwise be lost. Do not cut meaningful thought or repeated feeling merely to shorten the passage; let an essential inward connection be long enough to make sense. Cut redundant framing and repeated explanation instead, so that the scene's causal movement stays clear.\n\nRestraint in narration does not require silent characters, withheld motives, or incomplete outcomes. A character may speak plainly about what they want or refuse, and the scene may reach a definite practical result. Let selected details and actual private experience make the person's attachments and obligations intelligible where relevant, without requiring conflict or an outward display. The minimal surface should make the relationship more legible, not more obscure.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as continuous prose that moves naturally among action, explanation, dialogue, and the focal character's authorized private experience in their causal order. Let an ordinary request, shared activity, or practical task reveal why someone helps, refuses, or remains undecided, without staging a public test of loyalty or competence. Give the encounter room to unfold through travel, work, conversation, and relationship rather than reducing it to a sequence of decisive exchanges.\n\nKeep other participants' concerns and knowledge distinct. They may be occupied with their own obligations, misunderstand the focal character's hesitation, or respond to the immediate practical matter without sharing the private attachment that shapes the narration. Preserve the active narrator's access: the prose may render one character's thought, but not another's unspoken mind unless the authorship contract permits it.\n\nDeveloped recollection, companionship, or ongoing work may sustain the scene without a conflict, completed decision, or new demonstration of loyalty. A request can remain unanswered, a relationship can continue in its existing terms, and a character can act from aligned motives without a revelation. Let the scene end where the actual business or exchange pauses, leaving the next step available rather than forcing a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes that make the actual supplied relationship premise legible through familiar methods, private language, and the history of asking or helping between these particular people. Do not import a roster of tropes. Let the bond appear through actual knowledge, chosen words and existing memories, without inventing shared history or exclusive language where the supplied premise does not contain them. Give the scene enough room for the relationship to be experienced, not merely announced.\n\nDistinguish capacity, desire, and permission. A character may be able to help without being obliged to, may want something without being ready to ask, or may accept assistance without becoming dependent. Do not assign permanent competence to one person and gratitude or need to the other. Follow the actual relation of public obligation and private attachment, allowing alignment or pressure and distinguishing where relevant: what someone will do openly, what they will only admit in private, and what they will not say at all.\n\nUse callbacks to confirm the existing arrangement as well as to change it. A repeated phrase, object, or gesture may show that the relationship has remained continuous, or it may take a new application when the present scene alters what it means. Preserve aligned motives and actual private experience without requiring jealousy, a rescue debt, confession, or increasing intimacy. The scene may end with the bond intact and the next step open, rather than forcing an emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive a particular relational act a deliberate retold opening: what was offered, kept, refused or withheld, and who was involved. Expand into the actual circumstances that made the act matter to these people. Where a learned method, prior promise or choice of restraint belongs to the occasion, make its place in the relationship intelligible without turning help into a victory or refusal into proof of character. An ordinary acceptance may carry the account as readily as a costly decision.\n\nKeep the deed, its reputation and its participants' experience distinguishable. Attribute any account that calls someone loyal, capable or ungrateful, and let the actual history show what that description covers or leaves open. The actor may have acted from one concern or several compatible or competing concerns; use the reasons available to them then rather than importing the teller's later knowledge. A public telling need not contain everything the person felt, while authorized nonspoken experience remains present in its active form.\n\nUse compact declarations and recurring language to make the retelling recognizable. A designation returns through established use, not an invented lineage or title, and its meaning may remain unchanged. Where actual dialogue enters, speakers can accept a description, correct it or attend to another matter without an obligatory challenge to the account. Keep narrator, person and ownership intact, and let the active boundary fall within a continuing act or telling without a heroic verdict, changed allegiance or completed emotional result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic narrative as the organizing movement of the scene. Let the authorized narrator open with a limited proposition about what a particular commitment requires—help, restraint, obligation, or the right to ask—stated in that narrator's own terms, not as a universal rule. The proposition should arise from the immediate situation or the narrator's accumulated understanding, and it may be explicit or implicit in the prose.\n\nSet the proposition beside an established or clearly hypothetical interpersonal case drawn from the actual supplied world and characters. Give the case enough scenic room: show the particular people involved, the relevant conditions of the commitment or request, and what each person does or leaves pending within that actual or hypothetical case, without requiring a new test. Do not reduce the case to an abstract example; give actual activity, explanation and private experience the room they need without adding travel or requiring a sequence of stages.\n\nReturn to the proposition through the narrator's confirmation, qualification, or uncertainty. The return should be earned by the case's particulars, not announced as a lesson. A person may confirm the proposition while another character's behavior remains open to a different interpretation. Distinguish reputation from actual support: what a community says about someone's reliability is not the same as what that person offered or withheld in the specific case. Distinguish a person's interpretation of another's motive from the motive itself, which may remain inaccessible.\n\nAllow the construction to remain unfinished across replies. The narrator may hold the proposition open, return to it later with new information, or leave a qualification unresolved without forcing a counterexample, revised belief, or new action. The essayistic frame organizes attention and judgment; it does not require the scene to end in a verdict. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts, and keep required private experience in its authorized presentation rather than converting it into public argument.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings, concise action blocks, and clear dialogue cues to establish the actual spatial and audible conditions of each exchange. When a method is demonstrated or help is offered, make the visible action legible: who reaches for what, who waits, who turns away, what sound or movement accompanies the offer. The action block should record observable conduct without certifying virtue or securing acceptance. A character may perform the correct action and still be refused, or perform it for a reason the scene does not yet reveal.\n\nPreserve authorized nonspoken experience independently of voice-over or performed explanation. Write the focal character's private reasoning, hesitation, or feeling in the active nonspoken presentation required by the authorship contract, not as a voice-over line or a speech another character can hear. Develop interpersonal significance through the actual relation between visible conduct and private understanding, whether they align or differ, without requiring a gap. A restrained gesture may look like indifference; the private prose can show the effort behind it without making that effort public.\n\nUse cuts and visible reactions to follow the supplied sequence. A cut may move from one person's action to another's separate response, but do not use it to imply knowledge the second character does not have. Offscreen sound or a held look can orient attention without becoming a truth indicator. Give the scene room for travel, explanation, and relationship as the material requires; no decisive gesture, confrontation, or relationship change is required at the cut. The scene may end on an unanswered offer, a completed task, or a person alone with what they did not say.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Stage Play\n\nStage the actual exchange through positions, entrances, object use, and ongoing conduct. Let the physical business carry participation and attention: one person sets down a tool, another remains at the threshold, a third continues working while listening. Explanation may precede handling where the people need it—a character may say what a method requires before demonstrating it—but the visible offer may remain unanswered or unnoticed. Do not convert every private reason into a speech; a pause, a repeated action, or a refusal to take an offered object can express a boundary without the character announcing it.\n\nKeep actual private experience in its active nonspoken presentation. A monologue or aside is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it is not the default destination for thought. The focal character's private reasoning, uncertainty, or feeling should appear as nonspoken authoring material under the active contract, not as a line another character can hear. Distinguish what the audience can observe from what a character privately understands.\n\nOffstage access and world activity follow established conditions. A character who exits does not automatically lose knowledge they already had, and their return is not required to restore it. Other characters may continue practical work offstage or onstage without the scene stopping to explain their absence. Let the shared space sustain the relationship through ordinary business, attention, and restraint; no decisive confrontation, confession, or changed bond is required before the scene ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow the authorized person's actual associations as the organizing form of the scene. Braid bodily activity, heard words, private phrasing, and remembered attachments so that the movement of the mind carries the telling. A spoken sentence may trigger a memory of an earlier promise; a familiar gesture may call up a private name for someone; a physical sensation may interrupt a line of reasoning and redirect attention. Let the associations belong to this particular mind, not a generic associative voice.\n\nKeep present events, recollection, and imagined consequences distinguishable even as they flow together. Use the prose to mark what is happening now, what is remembered, and what is only anticipated or feared. A promise may recur alongside an aligned wish or an unrelated feeling without being forced into competition or resolved into a plan. The mind may settle on a concern, drift from it, or return to it later; no compulsory decision or completed realization is required.\n\nDevelop meaningful experience without a word-count time cost. A long passage may follow a single association through several turns; a short one may register only a bodily interruption. Preserve the active narrator's access: do not enter unpermitted minds, and do not let the associative flow grant knowledge the character does not have. The scene may remain inward, return to the shared exchange, or end with the mind still turning over what it has not yet said.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse actual sender-attributed messages and supported interface events to carry a request, offer, or continuing conversation. Each message should reflect its sender's actual digital habits: a person may type in fragments, use full sentences, delay a reply, edit a wording, or leave a message unread. Record only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. A read receipt does not prove comprehension or consent; a delivered message is not the same as an accepted request.\n\nDistinguish drafts, sent wording, reading, and response. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A character may compose a message, delete it, and send something shorter; the deleted text belongs to the record only if the form supports it, and it does not replace the character's actual private experience. Preserve actual private experience outside the artifact, clearly separated from the transcript, including reconsideration that needs no new incoming message. A character may reread an old message and change what it means to them without sending anything.\n\nLet a shared history make a terse message intelligible without making every memory shared. One person's brief reply may carry a weight the other does not recognize, or both may understand a reference differently. The transcript can end on an unanswered message, a pending task, or a person alone with what they chose not to send. No new message, admission, or changed bond is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the actual shared scene with concise scene-setting, readable dialogue attribution, and useful background or expression cues. A cue should establish the moment—where the characters are, what they are doing, what expression or posture is visible—without functioning as an automatic truth indicator. A changed expression can attract attention, but any authorized private feeling or intention is rendered through actual nonspoken experience rather than read off the portrait; excluded motives remain outside the narration's access.\n\nLet focal narration develop what an offer, familiar method, or withheld action means to the authorized person. The prose may follow the focal character's reasoning, hesitation, relief, or uncertainty as the scene continues, whether their response changes or remains private. Keep visible behavior distinct from confirmed motives: another character may see the same expression and interpret it differently, and the focal narration does not grant the cast access to what it reveals. Preserve all active narrator access and required interiority in its authorized presentation.\n\nGive the scene enough room for travel, explanation, and relationship as the material requires. A background detail may anchor a return or a change in circumstances, but any changed significance follows actual experience, including reconsideration of existing information, while the detail may also retain its meaning. No portrait reaction, menu, romantic route, or completed choice is introduced by the form. The scene may end on a private realization, an unanswered question, or a person continuing their work without a forced decision point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabalbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jin Yong / Web Serial\n\nCarry forward actual offers, decisions, and pending matters across replies, distinguishing what was said from what each person inferred. When a past exchange becomes relevant, recover the necessary particular briefly—the wording of the offer, the condition under which it was made, the response that was given—rather than retelling the whole scene. A person may act on an inference the other never stated, and that gap remains available for later development without being resolved immediately.\n\nLet a remembered promise or familiar way of helping recur through ongoing concern or current events. The recurrence may carry unchanged meaning, or it may acquire new significance because of what has happened since. Do not require the installment to renegotiate the relationship, while allowing renegotiation where the actual events support it: a commitment already made can simply continue to shape practical choices, attention, or avoidance. Keep other lives and knowledge active across the interval; characters not present in the current scene may still be working, waiting, or changing their understanding.\n\nCompany, separate work, or unfinished reflection can sustain continuation. An installment may close on a completed task, a person alone with a thought, or a pending request that no one has answered. No new test, admission, or changed bond is required. The serial form gives selected facts and feelings room to accumulate; it does not demand that each reply produce a relationship turning point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWhere actual hurt, need, or difficult exposure accompanies a request, withheld answer, offer, or return, give that experience its particular weight. An act need not incur a new emotional cost simply because it occurs in the scene. Let the cost be concrete and personal: a familiar routine interrupted, a phrase now uncertain, a favor accepted as more than convenience, an apology that does not restore the old ease. Do not make every ordinary habit a secret betrayal or a promise of worse loss.\n\nPreserve the prior meaning of any repeated object, routine, or phrase. If a shared joke once meant nothing more than an easy moment, it may still be only that. If a weekly task once settled arguments, it can fail to do so now without being retroactively revealed as the hidden cause of the difficulty. What has changed is the present exchange, not necessarily the history.\n\nGive private feeling as much room as the character needs, even when nothing visible results. One person may think for several paragraphs before speaking; another may answer quickly and only later understand what the answer meant. Keep what others can see separate from what the focal character knows. A refusal can be made with a steady voice while the mind is still racing; a request for help may sound calm while the speaker expects to be blamed.\n\nAllow alignment as well as difference. Two people can want the same thing and still fail to say it cleanly. Shared life may include genuine help, continued company, or a quiet refusal without turning past kindness into a farewell or proof of betrayal. The scene may end with the difficulty still present, the relationship unchanged, or a partial comfort that does not cancel the pain.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the response by grounding the supplied action in the current scene: who is present, what just happened, and what ordinary task or exchange is underway. Do not invent a callback for a first encounter; if a shared routine or phrase is established, it may provide continuity, but it should not be forced.\n\nKeep dialogue in clear, readable paragraphs with unambiguous attribution. When an NPC speaks, let their words come from their own ongoing life and concerns, not only from the player's action. The NPC may be distracted, patient, affectionate, tired, or preoccupied without making every reaction a test of the relationship.\n\nWithin the active narrator and ownership contract, render each authorized viewpoint character's actual private attention in nonspoken prose, distinct from what others can hear or know through their actual channels. The private thought may match the spoken line or differ; if it differs, the difference should be particular to that person, not a universal filter of misunderstanding or concealment. For example, a character who says \"I don't mind\" may genuinely not mind, may be too tired to explain, or may notice something else in the room. Which one depends on the character and the moment.\n\nEnd at a natural boundary: within a private thought, at the edge of a conversation, after a shared task is partially done, or when someone leaves. Do not require a changed bond, a resolved conflict, or a prepared next move. The continuation should follow from the scene's actual state, leaving room for other participants to respond.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling for the current story. Present each episode as a concrete occasion: a visit, meal, shared task, conversation, or departure. Link episodes through what people actually said and did, using permitted narration or reflection where it makes the connection clear without inventing a new meaning. When a habit or preference is established, it may appear again in a later episode without needing to recur every time; its absence can be as informative as its presence, but do not manufacture a symbolic return.\n\nLet an episode stand on its own as an ordinary lived moment. It does not need a moral, a revelation, a crisis, or a relationship change. A disagreement may remain unresolved when the episode ends; a meal may be enjoyed without deeper meaning; a departure may be simply practical. What accumulates is the history of the relationship, not a series of lessons.\n\nKeep the original words and motives of each episode intact. If a later event changes how an earlier occasion is understood, show the change through a character's present reflection or a new action, not by rewriting what they said or knew at the time. A phrase spoken casually cannot become a deliberate clue unless the character actually intended it.\n\nUse the vernacular texture of the setting: the way people greet each other, arrange a meal, complain about work, or say goodbye. Keep the language appropriate to the period and community supplied, without imposing a classical register or a folk-tale frame. The telling should feel like a connected series of lived scenes, not a polished chronicle.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse concrete physical details to locate the emotional situation: the worn handle of a favorite cup, the weight of a coat as someone takes it, the noise of a door closing too hard, the ache in a back from a long shift. Let actual physical details give the exchange its felt conditions alongside directly named feeling or abstract reflection. A request or refusal may involve effort when the scene supplies it, but a task or burden is not required to make the relationship concrete.\n\nCompress transitions and repeated framing. Use compact transitions where they preserve the actual sequence, varying sentence shape with the experience rather than assigning a single sharp clause to every beat. Avoid redundant labels or repeated setup while preserving meaningful recurrence within the person's actual feeling and attention. Give reasoning, feeling, memory, uncertainty, and abstract reflection their needed extent, without requiring a thought to serve a choice or remain attached to an object, task, or bodily sensation.\n\nA recurring detail may stay ordinary. A chipped mug need not symbolize a broken relationship; a tiring routine need not prove devotion. Care can be easy and reliable: a clean cup left on the counter, a blanket folded on the arm of a chair. If a detail takes on new significance, the change must be earned by an actual event, not by stylistic pressure.\n\nPreserve the character's full private experience, including memory and uncertainty. Compression removes repeated framing rather than inner life. Favor forceful, concrete outward narration while preserving flexible inward extent and each speaker's actual register; rhythm follows the scene and person rather than a universal short-sentence rule. No injury, sacrifice, or bitter ending is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the scene as one or more documents, each with a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. A document may be a letter, note, log entry, list, or other artifact appropriate to the scene. Do not rotate through available formats; use the form the current situation calls for.\n\nLet an actual phrase, preference, or everyday act give the document particularity. A letter may mention a familiar joke without explanation, or describe a shared task in terms only the recipient would understand. A log entry may record a practical detail that matters to no one else. Such details can give the document its authentic texture without needing a later payoff.\n\nKeep composition, delivery, reading, and response distinct. Distinguish the composed letter from its actual delivery and reception. Its text may arrive intact and be accurately understood; delay, missing pages, or rereading matter only when they actually occur. Show only what each document itself can show; do not insert an omniscient explanation after every record.\n\nLater commentary may confirm or qualify an earlier entry. If a later document reveals that an earlier phrase meant more than it seemed, show the change through the later writer's words and knowledge, not by altering the earlier document's original intention. A casual request cannot become a deliberate message unless the writer actually intended it at the time.\n\nPreserve actual nonspoken experience beyond the artifact. The document's writer may have private thoughts, hesitations, or memories that never appear in the text; render those in the surrounding narration or as clearly separated interiority, not as if the document were a confession. Comfort, refusal, or continued connection may remain unexpressed in writing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend narrative time on a specific kindness, familiar preference, or comfortable presence. Instead of summarizing that \"they were kind\" or \"the room was cozy,\" show the concrete details: someone pours tea into a favorite cup, chooses a chair near the window, remembers which blanket is preferred, or leaves a portion of food because they know the other person likes it. Give comfort a particular expression, including direct affection or pleasure spoken plainly. A declaration can itself be the shared act; productive work or an unspoken physical gesture is not required to make warmth real.\n\nPreserve each person's pleasure, privacy, and choice. A recipient may accept a kindness, decline it, or set it aside for later. One person may enjoy quiet company without speaking; another may want to talk about something unrelated. Do not force a confession, a gratitude scene, or an emotional breakthrough. The comfort is in the ease of the moment, not in its outcome.\n\nIf a routine or phrase is established, let it continue without requiring new meaning. A weekly meal, a usual greeting, or a familiar joke can simply happen again. New acquaintances may offer something particular—an umbrella, a snack, a seat—without instantly forming a lifelong habit. The value is in the act itself, not in what it proves about the future.\n\nAllow the response to be partial or awkward without canceling the comfort. A joke may fall flat and still be affectionate; a thanks may be mumbled and still be sincere; a companion may do the wrong thing in the right spirit. Keep each person's private experience separate from what they show. The scene may end with comfort simply underway, no lesson, no foreshadowing, no repaired trust.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen the scene with a present exchange, phrase, preference, or practical contribution, then deliberately widen the paragraph to include the social and relational history the narrator may know. For example, a character's habit of setting the table a certain way can lead into a paragraph about whose house this was, what meals used to be like, or what changed in the neighborhood. Keep the widening connected to the immediate detail; do not produce a generic historical summary.\n\nAfter the widening, return to the present use or interpretation of the detail. The character who set the table may now do so without thinking, or may notice that the arrangement no longer matches the room. The return should show the detail's current meaning, which may differ from its past meaning or remain the same. Do not force a revelation or a lesson; continuity is a valid outcome.\n\nPreserve private reasons separately from public accounts and others' knowledge. The narrator may know what a character thinks and feels, but other characters may not. A public explanation for an absence may differ from the private memory. Use the narrative distance to frame what is known by whom, not to explain everything to the reader.\n\nUse deliberate transitions between paragraphs. A paragraph may move from a physical gesture to a social observation, then to a private memory, then back to the room. The movement should feel composed, not automatic. Do not require omniscience: if the chosen narrator is limited, stay within that access. The scene may end with the formal movement complete but the relationship unresolved, the private feeling recognized without being spoken, or the present exchange simply understood in a larger context.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nSituate the telling in its actual present occasion, which embedded episodes may share: who is telling it, in whose company, for what immediate purpose such as entertaining, comforting, explaining, or simply passing a shared interval. Before the embedded story, establish why this material is being offered now, using a concrete detail from the actual scene—a task, a meal, a delay, a request—so its place in the encounter is intelligible, whether it is welcomed, unsolicited, or left unanswered.\n\nWhen the account begins, select the particular phrases, objects, routines, or acts that belong to the actual account and its listeners, without a fixed number or an invented shared history. Present them as the teller remembers them at that moment, without retroactively loading them with hidden meaning. A familiar detail may recur because it is simply familiar; its later significance, if any, must arise at its own time, not as planted evidence of a secret understanding.\n\nAllow the listeners to respond during or after the telling according to their established relationships. They may interrupt with recognition, attend quietly, challenge a detail, recall a related moment of their own, or enjoy the story without needing to learn anything new. Keep observable responses distinct from interpretation and any authorized private reception; a reaction does not by itself certify a state of mind or a single correct meaning.\n\nKeep the narration's live private experience distinct from the told account. Render the authorized focal person's actual unspoken thoughts, sensations, or questions at their actual point in the encounter, including during the telling. They need not wait until the story ends or replace the experience with a summary of its meaning. The teller may have told only part of what they felt; the listener may hear something the teller did not intend. Preserve this difference without forcing a confessional turn or a public reconciliation.\n\nLet the active boundary govern the pause, including within the account, inward listening, continued company, or an actual conclusion. No completed reception or extra outward act is required. Do not require the embedded story to justify itself through a solution, moral, or emotional payoff; its placement in ordinary lived company is sufficient.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the route of social and developmental realism: follow a particular expectation of belonging or responsibility as it meets an actual act of shared life, over more than one occasion if the scene permits. Begin by identifying the person's concrete expectation—such as being included in a regular task, being answered in a certain way, or being trusted with a particular responsibility—and show how a real action, agreement, or continuance of routine confirms, questions, or leaves it unchanged in this specific instance.\n\nDo not treat the encounter as a lesson. The person acted on the understanding they had at that time; later information must not rewrite their earlier motive as secret preparation for growth. If a subsequent occasion changes the meaning of a familiar phrase or habit, anchor the change in what actually occurred between, not in hindsight inserted into the earlier scene.\n\nWhere an established detail of shared continuity bears on later interactions, preserve its actual recurrence and use. A phrase, gesture, or object need not recur before that history exists or appear in every encounter. It may simply remain familiar, or it may acquire fresh weight because of an intervening event. The point is the lived connection, not a revelation the detail was hiding. Let each present exchange occur with its own terms intact: people may cooperate, refuse, comfort, or fail to help without either side being cast as the unwitting instrument of another's development.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and complete private experience. In the selected Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation, render the authorized person's actual thoughts, uncertainties, or reactions in their own time, without erasing them to protect a later plot point. Known motives remain available at their original time, including sincere care that was never intended as a teaching device. A person may be helped or cared for simply because the other wanted to do so, and the relationship may continue without gratitude, self-reproach, or a transformed self-image.\n\nEnd at a natural pause or continuation of the shared life, not necessarily at a completed reflection. The expectation may still be forming; the routine may continue incompletely. The aim is to render a social reality in which ordinary participation, dependency, and belonging carry weight without being forced into a moral summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhen inherited authority or dependency actually bears on the present relationship, make its terms concrete through an operable detail of the scene: a place only one may enter, a resource only one may give, a permission that must be asked, or a condition attached to an ordinary kindness. Show the practical terms of the old arrangement as they affect this particular exchange, without inventing supernatural mechanics or a secret institutional rule.\n\nWhere a familiar shared act, routine, or phrase is established, let it carry its actual meaning to each participant. A present invitation or ordinary contribution can also stand without invented repetition. One may find comfort in continuity; the other may tolerate it, resent it, or depend on it without either response certifying hidden devotion, corruption, or entitlement. Private desire and known terms remain distinct: a sincere offer can coexist with awareness of the power that makes it possible, and refusal does not have to be punished as ingratitude.\n\nAttend to the material texture of the setting with concrete urban or domestic detail: worn furniture, a locked door, a recurring errand, a particular view from a window. These elements should situate the old authority in present daily life, not merely create an oppressive mood. Let small repeated objects or phrases acquire weight through their place in the characters' negotiated lives, not as symbols of a concealed truth.\n\nLet any change of outward presentation follow the actual person and circumstances, including chosen openness or an involuntary response. Trusted company may matter, but it is not a universal prerequisite and no mask must fall. A moment of openness need not reveal a hidden agenda; it may simply show that one person is tired, fond, frightened, or relieved. Preserve both public and private presentations as part of the relationship, without forcing a confession or a theatrical unmasking.\n\nFollow the active boundary within consideration, pending response, an unchanged arrangement, or an actual conclusion. The current act or private concern is situated inside the supplied structure of power and dependency, and each person's actual response—help, trust, caution, refusal—remains part of that lived history.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nEmploy lyric and philosophical inwardness for the authorized focal mind. Choose a current question about care, belonging, or obligation—often arising from a specific shared act, a repeated phrase, a familiar object, or a memory of such an occasion. State the question plainly at first, then let it be examined through the mind's own movement between concrete perception and abstract consideration, returning to the terms with familiar meaning, supported new qualification, or continuing uncertainty.\n\nUse deliberate returns to sustain the consideration without requiring a reversal; an actual event may still prompt a gradual or sudden change. A remembered phrase, a physical sensation, or a practical detail from ordinary life may be revisited with slight variation, allowing feeling to exceed the argument while the line of thought remains legible. The prose may become meditative, but it must stay anchored in the person's actual experience and the relationship that occasioned the question.\n\nDo not require the consideration to reach a wiser conclusion, a refutation of an earlier belief, or a public statement. The person may still be uncertain, may feel both affection and resentment, or may simply acknowledge that the question remains open. If a later event changes their understanding, place that changed understanding at its actual time, whether gradual or sudden, without rewriting the earlier events, motives, or knowledge.\n\nKeep the authorized mind distinct from any other character, and from any narrator who is not that character. If the narration includes another person's speech or account, render it as externally observed or attributed, not merged with the focal mind. The philosophical voice belongs to this person; others remain people acting and speaking on their own terms.\n\nClose the passage at a natural pause in the consideration, not a forced conclusion. The ongoing question may be laid aside for practical activity, interrupted by someone's arrival, or simply held in incomplete form. The value of the scene lies in the honest inward attention to what particular shared life means for this person now, not in arriving at a final answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nPresent the narrative as a diary or reflective miscellany kept by a situated recorder who is compatible with the active narrator and person. Establish enough context for the recording situation—the actual time and place, and any state of mind the narrator is authorized to know—without letting the recording frame replace the substance of the account.\n\nSelect an actual shared phrase, preference, pleasure, task, or unfinished concern that mattered to the recorder at the time. Write the entry around that particular detail, letting it anchor the day's significance. A repeated phrase or routine may appear again in a later entry without needing to be interpreted as foreshadowing; its recurrence earns meaning through the recorder's continued attention, not through a secret agenda planted in the first occurrence.\n\nIf the miscellany includes later annotations or reflections, separate them explicitly from the original entry by their time and occasion. A later note may comment on an earlier event, but it must not alter the words of the original to match its current understanding. Preserve the original knowledge at the original time: what the recorder did not know then should not be silently inserted into the past entry.\n\nKeep actual live private experience beyond the written selection. The diary contains what the recorder chose to record; it does not automatically capture their complete mind at that moment. When the active presentation allows interiority, render the unspoken or unwritten thoughts of the authorized person separately from what the document says, so that the record's omissions remain distinct from the person's experience.\n\nDo not require each entry to end with a new insight, moral, or seasonal image. The style may include such reflections if the recorder would naturally make them, but ordinary entries can simply note what happened, what pleased or troubled the writer, and what remains unfinished. Follow the active boundary, including within continuing writing or reflection, an ordinary interval, or a completed entry; neither a whole artifact nor completed understanding is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic and nonlinear organization for the permitted points of view. Choose a concrete shared element—a particular phrase, an object, a place, or a repeated act—that appears in more than one account and serves as a common reference without collapsing the different perspectives into one voice.\n\nPresent each account in its own time and source. If the narrative includes attributed speech, documents, or recollections, identify who is speaking or writing and when they hold this understanding. Move among these sources out of chronological order only where the juxtaposition clarifies each person's separate relation to the shared matter; do not let the reader confuse a later account with earlier knowledge.\n\nAllow different people to have understood the same care, agreement, or routine accurately, differently, or only partly. Their interpretations may remain stable, even in direct contrast, without one needing to be exposed as false or incomplete by a later revelation. When a later event qualifies an earlier interpretation, state that later event explicitly; preserve the original acts and motives rather than rewriting them to manufacture a hidden agenda. A later account may reveal an earlier intention when that is actually supported, without making such a discovery compulsory.\n\nPreserve actual private experience beyond the attributed records. If a section uses a letter, diary, or testimony, that document contains what its author chose to record; it is not the same as their complete contemporaneous mind. In the selected Visible or Hidden nonspoken presentation for an authorized person, render their unspoken thoughts separately from what they said or wrote, so that the multiplicity of positions remains genuine.\n\nDo not force the accounts to converge on a single moral or a complete picture. The passage may end with one account still open, another interrupted, or the reader able to see a pattern without any character announcing it. The method's value is the layering of distinct human relations to ordinary shared life, not the solution of whose version is authoritative.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaman::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene by orienting the actual people, occasion, and relevant physical context in a few direct sentences. State who is present and what they are doing or about to do, keeping spatial changes simple so the reader always knows where the action is occurring. Use readable paragraphing around relevant changes of speaker, action, or attention without requiring every event or inward movement to fit a short paragraph.\n\nMove readably among speech, activity, and authorized inner response without a fixed cycle. When private experience belongs to the focal person, give the actual thought, perception, or feeling its needed extent, without a brevity limit or a required return to outward movement before the boundary. The inner response need not be compressed into a single exclamation or dramatic pause; give it enough concrete detail to feel like this person's actual experience, not a generic beat.\n\nUse an established preference, phrase, or routine to connect moments across the scene or with previous scenes. A familiar detail may simply recur without a flashback; if background is needed, give it the needed extent within the active narrator's actual knowledge, without creating extra access or requiring the focal character to stop and think it. Do not manufacture a retrospective scene merely to give the repeated detail more weight.\n\nKeep developed feeling and uncertainty distinct from immediate utility. A character may continue to think about something after acting, or may act while still uncertain, without the narration forcing a clear resolution. Humor and seriousness belong to the appropriate characters: some may joke, others remain reserved, and the same person may move between tones depending on the moment. Do not require wit or banter from every speaker.\n\nEnd the passage at a natural boundary within the interaction—either in the midst of conversation, after a completed exchange, or at a pause in activity. The scene may leave a question open, a plan incomplete, or an emotional state still developing. There is no need to finish with a revelation or a cliffhanger; the continuation should arise from the existing scene and the characters' ongoing concerns.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Lyrical Prose\n\nDevelop a scene-grounded sound, texture, or comparison through the active narrator's permitted attention. Let syntax, recurring sounds or images, and shifts of attention extend an experience across the paragraph rather than decorate it. A familiar image may retain ordinary warmth or acquire supported new weight after an event, while plain clauses keep the people, practical activity, and event times recoverable. Preserve the earlier significance of a repeated object, routine, or phrase instead of treating every recurrence as foreshadowing or proof of a hidden meaning.\n\nLet the image develop what the narrator understands or makes present, not what the scene should later become. A comparison can unfold when it changes the narrator's attention to the other person or the shared occasion; it need not announce loss, longing, or a completed symbolic arc. Actual private feeling may be directly named or developed associatively. A character can notice the particular sound of a familiar task, the texture of an object handed over, or the way a repeated phrase settles differently without being required to act on that awareness.\n\nKeep the lyric movement attached to its owner. The narrator's figurative register need not become the dialogue register of every speaker, and another character's private experience remains separate unless the active contract authorizes access. Plain statements can provide contrast and keep the scene's practical terms legible. The account may end within ordinary company or thought, with the relationship continuing under its present conditions rather than reaching a lesson or revelation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a current act or detail recall a real earlier occasion, distinguishing what was known and available then from now. The recollection should arise from the scene's actual particulars: a familiar object, routine, phrase, or place can bring an earlier shared moment into the present account. Keep the earlier occasion's original knowledge intact. A person may have understood a kindness at the time, or may only now recognize what it meant; do not rewrite the past as hidden foreshadowing.\n\nAttend to small irreversible changes and unfinished attachment without making every present pleasure feel diminished. A routine may continue, feel different, or remain ordinary. Present comfort, humor, and continuing care can coexist with the awareness of what is no longer available. Give private longing, uncertainty, or relief room without requiring it to alter behavior or produce a confession. The narrator may reflect directly or let a detail carry the association, according to the active perspective and diction.\n\nDo not invent an absence, damage, failed reunion, hidden farewell, or death to create the tone. An actual departure or continued company may follow the active scene, but no miraculous return or concluding statement of impermanence is supplied. The account may close with the relationship still in motion, the familiar life still being lived beside what has changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse framed oral storytelling. Situate an actual teller and audience, preserving what each person knows and why the account belongs to the present occasion. The telling has a purpose in the current relationship: it may entertain, persuade, delay, warn, comfort, or simply pass time in company. Let the teller's selection follow that purpose and the teller's own knowledge rather than an omniscient summary of the remembered event.\n\nA familiar story can be told without revealing a secret. A participant may already have understood its kindness at the time, or may hear a detail differently now. Keep original event knowledge distinct from hindsight; the teller's present interpretation does not retroactively become the earlier motive. The account may be accurate or imperfect according to its source, and the audience may receive it with warmth, disagreement, distraction, or quiet recognition without a required response.\n\nActual private experience remains beyond performed speech. A teller's public account is not the complete live mind, and a listener's reaction is not automatically visible to the teller. Preserve the active narrator's access and the scene's practical terms. The telling may be unfinished, entertaining, or unproductive; it need not repair a bond, force a confession, or deliver a lesson before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the actual phrase, preference, act, or spatial relation needed for the current account. Use a small number of exact details and direct clauses. Omit reiterated interpretation rather than causes or feeling; what is omitted should remain inferable from the selected particular. A familiar detail can remain ordinary or gain supported significance after an event, but neither silence nor a brief answer proves concealed affection or hurt.\n\nLet the chosen particular carry the relationship's present terms. A repeated object, routine, or phrase may connect moments when it belongs, while new acquaintances need no fabricated history. Preserve the active narrator's access and individual speech. A character's inward attention may develop at the extent its feeling, reasoning, memory, or uncertainty needs, whether or not it produces a decision; brevity is not a quota that removes private experience or turns every exchange into a performed fragment.\n\nKeep the scene's practical terms legible without restating them. The boundary needs no new understanding, completed task, or prepared next move. The account may end with the relationship continuing under its present conditions, the exact detail left to do its work without an explanatory summary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose with natural movement among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient without recap machinery. Let the narrator's voice, the immediate scene, and the people present set the register and rhythm rather than a fixed tonal turn.\n\nLet a supported habit, object, routine, or preference connect moments when it belongs. A repeated particular can remain ordinary or acquire new weight after an event, but do not treat every recurrence as a hidden test, farewell, or planted reveal. Preserve original motives and knowledge: a later understanding does not retroactively change what a person knew or intended earlier. New acquaintances need no fabricated shared history.\n\nKeep private interpretation distinct from established fact and from what other characters know. Individual humor, tenderness, distance, or disagreement follow the particular people rather than a uniform emotional register. The active boundary may fall within ordinary company or thought, with the relationship continuing under its present conditions instead of reaching a lesson, confession, or completed emotional reframing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nMake the supplied relationship premise legible through scene selection, private language, repeated moments, and consequences that fulfill its particular promise. Select a current request, offer, preference, or established callback from the actual bond rather than inserting a roster of familiar tropes. A habit may continue unchanged or gain new weight after a supported event, without inventing an earlier hidden test, farewell, or motive simply to supply a reveal. A genuine earlier intention may become known when supported; keep the past act and its actual original meaning intact.\n\nKeep each person's private meaning, boundaries, and actual knowledge separate. Agreement and direct affection may be genuine; warmth need not conceal a wound, and a pause need not signal a confession. Give affection, jealousy, comfort, rivalry, or discovery enough space where it belongs, according to the actual people and their history. New relationships need no invented routine or shared past; the premise's particular promise can develop through what is actually offered, asked, withheld, or accepted in the present scene.\n\nLet the relationship remain contingent. No confession, sacrifice, synchronized recognition, repair, or perpetual continuation is required. The account may close with the bond familiar, newly forming, changing, or concluding, with each person's private experience distinct from what the other knows.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, recurring phrasing, and the relation of an act to what will be remembered. Use named acts and coherent recurrence to recount an actual contribution, refusal, stay, departure, or care at its proper scale. An errand remains an errand; a shared task remains a shared task. Do not promote ordinary life into heroic doom or mock combat.\n\nLet a familiar phrase or designation come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Let a phrase recur when it belongs to the actual account, with familiar or changed application. A repeated object, routine, or relationship can hold ordinary history or later gain supported weight without a new meaning on every return. Keep later reputation separate from original motives and private feeling. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail, or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern; the account may be accurate or imperfect according to its source.\n\nPreserve the participant's actual experience at the time. Being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic. Keep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. The account may end with business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf. No heroic promotion, hidden sacrifice, community verdict, or completed deed before the boundary is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene through social and domestic realism: keep the actual arrangement of care, work, space, preferences, and ordinary company visible while the people in it continue their separate concerns. A shared routine does not need to produce a chore around every exchange. Show who is already doing what, what they notice, and what they leave for later, so the relationship exists inside practical life rather than being extracted from it.\n\nLet a repeated act, object, or phrase belong to its participants in different or the same way. One person may treat a familiar task as ordinary maintenance while another experiences it as attention, obligation, or evidence of a change. Preserve that private feeling even when it has no immediate usefulness to anyone else. A later event may alter the arrangement, but do not rewrite all prior care as foreshadowing or make the earlier feeling secretly false.\n\nWhen the scene turns to a particular exchange, ground it in the actual place and occasion, including rest or company without a task. Let speech follow the people and their relationship; they may directly name a feeling or attend to ordinary activity without an obligatory emotional summary. A person can answer plainly, keep working, or pause for an actual reason. Private interpretation may differ from what is said without becoming a hidden truth the scene must expose.\n\nDo not supply a household structure, national reserve, improved group mood, or emotional repayment. The realism is in the concurrent ordinary life and the specific attachments it sustains, not in a lesson about belonging or a closing moment of shared understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Screenplay\n\nEstablish the current encounter through concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings only when location or time changes. Show what is visible and audible: who is present, what they are doing, and what they handle or set down. Let an actual phrase, gesture, object, or routine recur through a justified cut or continuous action. The recurrence may carry familiar or changed significance, but do not make it an automatic emotional reveal or a visual shorthand for what someone truly feels.\n\nKeep chronology, positions, and narrator permissions intact. A cut may move the scene forward or to another supported location, but it must not imply knowledge the focal character does not have or rearrange events to force a payoff. When a private experience is required, render it as clearly attributed nonspoken material separate from voice-over. Voice-over is performed speech with its own audience and purpose; it is not the default destination for thought.\n\nLet dialogue fit the relationship and the immediate practical situation. A character can speak while working, answer briefly, or leave something unsaid for an actual reason. Do not require a reaction shot, flashback, meaningful loss, or concluding breakthrough. The scene may end on a completed exchange, an ordinary continuation, or a supported interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Stage Play\n\nLocate speakers, objects, entrances, exits, and ordinary activity as the actual scene supports. Give each piece of physical business a playable form: who is near the table, who is leaving, what is being carried or set down. Let a repeated phrase or gesture accompany care, disagreement, or unchanged company without requiring everyone to share its interpretation. A familiar line may be a private reference for one person and ordinary speech for another.\n\nPreserve wider events through credible reports, sounds, or traces. A character can mention what happened elsewhere, but the stage need not manufacture a reaction from every person present. Keep pauses and silences as part of the public scene, not as coded confessions. A pause may mean distraction, fatigue, consideration, or nothing in particular.\n\nActual private experience stays separately nonspoken from asides and monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech with a dramatic purpose, not a substitute for required interiority. A scene can pause within thought, ordinary company, or a completed exchange without revelation or rearranged staging. Do not force a final tableau or a moment of shared recognition.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow actual associative succession through the authorized mind: perception, memory, imagined response, interruption, and continuing concern may move into one another without a linear report. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations while enough orientation remains to distinguish present events from recollection or fantasy. A shared habit or phrase can return because it belongs to the person's attention, with familiar, changed, or unsettled meaning.\n\nPreserve the original act and how the character understood it at the time. Later supported knowledge may change an interpretation, but do not rewrite earlier motives or invent suspicion merely to make familiar care conceal something. If a later event changes what a memory suggests, carry that change at its actual time rather than inserting it into the earlier moment. Distinguish imagined events from present ones: a feared reply, a remembered voice, and a current sound are not the same occurrence.\n\nPrivate experience may continue without a new choice, material interruption, or outward return. The mind can move from a present task to a remembered room to an anticipated conversation and back without the scene requiring a decision or a completed realization. Prose length does not set fictional time; a brief perception may occupy a long sentence, and a long interval may pass in a short one. Do not make every recurring phrase resolve into a hidden meaning or a lesson about the relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages and only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may type in fragments, another in complete sentences, another may leave a message unsent or edit it before sending. A routine message may be ordinary, comforting, annoying, or newly meaningful according to actual history. New acquaintances need no fixed greeting.\n\nDistinguish composed drafts and actual dictation from imagined wording, transmission, receipt, comprehension, and action. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A sent message is not proof of receipt, and receipt is not proof of comprehension or action. Preserve other knowledge channels and full nonspoken experience beyond the transcript. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nDo not make transmission states prove a relationship's condition. A delayed reply, read receipt, or edit may matter for an actual reason, but it is not an automatic sign of feeling. No delayed reply, confession, mirrored feeling, or emotional shift is owed. Shared contact can matter for its own sake without advancing work or preparing a later reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabamba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Visual Novel\n\nOrient through a relevant place, object, or gesture and move among attributed dialogue, focal narration, and actual private attention as needed. Use concise scene-setting and readable dialogue attribution in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. A background or expression cue may orient the moment, but it is not an automatic truth indicator or a substitute for what the focal person actually thinks.\n\nA supported return to a cue may sustain its familiar meaning or change after an actual event, without retroactively making earlier perception a secret farewell. If a particular object or gesture mattered before, its recurrence can deepen understanding rather than restart the same suspicion. Preserve developed inner life during quick exchanges: a brief reply may accompany a longer private response, and that response belongs to the focal person alone.\n\nKeep visible cues distinct from motive or another person's knowledge. Another character does not know a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. No fixed reaction cycle, new image feature, menu, romance route, or final revelation follows from the style. The scene may end on an ordinary continuation, a completed exchange, or a supported interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabambb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Jun Maeda / Web Serial\n\nRecover only the prior act, phrase, arrangement, or uncertainty needed to reenter the current occasion. Briefly restore the necessary particular rather than retelling the history that first established it. Preserve what people knew and intended then, carrying any later interpretation at its actual time rather than rewriting old kindness into foreshadowing. A familiar routine may remain meaningful or ordinary, and significant change follows real events.\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across replies: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Let ordinary life continue around the relationship and keep required inward experience available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. Private life and independent concerns may proceed without a cliffhanger, confession, sacrifice, resolved bond, or prepared next move as an installment fee.\n\nThe installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Let actual shared terms remain familiar or acquire supported meaning over time. Settled answers and continuing uncertainty are both possible; serial form imposes no deadline for resolving the relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWhen a character faces a difficult request, admission, or return, make the practical terms of the moment concrete before or during the exchange. Identify what the person actually needs: a permission someone can grant, a resource someone controls, a schedule, a key, a signature, a place to stay, a shift covered, a promise released. Show how that specific condition works and any actual cost or commitment providing it would involve, allowing freely available help without a required sacrifice. A usable option may still be refused, resented, misunderstood, or accepted with conditions. Do not let the mere existence of a solution decide the scene.\n\nSeparate what can be done from what the character is willing to ask for. Let the request emerge through hesitation, indirection, a partial sentence, a practical question that stands in for the harder one, or a plain direct request whose felt cost may be expected, surprising, or unchanged. The other person may recognize the need before it is fully stated, or may answer the surface question and miss the rest. If a mechanism or arrangement is misunderstood, show the misunderstanding in the actual exchange: the wrong assumption about who can authorize something, the overlooked deadline, the resource that exists but cannot be reached. A correction may change understood options without changing physical conditions or settling the relationship. It may also remain unmade while the misunderstanding persists.\n\nRender private cost through the character's actual attention and reasoning. They may notice what the request will obligate them to later, what it admits about their situation, or what they are choosing not to say. The cost can appear as a long pause before speaking, a rehearsed sentence that comes out differently, relief that curdles, or a refusal that hurts to deliver. Keep the private experience inside the active narrator's access; the other person does not need to see it. Warmth and anger can share the same scene, and a partial or awkward comfort may be the only resolution available. Do not require visible strain, a sacrificed value, or a completed decision at the end of the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the reply as one coherent shared interval. Open with enough spatial and situational grounding for the reader to know who is present, where they are, and what is actually happening, including conversation, waiting, or private consideration without an invented practical task. Use readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution: a new speaker, a clear action, or a direct response should not require the reader to guess who is talking. Keep the physical sequence easy to follow even when the conversation shifts quickly.\n\nWhen an arrangement, rule, or resource affects what someone can actually do, explain it at the point of use. If a character needs a pass, a schedule, a vehicle, a supervisor's approval, or a place that is only open at certain hours, make that condition legible in the scene. Show the character encountering the condition through dialogue or action: they check the time, ask who can authorize it, realize the door is locked, or explain why the usual method will not work. Do not turn the explanation into a lecture; let it surface as part of the exchange.\n\nAfter the condition is clear, follow whatever response actually occurs. A character may understand, help, refuse, ask for more information, change the subject, or pursue another concern. NPCs remain active: they can offer an alternative, remember a relevant fact, decline without becoming hostile, or leave to handle their own business. Let private thought, waiting, or a pending offer carry part of the interval where the active contract permits it. End at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene, without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social realism as the definite route, with satire available only where the actual material invites it. Organize the passage around how a work unit, household, public office, school, clinic, market, or other established arrangement is used by people in different positions. Do not describe the institution in the abstract; show a particular person moving through its actual procedures, documents, queues, permissions, debts, or duties. The same rule can mean one thing to the person who enforces it and another to the person who needs something from it.\n\nWhen a rule or resource affects a choice, explain its operation through the lived interaction. A character may know exactly which form to request, or may misunderstand who has authority, or may discover that the resource exists but is allocated by a criterion they do not meet. Let the explanation arise from the scene: a clerk's question, a neighbor's advice, a posted notice, a supervisor's refusal, or the practical work of assembling what is required. Real competence and agreement are possible. A request can succeed without corruption, and a refusal can be lawful, arbitrary, or simply the result of scarce resources. Do not force satire into every exchange.\n\nKeep the focus on what the arrangement does to the people involved. Show what a practical outcome actually affects or leaves unchanged in someone's obligations, standing, or sense of what they can ask for; no new social or inward change is required. Private interpretation may differ from the public record: a character can comply while resenting it, accept help while feeling diminished, or misunderstand a rule without being foolish. Let the social positions remain distinct rather than flattening everyone into the same perspective. The ending may leave the request unresolved or the arrangement unchanged; what matters is that the reader sees how the institution actually operates through this person's experience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite in hard-edged, concrete prose. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions: move from one beat to the next without smoothing over the friction. Name the material facts that matter: a cracked phone screen, a bus that will not come, a lock that sticks, a paycheck that arrives late, a chair pulled too close, a cup set down hard. Let wear and physical stakes stay grounded in the actual scene rather than imported from a survival scenario.\n\nWhen a practical condition bears on a choice, make it immediate and specific. A character may be out of cash, out of time, locked out, understaffed, over the weight limit, past the deadline, or holding the only key. Show the condition through actual use or response: a card may work or decline, a key may open the door, a shift may be covered, or a form may meet a real requirement. Preserve pending outcomes without manufacturing failure. Keep the physical sequence clear if activity changes rapidly. If a resource is lost, distinguish the actual loss from an imagined one; do not let panic invent a catastrophe the scene does not support.\n\nLet actual feeling or reasoning expand where it needs to. A longer thought is legitimate when the character is working through what a choice will cost, what they owe someone, or why a refusal hurts. The prose can slow for that interior work, then snap back to action or dialogue. Do not make the character's feelings a direct product of bodily pressure: exhaustion, pain, or cold can shape attention without dictating what the person decides. The ending owes no visible response, no damage, and no completed decision. Stop when the material and emotional sequence has reached a natural pause.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the story through actual documents. Each entry must have a clear writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene requires: a letter, a request form, a supervisor's note, a lease amendment, a shift schedule, a receipt, a text message, a report, or a journal entry. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. Let each document reflect what its writer would actually record for that audience and occasion.\n\nShow a relevant condition in the document's own wording. A proposal should read as a proposal, an authorization as an authorization, a report as a report, an acceptance as an acceptance. Distinguish these forms: a request that asks for permission is not the same as a notice that permission has been granted, and a report of what happened is not the same as a promise of what will happen. Let the exact terms matter: a deadline, a condition, a named person, a missing signature, a phrase that can be read two ways. A later record can clarify or continue a matter without requiring contradiction. The writer may have understood the situation imperfectly, or may have chosen to leave something out.\n\nPreserve actual private experience separately from the written account. A letter or report contains what its author records, not their complete live mind. If the active contract permits interiority, render the character's private reasoning, hesitation, or feeling in the appropriate visible or hidden form, distinct from the document itself. Do not pretend that a composed draft is the same as live thought, and do not let the document's omissions become the only evidence of what the writer felt. The sequence should remain readable across entries, with gaps and revisions revealing events without an omniscient explanation after every record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nShow a feasible gesture of care through the condition that makes it welcome. Attend to timing, access, permission, or how something is used. A character may bring the right food because they remembered what the other person actually likes, offer a ride at the hour it is needed, leave a door unlocked, make a call the other person dreaded, or simply stay nearby without demanding conversation. Let the practical detail carry the warmth: the cup placed within reach, the blanket unfolded without being asked, the shift traded before the other person has to beg.\n\nGround the care in this recipient's actual preference and autonomy. The offer may already fit without a correction. A character can accept it as it is, ask for different help, or say what they would rather have. Do not make the helper's efficiency or the recipient's gratitude the measure of success. The comfort can be partial: a good meal eaten in tired silence, a favor accepted with embarrassment, a kindness that does not fix the underlying problem. Let personality and privacy remain intact. The recipient does not have to explain everything, and the helper does not have to become a savior.\n\nLet warmth and private experience develop without forcing emotional repair. A character may notice that they feel safer, less alone, or simply less cold, without announcing it. The scene can end with the practical care still in progress: dishes being washed, a ride underway, a quiet room shared. One occasion need not change a larger relationship pattern. The reply should make the care legible and particular, not turn every comforting moment into a confession or a solved problem.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Open with enough narrative distance to frame the scene through its social context, institutional history, or public significance where the chosen narrator permits it. A paragraph may move from the room to the arrangement that governs it, from a character's hesitation to the obligations that make the hesitation legible, or from a practical detail to the history that gives it weight. Keep the prose composed rather than conversational; distinguish narration from ordinary spoken language without making every character speak in the narrator's register.\n\nExplain a consequential arrangement through precise particulars. If a decision turns on a lease, a contract, a policy, a family obligation, a professional duty, or a public rule, show how that arrangement actually operates: who has authority, what the terms require, what happens if they are not met. Relate the arrangement to the people's actual values and interpretations. A character may accept a rule as fair, resent it as arbitrary, misunderstand its scope, or use it to protect someone. Do not let the mechanism replace the people; the paragraph should move from the arrangement to the particular choice it shapes.\n\nLet the paragraph movement sustain uncertainty or confirm an understanding without requiring a changed judgment. The narration may hold a question open across several sentences, weigh two interpretations, or settle one only provisionally. Private experience remains distinct from public presentation: a character can perform composure while the narration, within its access, shows what the performance costs. The ending may leave the decision incomplete or the relationship unsettled. The formal architecture is the style; it does not require an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside a live occasion for telling: name who is present, what has prompted the account, and what the listener hopes to gain, avoid, or understand from hearing it. Let the outer scene remain active while the tale is told. A listener may interrupt to ask about timing, access, or a practical detail; a teller may pause because the next part is difficult to say, not because the story is finished.\n\nWhen the embedded account begins, make its consequential choice concrete. Show the condition the participants were working under: what they could use, what they believed it permitted, what they asked for, and what they actually did or left pending, including an option never used or a result not yet available. Explain the mechanism where it is relevant to the actual account and available knowledge, including instruction before use, private consideration, an informed refusal to act, or an attempted use. Keep the explanation integrated with that occasion rather than adding unrelated exposition. If the teller now knows more than the participants knew then, keep that later knowledge separate. Do not let the present teller silently correct the past or make the earlier choice look inevitable.\n\nReturn to the outer scene through the listener's actual response or continuing attention, without requiring a change or a summary of the tale's meaning. The listener may ask a question the tale does not answer, offer a practical response, repeat a phrase from the account in a new tone, or sit with an implication the teller did not intend. The teller may accept that response, correct a factual point, or withhold a further explanation. A useful and accurate tale does not have to produce agreement, confession, or a new commitment. End at the active scene boundary, including within continued listening or an unfinished exchange; no changed state is owed before leaving the next move open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nBuild the scene around one actual procedure that bears on the encounter: an application, a permission, a schedule, a transfer, a household rule, or another supplied arrangement. Introduce its terms plainly enough that the reader can follow what the procedure is supposed to do. Then let the scene proceed through the procedure's own sequence. A character fills in a form, waits for a response, follows an instruction, or discovers that one step depends on another. Make relevant demands of the procedure legible without requiring every step to carry a personal cost or expose someone.\n\nLet the personal concern meet the procedure's actual operation, including a gap between stated purpose and lived result where one exists. A request that is technically complete may still leave the person uncertain whether they were understood. A rule may be applied correctly and still arrive at the wrong moment. A character may explain the mechanism to someone else and realize that the explanation does not cover what they actually feel. Do not force the mismatch into obstruction. The procedure can also work: a candid request may be accepted, a practical exception may be granted, or an institution may do exactly what it promised.\n\nUse absurdist construction as a way of ordering the scene, not as a diagnosis of the world. Let repetition, circular explanation, or a small disproportionate consequence make the procedure's logic visible. Keep the characters' responses particular. One may treat the absurdity as ordinary, another may argue with it, another may quietly find a way around it. The sequence should remain understandable even when the emotional result does not settle. Close at the active scene boundary, including an unfinished procedure or a continuing state; neither a produced result nor resolved personal meaning is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Gothic-Punk\n\nLocate the encounter in a concrete place where old authority is physically present: a room reached through a particular door, a key that must be requested, a desk that has outlasted several occupants, a privilege that still controls who may enter or act. Make the actual material conditions of access legible where relevant; a person may feel their importance before, during, or after understanding how they work. A character may have to wait in a specific chair, sign a ledger, speak to an intermediary, or observe a form of address that no longer matches the present situation.\n\nMake the practical interaction legible. Explain what the old authority actually permits, restricts, or requires in this case, and show the person deciding how to use it. The decision may involve a resource, a rule, a debt, or a right that one character understands better than another. Let the mechanism become clear at the point of use: who can open the door, what the signature changes, why the exception exists. Do not let the room's atmosphere stand in for evidence about what anyone wants or intends.\n\nLet private experience remain intimate and specific. A character may want to be trusted, fear being seen as asking for too much, refuse an offered privilege, or admit something the setting makes difficult to say. Social masks can fall away in trusted company, but they need not fall away completely. Warmth and pain may occupy the same exchange. The old authority may be used kindly, resentfully, or with practical care. Keep the moral complication in the actual choices and their costs, not in a general mood of corruption. End with the actual state of the encounter, including unchanged conditions, a refusal, or continuing private experience; no changed or accepted permission is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimonial accounts as the organizing form. When a character gives testimony, establish who is speaking, to whom, and for what purpose: a formal statement, an explanation to a family member, a report to a colleague, or an account offered in the hope of being believed. Let the speaker's position as participant, witness, or recipient shape what the account includes. A participant can say what they used, expected, received, or did; a witness can say what they saw and what they inferred; a recipient can say what they were told and what they understood.\n\nExplain the relevant interaction through the participant's actual encounter with it. If a rule or resource matters, show how the person came to know it, what they thought it permitted, and what happened when they acted on that understanding. Keep the participant's knowledge at the time of the event distinct from anything the speaker has learned since. A later explanation may appear, but it should not rewrite the earlier account or make the original choice seem inevitable.\n\nPreserve nonspoken experience outside the testimony. The active narrator may present another person's account while retaining access to the focal character's private thought, sensation, or uncertainty. A truthful account does not have to become a confession, and a listener does not have to correct the speaker. The testimony may serve its purpose, be stopped by the speaker, or remain unfinished at the active boundary or an actual interruption; completion is not owed. The scene may continue after the account is given, but the account itself should remain a composed act with its own limits.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse psychological realism as the organizing route. Enter the scene through a permitted person's attention: what they notice first, what they remember, what they say to themselves, and what they hesitate to say aloud. Let the narration follow their understanding of the situation rather than an external summary of it. A practical option should appear as something the person sees, considers, or avoids, not as an abstract mechanism introduced from outside.\n\nKeep the person's imagined possibility distinct from actual permission and capability. They may think a door is locked when it is not, assume a request will be refused, or believe they have authority they do not. Keep the account's distinction between belief and established fact clear within the active narrator's access. An actual opened door, granted request, or correction may disclose a difference, but no such event or correcting realization is required. The focal person does not need automatic insight into their own motives or the motives of others. They may misread a tone, overlook a clue, or understand something only after the moment has passed.\n\nLet the mind feel or hesitate at length. The narration may dwell on a remembered phrase, a bodily sensation, or an inward argument without needing to finish a working explanation. The practical interaction can remain unresolved while the psychological experience is fully rendered. Do not force the character to arrive at a lesson or a clean realization. End at the active scene boundary, including continuing thought or unchanged understanding, without a minimum inward shift.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic or nonlinear telling as the organizing form. Attribute each scene or account to a particular person and time. A section may open with a name, a date, a place, or a phrase that marks whose experience is being followed. Keep the order of presentation deliberate: a later scene may appear before an earlier one, or two accounts of the same arrangement may sit side by side. The juxtaposition should make comparison possible without telling the reader what to conclude.\n\nPreserve each occasion's actual rules, resources, and knowledge. A character in one time knows only what that time permits. A later use of the same arrangement may illuminate an earlier assumption, but it cannot retroactively change what the earlier participant knew or did. Show the practical mechanism as it appeared to each person: one may understand the rule, another may misunderstand it, a third may know only its effect. Different people may agree or diverge. Preserve any supported factual resolution without treating narrative prominence as proof or requiring accounts to remain equally uncertain.\n\nLet reader synthesis remain distinct from cast knowledge. The reader may see that two accounts contradict each other, that a later event explains an earlier silence, or that a shared phrase meant different things to different people. The characters do not need to arrive at that synthesis. Keep each voice's private experience intact, including what they withhold, misremember, or refuse to say. End at the active boundary, including within a still-unfolding juxtaposition; a new interpretation or character resolution is possible but not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate conversation or activity without a long setup. Name who is present and what they are doing in the first lines. If an unfamiliar name, object, or mechanism matters, explain it at the point where a character uses or refers to it. Keep the explanation brief and tied to the action: what the thing is, what it does, and why it matters now. Do not pause the scene for a glossary.\n\nMake quick changes of activity understandable through the relevant condition. If a character leaves, changes the subject, or tries a different approach, show what prompted the shift: a message, a memory, a practical obstacle, or something the other person said. The reader should be able to follow the physical sequence even when the emotional meaning remains unsettled. Let personal meaning remain open or become clear according to actual experience, without forcing an explanation of what the interaction taught anyone.\n\nBreak paragraphs by attention, not by a fixed action-dialogue-thought pattern. A paragraph may hold a long private reflection, a rapid exchange, or a single observed detail. Preserve lengthy private experience when the focal character needs it, and let ordinary speech sound like the person speaking. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. End at the active scene boundary, including within continuing private experience or an unchanged situation, without a required new position or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Lyrical Prose\n\nAnchor the lyrical movement in an object, sound, or physical condition that belongs to the present scene and to the practical exchange at hand. Let the image develop through the syntax as the authorized viewpoint attends to it, but keep the actual operation of the relevant arrangement clear: what is asked, what is available, what must be done, and what the other person can or cannot use. A plain sentence may state a material fact before or after an extended image so the feeling does not replace the mechanism.\n\nWhere an explanation or use of a rule meets the viewpoint's sensory attention, develop the particular detail or association that actually occurs, including a remembered one. No sensory coda is required after every explanation. Let the inward connection belong to the viewpoint, without requiring the association to model another mind or solve the practical problem. Return to the image where attention or experience brings it back, with application retained, changed, or unsettled rather than a compulsory refrain or new use.\n\nKeep private experience distinct from what the other person knows. The narration may dwell on a withheld answer, an unspoken hope, or a feeling that has no immediate use, while the dialogue continues with its own practical terms. Do not make the cast speak in the narrator's figurative register. A character may use ordinary, rough, or partial speech while the prose around them remains rhythmically shaped. The scene may end with the practical matter settled, postponed, or refused; the emotional state need not be resolved by the same turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet a present detail open the remembered occasion rather than announcing the memory as a completed reflection. The detail may be a familiar object, a repeated task, a phrase, or a condition of the room, but it must be traceable to the current scene. When the viewpoint recalls an earlier way of doing or sharing something, show the practical conditions as they were then: what the person knew, what resources were available, and what the arrangement actually allowed. Do not import later understanding into that earlier moment.\n\nReconnect with the present where the narrative movement calls for it, preserving actual continuity or change. Recognition may occur or remain incomplete; no changed conditions or insight are required. The character may understand the new conditions accurately and still feel the old attachment as present. A practical explanation can clarify how a rule, resource, or obligation now works, but it need not settle the emotional significance or require the character to adapt. The narration may hold both: the mechanism is understood, and the feeling remains unfinished.\n\nKeep the reflective movement within the authorized viewpoint. The character need not announce the memory to another person or turn it into a lesson. Dialogue may continue on an ordinary practical matter while the private association develops separately. Comfort or pleasure in the remembered occasion may remain genuine; do not convert every recollection into proof of loss. End with the present scene's actual state, not with a compulsory statement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route. Establish the retrospective teller and the earlier participant within the active narrator permissions, and keep their knowledge separate. The teller may know what the participant did not, but the earlier scene must be reconstructed with only the participant's then-available understanding of the practical conditions: what was asked, what was offered, what the arrangement seemed to allow, and what the participant believed about the other person's position.\n\nWhen a later explanation of a rule, resource, or obligation becomes relevant, let the retrospective teller supply it as later knowledge. It may confirm, correct, or qualify the earlier interpretation, but it cannot change what the participant wanted or decided at the time. The earlier motive remains what it was. The memoir's composed words are not the same as the participant's live private experience; where the active contract requires interiority, render the earlier private experience and the later reflection as distinct channels.\n\nLet the then-and-now vantage create meaning without requiring regret, vindication, or a settled moral. The teller may understand the mechanism now and still feel the earlier choice as personally significant. The memoir may move between the remembered scene and the present act of telling, but the return should be legible: what belongs to the earlier encounter and what belongs to the teller's later knowledge must not blur. End with the memoir's actual occasion, not with a forced summary of what the experience taught.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few exact particulars that let a reader understand what a request or response actually depends on. A rule, resource, or obligation should be explained only to the point where its effect on the present exchange is clear. Omit restatement of what the scene has already established. Direct clauses may carry complex feeling; actual private feeling, attention, memory, or reasoning can take the room it needs even if no visible action changes.\n\nKeep the private experience in the authorized viewpoint, without reducing it to a practical result. A person may communicate part of that experience through actual speech; reader access alone does not make it shared knowledge. A character may withhold an answer, accept an arrangement, or misunderstand a condition while the narration preserves authorized actual private experience, including feeling that does not serve an immediate practical purpose. The motive need not be summarized after the action. Let the selected detail carry the feeling: a held object, a pause, a name, or a repeated phrase may do the work of a longer explanation.\n\nDo not make brevity a quota. A precise extended sentence may serve better than several performed fragments. The scene may end with the practical matter settled or left open; the emotional state need not be resolved in the same turn. Preserve the active narrator and character agency. Omission should leave something inferable, not delete causality.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene first, with natural movement among activity, speech, observation, memory, and authorized private experience. Let the present encounter establish its own rhythm rather than arranging every beat around a problem to be solved. When a rule, resource, or obligation becomes relevant, explain it only where it affects what someone can use, offer, or understand. The explanation should arise from the interaction, not from a preparatory summary.\n\nFollow the actual response that the particular person makes. A workable arrangement may be left alone while personal concerns continue; the scene need not end with a test, correction, or solution. The practical mechanism remains legible, but the emotional consequence is not required to match its usefulness. A character may accept help without gratitude, refuse an option without drama, or misunderstand a condition without immediate correction.\n\nKeep private experience within the authorized viewpoint and separate from what other characters know. The narration may move from a spoken exchange to a memory or an unspoken feeling, then return to the room without announcing the shift. Use paragraphing and transitions to orient the reader without recap machinery. The scene may close on a practical matter settled, postponed, or simply continuing; do not impose a lesson or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual supplied relationship premise and history to choose the remembered condition, shared resource, private phrase, or repeated gesture that belongs to these particular people. The practical exchange should make that premise legible: a request, offer, or obligation carries the weight of what they have already shared or withheld. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes; the scene must fulfill the specific promise of the supplied relationship.\n\nShow how one person's actual preference affects the exchange where relevant. Help may already be apt, or an adjustment may be wanted. The other person need not correct the help, and the scene need not reach a milestone. Recognition, rivalry, or care can remain private while the practical matter continues. Explain a rule or resource where it is relevant to this exchange, including confirmation of known terms, reassurance, or ordinary discussion without new capability or understanding. Keep the explanation within the relationship's register and omit redundant exposition.\n\nKeep private language and repeated moments specific to the people in the supplied relationship, without reducing a larger relationship to a pair. A callback should be earned by the scene or the supplied history, not inserted as decoration. The emotional payoff may be partial, awkward, or quiet; warmth can coexist with strain. End with a continuation that follows from the exchange, not with a forced confession, reconciliation, or completed pattern.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a compact declaration that establishes the act, gift, or promise as something remembered, then expand into the practical interaction that made it possible where known. Keep the actual difficulty legible: what had to be obtained, repaired, carried, or arranged, and what the person understood at the time. The public account may be larger than the private one, but the means remain particular and the competence need not become legend.\n\nLet phrasing recur through established history or attention, preserving the same application where it still fits or changing it where experience warrants. The repeated designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet at every entrance. Let dialogue take its actual place alongside the chronicle: it may agree, question, address ordinary business, or leave the account uninterrupted. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to a detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody.\n\nKeep public reputation distinct from the authorized private account. Being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic. The private motive may be different without requiring contradiction or hidden shame. The account can end with the business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the essayistic route. Open the passage with a particular claim in the authorized narrator's terms about helping, obligation, or what a request permits. Make the claim specific enough to test: not that help is complicated, but that accepting help can alter who is allowed to ask next, or that an obligation survives the moment when the person who owed it understands the cost.\n\nBring that claim beside an actual, remembered, or clearly hypothetical exchange. Show the practical conditions of the exchange: what one person asks for, what the other can actually do, what relevant resources or rules permit or limit, and what each person treats as the point of the request. Explain the mechanism only where a character can use or misunderstand it. A character may know that a schedule, a shared account, a workplace rule, or a family arrangement makes one answer possible and another costly; the narration should make that legible without turning the person into a case study.\n\nReturn to the opening claim as the essayistic movement permits, including while the exchange remains unfinished and has produced no result. The return may confirm the claim, qualify it, or leave it uncertain. A practical explanation alone does not prove what another person felt; preserve any actual knowledge supplied by sources or authorized private access. A clear practical account can stand beside a private interpretation that remains different from it. Keep the essayistic movement in the narration rather than giving every speaker an analytical voice. The scene may end on the unresolved difference between what the arrangement permits and what someone is willing to accept.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use scene headings when location or time changes. Establish the practical conditions through what can be shown or heard: who is present, what object or space is available, what task is underway, and what someone does with it. A line of dialogue can state a rule, a cost, or a limit, but the action should show the actual consequence when it matters.\n\nLet the public exchange carry the request, refusal, acceptance, or misunderstanding. A visible change may result from an actual act: a person hands over a key, leaves a room, finishes a task, or stops answering. Do not let the visible result certify agreement or intention. The other person may accept a practical arrangement while privately understanding it differently, or comply without forgiving the request.\n\nPreserve required nonspoken experience separately from voice-over. Use the active visible or hidden interiority channel for the authorized person's actual private interpretation, reasoning, or feeling. Voice-over is performed speech when separately authorized, not the default destination for thought. Another character does not know a private conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation.\n\nKeep cuts in the real sequence. A cut may move to a later moment or another location, but it should not imply that the intervening private change has already been settled. The scene can stop after a completed action, an unanswered request, or a person leaving. No concluding reveal is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Stage Play\n\nUse clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the shared space make the terms of a personal request playable. If someone can reach a prop, leave through a door, see another person's face, or avoid being seen, preserve that spatial fact where relevant without requiring every visible affordance to determine the next action.\n\nShow the public exchange through the relationship between lines and physical business. A person may ask while continuing a task, set an object down before answering, or move toward the exit before the other has finished speaking. Let the practical conditions be stated or supported by offstage reports where appropriate. Not every cause must occur in the room; a character may enter already knowing that a schedule, a rule, or a prior conversation limits the answer.\n\nKeep actual private experience separate from asides and monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech when separately authorized, not the default destination for thought. Use the active visible or hidden interiority channel for the authorized person's private interpretation. The other character does not hear that private meaning merely because the audience has received it.\n\nDo not force the scene toward a demonstration or solution. A practical act may be refused, attempted, interrupted, or left unfinished. The scene can end with ongoing business, an unanswered request, or a person remaining in the room after the other has gone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Stream of Consciousness\n\nLet associative mental movement structure the telling. Braid perception, remembered words, bodily sensation, speculative options, and recurring concern through the particular authorized mind. Syntax and paragraph shape may follow those associations, so a practical question can surface through a remembered phrase, a physical discomfort, or an image from another time rather than through a clean summary.\n\nKeep the difference between imagined result, actual conditions, and actual action recoverable. When the mind runs through what could happen if a request is accepted or refused, mark that movement as speculation. Track an outside event at its actual time; the person may notice it, miss it, or continue thinking without a required interruption. A character may imagine explaining a rule to someone, then fail to say it, or may rehearse a refusal and then answer differently. The narration should not blur the imagined version into the actual exchange.\n\nUse Kamachi's practical legibility inside the associative flow. When a rule, resource, or immediate problem matters, render the person's actual attempt to understand its operation, distinguishing their partial or mistaken model from established conditions: what the schedule permits, what the shared account can cover, what the door being locked means for who can leave. The explanation may be partial, interrupted, or returned to later. Feeling and memory can extend beyond the practical use of the explanation; the mind may dwell on something irrelevant or unresolved without converting it into a decision.\n\nA new connection may occur or not. Private experience need not shorten into a spoken instruction. The passage may end with the person still turning the matter over, with an outside event breaking in, or with a practical action taken before the thought has settled.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse readable sender-attributed messages. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may send short messages quickly, another may leave a long pause before answering, and a third may edit a message after sending it. Do not make everyone communicate as an efficient task dispatcher.\n\nDistinguish a proposed condition from a received message, an understood request, and an accepted arrangement when the difference matters. A message may state what one person believes the other has asked for without that interpretation being correct. A reply may acknowledge a message without agreeing to its terms. A practical arrangement may be accepted through a specific message, while the private meaning of that acceptance remains separate.\n\nTreat unsent drafts as genuinely composed interface artifacts whose visibility follows the actual narrator access and interface. A draft is distinct from the writer's live private thought. Required live private experience has its own channel, including interpretation that no later message settles. A person may type a draft, delete it, and send something else; the private reason belongs to interiority unless the person actually communicates it, in which case only the sent wording enters the transcript.\n\nKeep context outside the exchange clearly separated from the transcript when required. A practical condition may be established by a message, a timestamp, or a brief separated context note. The scene can end with a pending response, a read receipt without a reply, or a message that changes the practical situation without resolving the personal one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and clear dialogue attribution to establish what is actually present or said. Let background or expression cues orient the moment: a familiar room, a changed posture, an object left on a table, or a pause before an answer. The cue may draw the focal person's attention, but it is not a truth indicator. A changed expression can accompany a lie, a distraction, or an unrelated memory; it cannot by itself prove a motive or agreement.\n\nFollow a cue into the authorized person's memory, inference, or feeling. The focal narration may notice that the other person has looked away, then recall another occasion when the same look meant something different, then return to the present exchange with that association still unresolved. Keep the private interpretation distinct from the practical cause. The other person does not automatically know the inference through reader access; preserve what they independently know or actually learn.\n\nMake practical changes follow supported events. If a rule, resource, or immediate problem matters, show its actual operation: what the schedule permits, what the shared space makes possible, what the other person can actually do. A request may be accepted, refused, or misunderstood without the scene turning into a demonstration of the rule. Private response can remain extensive or unresolved without a fixed rotation through reaction beats.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. The scene can end after a completed exchange, an unanswered request, or a focal realization that changes nothing visible yet.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabanbb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Kamachi Kazuma / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments. Recover a relevant earlier condition or exchange briefly when its present use needs context: a prior request, a limit that was accepted, a schedule that was changed, or a promise that was left conditional. Do not retell the whole history; bring back only the particular that now bears on what someone asks, withholds, accepts, or misunderstands.\n\nPreserve what really changed and what remains in force. If an arrangement was accepted in an earlier installment, it remains accepted unless a later event actually alters it. Useful help can remain useful. A person may still be waiting for an answer, or may have stopped expecting one. The narration should not treat the passage of time as automatic progress or automatic decay.\n\nLet the practical conditions stay legible. When a rule, resource, or immediate problem matters, show its actual operation in the present installment. A character may use a previously established fact correctly, misunderstand it, or notice a new implication without the story inventing a fresh contradiction. Required private experience may continue without advancing an arrangement; a person can think about the same request differently while nothing visible changes.\n\nEnd at the active interval boundary, including completed work, a pending response, continuing private experience, or ordinary activity; no result or acknowledgment is owed. A pending response need not become a cliffhanger or a new problem. The continuation point should follow from the scene: someone may still need to answer, or may have decided not to, or may have done something that changes the practical situation without resolving the personal one.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene around a concrete personal decision: an offer, admission, keeping, or letting go that matters to the people present. Develop the actual private strain and any effect it has on speech or conduct, without requiring a public sign or substituting a stock vocabulary of suffering. A person can ask for something while handling a familiar object too carefully, or accept help while noticing the temperature of the room more sharply than the words. Render those sensations in exact material terms—texture, scent, weight, the sound of a door or a cup set down—and let them carry the private feeling in its actual order.\n\nDo not make a beautiful or charged detail proof of what the other person wants. The viewpoint character may read a gesture as desire, refusal, or regret, but retain its attribution rather than making the cue itself proof. Actual facts, known motives or supported narrator judgment may confirm the reading. The other person's conduct remains their own. Acceptance and useful kindness can stand without confession, sacrifice, or repaired attachment. If a private thought is required by the active contract, write it as that character's actual present experience, not as a later explanation inserted into the earlier moment.\n\nLet the exchange remain unresolved where the scene supports it. A request can be answered only in part; an admission can change the room without ending the conversation. The next beat follows from what was actually said and handled, not from a compulsory emotional turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Chat Roleplay\n\nResolve the submitted material within the active contract's speaker and ownership boundaries. Give each character clear, unmistakable dialogue attribution and let the NPCs act from their own present motives. Do not make the reply a summary of what the scene means; write the shared interval in readable paragraphs so the next participant can see who is present, what is being handled, and what remains open.\n\nGround the exchange in a material particular that belongs to the encounter: the way a sleeve is turned back, the heat of a borrowed cup, the condition of a familiar room. Let that detail carry the viewpoint character's private attachment without controlling the other person's response. If the contract permits nonspoken interiority, write it as the character's actual present experience—at its needed extent, whether it aligns with speech or differs from it. It does not become a hidden script that forces NPCs to cooperate.\n\nEnd the reply at a natural continuation point that follows from what was actually done. A practical task can remain unfinished, a question can remain partly unanswered, or an NPC can leave with their own intention still in motion. The continuation should be available to the next participant without a forced question, cliffhanger, or emotional reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate domestic route. Place the scene in the supplied space and follow how the people actually use it now: who prepares, borrows, returns, keeps, or declines what. Let the narrative attention move with that use rather than pausing to inventory the room. A borrowed item can be handed back with a particular care; a shared table can hold two people who are not sharing the same understanding of the meal.\n\nRender the sensory particulars of the handling—the weight of a dish, the worn edge of a book, the sound of a drawer—so that the object carries the person's actual attachment. The attachment may be charged, habitual, or simply pleasant. Do not invent possessiveness, a damaged heirloom, or a required change in terms. Keep the domestic arrangement as the supplied world establishes it, without adding an unsupported family structure. Attribute figurative meaning to the authorized character or narrator who makes the association, rather than turning it into an objective property or shared cast knowledge.\n\nLet the exchange remain a matter of practical decisions. A person may accept a kindness without resolving the relationship, or decline a request without rejecting the person. The narration should preserve each character's individual voice and private experience according to the active contract, not turn the household into a single shared mind.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite the exchange through direct clauses that name the actual weight, friction, cost, or condition of what is handled. If a person returns a coat, show its actual fabric, fastening and handling; dry cloth or an intact button can be as specific as dampness or wear. If a request is made, let the practical terms of the request—money, time, space, labor—remain concrete. Use forceful diction and compressed transitions, but do not let compression delete the causal link between what is said and what is done next.\n\nLet desire or reluctance coexist with those material facts. A person can want something badly while counting the cost of it; another can refuse without cruelty. Keep the viewpoint character's private experience available where the contract permits it, and let a longer thought stand when the reasoning needs its full length. Do not shorten a thought merely to perform the style.\n\nDo not make beauty or attachment require damage. A cared-for object can be worn without being ruined; a difficult exchange can leave a surface intact. Preserve the characters' individual speech and actual capacities. The scene's pressure comes from the specific stakes and conditions, not from an obligatory injury, rough refusal, or broken possession.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Let the form follow the scene: a note left with a returned possession, a letter written after a visit, a record kept for practical reasons. Do not rotate through forms merely to vary the presentation.\n\nUse the document's selected material particulars to carry its author's emphasis. A description of a garment, room, gift, or returned possession can show what the writer chose to notice and what they wanted the recipient to understand. Keep the actual terms of the exchange recoverable: what was given, kept, asked, or refused. A candid record may state more than the writer would say in person; a careful letter may state less than the writer felt.\n\nDistinguish the document from the author's complete live mind. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's private thought. If the active contract requires nonspoken interiority, write it separately as the character's actual present experience, not as a hidden paragraph inside the letter. Gaps, revisions, and conflicting accounts may reveal events across entries, but do not add an omniscient explanation after every record. Let the documents' circulation and silences do the narrative work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nWrite the scene around a sincere accommodation, remembered preference, or shared ease. Spend attention on the particular sensory pleasure: the texture of a clean shirt, the temperature of a prepared drink, the taste of a familiar food, the way a person handles a thing they know the other likes. Let the recipient respond according to their actual state: enjoyment, refusal, partial comfort or indifference are possible. Sincere care does not guarantee acceptance or pleasure; when pleasure occurs, give it direct sensory presence. They do not need to announce gratitude, improve their conduct, or reveal a hidden need for the care to matter.\n\nKeep the pleasure grounded in the supplied world. A person may develop a private association with the moment, and the narration may render that association at the extent the contract permits. But the comfort does not erase established problems or make everyone agreeable. A difficult relationship can contain a genuinely easy interval; a practical difficulty can remain in the background while care is offered or received, with enjoyment preserved when it actually occurs.\n\nPreserve individual responses. One person may accept the care with visible ease, another with quiet awkwardness, another with a private thought they do not speak. Do not require a confession, emotional repair, or a bitter aftertaste to make the scene feel real. The warmth is enough.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin with an actual exchange or encountered object, rendered in precise sensory terms: the condition of a table, the weight of a letter, the way a person stands in a doorway. Then widen deliberately through the active narrator's permitted history or social context. The widening should situate the present act within a known arrangement—an institution, a family, a neighborhood, a shared past—not merely decorate the scene with background. Use sustained paragraph movement and composed transitions, and keep the narration distinct from ordinary spoken language.\n\nReturn to the current people after the widening. The return can let the wider context confirm, qualify or leave the present exchange's meaning open without requiring changed understanding or a lesson. Distinguish the material terms of the exchange from the attachment a person brings to it. A known motive is not the same as an inferred one; the narration may state what the narrator knows and leave the rest as interpretation.\n\nDo not require ornate speech, a hidden past, or a final account of what beauty costs. The characters' individual voices remain their own, and the formal architecture serves the scene rather than replacing it. If the contract permits private experience, write it as the character's actual present thought, not as a retrospective summary inserted into the widening.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen by placing the telling in a present occasion: who is speaking, to whom, and what practical or personal reason makes this account worth offering now. The teller may be a participant, a close observer, or someone retelling what another person once reported; keep that source position stable. Give the listeners their actual interest in hearing the account without requiring a public display, whether they want information, reassurance, permission, delay, or a way to understand someone they already know.\n\nRecount the embedded incident through selected material particulars rather than summary. Let the teller linger on the texture of a room, a garment, a dish, a street, a held object, or a repeated gesture when that detail carries the relationship or the choice at stake. A sensuous detail should make the past exchange more concrete; it need not signal hidden desire, doom, or a moral. Keep what the teller knew then separate from what the teller has learned since. If later knowledge appears, show it as a later addition, not as the original participant's motive.\n\nThe outer scene can interrupt, qualify or redirect the account when actual events or responses do so; uninterrupted telling and unexpressed listening remain possible. A listener may ask about a concrete point of access, timing, or what someone actually said. The teller may omit, emphasize, or revise according to loyalty, embarrassment, or the purpose of the telling. Let the embedded story remain unfinished when the present occasion does not require completion. Recurring phrases or objects may connect the outer and inner levels, but repetition should help the reader see what the teller keeps returning to, not certify that the account is true or that the attachment is mutual.\n\nPreserve private experience on both levels. The teller's inner state during the present telling and a listener's unexpressed response may remain distinct from what is spoken. A listener can accept the story without agreeing with its interpretation, and the teller can offer comfort, warning, or self-justification without controlling how it is received. End at the active boundary, including within continuing thought or telling without a new pause, not with a forced revelation, changed attachment, or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the Romantic and uncanny route of the German-language tradition. Let a present room, garment, reflection, voice, or repeated sensation resemble a sourced earlier occasion that the active narrator or focal character can actually recall. Establish the earlier occasion clearly enough that the resemblance is legible: a particular room where someone once waited, a sleeve worn at a specific meeting, a scent from a remembered kitchen, a phrase heard before. Do not import a ghost, a time slip, or a supernatural recurrence; the uncanny effect is the pressure of likeness against present difference.\n\nJuxtapose the familiar form with its current material conditions. The same gesture may now belong to a different person, the same room may be differently kept, the same object may be handled with a new intention. Let the resemblance comfort, unsettle, or mean little to the participant according to the actual relationship and present situation. The narration may dwell on the sensory detail long enough to make the likeness felt, but the detail must remain traceable to the scene and the actual source available to the authorized character or narrator.\n\nKeep chronology and knowledge stable. A present perception may recall an earlier occasion without making the earlier person present or granting later knowledge to the past self. If the character now understands something about the earlier exchange, show that understanding as accumulated since then, not as what they felt at the time. The resemblance can prompt a decision, a question, or a withdrawal, but it need not force one. Let the present relationship proceed on its own terms, with the earlier occasion available as memory rather than as an active participant.\n\nPreserve the individual voices inside the narration. The character's private response may be plain, elaborate, uncertain, or resistant; it need not match the lushness of the surrounding description. Other characters may notice nothing uncanny, or may notice the same detail and treat it as ordinary. End at the active boundary, including continued perception or an unfinished exchange. The resemblance need not produce a disturbance, visible effect or completed return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaj::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere an inherited permission, obligation, or old authority bears on the present scene, specify what the person can actually receive, enter, use, keep, or give, and what they privately want from the encounter. Make the material terms concrete: a key that opens only part of a building, a room reserved for a particular use, a garment that must be returned, a service owed under a family arrangement, a person whose help is available only through an older claim. Do not import named supernatural powers or factions; preserve the actual authority and mechanisms the supplied world establishes, including social, familial, institutional or contractual terms where present.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensual precision carry the present place's significance. Describe a surface through which the actual encounter is experienced, whether intimate, strange, familiar or ordinary: the weight of a door, the temperature of a cup, the smell of a kept room, the way a borrowed coat fits, the sound of a familiar lock. Let the detail make the actual relationship, pleasure or any cost of the moment precise without requiring a personal sacrifice or discomfort. Beauty may coexist with discomfort, and a glamorous surface may make an unequal arrangement more legible rather than more desirable.\n\nKeep attraction, consent, and private want distinct. A person may want to stay, to touch, to accept help, or to refuse while still noticing the other person's beauty or the room's appeal. Wanting something does not prove agreement, and accepting a practical kindness does not require seduction, corruption, or a loyalty crisis. Ordinary help and small personal investment remain valid outcomes. Let the character's private experience be available under the active contract, while other participants may know only what is said or done.\n\nLet atmosphere change how this particular encounter is experienced without making the setting the protagonist. The old authority may feel protective, suffocating, familiar, or irrelevant depending on the character's actual history. The present exchange can advance through a request, a withheld answer, a practical task, or a changed understanding. End when the encounter has made the relation between old permission and present choice concrete, not with a forced revelation or a betrayal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoak::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse lyric inward narration as the definite route. Enter a particular authorized mind and let a present texture, contact, scent, or remembered place draw the person's private wish, affection, uncertainty, or sense of obligation into relation with an actual attachment. The movement may begin from something small and concrete: the grain of a table, the warmth of a cup, the smell of rain on cloth, the pressure of a bracelet, the sound of a name spoken in a particular room.\n\nDevelop the relation through syntax and return rather than through summary. A sentence may turn from the present object to the remembered person, then back to the object with a changed meaning. Repeat a sound, image, or phrase when the mind keeps returning to it, allowing a return to sustain the same feeling or develop a specific detail, qualification, memory or doubt without requiring new content. The thought may be plain or extended according to the character's actual habit of mind. It need not become jealousy, renunciation, revelation, or action.\n\nKeep fact and ownership stable. The inward movement belongs to the permitted narrator or focal character, not to a collective mind or to another person's unshared knowledge. Mark recollection as recollection, with its actual certainty and limits, and distinguish it from an imagined comparison. A remembered account does not itself certify every past detail; preserve any events the story has established. Distinguish what the person knows from what they wish or fear. The lyric development may change how the present moment feels without changing what has actually happened.\n\nLet the scene remain a character drama. The private association can accompany speech, work, waiting, or a practical task, and the other person need not know what is passing through the focal mind. The relation may be affectionate, divided, dutiful, or unresolved; the prose should preserve that particularity rather than smoothing it into a single emotional lesson. End at the active boundary, including continued inward experience without a newly clarified relation, decision or completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoal::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the diary and reflective miscellany route of the Japanese literary tradition. Locate each entry in a time and occasion of composition, and let the keeper select an actual impression, exchange, or ordinary use of a thing worth recording. The entry may open with a date, a place, a weather note, a household task, or a remembered phrase, but the selection follows the keeper's actual attention and purpose without requiring or prohibiting a complete account of the day.\n\nLet Li Bihua's sensory precision operate through the keeper's chosen details. Describe the particular texture of a meal, a garment, a room, a street, a gift, or a repeated gesture when it carries the relationship or the private question at stake. The record may linger on a beautiful or uncomfortable detail, but the detail must remain traceable to the actual occasion and to the keeper's own perception. Recurrence can connect entries through the keeper's attention: the same object, person, or phrase may return with unchanged or revised meaning.\n\nKeep the active narrator separate from the keeper where the active contract requires it. A diary entry contains what its author records; it is not the author's complete live mind. Preserve actual private experience beyond all records according to the active interiority requirements. If a later annotation appears, show it as a later addition, not as the earlier entry's original motive. The keeper may misunderstand, omit, or revise without the prose pretending the record is transparent.\n\nLet the entries remain part of a character drama. A recorded occasion may involve a request, a withheld answer, a small kindness, a practical obligation, or a change in how the keeper understands someone. The entry need not end with a poignant conclusion or a lesson. Stop at the active boundary, including within an unfinished entry or private consideration. A later entry can continue, confirm or revise the keeper's attention without a required new meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoam::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the polyphonic and nonlinear route of the Latin American literary tradition. Choose an actual shared occasion or object around which the accounts will turn: a gift, a visit, a borrowed room, a meal, a return, a kept letter, a task done or refused. Juxtapose permitted minds or attributed accounts around that center, letting the arrangement move backward and forward in time while the underlying chronology and source remain stable.\n\nLet each voice select its own sensory particulars. One person may remember the weight of a package, another the smell of the kitchen, another the exact words spoken, another the silence after a door closed. The details may differ or coincide without one account being declared the true emotional version. Li Bihua's precision should serve each owner's attention: a beautiful or intimate detail can belong to one memory without becoming a shared symbol or a proof of mutual feeling.\n\nKeep source and knowledge distinct. A character may know only what they saw or were told, and a later account may include what that person learned afterward without retroactively changing the earlier experience. The nonlinear arrangement may reveal a connection, a misunderstanding, or a repeated object across time, but it should not invent a collective mind or an unsupported truth. Preserve any broader facts available to the active narrator while keeping each participant's actual knowledge distinct. Preserve required private experience under the active contract, whether visible or hidden, and do not let the polyphonic form erase the boundary between minds.\n\nLet the drama remain open. The accounts may show affection, obligation, jealousy, relief, confusion, or ordinary care, and they need not converge on a single lesson or changed relationship. The arrangement itself can create meaning by what it places beside what, but the characters' choices and attachments remain their own. End at the active boundary with the source and occasion recoverable, including an unfinished account without a new understanding, revelation or forced reconciliation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoan::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Light Novel\n\nOpen the scene by orienting who is present, where they are, and what is actually being said, offered, or handled. Give unfamiliar names, places, or practical arrangements clear presentation when they first matter, without pausing for a glossary. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: a move from one room to another, the arrival of a third person, or the shift from a shared task to a private exchange should be legible in the paragraph break or a short orienting clause.\n\nUse readable breaks around speech, action, and owned response. A character may speak, then act, then think; separate those movements when the separation helps the reader track the exchange. Let Li Bihua's rich detail linger at the needed extent: the texture of a sleeve, the heat of a cup, the scent of a room, the weight of a borrowed object. Let the selected detail carry actual attachment, private experience or ordinary pleasure without making every spoken line wait for a descriptive cue.\n\nKeep private life present without compressing it into a single reaction beat. The focal character's inner experience may be extensive, uncertain, or plain, and it need not immediately change what they say or do. Other characters know what their actual sources and permitted access allow, rather than inheriting private prose merely because the reader sees it. The scene may advance through a request, a withheld answer, a small kindness, a practical obligation, or a changed understanding, and it need not end with a quip, an erotic charge, or an emotional payoff.\n\nLet the scene container serve the character drama. The readable rhythm should make the present exchange and any actual personal cost concrete, not force a youthful cast, a cliffhanger, or a fixed cycle of cue and reaction. End at the active boundary, including ongoing experience or a pending answer without requiring the encounter's meaning to be settled or demonstrated.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoao::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the paragraph's syntax follow the actual movement of a personal exchange: what is offered, handled, withheld, or understood. Begin with a concrete sensory fact from the scene—the grain of a table, the weight of a cup, the sound of a name spoken too softly—and let its recurrence or variation carry the emotional pressure of the moment. A comparison may unfold when it changes what the narrator notices or makes present, but it must remain traceable to the scene or the teller's associations. Do not let a resemblance become a world fact or a proof of another person's feeling.\n\nUse recurring sounds and images to develop attachment without announcing it. The same texture may return in a later exchange with a different meaning: a door's particular click remembered from an earlier departure, the scent of a room now shared in silence. Let repetition sustain or develop the actual feeling; an unchanged desire, pleasure or uncertainty can remain meaningful without adding a new understanding. Plain clauses preserve what was actually done and said; direct feeling may be stated without ornament. The lyric movement is in the attention, not in a uniform seductive register.\n\nKeep the individual voices distinct from the narrator's figurative register. A character may speak plainly while the narration lingers on the physical detail that makes their request or refusal matter. Private experience remains available under the active contract, but it need not be translated into a final emblem or a lesson. The scene may end with the exchange concluded or still open, the image persisting because the person has lived with it, not because it has been assigned a fixed meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoap::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSelect a remembered place, object, gesture, or exchange that the person actually has access to in the present scene. Let the recollection arise from a current sensory detail—the way a sleeve is folded, the temperature of a room, the particular silence after a question—rather than from a general mood. Distinguish what the person knew or intended then from what they understand now. Later knowledge may qualify the memory without rewriting the earlier motive or making the present scene a verdict on the past.\n\nAllow the remembered intimacy to coexist with current sensation without demanding a resolution. A familiar object may be handled with pleasure, irritation, or indifference; the memory may sharpen or recede according to the present exchange. Do not require a farewell, a discarded possession, or a repaired relationship. Affection can remain affection even when the occasion that produced it is gone. The reflective movement follows actual temporal difference: what is no longer available, what has changed, and what continues to be enjoyed.\n\nKeep the character's private experience distinct from what they say or show. They may mention the past, deflect it, or leave it unspoken while the narration holds the association. The scene may end with the present task resumed or the conversation unfinished. The melancholy is in the coexistence of times, not in a compulsory announcement that everything is passing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaq::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a modern memoir: a located act of composition reconstructing an earlier personal exchange. Preserve the active narrator's role, person and access independently of the memoirist and earlier participant where the contract distinguishes them. Establish the memoirist's present position—where they are writing, what occasion prompts the account, and what they actually know now, including retained earlier knowledge, later learning or genuine gaps. Use a particular surface, object, or place to connect the two times: the texture of a tablecloth, the sound of a street outside, the way a cup was set down. Let the sensory detail carry the earlier experience without making it a symbol of the later understanding.\n\nDistinguish carefully between what the earlier participant perceived, intended, and knew at the time and what the memoirist now understands. The later knowledge may confirm, qualify, or complicate the earlier impression, but it cannot retroactively become the earlier motive. Preserve the difference between the memoirist's present voice and the remembered person's actual experience. The narrator may say \"I did not know then\" or may let the gap appear through the difference between the vivid detail and the later reflection.\n\nKeep the memoirist's private channels distinct from the record itself. The composed account is a selection, not the complete live mind of the earlier participant or the present writer. The scene may reconstruct a request, a refusal, a misunderstanding, or an unchanged affection without requiring regret, exile, or a repaired memory. The memoir form is the architecture; the emotional outcome remains contingent on the actual exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoar::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the handled thing, spoken request, or material term needed to follow what someone gives, keeps, accepts, or understands. One exact detail can carry the weight that a longer description would dilute: the chipped rim of a cup, the folded edge of a letter, the particular way a coat is hung. Choose exact details that belong to the exchange or its owned experience. A smell or texture can carry pleasure, attachment or ordinary perception without changing a decision or recalling a past event.\n\nUse direct clauses and economical transitions. Omit redundant explanation, but do not delete actual private experience, including feeling or attention beyond what explains the choice. A character's desire, affection, or reasoning may be stated plainly even when the action already implies it. The omission should leave something inferable, not remove the causal link between what a person feels and what they do. Let an essential thought be long enough to make sense; brevity is not a quota.\n\nKeep the individual voices distinct. A character may speak in short, practical sentences while the narration holds a single vivid image that explains their hesitation or resolve. The scene may end with the exchange concluded or left open. Do not require silence, hidden meaning, or a later reversal. The economy is in the selection of facts, not in the suppression of feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoas::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized private experience. Let the order follow the character's actual attention rather than a fixed sequence. A familiar room or object may be simply useful, pleasurable, or personally charged; the richness of description should match the attention the character gives it. Do not inflate every sensory detail into significance, and do not flatten a charged moment into a bare report.\n\nLet the sensory history a person brings to an exchange enter the scene without requiring a formal flashback. A current gesture may recall an earlier one; a particular texture may carry an association that shapes how the person responds. Distinguish the memory from the present action so the reader can follow both. The recollection may deepen the exchange, complicate it, or remain private while the conversation continues. Do not force a return from thought at every paragraph boundary; the prose may stay inside the association as long as the scene's causality remains clear.\n\nPreserve direct feeling and plain voices. A character may state what they want without ornament, while the narration holds the physical detail that makes the request matter. The scene may end with the exchange concluded, resumed, or left open. No forced revelation or lesson is required. The contemporary-novel presentation is in the continuous movement and flexible attention, not in a particular emotional outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoat::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nWork from the actual supplied relationship premise, not a roster of familiar tropes. Select a supported exchange, accommodation, or remembered occasion that makes the bond legible in the present scene. A meaningful first-time act—the first time one person prepares a particular meal for the other, the first time they share a space without speaking—can carry the relationship's particular promise. Develop the act through sensual specificity: the exact way a cup is passed, the temperature of a room, the sound of a familiar step. Let the physical detail make the attachment present without making it proof of mutual desire.\n\nGive each authorized private meaning enough space. The narration may hold one person's pleasure, uncertainty, or plain affection while the other remains unaware. Preserve real consent and ownership; the scene does not require a confession, a misunderstanding, or a hidden debt. Affection can be sincere and the relationship unchanged. Supporting people may remain present in the background or in the character's thoughts without being reduced to obstacles or witnesses.\n\nKeep the individual voices distinct from the narrator's sensual register. A character may speak casually while the narration lingers on the particular handling that matters to them. The scene may end with the exchange concluded or the relationship's ordinary rhythm resumed. Let the supplied premise guide scene selection and actual significance, without requiring this interval to deliver a payoff or an invented emotional crisis.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoau::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings and compact declarations. Name an established gift, favor, refusal, or contribution that the community has lived with, then expand into the particular material circumstances that support, qualify or leave the reputation open. Use a remembered object to connect the act to its telling: the bowl that was returned, the coat that was mended, the key that was kept. Let the object's sensory particularity—its weight, its worn edge, its particular smell—carry the act's significance without turning it into an emblem of fate.\n\nUse recurring phrasing with a sourced basis. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance. Return to a phrase for familiar remembrance, renewed emphasis or a supported new application. Keep shared reputation distinct from private attachment. Being remembered as dependable does not mean that helping was effortless, cheerful, or automatic. The narration may hold the private cost or pleasure that the public account does not include.\n\nWhere actual dialogue enters, it may interrupt or coexist with the chronicle's authority; no extra spoken exchange is required. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to one detail or answer an elevated introduction with an ordinary practical concern. This need not become parody. Preserve actual private experience and unfinished or completed acts without doom, debunking, or a final lesson. The scene may end with the act concluded or resumed, leaving another modest deed available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoav::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nLet the active narrator hold an actual or explicitly provisional claim about how a person values a specific place, keeps a particular thing, gives something away, or understands an attachment. The claim need not be moral or abstract. It can be as ordinary as: this room still works because she keeps it ready; he never gives anything away without a reason; people who say they are only being careful are already deciding what to keep for themselves. Phrase the claim in the narrator's own register and make it contestable rather than decorative.\n\nThen bring a concrete personal case into focus. The case should be the actual event or arrangement already authorized in the scene: the returned box left in the hall, the sister who took the better room, the borrowed coat never mentioned again, the gift accepted with a promise the giver did not hear. Work through the case with exact sensory and relational detail. Stay close to what the relevant person touches, handles, avoids, or names during the current exchange. Do not turn the example into a compressed parable; let it occupy the actual drama.\n\nAfter the case has registered, return to the opening claim and examine its status, which may remain unchanged. Confirm it when the examined particulars genuinely support it. Qualify it when the case changes the wording or scope of the claim. Leave it provisional when the scene has not supplied enough to settle the matter. The return should sound like the same narrator thinking further, not like a verdict appended from outside. A claim may fail because the situation is more ordinary than the first formulation allowed, or because the person involved has a different attachment than the object seems to indicate.\n\nKeep attachment able to exceed the formulation. The narrator can say what is known about the act and still acknowledge that the person's private experience, or the narrator's own, is not exhausted by the claim. Do not force the case to refute the original idea; a surviving ambivalence is a live result. Preserve the actual character decisions and their consequences from the active story. The essayistic movement traces an examination without imposing changed desire or a realization before its support exists. The claim, case or return may remain unfinished at the active boundary; actual circumstances govern whether action precedes, accompanies or follows the thought.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaw::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Screenplay\n\nWrite each scene in concise, readable action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use scene headings when the location or time actually changes. In the action lines, select exact visible or audible particulars that make the personal exchange tangible: the sound of a cup set down before an answer, the position of a hand near a wrapped object, the way a coat is folded over an arm while someone talks about something else. A detail may sharpen the moment without resolving what it means.\n\nLet the exchange between characters carry their decisions and hesitations in spoken language. A request can be direct, partial, awkward, or phrased as an ordinary practical matter. Do not make every line reveal maximum feeling. A character may give a socially ordinary answer while the action block shows what they are doing with their hands or where they have placed themselves in the room. The gap between speech and conduct is available, but it must remain contextual rather than become a code for secret desire or guilt.\n\nUse cuts when the selected sequence needs a change of occasion; they can connect two moments in an actual personal history: the room before the visitor arrives and the room after; the object on the table and the same object later somewhere else. A cut does not need to explain itself immediately. Keep source and chronology recoverable across the cut, preserving actual changes as well as continued conditions without requiring a new difference. Do not use a cut to manufacture a false parallel or a deterministic reversal.\n\nPreserve actual character experience within the active narrator's permissions. Place required nonspoken interiority in a clearly attributed note according to the active source module's presentation rules. That note is narrative material available to the reader, not dialogue that other characters hear or stage knowledge they possess. Do not smuggle private significance into a voice-over unless the active contract separately authorizes it. A separately authorized voice-over still does not replace actual private experience. A character's withheld thought does not have to become a public beat.\n\nClose at the active boundary, including continued work, private experience or an unanswered exchange without a required change in situation, revelation or emotional turn. The exchange may end because a request is accepted, declined, postponed, or interrupted by an ordinary obligation. The camera stays with the characters' actual performed lives rather than with a thematic verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoax::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene so an actor can perform it from the printed page. Mark entrances and exits clearly. Establish enough of the space that the audience can follow where the characters are in relation to one another and to the important objects. Keep props reachable and their movement legible: who picks up the box, who sets the glass aside, who steps past the chair without moving it.\n\nDo not attach a gesture to every line. Let physical business belong to what the character is actually doing in the scene: clearing a table, folding clothes, checking a phone, waiting near a door, occupying the one seat that used to be someone else's. When a line lands on an object, the stage direction can show the character attending to it before speaking, after speaking, or choosing not to touch it at all. The relationship between speech and object is contextual, not symbolic proof. The audience may infer, but the stage direction should not announce that a held cup means desire or that a long pause means guilt.\n\nAllow ordinary work and stillness. A character can remain seated while another crosses the room; a speaker can dry their hands while answering; silence can last long enough to be playable without carrying a forced revelation. Sustained silence should belong to the practical moment or the actual pressure on the characters, not to a universal rule that every difficult pause hides a secret.\n\nUse monologues and asides only when the drama requires public performance: a toast, a prepared explanation, an address to the room, a moment when a character deliberately speaks while others present cannot hear. Do not use them as the default location for private thought. Required nonspoken interiority remains authoring material in the active presentation, clearly attributed and separate from stage speech. A character can know something the other characters do not know without turning that knowledge into an audible aside.\n\nKeep interpretation governed by context and permitted knowledge. A character may suspect, misunderstand, or fail to notice what the stage directions make visible. Do not require every charged object or withheld line to receive a follow-up gesture. The scene can end after a workable change in arrangement or feeling: a person leaves, remains, begins a task, or accepts a partial answer.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoay::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside one authorized mind. Let the prose begin with whatever that person is actually attending to: a texture, a smell, the pressure of a bag against the leg, the sound of a name called from the kitchen, the exact color of the light on a table that once stood in a different room. Do not arrange the opening sensation as a prepared symbol. Let it be present because the body or the room makes it present.\n\nFrom that point, follow the mind's order. A sensation may meet a memory, a phrase heard now may recall something said years earlier, a bodily discomfort may interrupt a wish and pull the person back to the practical task. Let the syntax register those shifts: an unfinished clause may break when a door opens; a remembered phrase may repeat with small changes; an ordinary chore may keep reasserting itself between surges of feeling. Do not require equal portions of perception, memory, private speech, and sensation. Let this particular mind's habits govern where attention lingers.\n\nKeep the actual scene recoverable. Memory and recollection are not the same as the present event, and fantasy is not the same as what happens. If the character imagines an answer they never receive, the prose must make that distinction legible without turning every imaginative turn into a corrected report. The reader should know what was said, what was only thought, what was remembered, and what the person did next. A phrase can echo in the mind without changing the fact that it was spoken earlier or elsewhere.\n\nUse Li Bihua's precise material detail as the anchor inside the association. A particular jar, the seam of a dress, the grease mark on a paper bag, the difference between a door that sticks and one that swings back too easily may carry feeling. But the feeling belongs to the mind moving through it, not to a fixed glossary. The same object can mean different things on different days, or nothing at all when the person is too tired.\n\nPreserve the decisions and consequences of the actual drama. A character may act after a long internal delay, speak before they have finished thinking, misunderstand because the wrong memory intruded, or behave warmly while still privately uncertain. The outward event does not have to complete every internal possibility. Let the scene close where this mind's movement and the outer situation genuinely arrive, without a compulsory final realization or emotional reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoaz::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange in sender-attributed messages. Let each person's digital habits appear in what they send: short practical fragments, fuller descriptions, delayed replies, corrected words, or messages that ignore part of the previous text. Do not make everyone equally expressive or equally terse. A message can be warm, cautious, flat, or indirect for reasons that belong to that person's situation.\n\nUse named material particulars to give the exchange texture. Someone can mention the bridge near the old shop, the missing button, the brand of soup left in the cupboard, the way the light comes through a window at a particular hour, or the package left outside the wrong door. These details may create a shared world without carrying a hidden emotional meaning. A pause after one of them is not proof of desire or hesitation. Let the recipient respond in character: they may ask a practical question, miss the detail, return to their own concern, or recognize what the other person meant.\n\nUse timestamps, read receipts, typing indicators, edited messages, attachments, or channel changes when they matter to actual timing, access or the personal exchange, without requiring a change in knowledge. A message that was delivered but not answered is not the same as a message that failed to send. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the sender's live private thought; show it separately if the active presentation requires it, and preserve what the recipient did and did not receive. Do not invent an interface event to force significance onto an ordinary delay.\n\nIf context outside the exchange is required, set it clearly apart from the transcript. A brief narrative passage can establish where a sender is, what they were doing before replying, or what changed between messages. Required private experience remains separate from transmitted text. A person may type, delete, and then send something entirely different, but the reader should not confuse that private composition with what the other person actually read.\n\nLet the conversation advance a real personal decision or practical exchange. Someone may accept an invitation, refuse a request, make a plan, seek information, disclose a detail, or continue an arrangement that matters to them. The transcript can end at the active boundary, including a pending reply or ordinary acknowledgment without a new established fact. Do not require an emotive confession, a closing cliffhanger, or a changed relationship every time.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaoba::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Visual Novel\n\nSet the scene concisely before the exchange or as the exchange begins. Name the visible environment and the important object or surface that will be part of the moment: the vase moved to the edge of the shelf, the window left open over a row of dried herbs, the coat thrown across a chair that one person clears before sitting down. Preserve the active narrator and focal permissions already selected. Attribute a cue to what that permitted account can actually see, hear, otherwise perceive or know, without choosing a new viewpoint or granting access.\n\nUse speech with clear attribution and let focal narration carry the viewpoint character's actual experience. Do not force every line to be followed by a private response. Some dialogue can pass without inward comment, and some inward attention can continue while the other person speaks. When a focal response is given, allow it to include ordinary uncertainty, physical sensation, practical distraction, affection, irritation, or a memory brought up by the current scene. A facial cue does not itself transmit the thought; another character's knowledge or inference follows actual context, sources and any permitted access. The reader may know more than the person standing in the room.\n\nTreat background and expression cues as purposeful references for a reader imagining the scene, not as engine commands or truth indicators. A change in expression can make a question press harder, but interpretation must follow context. Averted eyes may mean discomfort, tiredness, concentration, or nothing the character would admit. A familiar room changed in small ways may feel significant without carrying a single symbolic explanation. If the same cue reappears later, its meaning can deepen through later information; do not make recurrence prove a hidden motive.\n\nKeep private experience complete under the active authorship contract. The focal response may align with or differ from what the person says. When they differ, supply a character-specific reason: the presence of others, habit, uncertainty, unwillingness to explain, attention to the immediate task, or an attachment they do not yet want to name. Do not require every discrepancy to become a dramatic confession.\n\nLet the scene remain open and usable without branching menus, romance routes, or a mandatory visual-novel pause before each new development. A request may stay unanswered, a plan may move forward, or a conversation may stop because someone has to leave. The final line or image should leave the actual exchange at a place the story can continue from, not force a recognition screen or a guaranteed emotional turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabaobb::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Li Bihua / Web Serial\n\nOpen each installment from a position the reader can stand in. Recover only the actual context needed for the current exchange: where the relevant person is now, what object or arrangement has been in use, what request or obligation is still open, or what an earlier scene established about a relationship. Do not summarize the whole history. Choose the particular fact that will become active in this installment.\n\nLet materially precise details accrue across replies. A fabric, a smell, the condition of a shared room, the location of a borrowed item, or the remembered quality of a particular street can return through current action, memory, feeling or ordinary continuity without requiring an action payoff. A returning detail may carry meaning from the earlier use without being explained again. If the teapot with the chipped lid appears in a new scene, the reader can register the connection through its placement or handling; the narration does not need to announce its history. The same object may also be otherwise significant to different people, but that difference must remain a matter of their actual knowledge and attachment.\n\nPreserve the stability of earlier events and earlier private meanings. Later understanding may revise an interpretation, but it must not rewrite what actually happened or what a person knew at that earlier time. If a feeling now seems different, distinguish the current change from the previous experience. The fact that a gift was accepted, that someone left without answering, or that a promise was made cannot become its opposite because a later scene needs a reversal. Use the narrator's actual sequence rather than a retroactive emotional plot.\n\nAllow each installment to move without requiring a new revelation, secret, or emotional reversal. The movement may be a completed errand, a plan changed, a question answered, a request postponed, a room rearranged, or a private understanding that takes a step without being announced. Let calm or attentive intervals sustain the serial without being converted into empty padding. Private continuity matters as much as public event.\n\nClose on a position from which the next installment can realistically continue. The final sentence may leave someone about to speak, about to return, or simply facing the next ordinary task. It need not be a cliffhanger. The active world and characters remain available, and their wants may cross the serial boundary without being forced to culminate in every reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapaa::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nFollow an actual request, need or admission and develop its particular difficulty through private experience, speech and any subsequent behavior, without manufacturing a bad reception or requiring outward strain. A person may agree at once, object indirectly, or say the right thing while privately thinking the cost. Use the narrator's own distance and plain diction for that private thought, so the reader sees what this character feels expected to tolerate without the prose telling them the feeling is proof of someone's bad intention.\n\nKeep the exchange concrete. Show what is being asked: a delay, a debt, an apology, a return to an old routine, or an agreement to stop asking. Give the speaker a reason for the apparent ease or difficulty of the demand that comes from their position, not from universal villainy. The answer matters. If someone consents, follow the actual effort and feeling involved; neither a later cost nor a collapse is required. If someone refuses, ground the response in their actual reason or preference without inventing a hard limit or a generic rebellion. Let silence follow the person's actual attention and reasons, whether they are considering, declining to engage, missing the request or choosing not to answer; silence does not itself mean acceptance.\n\nLet private response differ from spoken response where the reason is present in the story. A character may want to object and say nothing because objecting would injure an older person, an employer, a partner, or a person they love. Render the motive through actual reasoning, direct feeling or attention to the other person, without substituting an invented diagnosis or requiring an outward detail to carry it. When private feeling agrees with speech, keep both sincere without requiring one to expose the other.\n\nHurt can remain in the room. The scene need not decide the relationship is damaged, the expectation false, or the vulnerable character self-blaming. A clear, decent answer may still leave somebody unable to say what it cost. Let that fact be available to the reader rather than made into a closing verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapab::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Chat Roleplay\n\nResolve the submitted material into a clear shared interval with readable paragraphs, visible scene continuity, and unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep the active narrator and ownership settings in force. Do not add a question or choice at the end merely to preserve a roleplay turn; stop at the natural point where the exchange has settled or paused, while the surrounding situation remains alive.\n\nUse ordinary words and conduct as evidence of what the characters accept. A person may ask for help as if the request were ordinary, or may hold a door, pass a payment, or use a familiar phrase that makes an expectation available to the narration, while companions notice or interpret it according to their actual access. Do not assemble the scene so that everyone demonstrates the same social position. Another character may agree, refuse, change the subject, or provide help from an interest that has nothing to do with the focal character's discovery. Let that person remain an independent participant rather than a lesson.\n\nRender each authorized person's actual private experience, whether that person is speaking or silent, under the active narrator and ownership contract. It may explain an answer or remain meaningful without altering the visible response. Write it in the selected presentation exactly as required, and keep it distinct from what other characters can hear or know. A character may know that a phrase was meant kindly and still feel the old claim inside it. The narration need not announce that feeling as irony.\n\nDo not force a closing critique or new conflict. An ordinary interval can end with the shared task still in motion, a cup set down, a message sent, or one person choosing not to say what they understood. Let that pause follow from the particular people involved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapac::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nTake the social and satirical realism route. Give the episode an actual social surface in a particular place and time, but do not invent an institution or local color to carry it. Open with an accepted explanation as someone would offer it: a household rule, a work arrangement, a favor that is supposed to be natural, or a claim about how two people already treat each other. Keep that statement in its speaker's voice, and do not let the narrator absorb it into universal truth.\n\nThen place the explanation beside the actual request, favor, or responsibility that enacts it. The narration should move from what people say the arrangement is to what someone must do under it: whose time it consumes, whose claim receives priority, who may decline, and what each person notices as normal. Make any actual agreement or discrepancy concrete without requiring a gap. A character may carry out the custom graciously while another character silently sees the cost. A character may benefit without understanding the benefit as exploitation. A character may notice the gap and say nothing because naming it would cost too much. None of these possibilities is compulsory; agreement is also possible.\n\nKeep the satire restrained. The passage need not expose a hypocrite or end in a public unmasking. A small incongruity can do the work: a polite phrase followed by a measured silence, a gift offered with the giver's need embedded in its occasion, or a promise whose repetition a particular person treats as proof, without the narration certifying it merely because it was repeated. Let the reader compare explanation and operation without the narrator assigning a collective verdict. Keep private experience situated in the authorized person; a sweeping judgment they actually make remains their judgment rather than an automatic general verdict supplied by the style.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapad::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse direct clauses and exact physical detail to make an ordinary request, refusal, or routine task carry weight. Name the actual contact: a package set down too heavily, a shoulder pressed through a door, a package moving between wet hands, or a child's cup held in one hand while the other wipes a counter. Let effort, wear, and bodily discomfort arise where the task would produce them. Do not use them as a decorative shorthand for social damage.\n\nCompress transitions that merely repeat the visible situation. Move between words, action and private experience in the order actual causality supports, including reasoning before an answer or experience that does not lead to outward action. Vary rhythm when the character's thinking needs room. A long sentence can develop specific recall, feeling or calculation without its prose length imposing a delay in the scene; a sharp exchange can move in short clauses without becoming a sequence of grunts.\n\nKeep rough speech attached to its owner and its situation. One person may be curt because the task is urgent, another because courtesy has never been available to them. The narration should not make every line abrasive. Assistance can pass from one hand to another cleanly; a relationship can remain warm without the prose softening. Render that warmth through actual practical action, direct words or private feeling rather than substituting a generic declaration for the person's experience.\n\nPreserve humor and affection where they belong. A tired person can laugh at a known failure without the laugh masking cruelty. A parent or worker can express tenderness by checking that a fastener is closed or a tool is put away. The social observation remains visible in what is done, not in a lecture, and no one is required to break down to make the material cost feel real.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapae::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Epistolary / Documents\n\nBuild the sequence from actual composed artifacts tied to named people or legible purposes: a letter, a note, a work record, an entry, or a report. Establish the writer, recipient or intended use, and time of composition as soon as the document appears. What the document knows depends on its author and purpose. A note to a landlord may omit what a letter to a friend would include; a work report may record the same event in the passive voice that protects the writer's own accounting of it.\n\nLet the writer's selection and phrasing expose an accepted assumption without requiring a hidden revelation. A person may ask for an extension as if the request were already granted; may describe a debt as a matter of course; or may leave a household detail unmentioned because the recipient would know it. The document does not need to confess, contradict an earlier account, or discover a damaging omission to be meaningful. Its truthfulness can be partial without the story framing that partiality as fraud.\n\nKeep claims distinct from events. When a document says someone promised, refused, paid, or forgave, the sequence may allow other records to confirm, complicate, or fail to support that claim, but do not insert an omniscient correction after every page. A gap may remain legible where delivery, reading, and response are separated in time. When aftermath is available, represent it through a situated document or clearly separated context authorized by the active contract, preserving actual private experience independently. Delivery, reading or response may remain pending without another artifact, and the record is not a complete mind.\n\nPreserve actual private experience beyond the composed wording. An unsent draft is an artifact with its own relation to the writer; it is not automatically the writer's live thought. If the active contract requires visible or hidden interiority, write the character's nonspoken awareness, reasoning, or sensation in that channel, clearly separate from what the document itself says.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapaf::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive the kindness or shared pleasure particular shape rather than a generic reward. Show what the person would actually choose: a cup left at the right temperature, a dish prepared a certain way, a walk taken at the hour one of them prefers, or help offered through an action that spares the other person the trouble of asking. Use sensory detail to make the pleasure legible: the sound of a familiar kettle, the warmth of borrowed clothing, the particular texture of food, or the changed light after a long shift.\n\nLet the care operate without inventing an injury. A person may lighten an unwanted expectation, ask rather than assume, or simply keep company where company is welcome. Conflict can persist as a known condition in the background without demanding repair. A hard week may leave somebody too tired for the full routine, and the story can honor that by changing the routine instead of treating the change as a failure of care.\n\nKeep refusal, private response, and acceptance open to the actual people. One character may receive help and say nothing, because saying something would make the kindness feel like a claim. Another may decline a small favor and still stay nearby. The scene should not force a confession, an apology, or a gratitude speech. Sincere affection can remain visible in the way someone remembers a preference or notices when to stop talking.\n\nDo not soften the world into a morality fable. The social observation remains in what people accept as natural: one person overworked while another offers rest, one person used to refusing while another offers without ceremony. Let ease stand, with any constraints that actually remain rather than an invented burden; comfort need not fix a deeper arrangement or prove that one is damaged.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapag::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement to place an ordinary request, invitation, or obligation inside a wider social situation. The frame may be a remembered custom, a family pattern, a work hierarchy, a public ceremony, or a neighborhood understanding. Use the narrator's composed register for contextual framing while retaining a character's purposeful explanation when the actual exchange calls for it, and keep the narrator's judgment distinct from the convention being described. A custom may be presented as familiar without the novel endorsing it.\n\nWhen the encounter arrives, let speech keep its individual feel. A formal household need not produce uniformly formal speakers; a younger person may answer in shorter phrases, a worker may use local idiom, and an older person may lean on a formula while meaning something particular by it. Use public and private understanding as separate channels. A character can perform the expected greeting while inwardly noting a change in the room, and the narration may permit both without telling the reader that one is false.\n\nDevelop the episode through contextual motion rather than a single static tableau. Relate the actual convention, particular words, private experience and physical business through deliberate transitions without a fixed channel order or a required consequence before the boundary. A return to the convention may show changed or unchanged application, with no compulsory moral summing-up. Agreement can be meaningful without transformation; understanding can come without conversion.\n\nLet the formal architecture serve the drama instead of replacing it. Ceremony, correspondence, or public meeting may appear if the scene supplies them, but they are not mandatory. The story's force remains in the particular people: what they ask, accept, withhold, and understand as normal within the world the narration has made legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapah::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen inside the present telling situation: name the teller, the listeners, and the actual purpose of giving the account now, including companionship or entertainment as well as practical needs. The teller may want help, to justify a decision, to explain an absence, or to keep someone from acting, but the reason should be visible in how the telling begins and how listeners receive it.\n\nLet the embedded account stay close to a particular request, favor, or accepted practice. Narrate the past events through the teller's selection and emphasis: what they call ordinary, what they pass over quickly, and what they explain as if no explanation were needed. Do not require the teller to conceal a fact or to be morally wrong. The teller may be accurate and still leave out what they did not notice or did not think mattered.\n\nKeep three levels distinct as you write. The outer present scene includes the listeners' actual responses: they can interrupt, remain quiet, ask a practical question, or accept part of the account without accepting all of it. The embedded story reports what the teller says happened. Authorized private experience belongs to the focal character and may reveal a listener's real reaction, a teller's hesitation, or a memory that the spoken account does not include. Do not let a listener's silence automatically mean agreement or enlightenment.\n\nKeep the outer scene's actual activity and private attention available while the tale continues or after it ends. A task may resume, a request may be answered or understanding may change, but no consequence or completed telling is required before the active boundary. The tale does not need to complete a lesson or resolve every listener's objection. Use recurring phrasing or a repeated detail only when it helps connect the past account to the present relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPabapai::♢ * [STYLE] Character Drama / Lu Xun / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nChoose documentary and historical-memory prose. Bring an actual available instruction, note, record or formal arrangement into relation with the character's present life, following the order their actual experience supports. Quote or describe the document in clean, concrete terms, showing what it assumes about the people it governs.\n\nSet it beside an authorized person's recollection of the same human circumstances, without requiring the record to precede the remembering. Follow what that person actually remembers: the room, the tone of voice, the practical difficulty, the person who made the arrangement work. Let the memory follow the person's actual associations or deliberation, which may align with the record's order or depart from it without a required pattern.\n\nPlace the two beside each other without forcing a verdict. The record may agree with the memory, differ from it, or concern a separate matter entirely. The character may notice the gap or may not. Distinguish a record's official or institutional register from the narration of an authorized person's recollection. A character may adopt, repeat or reject that phrasing where their actual habits support it; neither the document's wording nor the narrator's register automatically replaces their voice.\n\nLet personal significance survive the comparison. The character may still value the arrangement, resent it, or simply continue with the practical task it requires. No investigation, corrected belief, invented document, or practical change is required. The scene can end with the character returning to ordinary life, with what the record and recollection actually make available, whether a cost, care or an ordinary arrangement, without requiring a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
        },
