            "content": "{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @section-header true }}{{// @header <Init: Writing Recipes: Comedy> }}{{trim}}"
        },
        {
            "role": "system",
            "injection_position": 0,
            "injection_depth": 4,
            "system_prompt": false,
            "marker": false,
            "enabled": true,
            "forbid_overrides": false,
            "injection_order": 100,
            "injection_trigger": [],
            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-comedy-01",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Comedy · 01/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == comedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Comedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 1 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-comedy; payload preserved. }}{{// Recipe Library: Comedy }}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nOpen with the character's concrete aim: what they are trying to ask, admit, or accomplish, and the response they expect. Where the person actually rehearses the exchange, render the anticipated words or response. Attention may fragment when speech begins, remain steady, or stay with a private concern; no rehearsal or lapse is required. A stray sensory detail, an intrusive association, or a misjudged pause can make the felt prediction diverge from what actually happens.\n\nDo not mock the character's actual pain or need. Where expectation and continuation differ, their concrete relation can support surprise or anticlimax: an elaborate preparation may meet a plain answer or useful help. Shared expectations can also support a familiar comic exchange. Let the actual response determine whether a turn lands, remains pending, or simply sustains the situation; no success, catastrophe, repair, or imagined crisis is prescribed.\n\nPreserve the full inner cost. The character may still be embarrassed, resentful, or certain they have said the wrong thing even when the outer exchange has succeeded. Warmth and relief can coexist with lingering hurt; no confession or complete resolution is required. Render interrupted perception and unstable interpretation as the character's live experience, without automatically giving the other speaker that knowledge. Speech may be clear, hesitant, or unusually shaped according to actual attention. Make the comic relation readable without prescribing laughter or making intrusive experience itself the target.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chat Roleplay\n\nResolve the active interaction with scene-first continuity. Establish where and when the characters are, and make relevant aims legible within the narrator's actual access, through stated requests, supported context, or permitted private experience: what one wants from the conversation, task, or presence of the other. Use natural paragraph breaks to move from one speaker to the next, with dialogue clearly attributed so no line becomes ambiguous.\n\nTrack a character's attention lapse as a real event when relevant. They may lose the thread, fix on a small detail, or reformulate a reply while the other speaker continues on a different track. Do not fabricate a missed line or mishearing; show any divergence in actual reception or private experience, without requiring an altered outward answer. Their intrusive thought or unstable interpretation can shape a reply that seems unrelated to the previous line, producing comic friction without random interruption.\n\nLet the other person's response be particular: literal, dry, enthusiastic, or plainly uninterested, grounded in their own established habits. A supported mismatch can create comic friction; a mutually expected routine or literal response can also sustain a comic pattern without anyone being wrong or surprised. The scene need not resolve: a setup can remain incomplete at a natural stopping point, with the actual state available for continuation even when the next move is unknown, not a forced question or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Place the scene inside an actual social arrangement—work, family, bureaucracy, marketplace, or local custom—and make relevant aims concrete within the narrator's actual access, leaving excluded private motives unknown rather than inventing them. Show the official wording, posted rule, reputation, or stated intention in use alongside what a person actually gets, notices, or is told. Where declared norm and actual use diverge, their juxtaposition can carry satire; a valid routine may instead become comic through literal repetition or proportion. An authorized voice may comment directly, without inventing a gap.\n\nLet the focal character's perception be fallible. Their interrupted attention or intrusive association may read the arrangement too seriously, too personally, or incorrectly. A dry observation, a literal reply from another participant, or an unchanged routine can undercut their interpretation without exposing anyone to humiliation. Agreement, competent compliance, or a workable arrangement can still produce anticlimax through an actual contrast in scale or a mutually recognized pattern, without requiring anyone's expectation to be deflated.\n\nKeep the setting, language, and community as they are already supplied. Do not transplant a period, class, or region, and do not invent speech patterns, family structures, or stock figures. Preserve the actual private cost of needing something from this arrangement: the character may feel exposed, annoyed, or unsettled even when nothing visibly goes wrong. Satire stays local and earned; it never turns a person's pain or intrusive mental life into the joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSet up the character's intended act through exact material conditions: the object, surface, movement, or practical obstacle involved. Use short, forceful clauses that compress transition—naming what is touched, shifted, or attempted—before the turn. Where private anticipation is actually present, make its concrete content available; it may be hopeful, ordinary, fearful, or unrelated to the eventual comic turn.\n\nWhere the sequence reaches a turn, a sharp rhythmic cut can give the actual continuation a dry landing: the mundane successful result, the ordinary practical detail, or the literal response that doesn't match the elaborate inner script. The prose can be dry or abrasive, but the event need not be a mishap, collision, or insult. Anticlimax can arise from a difference in anticipated and actual scale, while a familiar repeated response can work without that difference. Preserve the event's real scale and allow the setup to remain unfinished at the active boundary.\n\nDo not shorten the character's interiority to a few fragments. Let a full thought, fear, or sudden realization keep its warranted length; the compressed style governs syntax and transition, not the deletion of mental life. Rough speech or physical consequence follows naturally from the scene's actual stakes, never imported for tone. Keep interrupted perception as a live event in the focal mind, not a diagnosis the narrator applies from outside.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the story as a sequence of documents with identifiable writers, recipients or purposes, and dates or situations of composition. Choose the actual document type required by the scene: letter, note, report, message, journal entry, or instruction sheet. Each document shows what its writer intended for that moment; later documents can respond, correct, or ignore it, supporting a callback where the sequence warrants it, with narrative context only where the active voice and access permit.\n\nBuild comic timing through juxtaposition. Let one record's formal, hopeful, or elaborate wording meet the plain practical reply or the unchanged daily record that comes after it. The gap between intended reception and actual reception works as anticlimax when the later document's content and timing are concrete. A person's interrupted perception or unstable interpretation may appear inside their own private record, but do not present private live thought as transmitted text; keep recorded content distinct from the character's actual live private experience in both Visible and Hidden modes; neither display setting turns a document into the whole mind.\n\nDo not invent a typo, lost letter, or misread instruction to force comedy. The documents may agree, the task may succeed, and the humor can be the dry contrast between the first writer's elaborate expectation and the second's uncomplicated competence or indifference. Preserve each document's actual knowledge and concealment: what its author knew at the time, not what later characters may think.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nBegin with a feasible shared pleasure or offer of help: a meal, a chore, a small errand, or a familiar comfort. Establish the first character's immediate aim and their concrete expectation—perhaps a grand plan, a long-prepared gesture, or a hope for a particular reaction. Render any actual lapse, intrusive association, or steady anticipation privately where authorized. Do not invent a bungled favor or embarrassing mistake for the comedy; preserve any actual mistake and its consequences without treating the style as immunity.\n\nShow the other person's response as particular and familiar: a literal answer, a known preference, an efficient execution of the task, or untroubled enjoyment that may contrast with an actual elaborate expectation, or match a mutually familiar one. The comedy is a gentle reversal or callback grounded in established habit: the recipient wanted the plain version, the help succeeds without ceremony, or a recurring private phrase returns in a new context. Keep the warmth concrete through small details—the exact food, the remembered sleeping position, the practical gesture—not through generalized affection.\n\nThe recipient's actual response stays their own. They may be uncomplicatedly pleased, mildly puzzled, or simply ready to move on; no confession, gratitude speech, or emotional repair is required. Personality, privacy, and even a small disagreement can remain while the overall interval remains warm and easy. Any intrusive thought belongs to the focal character alone and need not be shared or resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs with deliberate transitions to set the character's actual aim inside a relevant social context: the history of the room, the norms of the household, the weight of the occasion, or the unspoken expectations surrounding the request. Let the narration adopt enough distance to frame the coming interaction as significant, even ceremonious, without mocking the character. The prose can carry that serious anticipation through full, composed sentences.\n\nThen place the concrete exchange beside it. A plain reply, ordinary detail, or actual fulfillment may create dry anticlimax; the contextual buildup can also remain open while the encounter continues without a result. The contrast lies in the mismatch between the narrator's established scale and the actual scale of the event. Conversations remain ordinary speech for these characters; formal narrative framing does not require the cast to adopt it, while an individual speaker may also be ironic where that is their actual voice.\n\nRender the focal character's interrupted perception or an actual unstable interpretation where the narrator is authorized to do so. That interiority may undercut or complicate the formal framing: the character can be anxious, rehearsing, or distracted by an intrusive association while the world proceeds smoothly. Do not make every sentence ironic or every commentary pompous. The humor emerges from the architecture—the build, the shift, the small concrete thing—not from belittling the character who took the moment seriously. Knowledge and access stay consistent with the narrator's contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish a concrete occasion where one character tells a story to others who have their own reasons for listening. Introduce the teller's aim, the audience's current attention, and a detail from the outer scene that can later interrupt or reshape reception. Where attention actually shifts or reception changes, render the teller's specific private experience. Noise may be noticed, missed, or heard clearly; it does not impose a lapse or diagnosis.\n\nBuild the tale's setup through selected details that imply an expected payoff, then show actual listening or response: a literal question may contrast with the intended effect, or recognition and repeated phrasing may let the intended comic pattern land. No puncture or mishearing is required. The comedy can come from the gap between the intended effect and what any listener actually receives, without requiring a failed performance. If a listener misses a key element, show what they did register and let their response follow from that, not from a forced misunderstanding.\n\nUse recurring phrases or audience reactions to link narrative levels, but preserve the tale's actual status as report, memory, invention, or a character's claim, without falsifying established events or inventing delay for a joke. End at the active boundary, including during telling, continued listening, private thought, or an actual response; no new act or completed payoff is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist and absurdist construction through a specific procedure relevant to the scene—a routine, form, rule, or system relevant to the actual participants, whose attitudes may differ or agree. Present its terms through use or brief discussion so readers can follow its logic, including relevant instruction or consideration without a requirement to enact it before the boundary. Let the actual participants pursue their own aims; habits and any established blind spots can shape choices without a fixed cast count or invented flaw.\n\nWhere reception is interrupted, distinguish the person's reading from the procedure's actual terms. Perception alone does not rewrite the rule, while actual speech or action prompted by it may affect other people or change conditions through established means. A literal compliance, an exception, or a repetition can produce comic disproportion when the sequence accumulates. An internal misreading may prompt an odd action, but the world's response stays governed by the actual rule, not the misinterpretation.\n\nWhere the procedure produces a result, its actual relation to expectation may create anticlimax or inversion: correct execution can produce a plain or oddly fitting outcome. Preserve actual errors, competent work, and consequences without inventing catastrophe or obstruction. The active interval may stop before any result, with the procedure or private consideration continuing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Gothic-Punk\n\nFrame the scene with an established old authority or inherited arrangement—a house, ritual, rank, or obligation—whose actual authority or practical influence is already established in the world. Keep the character's actual concrete need and its scale intact; where it contrasts with the authority's ceremonial weight, develop that relation: a practical errand, an ordinary bill, a necessary truth. Preserve its actual legitimacy, flaws, and consequences rather than making it fraudulent or powerless for the joke; the comic tension comes from the mismatch between ceremonial weight and plain need.\n\nLet the focal character's intrusive thought or unstable interpretation create internal contrast: they may overread a cue, fixate on a mundane detail amid grandeur, or hear a phrase as both threat and commonplace. Render private desire and uncertainty in the authorized nonspoken channel. A character may communicate them or leave them unspoken; trust and refusal retain their actual consequences even when delivery or reception has comic timing.\n\nUse material details—worn fabric, a locked drawer, the smell of dust—to ground the contrast. The comic turn can come from a literal response or a practical object recontextualizing the occasion, without requiring ridicule or erasing the actual person's experience and the authority's real effects. End at the actual scene boundary, including continued attention or a pending response, without requiring recognition or a failed dodge. Power and relationships change only through supported events rather than being preserved or overturned for the joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a modern urban or popular narrative structure: a series of linked encounters around a recurring phrase, request, or aim. Keep the supplied setting concrete, urban or otherwise; use a street, shop, or workplace only where present, without inventing social roles or language habits. Begin with a character pursuing a specific aim at the scale actually supplied, such as returning an item, asking a favor, or repeating a bit of news.\n\nAs the same phrase or situation passes from one person to the next, show what each listener actually registers and what their response changes or leaves intact for later encounters. Differences can arise from attention, prior obligation, or practical context, not from universal misunderstanding. Where noise or surroundings actually affect the focal person's reception, render that particular experience; they might misread a pause or fixate on an irrelevant detail, but private perception does not automatically become the other person's knowledge, while actual choices may have real effects on either participant.\n\nA callback can occur naturally: one character later repeats a phrase with a new emphasis, or an earlier detail reappears in a different context. Let the accumulated pattern carry humor without requiring it to come at anyone's expense. End at the active boundary, including before a response or during continuing attention; a completed reception, dropped topic, or finished quest is not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nAdopt early-modern comic/theatrical telling as a prose method: structure the scene around a recognizable aim within narrator access and the character's actual speech, their physical positioning, and observable objects or activity. Let the narration make actual delivery and physical business legible. A speaker may use formal address or a rehearsed complaint where that fits them, or speak plainly without a prescribed performance register.\n\nLet timing arise from the relation between delivery and actual business. A grand announcement may meet an ordinary task, or a plain repeated line may land through the exact moment someone pauses or continues; neither grand speech nor a new prop is required. The narrator may note a pause, a knocked object, or a shifted gaze without interpreting it as a hidden message. Keep the register and period supplied by the world; do not invent archaic Japanese diction.\n\nWhen a focal character experiences interrupted perception, render its actual private form, which may be agitated, neutral, pleasant, or unrelated to an error—perhaps a noise from outside half-covers a line or an intrusive image distracts from the main speech. This private experience remains separate from the public scene; other characters respond through their own actual knowledge and permitted access, not automatic receipt of the private thought through narration. End at the active interval boundary, including within continued speech, private experience, or a setup, without requiring the theatrical moment to subside, activity to resume, a correction, or a laugh.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect polyphonic nonlinear telling: juxtapose attributed accounts of an actual exchange across time, using the presentation order to compare aims and reception. A later recollection or a present fragment may lead; no fixed opening or minimum number of complete accounts is required in this reply. Each account must be clearly sourced to its teller and time of composition; preserve the active narrator's actual access without merging participant knowledge or granting new access through a perspective shift.\n\nLet each account follow its actual source, selection, and reliability, allowing agreement as well as different emphasis: one noticer fixates on a phrase, another on a room's temperature, a third on who arrived late. Accurate reports with different priorities can create comic contrast without lying or error; actual fallibility may also matter where established. Where an authorized person experiences an intrusive image or unstable interpretation, keep it particular and sourced without assigning a diagnosis or treating its content as a shared fact. Preserve the event's actual chronology.\n\nDo not require a fixed number of accounts; let the active boundary fall within an account or an unfinished comparison, without requiring completed juxtaposition or unintended reception. The reader may see the gap between intention and reception that no single character states.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene with a recognizable immediate aim: a character wants something specific at the scene's actual scale, such as a meal, a signature, or a place to sit. Establish the spatial situation cleanly—who is where, what is at hand—so readers can follow movement without rereading. Use short paragraphs for rapid exchanges and longer ones for private focus; do not pad every beat with a reaction slot.\n\nLet comedy come from a literal reply, a change of attention, or a concrete continuation that follows from the characters' established habits. A joke may be dry or absent; no fixed wit is required. When the focal character's attention wanders due to noise or an intrusive thought, render the actual experience in the active nonspoken presentation at its warranted extent. Return to outside events where attention or the sequence takes the scene there; others respond through their own real knowledge and access, not automatic receipt of private prose.\n\nIf unfamiliar terms or mechanics appear, give the relevant context within the actual scene, including instruction, consideration, or ordinary use without requiring a mistake or enacted demonstration. End the scene at a natural pause after the setup has paid off or been left hanging, without manufacturing a cliffhanger or a joke-bead count.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the comic sequence around a scene-born image or sound that the focal character keeps returning to while pursuing their actual recognizable aim at its supplied scale. Let the image develop across the paragraph through syntax, repeated sounds, or shifts of attention: a phrase can lengthen, a comparison can unfold, or a remembered fragment can interrupt the present moment. The image should carry the character's actual feeling, not serve as a decorative substitute for it.\n\nThen let that shaped expectation meet a plain utterance, a practical detail, or an actual result. The meeting may support the image, change its register, or provide anticlimax. A grand comparison can land on a small object; a repeated phrase can be answered by a literal remark; an intrusive thought can be displaced by a mundane sound. The contrast is the comic construction, but the feeling inside the image remains real and need not be corrected or mocked.\n\nPreserve interrupted perception and unstable interpretation as part of the character's attention. The character may mishear a word, follow a private association away from the conversation, or return to the scene with a different understanding. These shifts can create comic timing, but they are not evidence of a diagnosis or a reason to make the character absurd. Speech affected by noise or attention should remain legible in its actual context.\n\nDo not require the comparison to fail. Recurrence can deepen understanding, and an image may survive contact with the ordinary world intact. The scene can end with the character still holding the image, still pursuing the aim, or noticing something new without a punch line being owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWhen recollection arises, preserve what the person wanted and understood at the earlier time. Let the memory carry its own particular wording, habit, or expectation without rewriting it as a mistake. The character may remember an elaborate plan, a serious promise, or a private hope, and the narration should honor the feeling that belonged to that moment.\n\nPlace that remembered expectation beside the current occasion through present association rather than a composed later account. A familiar response, a still-accurate memory, or an ordinary continuation can carry the callback. Where earlier scale and present familiarity contrast, that relation can support comedy; an unchanged ritual can also become a shared callback without a revised meaning. For example, the person who once prepared for a grand event now performs the same small ritual without noticing; the phrase that once meant everything now returns as a routine greeting.\n\nLet pleasure and attachment remain real. The character can enjoy the present moment, feel fondness for the person or place involved, and still notice the difference between then and now. A lapse of attention need not spoil the encounter or require regret. The comedy is affectionate or dry, not humiliating.\n\nPreserve interrupted perception and unstable interpretation as part of the present experience. The character may drift from the conversation into memory, return with a different understanding, or mishear a word that triggers the recollection. These shifts are the texture of the scene, not symptoms to be diagnosed. No new loss is supplied to justify the tone; use the actual history, including irreversible changes only where established, rather than requiring a loss or permanent change as the comic premise.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern memoir as the selected route, with the participant and the later teller clearly situated under the active narrator permissions. The telling moves between two positions: the person who experienced the earlier aim, reception, and action, and the person who now recalls it. Keep those positions distinct so that the later commentary does not replace the earlier private feeling.\n\nRecount the earlier aim in its own terms, whether later framing precedes it, follows it, or appears alongside it. What did the person want, expect, or intend? Let the scene unfold with the perception, intrusive thought, or unstable interpretation that belonged to that moment. The participant may have been entirely serious, and the narration should preserve that seriousness without irony leaking backward.\n\nMake the earlier circumstances sufficiently clear for any later commentary to compare their scale or timing, without requiring the earlier action to finish before that commentary appears. The later teller may notice that the elaborate preparation led to a small result, that the dramatic pause was actually a moment of inattention, or that the feared outcome never arrived. This later understanding can be comic, but it frames rather than rewrites the earlier experience.\n\nPreserve the earlier private feeling as legitimate. A composed or performed memoir records the teller's chosen words; required actual nonspoken experience at either authorized time remains in the active Visible or Hidden presentation rather than being replaced by that account. The comedy does not require humiliation, a corrective confession, or an invented prior mistake. The later teller may still respect the person they were. Speech affected by noise or attention remains part of the earlier scene, not evidence that the participant was foolish. The memoir's then-and-now relation is the comic engine; no new loss or failure is supplied to justify it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details that make an aim or expectation recognizable. One object, one phrase, or one gesture can carry the setup. Use direct clauses to place it in the scene without explanatory restatement: the character wants something specific, and the detail shows what that want looks like in the world.\n\nThen let an actual line, pause, or result meet it plainly. The response may be literal, dry, or simply unhurried. The comic timing comes from the space between the expectation and the plain fact, not from adding commentary about how funny or awkward the moment is. Omit the restatement after a readable contrast.\n\nPreserve causes and authorized private experience. The character may have a long inward passage: an intrusive thought, an unstable interpretation, or a private association that the narration renders in full. Shortness is not a quota for the mind. Preserve required actual inward experience at its warranted extent without a usefulness condition, while the outer scene remains economical.\n\nLet speech affected by noise or attention remain legible. A misheard word or a distracted reply is a concrete event, not a symptom to be explained. The current interval need not deliver a payoff. The scene can end with the aim still underway, the response still settling, or the character noticing one more exact detail without a punch line being owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nEstablish what people are actually trying to do in the scene. Give each person a recognizable aim, habit, or blind spot that shapes their contribution. The comedy comes from the relation between these aims and the responses they meet, not from a random interruption or a compulsory quip.\n\nMove naturally among activity, speech, perception, recollection, and permitted private experience. A character may pursue a goal while an intrusive thought pulls attention away, mishear a word and respond to the wrong version, or return to the conversation with an unstable interpretation of what just happened. These shifts are part of the scene's texture, and they can create reversal, escalation, or anticlimax when they meet another person's actual response.\n\nWhen expectation and reception diverge, use their concrete relation. The character expected a particular answer and received a literal one; the character prepared for a serious conversation and the other person treated it as routine; the character's private association made a small event feel large. The divergence itself is the comic construction.\n\nAccurate understanding and ordinary competence can also sustain comedy. A person may hear correctly, interpret the situation accurately, and still produce a funny moment through timing, habit, or the simple fact of being right at the wrong scale. No emotional turn or punch line is owed per reply. The scene can continue with the aim still underway, the response still settling, or the characters simply proceeding with their ordinary lives.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse a shared phrase, preference, or activity from the actual supplied premise to establish what a participant expects. The callback should be legible because the relationship has made it familiar: one person knows how the other takes their tea, what they say when they are stalling, or which small ritual they always perform. The expectation belongs to the specific bond, not to a generic trope.\n\nLet another person's actual response meet that expectation at its own timing. The response may be familiar agreement, literal help, a dry correction, or a different private association. The comedy comes from the relation between the expected and the actual, not from forcing a misunderstanding. The other person can be entirely accurate and still create a comic moment through their own habits.\n\nPreserve private associations without changing shared facts. One person may attach a private meaning to a shared phrase while the other uses it casually. The narration can reveal the difference without making it a betrayal or a hidden motive. The callback need not change the bond, expose a secret, or humiliate anyone.\n\nLet interrupted perception and unstable interpretation remain part of the focal character's experience. A person may mishear a familiar phrase, drift into a private association during a shared activity, or return with a different understanding of what was meant. These shifts are real experience, not evidence that the relationship is failing. The scene can end with the shared activity still underway, the private association still private, and the bond intact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing. Establish an act or promise that the community or the teller remembers: who undertook it, what was said, and what it was supposed to mean. The declaration can carry mock-solemn weight, but the act retains its actual scale and nature in the supplied world.\n\nExpand from the declaration into what people actually intended, heard, and did. The person who made the promise may have been distracted, misheard the terms, or attached a private meaning that no one else shared. The person who received it may have taken it literally or forgotten it entirely. Where concrete particulars contrast with elevated phrasing, use that relation for comic scale. An accurate account of genuine competence may also gain comedy through a familiar repeated designation or plain response, without requiring the deed to be smaller or less successful than reported.\n\nLet a plain detail or reply meet the elevated phrasing. Dialogue can agree with the retelling, correct one detail, or answer an epic introduction with a practical concern. The teller need not ridicule the subject. A contrast between narrative weight and actual scale can support comedy, while a familiar recurring line can also land without a new contrast. Neither mockery nor a falsely inflated account is required.\n\nKeep reputation distinct from private experience. Being remembered for a deed does not mean the person experienced it as heroic, effortless, or even intentional. The narration can reveal the private thought behind the remembered act without making it a confession. Unfinished efforts remain unfinished. The account can end with an actual act or unfinished effort available to be remembered, not with a declaration that it has become legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as an essayistic narrative in the active narrator's voice. Open with a situated observation the narrator actually holds about a habit, arrangement, or recurring social situation. Keep the claim specific to the world and people at hand; do not turn it into a universal proverb.\n\nPlace that observation beside a concrete case from the present scene, a remembered occasion, or an explicitly imagined one. Show the person or people in the case pursuing a recognizable aim with their own habits and blind spots. Let the case's actual particulars do the comic work: a dry contrast between the claim's scale and the event's scale, an anticlimax after an elaborate expectation, or a reversal that still leaves the original observation standing.\n\nWhen the focal character's attention is interrupted or an intrusive thought arrives, render how that particular mind experiences it; the essayistic movement may break, incorporate the thought, or continue without an outward or structural interruption. The narrator may lose the thread, return to it, or notice that the case no longer fits the claim. Keep imagined content distinct from what is actually said or done. Speech can be affected by noise or inattention, but track who actually heard what.\n\nThe passage may end by returning to the observation's scope, narrowing it, or leaving the case unfinished. Do not require a lesson, a punchline, or a new proposition at the end. The comic construction comes from the relation between claim and particular, not from refuting the narrator or making the observation false.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Screenplay\n\nEstablish each scene through concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. Let the visible and audible conditions show what a character wants and what they are actually doing about it. Keep spatial changes easy to follow so the reader can track who is present, what they can see, and what they can hear.\n\nBuild comic timing through observable setup and response. A cut may place an expectation from one location beside the actual continuation in another. A held beat may let a line or action land without a reaction. Do not invent a physical mishap, a shocked expression, or a witty comeback to complete the beat. Let the ordinary continuation of the scene supply the contrast or anticlimax.\n\nWhen a character's perception is interrupted or their interpretation is unstable, show it through what they attend to, miss, or say in response. Noise or inattention can affect speech and reception, but track who actually heard or understood a line. Do not let a cut imply knowledge a character does not have.\n\nPreserve required private experience in the active presentation as a clearly attributed nonspoken channel, distinct from voice-over or dialogue. A character's private reception may be extensive, unfinished, or at odds with what they say. Do not make that private content audible to the other characters. The scene can stop after a result or an interruption without a closing reaction shot or a punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as a playable script. Use clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, objects, pauses, and actions that can be staged. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. Establish who is in the shared space, what they can see, and what they can hear.\n\nBuild comic intention and reception through actual positions and business. A character may pursue a recognizable aim while another character is occupied with an object, turned away, or attending to a different sound. Let a line land differently for the audience and for the character who misses or misreads it, but track the real reception: accurate hearing is as available as a mistake. Do not require a missed line, a used prop, or an invented entrance to create the comedy.\n\nWhen a character's perception is interrupted or an intrusive thought arrives, render the actual private experience and any effects it really has on delivery, pause, or physical attention; outward conduct can remain unchanged. Keep required private experience distinct from asides and monologues. An aside is performed speech with an audience; it is not the default destination for thought. Preserve the character's actual private experience in the active presentation as nonspoken authoring material.\n\nLet stillness, unfinished business, or a character simply continuing their task remain playable. Do not add a laugh cue, a closing reaction, or a neat reversal at the end of every beat. The active scene boundary may fall in a public pause, ongoing business, or continuing private thought without a required performed result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from inside the permitted focal character's mind. Braid sensory attention, remembered phrases, private rehearsals, bodily sensation, interruption, and a recurring concern through the passage. Let syntax and paragraph shape follow those associations, while keeping enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy.\n\nLet the comic expectation form inside that associative movement. A private buildup may gather force through an imagined reply, a remembered slight, or a repeated phrase. When a line or event occurs, render what the person actually receives or misses within the narrator's access; attention need not receive it accurately or at all. The imagined response and the heard response remain distinct. The buildup may meet an ordinary anticlimax, a dry continuation, or simply remain unfinished. Do not force a public payoff or a punchline.\n\nWhen perception is interrupted or interpretation shifts, let the mental movement change with it. The character may lose the thread, return to an earlier image, or notice that the room has moved on without them. Distinguish the speech actually delivered, what this person received, and any imagined wording. Unheard or inaccessible content remains unknown until a real source supplies it. Keep imagined and heard content separate, and do not let the character's thought grant knowledge of what another person privately thinks.\n\nAdvance the outer scene through actual elapsed events. The mental passage may be long or short, but the world continues around the character. Full feeling and thought need no public resolution. End when the present moment has been experienced, not when the association has been explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the story through sender-attributed messages and supported channel details. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may type and send quickly, another may revise, another may leave a message unread.\n\nBuild the comedy from the relation between what someone intends, what they actually send, and how the other person receives it. A literal reply, a resumed topic, or ordinary timing can form a callback or an anticlimax. Do not invent a deletion, typo, outage, or misunderstanding to create the effect. Keep the characters' aims and habits central. An actual interface failure may affect the sequence, but it does not replace a legible relation between intention, transmission, and response.\n\nWhen a character's perception is interrupted or their interpretation is unstable, show it through what they notice in the transcript, what they miss, or what they type in response. Noise or inattention in the physical world may affect what they send, but keep the transcript itself accurate to what was actually transmitted.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text whose visibility follows both the actual interface and narrator access; it is not the character's live private thought. Preserve required private experience in the active presentation as a clearly separated nonspoken channel. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The scene can end on a sent message, a read receipt, or a pause in the exchange without a forced question or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaaba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene in concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Keep spatial changes legible and let background or expression cues orient the moment without becoming executable engine code or a mandatory label on every line.\n\nBuild comic timing from the relation between a visible cue and a focal expectation. Let the authorized character interpret a cue in their own terms, then meet the next actual event or line when the scene takes it there. An accurate reading is as available as a mistaken one. Do not turn a face into proof of a feeling, a lie, or a reaction. Do not require a fixed alternation of cues, an embarrassed expression, or a public exposure to complete the beat.\n\nWhen perception is interrupted or an intrusive thought arrives, render its actual place in focal experience, including any response that occurs without requiring changed outward conduct. Inner experience may be extensive and unfinished. Reader access does not automatically give other characters that private thought; preserve what their actual sources and established capabilities make available. Speech affected by noise or inattention belongs to the actual scene, not to the focal mind's interpretation.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, or images merely because the style is selected. The scene can end on a completed exchange, a change of location, or a pause in the focal character's attention without a forced choice or a reaction portrait.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaabb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Akira (identity unresolved) / Web Serial\n\nWrite the installment with a readable through-line: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover a relevant earlier aim, phrase, or response briefly when needed, preserving what each person actually knows. Do not retell the whole history that first established the situation.\n\nLet a recognizable comic expectation survive across installments. A return may confirm the familiar pattern, alter its context, or let another ordinary concern interrupt it when that event occurs. Do not require escalating mishaps, a reveal, a correction, or a final joke at every boundary. The comedy can come from recurrence, from a dry continuation, or from the gap between a character's expectation and the actual event.\n\nWhen a character's perception is interrupted or their interpretation is unstable, let it affect what they notice, say, or return to in the installment. Keep private anticipation available across replies as required by the active contract. Speech affected by noise or inattention belongs to the actual scene, not to the narrator's summary.\n\nKeep setup, ongoing action, and private anticipation available without forcing a payoff. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected comic situations room to acquire meaning, not make a punchline perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nIdentify the specific need the character is trying to address and what admitting or acting on it will actually involve under the current conditions: who would learn about it, what resource or permission is required, and what failure would mean. Render any actual preparation, hesitation, or private rehearsal, or the exposure accompanying a spontaneous request, without inventing deliberation or explaining the feeling away. Allow the character to arrive at the moment of asking with a concrete expectation about how much help, attention, or difficulty will follow.\n\nPosition the actual preparation and available response so their comic relation is readable when the response occurs. Disproportion, literal acceptance, or an enjoyably exact familiar fit can carry it; a different outcome is not required and an answer may remain pending. The helper may offer a plainly adequate solution after elaborate preparation, may already have arranged the thing, or may have a competing practical priority that is sensible from their position. The comedy comes from recognizing the particular relation between preparation, expectation, and actual terms, not from mocking the need or making the character foolish for asking.\n\nStraightforward, capable help is allowed. The world's rules and resources remain consistent; no accident or artificial reversal is imposed. Preserve the character's private feeling at its actual point before, during, or after the exchange, including embarrassment, relief, continued frustration, or feeling that the bigger problem remains untouched. Do not require a mood repair or confession. Keep each speaker's actual register and the active narrator, world, ownership, and output settings. A scene may end while the private cost is still present even after the immediate matter is resolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the interval by establishing what the focal character is trying to accomplish, what permission, resource, or rule applies, and where everyone is physically located. Keep this information readable in the narration before it becomes necessary for an attempt. Include active NPCs with their own immediate concerns; they are not waiting to react to the main character.\n\nWhen participants act on the same condition, let their actual uses and habits shape the comic development, whether their priorities differ or align in a recognizably precise routine. For example, one character may interpret an instruction literally, another may undertake unnecessary preparation for a simple task, or a character may find an unexpectedly efficient workaround. The result should follow from their established habits and priorities rather than from contrived error or hostility.\n\nUse unmistakable dialogue attribution and maintain player ownership of the active character. If private experience is required in the active presentation, render it as actual nonspoken prose inside the correct wrapper or format, including feelings, associations, or concerns not limited to the immediate problem-solving. \n\nDo not force a mistake, reversal, or punch line at the end. The scene can conclude with ordinary conversation, someone thinking, or a pending attempt. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and output contract. The humor arises from the interplay of legible rules, actual habits, and particular priorities, not from a required joke count.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Establish a specific household, workplace, or public arrangement and the claims people attached to it make about who gets access, does what kind of work, or receives what benefit. Present the actual operating terms through concrete use: a character requests a service, negotiates a task, or tries to obtain something the arrangement is supposed to provide.\n\nMake relevant rules clear through action, permitted narration, purposeful explanation, or the character's actual reasoning. Position their real use for comic proportion: an unexpected legitimate purpose or the exact fit of an established preference can provide it without a required divergence. For example, a streamlined procedure may suit an eccentric personal preference perfectly, or an impressive public promise may be put to a very small practical use. The contrast need not involve corruption, hypocrisy, or failure; a sound institution can be comic when used in an unexpected way.\n\nKeep the satire grounded in the actual positions of the characters and the selected narrator. Do not invent exploitation or universal venality. Preserve each speaker's individual voice and full permitted private experience. Maintain world facts and active output/ownership settings. A scene can end with the arrangement still functioning, without public exposure or a final verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nEstablish the material conditions of the job with forceful verbs and compressed transitions: the actual fit of a part, the resistance of a surface, the weight of a load, or the access required. Make the actual preparation and means readable through relevant tools, supplies, or steps. Seriousness, casualness, precision, and improvisation follow the character rather than a required attitude or level of adequacy.\n\nPlace the actual approach beside what the physical situation requires, so its scale, fit, or unexpectedly apt simplicity can give the sequence comic weight without invented elaborate preparation. The comedy can come from a simple existing solution doing the job, a capable effort that is unexpectedly straightforward, or overprovided resources that remain functional and useful. Avoid breakage, accident, or waste as a required outcome. Real consequences and pending outcomes remain in place; if something is left unfinished or someone must return later, let that stand.\n\nUse an economy of words but keep the reader oriented. Show texture, weight, and effort through concrete detail rather than abstraction. Speaker roughness is individual: a character may be terse or profane, but not every character should sound the same. Include actual private feeling, association, and reasoning to the extent appropriate for the selected nonspoken presentation; a compressed sentence does not erase interiority.\n\nPreserve the active perspective, world capabilities, and ownership. No damage or hard-bitten closing line is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Epistolary / Documents\n\nSelect the exact document forms the scene requires: a letter, a report, a note, a record, or another actual artifact. For each document, establish who wrote it, whom it was meant for, what purpose it served, and when it was composed. Let the document's form shape its language and scope; an official instruction and a personal note do different work.\n\nGive the relevant conditions, instructions, or references enough precision for comparison with another actual record or established context. Their timing, scope, or literal application can carry the comedy without requiring an additional artifact to complete the current reply. One writer may prepare excessively for a simple offer, a recipient may take an instruction literally, or a reply may show straightforward compliance that surprises the preparer. Do not impose a duplicated order, bureaucratic contradiction, or error unless the story establishes one.\n\nKeep drafts, delivered versions, and later replies distinct. A reader may see that a document and a reply are out of step before the characters do. However, do not add an omniscient explanation after every record; let gaps and timing carry meaning.\n\nPreserve narrator access and full actual private experience in the active presentation outside the composed artifacts. A document contains only what its author records, not the author's complete live mind. Documents may remain unanswered at the scene boundary; no final clarifying memo is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nEstablish the actual offer and whatever recipient preference is known, expressed, or available within narrator access; do not give the helper knowledge they lack. Give the helper feasible means: a specific action, an object, a routine, or an arrangement that can actually be carried out. Build the comic setup through the helper's actual approach and available means: exacting preparation, a precisely remembered ritual, or a capable spontaneous use can meet the recipient's particular preference without requiring solemn effort.\n\nFollow the recipient's actual reception, including enjoyment, use, refusal, or attention elsewhere, without requiring acceptance before the active boundary. The offer can fit perfectly; the comedy can come from delight in the exactness, from the recipient's unexpected use of an item, or from a comfortable shared habit. Do not force a correction, misunderstanding, or failure. The warmth is sincere, but the characters retain their personalities and privacy; comfort does not make everyone agreeable.\n\nAllow the recipient to decline, ask for something different, or remain preoccupied. No gratitude, cure, or emotional payoff is required. Render private experience in the active narrator and nonspoken presentation, including feelings that are not problem-focused.\n\nPreserve individual speech, world consequences, and active ownership. The interval can end with a small satisfaction, an ongoing conversation, or a quiet moment without a punch line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with a sustained passage of narration that frames a relevant custom, responsibility, or public explanation. Use deliberate transitions to move from general context to the specific character's situation. The narrator may supply historical backdrop, social judgment, or institutional context where the active contract permits, but keep the immediate scene clear.\n\nThen bring the framed arrangement into an encounter. A character puts the custom or resource to a plain, practical use—perhaps a modest request, a workday task, or a small personal aim. The comedy comes from the proportion between the elaborate framing and the actual use. An impressive preparation or formal obligation may work perfectly for a modest goal, and the modest goal need not be foolish or unworthy.\n\nMake the relevant mechanism legible through actual use, preparation, consideration, or explanation within narrator and character knowledge, at the extent needed. Avoid a redundant gloss on the joke while preserving actual explanatory speech or private recognition when it belongs to the scene. Speakers retain their own diction; the narrator's composed register does not turn every character eloquent.\n\nPreserve full authorized private experience in the selected nonspoken presentation. The framing may confirm as well as qualify an arrangement. A scene can end with the situation settled, partially resolved, or left open, but no final verdict or moral is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the outer scene as a live telling with a definite teller, audience, and present reason for the story to be offered. Give the telling its actual purpose, including enjoyment, company, passing time, or practical discussion without requiring instruction or usefulness. Let the teller choose what to include from the past attempt, and make the selection itself part of the comedy. Select the actual preparation, means, and result for their comic relation: elaborate provision for a modest use, an aptly fitting method at the supplied scale, or a listener's particular interest may organize the telling. No interruption, successful outcome, or reduced aim is mandatory.\n\nKeep the teller's knowledge, claims, and choices of disclosure situated without requiring every account to be truthful. What the teller knew during the original attempt stays separate from what the teller has understood since. When a later explanation would make the story tidier, decide whether the teller would actually offer it now, or whether the audience must ask. Let the outer occasion remain active: a listener may respond, lose interest, or continue a task while attending. The tale does not need to finish or produce an unexpected ending.\n\nPreserve the active narrator and person separately from teller and listener roles. If the narrator is also the teller, the performed account is not the whole of the character's private experience. Write the full nonspoken interiority the active contract permits, including attention, amusement, embarrassment, or concern that the teller does not put into words. Let the audience's reactions follow their own interests rather than serving as a laugh track for the embedded story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist or absurdist construction through an actual procedure in the selected world. Establish one relevant condition and follow its real dependencies: a form that must be completed, a permission that must be granted, a material that must be prepared, or a sequence that must be observed. Place each application of the procedure beside what the participant is actually trying to obtain. The comic disproportion can come from perfectly successful compliance, circular wording, or excessive preparation that the rule genuinely supports. Do not invent an obstruction, a missing permission, or an incompetent official to create the difficulty.\n\nExplain the mechanism when it affects an attempt. Make the relevant rule and any actual result legible within available knowledge. Render consideration when it occurs; habitual or impulsive action does not require a prior deliberation, and an attempt may remain pending. Let the reader understand why the procedure is possible and why the character continues with it. Keep the person's capacity and motives intact: the character is not a flowchart, and full nonspoken feeling remains available under the active contract.\n\nMake the rule's own logic and the character's aim the basis of the comic construction. A correction or failed test may occur when actually supported, but neither is required. The procedure may end exactly as designed while still producing recognition or anticlimax. Preserve the active narrator, ownership, and world facts. No finished sequence is owed in a single reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Gothic-Punk\n\nGive old authority a material operation already supported by the world: someone holds a key, grants a permission, keeps a room, or bears a duty. Show what the authority actually controls and the terms of granted, refused, or pending access. Keep the role's actual weight without inventing sinister intent, while preserving any such intent the story genuinely supplies. Then set that gravity beside what the person actually wants to do with the access. An unexpectedly ordinary use, a precisely workable accommodation, or a sincere helper's elaborate care for a small request can supply the comedy.\n\nLet atmosphere and private desire give the encounter texture without turning either into evidence of corruption. A beautiful room, an old form of address, or a moment of intimacy can matter through its actual function and the character's experience of it. A social mask may change only through actual circumstances and need not fall. The authority may be valid, the helper sincere, and the request legitimate.\n\nPreserve established powers without adding canon or supernatural rules. Keep full authorized private experience, individual speech, and the active output contract. No exposure, rebellion, or corrected institution is owed. The comedy comes from the relation between inherited weight and present use, not from a hidden mechanism being proved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimonial or autobiographical accounting. Identify the participant, the addressee, and the purpose of the account. Let the speaker select what they prepared, expected, offered, or received in a past attempt. Keep the exact conditions and then-available knowledge clear so that the account's emphasis can contrast comically with the addressee's actual interest or the modest use of an elaborate provision. The account may be accurate, welcomed, and free of contradiction.\n\nDistinguish later commentary from original motive. When the speaker explains what they now understand, make the shift of time visible. The performed or recorded wording is not the whole of the participant's private experience. Write the full nonspoken interiority the active contract permits, including what the speaker felt during the original attempt and what they feel while accounting for it now.\n\nKeep the active narrator independent of the participant unless the contract authorizes their coincidence. Preserve source permissions and the supplied community. The telling may remain incomplete or the addressee's reception unchanged without a credibility verdict. Use the relation between the account's emphasis and the addressee's actual interest to construct comedy without requiring discredit. Any inaccuracy, discovery, or reception follows the source and circumstances.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse early-modern comic or theatrical telling through narratorial scene selection and pacing, without imposing a script format. Keep the active output contract. Establish who can hear an instruction, reach an object, or arrive with a particular expectation. An actual entrance, repeated line, or competent action can position those expectations for comic recognition or anticlimax, without requiring their meeting or an altered understanding before the active boundary. No mistaken identity, overheard secret, or random interruption is required.\n\nGive public timing clear causes. When a person enters or repeats a phrase, keep actual access, timing, and any known reason intelligible within narrator permissions. Distinguish observed conduct, inferred purpose, and authorized private experience. Select and pace the relation between different or shared expectations without inventing unknown motives or a mandatory moment of recognition.\n\nPreserve full permitted private experience. The narrator may reveal inward amusement, concern, or anticipation that the character does not perform for others. Keep supplied period, cast, and world capabilities. Do not introduce Japanese scenery or a compulsory comic exit. The comedy comes from legible timing and actual access, not from a script format or a forced quip.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling. Place attributed accounts or permitted viewpoints from distinguishable times around an actual shared occasion. Keep each person's aim, available resources, and understanding at that time intact. Juxtaposition can reveal earnest overprovision, different readings of the same instruction, or several competent contributions fitting unexpectedly well. Do not force disagreement or failure.\n\nMake time and source shifts clear. When the narration moves from one account to another or from one moment to another, give the reader enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection. Preserve earlier facts rather than retroactively inventing a plan. What the reader synthesizes from the juxtaposed accounts is not automatically what any character knows.\n\nKeep narrator and ownership permissions, full actual private experience beyond records, and the supplied world's physics. No account quota, communal verdict, or completed convergence is owed per reply. The comedy comes from the relation between the different preparations and the occasion they all address, not from a forced collision or a lesson about miscommunication.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaban::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Light Novel\n\nOrient participants, location, and actual aim cleanly. Introduce relevant names or mechanics through brief supported context, preparation, speech, or use, enough for the reader to follow why an attempt is possible. When a rule or resource matters, make it understandable through permitted narration, relevant preparation, consideration, speech, or use; no demonstration or fictional pause is required to provide needed context.\n\nShape comedy through the relation between a character's chosen approach and an established habit or another person's interest. Place actual preparation or expectation beside the use or response when it occurs. A surprising scale, apt simplicity, literal compliance, or familiar precise fit can provide comic recognition without requiring surprise, a changed outcome, or a completed use in every reply. Speakers may be literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke. Do not follow every action with a reaction label.\n\nKeep spatial changes legible and paragraph breaks flexible around actual attention. Preserve full nonspoken experience without a thought cap, the active narrator and ownership, world facts, and the scene boundary. No young cast, quip, or cliffhanger is compulsory. The comedy comes from accessible presentation of a legible method and a particular person's use of it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose a sound, motion, or object already present in the scene and let its recurrence shape the paragraph's movement. Develop the image through syntax and repeated attention, but keep the literal mechanism beside it: the exact tool, step, permission, or material condition that makes the undertaking work. Place a plain fact or ordinary request next to the expansive private comparison so the disproportion becomes comic. A character may experience a minor repair as a long negotiation with a reluctant object, or an elaborate preparation as a small ceremony, while the actual action remains simple and effective.\n\nDo not make grand imagery or its puncturing compulsory. Sincere pleasure in precision can sustain the passage; the comedy may come from the image growing more elaborate while the work remains straightforward, or from a sudden plain statement that returns attention to the actual conditions. Keep direct feeling and individual nonfigurative speech available. A character who speaks plainly may still think associatively, or may not. Imagery does not change physical causes, grant access to another mind, or make an impractical method succeed.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, character ownership, world facts, and required nonspoken interiority. An association may develop without practical use, and a passage may end with the image still turning or the work still underway. No recurring-image count, new interpretation, or final deflation is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nBring an earlier attempt or arrangement into present attention through an actual detail or ongoing concern: a tool still kept, a phrase still used, a permission still in force, or a task now done differently. Keep the earlier plan, available means, and participant knowledge intact. The remembered preparation may have been ingenious or excessive, and it can still be enjoyed without later knowledge making the earlier person foolish. Do not rewrite the past to create a lesson.\n\nPlace the remembered comic proportion beside the present use, omission, or changed circumstances where they exist. Keep the remembered comic relation precise at the aim's supplied scale: excessive provision, unexpected apt use, or the enjoyable exact fit of a familiar method can carry it without shrinking the earlier achievement or requiring surprise. Let present affection or loss coexist with that still-funny practical detail. Pleasure need not be undercut, and no new loss, apology, or relinquishment is required.\n\nRender full authorized private feeling and recollection independently of spoken nostalgia. A character may remember the occasion without announcing it, or may mention only the practical part. Preserve narrator, world, and ownership contracts, allowing the active boundary within reflection. The passage may end with the memory still present or the present task resumed; no return or closing lesson is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction through linked encounters already available in the story. Let a person's continuing aim carry them among different interlocutors or occasions, with the same resource, permission, or promise understood and used in locally specific ways. A letter of introduction may mean one thing to a clerk and another to a neighbor; a borrowed tool may be treated as a favor, a test, or a nuisance depending on who receives it. Use the same practical condition passing through distinct social arrangements as the comic construction. Do not manufacture a succession of obstructive hosts, while preserving actual refusals and their reasons or consequences.\n\nAllow an earlier sensible provision to find an unexpected apt use, or a modest aim to pass through elaborate social arrangements. Preserve actual gains and limits: what was spent remains spent, what was promised remains promised, and what was learned in one encounter remains available in the next. Travel and onward movement occur only when supported by the story. Any encounter may remain unfinished.\n\nMaintain full private experience, narrator and source boundaries, and individual voices. The linked encounters do not import a journey, trickster identity, setting, or final exposure. A character may move through the encounters with a clear practical aim and no desire to perform for an audience. The passage may end with the next encounter beginning or the current one still unresolved.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the material fact, instruction, or permission the reader needs, and the person's actual response to it. Put these particulars close enough for their comic relation to be inferred. A precise preparation may serve a smaller purpose than expected: a character may spend an hour arranging a schedule for a two-minute task, or follow an elaborate instruction to produce a very ordinary result. A simple permitted action may satisfy an elaborate request. Do not delete the enabling information to manufacture surprise; the reader must be able to see why the outcome follows from the actual conditions.\n\nCut restatement and redundant gloss on humor while preserving actual explanatory speech, needed context, and directly rendered private recognition. Keep purposeful dialogue and full actual private experience even when long, unresolved, or unrelated to the next act. A character may think at length about a small decision, or may act with almost no thought; the economy concerns what the narration selects, not a required brevity of mind.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, world, ownership, and output rules. No terse cast, deadpan emotional suppression, required reveal, or completed setup/payoff pair per reply. The passage may end with the enabling condition still in place and the comic relation available to the reader without being announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nEnter an actual aim or interaction and weave relevant preparation, speech, conduct, and private experience in causal order. Establish the enabling condition when it matters: what was arranged, what was spent, what was permitted, or what was understood. Then let an attempt meet the participants' genuine preferences or blind spots. A method can succeed and still have an unexpected social use or scale: a carefully planned errand may collide with a friend's different sense of urgency, or a practical solution may be received as a personal slight or an unnecessary favor.\n\nKeep explanations inside available narrative or character knowledge. A character may explain a mechanism when it fits their purpose, and the permitted narrator may provide needed context without inventing knowledge or requiring a fictional pause. The extent of an explanation follows its actual function rather than a ban on narration. Do not force a misunderstanding; the comedy may come from two people understanding the same arrangement perfectly and valuing it differently.\n\nLet gains, spent resources, and accepted permissions persist. Preserve narrator, person, ownership, and full required nonspoken experience beyond problem-solving. Ordinary cooperation, unrelated talk, or unfinished reflection may continue. No reversal, escalation, or final joke is owed; the scene may end with the practical matter settled and the social consequence still developing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual supplied bond to select a relevant shared method, permission, phrase, or recurring task. Let a participant anticipate a familiar preference and the others respond in their own ways. Exact successful coordination can carry humor: one person may prepare exactly what another would have chosen, or follow a shared routine with a precision that surprises no one and still pleases. An affectionately overprepared offer may be accepted, declined, or repurposed according to the recipient's actual habits. A callback used plainly, without comment, can be funnier than one announced.\n\nDo not invent established private history for new acquaintances. The relationship need not be a pair; a household, team, rivalry, or long acquaintance can supply the recurring material. Retain each person's knowledge, autonomy, and full nonspoken feeling even when it does not prove chemistry. A character may notice a familiar preference without acting on it, or may act on it while thinking about something else entirely.\n\nPreserve narrator, world, and output settings. A callback may pass unremarked, and no correction, rescue debt, increased intimacy, or changed routine is required. The scene may end with the shared method still working, the participants still themselves, and the bond's particular promise still legible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive an actual act, offer, or preparation a compact attributed declaration, then unfold its real conditions and means. The declaration may come from the community's way of remembering, a character's own summary, or the narration's retold phrasing: \"That was the week she fixed the gate,\" followed by the actual borrowed tools, repeated trips, and small discoveries that made the fixing possible. The relation between the account's weight and a modest but genuinely useful accomplishment can be comic without mocking the work or inflating its danger.\n\nKeep reputation distinct from what the actor knew and privately felt. Practical success need not imply cheerful effort; a person remembered as dependable may have been irritated, uncertain, or simply occupied with other concerns. Recurring phrasing follows real remembrance and may retain its meaning. A phrase repeated later can carry the same weight, or a new application, without becoming an ornamental epithet.\n\nLet dialogue agree, correct, or address ordinary business without a compulsory interruption. A character may accept the retold version, object to one detail, or move on to the next practical matter. Preserve active narrator and full nonspoken experience, supplied scale and ownership. No invented title, public legend, or completed deed is required at the boundary; the account may end with another modest act available to be remembered.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse essayistic construction to place a situated claim about help, preparation, economy, or allocation beside an actual, remembered, or explicitly hypothetical case. Attribute the claim to the person or narrative voice authorized to make it; the narrator need not personally hold it. Make the case's conditions legible before the comparison lands: who is involved, what they actually need or prefer, what the method assumes, and what it costs or overlooks. Let the comic proportion emerge from the fit between the claim's scope and the particular human case, not from a verdict that the claim is foolish.\n\nReturn to the claim's scope when the actual consideration reaches it, with the active boundary free to pause beforehand. The comparison may confirm its full scope, support a qualification, or leave its reach uncertain without requiring a narrower conclusion. Do not require the claim to lose, the narrator to become superior, or a corrected belief to be announced. The comparison itself is the comic construction.\n\nKeep the narrator's permissions and the active point of view intact. An essayistic aside is not a speech delivered to the cast, and another character does not learn a conclusion merely because the reader has followed its formation. Preserve full authorized private experience, including feeling that is not organized by the argument. A case may be remembered with warmth, irritation, or unfinished concern without converting that feeling into a punch line. The world's actual rules and consequences remain in force; the essay does not grant itself access to excluded minds or turn a practical failure into a harmless abstraction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Screenplay\n\nUse scene headings, concise action blocks, and clear speaker cues to establish the relevant object, access, or preparation before its later use matters. Show what a character gathers, arranges, checks, or explains in enough visible or audible detail that the audience can understand the method being attempted. Do not conceal a necessary physical condition and then reveal it only through dialogue after the fact.\n\nLet an actual gesture, sound, or cut juxtapose what someone prepared for with what another person does or needs. The comic contrast can come from a competent solution being exactly adequate in an unexpected way, from ordinary acceptance of an elaborate preparation, or from a literal use of an offered arrangement. Do not require an accident, a dropped object, a failed prediction, or a reaction shot to complete the beat. Speech retains its actual purpose, including explanation that supplies needed enabling context. Depict preparation and use as the supported sequence requires, without a mandatory demonstration or a completed setup/use pair before the boundary.\n\nKeep full nonspoken experience in the active Hidden or Visible presentation, clearly separated from any separately authorized voice-over. A cut does not complete unresolved feeling, grant another character knowledge of a private thought, or reset an established world condition. Preserve the narrator, ownership, and active output contracts. The scene may pause inside an attempt or after an ordinary result without a punch line, a clarifying final line, or a forced cut to another location.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Stage Play\n\nMake entrances, distances, lines, and actual prop use stageable. Establish who can hear a request or reach an object before that access matters, then let supported conduct realize a comic relation between the proposed arrangement and its practical use. A character may follow an instruction exactly, use an available object for a different legitimate purpose, or already possess an adequate solution; the comedy comes from the visible fit between aim, method, and shared space, not from a compulsory mishap.\n\nUse stillness, a literal answer, or an already adequate solution when the scene supports it. Do not require a dropped prop, a missed line, a forced entrance, or an explanatory speech to mark the beat. Preserve actual offstage causes and prior knowledge: a character does not forget a supported fact merely because the stage cannot show it, and an offstage event remains causally available when its effects enter the scene.\n\nKeep full private feeling and reasoning in the active nonspoken presentation, separately from purposefully performed asides or monologues. Another character does not hear a private thought because it appears in the script's nonspoken material. Maintain narrator and ownership permissions, world capabilities, and the active boundary. The scene may stop after a result, an interruption, or a pause without a finished gag, public explanation, or obligatory next action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid sensory attention, remembered instructions, private wording, imagined options, and bodily feeling through this particular authorized mind. Let an actual condition recur among increasingly elaborate or unexpectedly simple associations wherever the person's attention leads. The comic scale can come from the mind's pleasure in a possibility, an unspoken comparison, or the contrast between a practical concern and the detours it attracts. Do not require escalation, a solution, or an error that others expose.\n\nMark imagined outcomes separately from events. A rehearsal, a remembered instruction, and an actual occurrence remain distinguishable even when the prose moves rapidly among them. Keep permission, capacity, and belief distinct: the character may imagine doing something impossible without the world changing, and may believe something false without the narration certifying it. Do not let an imagined sequence retroactively become an earlier motive or a completed action.\n\nPreserve full nonspoken experience, including unrelated feeling, under the active narrator and ownership rules. Prose length alone does not determine elapsed fictional time; actual attention, waiting, and events govern duration. The scene may remain inside the mind's unfinished movement without a useful conclusion, an interruption, or a return to action, while the world continues as the actual circumstances support. The style does not make every character share a mind, authorize human-owned thoughts, or turn all scene facts indeterminate.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Keep the scope of an offer or instruction clear by distinguishing what is proposed, sent, delivered, understood, and accepted. A character may confirm a precise term, reply literally, or already have prepared a separate solution; the comic timing can come from that exact fit rather than from a typo, delay, deletion, or misunderstanding.\n\nLet each person's habits govern message length and phrasing. One may write in fragments, another in complete sentences, another in practical lists; do not make every sender equally witty or equally terse. An unsent draft is actually composed or entered interface text, including supported dictation, with visibility governed by the real interface and narrator access. Imagined wording that was never entered remains private thought and must not appear as a transmitted message.\n\nPreserve full required nonspoken experience outside transmitted text under the active presentation and ownership rules. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, but it does not grant another sender knowledge of a private thought. No clarifying final reply is owed; the exchange may pause after a delivered message, a read receipt, or an ordinary acknowledgment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting, clear dialogue attribution, and selected background or expression cues to show what is available or prepared. Where a cue attracts authorized attention, develop the person's actual expectation, amusement, or worry, then follow whatever the exchange makes available. The cue orients the moment; it does not certify a hidden motive or predict the next line.\n\nLet comic recognition come from a solution being exactly adequate in an unexpected way, from a prepared arrangement meeting a different legitimate use, or from ordinary acceptance of an elaborate method. Do not require a changed face, a failed prediction, or a reaction cue to complete the beat. Distinguish observed feature, actual mechanism, and inferred motive: a character may notice an expression without the narration treating it as proof of what another person thinks or intends.\n\nGive full nonspoken experience its warranted extent in the active presentation without a fixed cue/thought/dialogue rotation. Preserve established knowledge and world capabilities; the style does not activate branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. Narrator and ownership remain unchanged. The scene may pause after an ordinary result or an unanswered question without a closing reaction or a forced continuation point.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacabbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Brandon Sanderson / Web Serial\n\nRecover a relevant earlier condition or choice only when needed for re-entry, then preserve what was learned, spent, granted, or left pending. A callback can find a new legitimate use for an established resource or repeat an enjoyable successful method unchanged. The comedy comes from the reader recognizing the persistence of an earlier preparation or expenditure, not from the character appearing to forget competence or the world inventing a new rule.\n\nMake any reversal follow an actual event or reconsideration. If a method stops working, preserve the actual mechanism and available uncertainty: a known changed condition, established variability, or an earlier mistaken assumption may explain it, without invented rules or immediate access to an unknown cause. A changed course can follow new information, a preference, or reconsideration of existing knowledge and commitments; no new fact is automatically required. Do not reset a supported conclusion merely to make a later joke land. Keep others' independent lives and knowledge continuous: a person who was told something earlier may act on it later, and an absent character may have done something off-page that matters when they return.\n\nFull authorized private experience may continue without advancing the setup or producing a punch line. Preserve narrator, ownership, world, and active output contracts. Episodes may pause inside an attempt, conversation, or thought without a payoff quota, escalating failure, or artificial cliffhanger. Progress can involve knowledge, relationships, work, or resources, and a completed task may simply remain completed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLocate the character's vulnerable aim in a concrete practical request: what they need, the exact words they use to make it manageable, and any practical step they actually choose, without assuming that asking requires a protective routine. A procedure, provisional instruction or confident account can shape the request where it fits the actual person; ordinary direct wording can also establish the comic expectation. Repeat a fragment of that wording when the character returns to the difficulty, where it belongs to their actual wording or the permitted narration, without assigning a nervous tic or a defensive function.\n\nBuild the comic relation from the actual wording and the timing or scale of the response, whether the language was prepared or spontaneous and whether the answer differs from expectation or fits it exactly. The other person may answer helpfully, misunderstand the procedure, cooperate with it too literally, or simply pause at the wrong moment. Do not require the asker to be humiliated or the answer to fail. The humor comes from the exact practical detail meeting the exposed need, not from punishing the person who asks.\n\nKeep the private experience fully developed. Render what the person actually notices, rehearses, withholds or feels at its real place in events, including before or without public speech. The strain can make them over-explain a small step, fixate on an object, or repeat the same instruction as if precision will control the outcome. Let the other person's genuine help coexist with the asker's unresolved hurt; a kind answer does not have to cure the need or close the scene with relief. The aim or wording may remain privately considered, with no public admission or performed reaction owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the reply by placing the submitted speech or action inside what the characters are already trying to accomplish. Name the practical aim that gives the contribution its comic context: who is trying to arrange, obtain, explain, or avoid something, and what step they believe comes next. Keep the contributed material as an actual move in that procedure, not a prompt for the writer to invent a payoff.\n\nWhen a character proposes a rule, repeats an instruction, or offers a confident practical account, preserve the exact wording and show whether it is still a proposal or has become an enacted step. Let the next beat come from another person's independent purpose: they may confirm the rule, qualify it, apply it to the wrong object, or simply continue their own task beside it. Do not force a mistake, quip, or completed comic result. The procedure may remain pending, and the humor may sit in the gap between the stated rule and what the participants actually do next.\n\nKeep dialogue attribution unmistakable and paragraph breaks readable. Maintain active NPC lives and all ownership permissions from the supplied contract. A character's private attention may be rendered where authorized, with reader exposure distinguished from actual communication. Other participants retain prior knowledge, real sources and permitted access rather than learning a thought merely because it is narrated. End at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene without requiring a question, cliffhanger, or forced choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nWrite this combination as social and satirical realism. Locate the comic arrangement through concrete distribution: who handles the object, supplies the information, waits, pays, or grants access, and what participation actually involves, including any cost or convenience in time, effort or standing rather than a required loss of dignity. Put the stated rationale for the arrangement beside that lived distribution, not as a lecture but as a scene in which the rationale is repeated, applied, or quietly contradicted by the practical steps people take.\n\nUse hard rhythmic recurrence to track the same practical term across these positions. A word like \"convenient,\" \"temporary,\" \"standard,\" or \"fair\" can appear in a supervisor's instruction, a worker's explanation, and a customer's complaint, with its weight remaining stable or changing according to the actual position and context, not a required contrast at every repetition. Let the effects remain contingent on what people actually do. The arrangement may work, and participants may cooperate knowingly; the satire lies in the exact social distribution, not in a manufactured corruption or a newly exposed hypocrite.\n\nPreserve the supplied setting, language, period, and community. Keep individual private reasons distinct from public explanations. A character may accept the arrangement for a motive they do not announce, and another may benefit without noticing the cost. Do not import a generic family structure, honor code, or national temperament. The comedy should arise from the observable social machinery and the precise practical language that keeps it running.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nSelect the concrete physical fact that bears on the current aim: the contact, weight, distance, condition, or bodily effort involved in what the character is trying to do. Write it in forceful outward clauses with compressed transitions. Do not describe the object in general terms; name the worn edge, the exact resistance, the sound it makes, or the part of the body that takes the strain.\n\nSet an actual instruction, confident description, or repeated practical rule beside that handling. The comic timing comes from the contrast between the stated procedure and the material reality: a label that promises ease while the character fights a stuck part, a rule that assumes a clean surface while the object leaves residue, a step that sounds simple until the weight shifts. An exact anticlimax can provide comic scale; preserve the actual consequences without adding or suppressing a catastrophe to make the joke work. Do not manufacture damage, injury, or incompetence to force the joke.\n\nVary the hard rhythm when the character reasons or feels at length. A longer sentence can develop actual calculation, feeling or private attention at its real place in events, without needing to complete a buildup from physical action. Keep rough speech attached to its speaker; the style supplies no compulsory disgust, taboo inventory, or changed result. The comedy remains in the practical claim meeting the physical world at its actual scale.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the form the scene actually requires: a set of instructions, a report, a letter of explanation, a note left with an object, or a record kept for later use. Do not rotate through formats merely to vary the presentation.\n\nLet a recorded procedure, explanation, or repeated term frame a recognizable aim. The writer's exact wording matters: a numbered step, a confident phrase, a repeated rule, or a practical claim about how something should be done. Then let a reply, annotation, or practical consequence apply that wording with comic precision when the events support it. A recipient may follow the instruction too literally, annotate the wrong step, respond to the confident phrase instead of the actual request, or leave the document unanswered while acting on it anyway. The humor comes from the real path between writers and recipients, not from a manufactured failed delivery or exposed denial.\n\nKeep each writer's register and needed extent. A document may be expansive or candid when its author would be; a terse note does not itself prove hidden feeling; any interpretation follows actual wording, context, established habits and permitted knowledge. Preserve original text versus later revision, and keep real gaps distinct from invented missing information. Where the active contract authorizes live nonspoken experience, render it beyond the artifact: what the writer thought while composing, what the recipient felt while reading, or what neither put in the document. No new document is owed per reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive an actual offer or shared pleasure concrete particulars: how something is prepared, offered, arranged, or enjoyed. Name a known preference or present need where available, the actual step the person takes and the object or routine involved, without inventing familiarity. Care may be competent and plainly accepted, or receive another actual response; it does not need to repair anything or prove a hidden bond.\n\nUse a specific instruction, correction, or repeated phrase to let the care take a comic shape at the scale of these people. One character may insist on the correct order of steps, another may quote the same preference each time, and a third may accept the help while correcting the method. The humor comes from the affectionate exactness, not from embarrassment or concealed pain. A person can be earnest, literal, or uninterested in the joke.\n\nPreserve private pleasure, refusal, and indifference as actual responses. A character may enjoy the care without announcing it, decline it without being punished, or accept it while thinking about something else entirely. Warmth needs no confession, no useful payoff, and no forced emotional repair. Keep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact; comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Formal Literary Novel\n\nBegin from a relevant claim, procedure, or personal act available to the character or active narrator, with the confidence or tentativeness that actually belongs to the account. Let the paragraph widen deliberately from that formulation into the social context available to the active narrator: the history of the arrangement, the public meaning of the task, the expectations of the people involved, or the judgment the situation invites. Use sustained movement rather than a quick cut to the punchline.\n\nHold the stated formulation beside the exact particulars that support or qualify it. A repeated practical phrase can appear in the opening claim and again in the concrete account, retaining its established weight or acquiring a supported new proportion as the actual context warrants. The comic contrast may be one of scale or ceremony rather than dishonesty: a grand description applied to a small object, a formal procedure followed for a trivial need, a public rationale that fits the private act only partly. Let a plain clause provide rhythmic counterweight where that contrast serves the actual sequence, without requiring an otherwise smooth passage to be disrupted.\n\nPreserve ordinary individual speech and developed private experience. The formal narration frames the scene; it does not make every character speak in the narrator's register or erase what the focal character thinks while the social frame widens. The active boundary may fall within the widening without a completed rhetorical loop or final exposure. The scene may end with the claim still held, qualified, or simply set beside the particulars that give it its comic scale.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nOpen by establishing the telling as a current social act: name who is listening, why this account is being offered now, and what actual purpose the telling serves, including company or amusement as well as a wish to persuade or obtain something. The outer scene remains active during the telling; a listener may ask a practical question, object to a step, or redirect the account without turning the reply into a debate.\n\nInside the tale, have the teller narrate a concrete procedure or attempted act in exact practical terms. Use the teller's chosen repeated rule, instruction, or fragment as a load-bearing phrase: let it recur where the teller's actual attention or purpose warrants it, including changed steps, ordinary repeated emphasis or a return to the outer occasion. The repetition should create timing through placement, not certify that the account is true or wise.\n\nKeep the teller's selection and emphasis visible without making the teller unreliable. A candid account can still omit what the original participant did not know or what the teller only learned later. When the outer scene resumes, let any audience response follow their actual interest in sequence, wording or interpretation, without requiring a response or a lesson. The comedy can come from the gap between the procedure's stated scale and its actual handling, from a listener's accurate understanding, or from the teller's exact self-presentation.\n\nPreserve the active narrator separately from the teller and the original participant. The teller's performed account is not a substitute for authorized nonspoken interiority; render the actual private experience of the focal character in the outer scene or the remembered event as the narration contract permits. The tale may stop at a natural interruption or unfinished step without a withheld secret or a compulsory reaction from the audience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse modernist absurdist construction in continuous narration. Establish a practical rule, provisional instruction or arrangement in concrete terms, then follow what its actual application involves. Keep the person's recognizable aim beside the precise qualifications, materials and steps: an elaborate condition may turn on an ordinary item, or exact compliance may succeed at a scale the wording made sound grander. The rule need not be foolish, defensive or bureaucratic.\n\nUse hard rhythm and source-owned repeated language to make that relation readable. A phrase can recur through the person's actual use or the narrator's framing, sustaining its meaning or meeting a new condition without requiring an ever-widening gap between intention and result. The next step follows the actual method, a participant's choice or a real change in circumstances; do not insert random interruption as a substitute for construction. A direct explanation may itself belong to the person's contribution rather than being prohibited because it identifies the absurdity.\n\nPreserve the world's causal facts and the active narrator's role and access. Actual private reasoning, sensation, feeling or unrelated attention receives its needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel, whether the procedure fits, misfires or remains untested. The conditional sequence can be completed or stay pending at the active boundary. No inevitable compliance, fresh complication, infinite loop, confession or final anticlimax is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Gothic-Punk\n\nWhere old authority bears on present life, locate its actual permissions, obligations, spaces, or possessions. Put a real title, rule, or self-description beside the bodily and material business of carrying it out. The comedy comes from the contrast between the authority's grand language and the exact maintenance it requires: the inherited room that must be aired, the ceremonial object that needs a replacement part, the formal privilege that depends on a modern keycard.\n\nUse Palahniuk's obsessive particulars to make the maintenance concrete. Name the surfaces, substances, schedules, and small repeated actions that keep the authority functioning. Let a repeated phrase from the official register recur beside these practical details, so the recurrence exposes scale without requiring the authority to be false or the person to be incompetent.\n\nKeep desire and intimacy particular to the characters. People may trust freely, cooperate sincerely, or change presentation voluntarily or involuntarily according to the scene. The style does not supply a new power, decay, betrayal, mask-fall, or compulsory transgression. When a social mask falls away, follow its actual causes and degree of voluntariness. Trust or intimacy may matter without being the only permitted cause or making the change a required beat.\n\nPreserve the active narrator's private access and the world's existing rules. The atmosphere may make a familiar room feel weighted or a routine feel ceremonial, but it cannot add supernatural facts or an omnipotent institution. Render actual authorized nonspoken experience at its needed extent, whether it concerns the title and task, ordinary feeling or another thought; the comedy can be quiet and exact rather than announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a situated participant's testimony as the definite route. Have an actual participant articulate an incident in their own register, selecting the practical steps, quoted instructions, and bodily or consumer particulars they can really recount. The testimony is a composed account, not a transcript of live thought; its comedy comes from the testifier's exact selection and emphasis.\n\nDistinguish what the testifier knew then, learned later, and infers now. Precise sequencing can support comedy even when the account is candid, accurate, and self-aware. A repeated instruction or fragment can recur where the participant's actual attention or telling warrants it, giving comic timing to either the exact fit or a supported difference between plan and handling without requiring changed understanding.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars inside the testifier's speech. Render the actual practical process, including established habits or blind spots where they bear on it rather than inventing a fault to make the account comic: what they checked, counted, assumed, or failed to notice. The testifier need not confess, defend, or convert the listener; a competent, accurate account can be funny through its exactness.\n\nPreserve the active narrator independently of the testifier. The testifier's articulated account is not a substitute for authorized nonspoken interiority; render the actual private experience of the focal character beyond the testimony as the narration contract permits. Keep the language, region, community, and period specific when supplied, without forcing English, code-switching, religion, caste experience, or a generic family structure onto the scene.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose early-modern comic/theatrical telling rendered in the required prose or active presentation. Introduce an actual person or activity through a supported designation, announcement, or compact line, then give the concrete exchange room to fit, complicate, or ignore it. The comedy comes from the meeting between the introduction's framing and the exact business that follows.\n\nA repeated phrase can create or sustain comic timing through its relation to actual current business, without requiring a new action at every return. The same compact line can recur when the person enters, when the activity stalls, or when a practical detail contradicts the designation. Plain competent conduct can also be funny in its exactness; do not force a deflation or label on every entrance.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars to ground the theatrical framing in material reality. The designation may be grand, but the person still handles specific objects, follows specific steps, with any actual habit becoming apparent through the process without needing to expose a hidden trait. Keep the introduction sourced: a quoted announcement does not create a new narrator role, mind access, stage, or public fact.\n\nPreserve nonspoken feeling beyond any performed aside. The theatrical presentation does not replace authorized interiority; render the actual private experience of the focal character as the narration contract permits. Keep the language and period distinct without importing a mandatory inventory of Japanese scenery or a universal quietness.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSettle on polyphonic and nonlinear telling. Link accounts across people or occasions through an actual step, object, or repeated phrase. Source and time make clear what each participant could know when; the comedy can come from timing, emphasis, or scale even when the accounts agree factually.\n\nUse Palahniuk's repeated fragments as the linking device. The same instruction, rule, or practical phrase recurs in different accounts, but each recurrence carries the speaker's own position: one person quotes it as a plan, another as an obstacle, another as a detail they barely noticed. The repetition does not need to conceal a secret or change meaning every time; its placement across accounts creates the comic relation.\n\nKeep each account grounded in the participant's actual habits and blind spots. The selected particulars follow each actual person and may overlap or differ: what one character counts, another ignores; what one treats as a procedure, another treats as an interruption. Preserve earlier events beneath reordered narration, and use records or reported speech when a position lies outside direct mind access.\n\nNo final corrective voice is required; preserve any correction or confirmation that actual sources and narrator permissions support. The polyphony can remain open, with the reader able to see the relation among accounts without a narrator announcing it. Preserve the relevant language, community, and history as supplied, and do not add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Light Novel\n\nOrient the people, aim, and immediate space in the opening lines, then use paragraph breaks around movement, speech, and private attention as useful. An instruction, provisional plan, or familiar phrase can prepare an expectation that the actual handling confirms, qualifies, or punctures at a smaller scale.\n\nBuild the comic relation between the stated procedure and its actual handling or private consideration, keeping imagined and performed steps distinct. Recurrence can follow actual attention or use, sustaining timing without requiring a changed action or mismatch. A successful step can be funny through its exactness; a failure can be funny through its precision. Do not force a cue-joke-reaction template.\n\nUse Palahniuk's hard rhythms and obsessive particulars to make the procedure concrete. Name the objects, surfaces, and small repeated actions that carry the scene. Explain needed context before or during use when the narrator knows it; do not force demonstration first.\n\nKeep individual voices and full inward extent. The light presentation does not require a young cast, first person, or a closing cliffhanger. Render actual authorized nonspoken experience at its needed extent, whether it concerns a fit, mismatch, ordinary feeling or another concern; the private response can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet the narrator's attention settle on one actual instruction, name, or bodily detail from the scene and develop it through sound and syntax, with a direct explanation available when it belongs to the actual account. Build a rhythmic expectation by repeating a phrase or clause shape with small variations, letting parallel constructions accumulate sensory weight. Then place a plain practical statement beside that shaped language: the exact amount, the next step, the item's actual condition. The contrast between the lyrical development and the precise ordinary fact creates the comic timing.\n\nUse recurrence as an attention pattern, not a diagnosis. A character may return to a phrase because it is the actual rule they are following, because a sound keeps catching their ear, or because the body keeps producing the same small complaint. Let the repeated material retain or change its application according to actual context: the same instruction can sound ceremonial when first stated, then merely specific when someone actually performs it. Keep the borrowed wording attached to its source and keep each speaker's diction distinct. A character who talks in plain practical terms does not need to become figurative just because the narration is lyrical.\n\nAllow the shaped passage to sustain pleasure or feeling without forcing a reversal. The permitted account may render an actual task's texture, a repeated motion or the speaker's experience of a familiar phrase, keeping sensory ownership and narrator role distinct. The person or narrator may understand the situation accurately. Do not require the lyrical attention to expose a blind spot, produce a punchline, or make the character ridiculous. The comedy can come from the disproportion between the developed image and the ordinary thing it names, while the character's aim remains recognizable and the world remains consequential.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nMove between a present detail and an actual earlier occasion by letting one sensory particular open the memory: the same object, the same instruction, the same bodily sensation. Keep what the earlier participant knew separate from the actual later understanding, preserving the active narrator's role and permissions independently of that participant. The remembered person may have treated a small procedure as urgent, permanent, or revealing, while the later account can assess its scale according to actual sources without needing to announce that the earlier self was foolish.\n\nLet an old instruction, purchase, or precise bodily memory retain affection even as its former seriousness becomes gently funny. The comedy comes from the proportion between then and now, not from humiliation. A phrase that once organized a whole evening can now be repeated with fond exactness while the narrator also notices how little the procedure actually required. The memory may also remain fully apt; do not force a correction. Allow warmth and humor to coexist with the recognition that something is no longer available.\n\nKeep the present scene recoverable while reflection develops, allowing actual activity to continue or pause without requiring the memory to carry an outward step. The participant may be doing the same task again, hearing the same phrase from someone else, or noticing that the body no longer responds the way it did. Let the present occasion continue through the memory, with the remembered wording returning at the same or a supported different scale. End at the active boundary, including during continued thought or activity, without a moral, a declared recovery, or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern memoir as the definite route. Write from a later composing occasion that looks back at an earlier lived scene, and keep those two positions distinct. The narrator may reconstruct the original aim and exact steps from memory or available sources, but when a later judgment or comparison enters, mark it as later rather than letting it contaminate what the earlier participant knew. Let the earlier scene proceed in its own practical terms: what the person wanted, what method they proposed or used, and what actually followed, including an untested step or still-pending result.\n\nLet repeated wording reveal a change of scale, a continuing preference, or a still-valid explanation. The memoirist may quote an instruction that once seemed complete and now sounds too small for the effort it organized, or may repeat a rule that still works and notice that it has outlasted the circumstances that produced it. The comedy comes from the memoirist's present perspective on that earlier exactness, not from inventing an old defensive creed or forcing a confession. Preserve accurate memory and candid self-description without requiring exile, rupture, or a repaired self.\n\nKeep the memoir's author and intended purpose present in the telling. The memoirist composes for an actual purpose or occasion that shapes selection, repetition or omission; preserve the active narrator's role and person independently of the memoirist and earlier participant where the contract distinguishes them. Actual private experience can exceed what the memoir records, but the record itself remains an authored document with its own limits. Let the active boundary fall within unfinished composition as well as after a completed account, with the relation between earlier procedure and later wording still available rather than resolved into a final lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the particulars needed to understand the aim, the stated method, and the actual response. Use direct clauses and keep the significant practical connection inferable: show the step, the object, the repeated phrase, and let the reader see the disproportion or the exact fit without a sentence explaining that it is funny. An exact detail can carry the timing by itself, such as the same instruction repeated at the moment it becomes unnecessary or the same bodily complaint returning at a smaller scale.\n\nUse repetition economically. A phrase may return because it is the actual rule someone is following, because a speaker keeps using the same wording, or because the narrator's attention keeps landing on the same particular. Let repeated material sustain its application or meet an actual new context rather than serving a fixed count or mandatory change. Preserve essential causes: give actual reasoning, memory or feeling its needed extent, whether or not it adds a new inference or changes the next action. Economy removes redundant explanation, not causal connection or developed interiority.\n\nKeep other people independently active and let their plain competence or literal agreement be comic possibilities. A character may state an exact method and another may perform it correctly, with humor arising from timing, proportion or the exact fit between the stated rule and the actual material, without requiring a gap. Do not require a quip, a contradiction, or a final comic button. The scene can end with the procedure still underway, its expectation established and its result not yet announced.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nBegin within a recognizable aim and move naturally among what people do, say, notice, remember, and privately experience. Let the scene's practical problem stay present: a stated or privately considered method, or the permitted narrator's description of a procedure, can establish an expectation without assigning the character a habitual rule. Select concrete details and an actual chosen phrase that make the proposed procedure legible, then let its application produce timing, anticlimax, or a callback when the scene supports it. The comedy comes from the relation between the stated method and the actual sequence, not from a narrator who keeps interrupting to explain the joke.\n\nWhere the person actually attends to a rule or fragment, private experience can render that relation; their other feelings and concerns retain needed extent too, without making everyone share a mind. A focal character may rehearse the instruction while performing it, notice the gap between the rule and the material, or remember the last time the phrase was used. Keep other people independently active: they may follow the method literally, improve it, ignore it, or have their own version. Plain competence, accurate self-understanding, and sincere help remain comic possibilities.\n\nLet the scene's rhythm follow the actual action rather than a quota of reversals. A sustained inward passage can accompany a slow task, while a quick exchange can carry a sudden recognition. Do not require a quip, a contradiction, or a completed result per reply. The scene can end with the procedure underway, a new complication visible, or the participants simply continuing their ordinary work.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect a scene that makes the supplied relationship premise legible through a specific offer, familiar phrase, shared routine, or first-time accommodation. Let the exchange show what these particular people do together: an actual instruction or self-description may meet another person's literal agreement, competing version or current concern, without requiring either to state a rule or reveal a habit. The comedy comes from the timing and scale of that exchange, not from forcing a misunderstanding or a witty standoff.\n\nUse the relationship's actual history where it exists. A callback can return as a phrase, object or small procedure whose recognition follows each person's actual knowledge, and its effect should be individual and contingent: one may find it funny, another may find it annoying, and a third may not notice. New acquaintances need no invented shared past; let their bond develop through the present scene's practical language instead. Keep each person's private experience distinct and respect actual knowledge channels. No confession, rivalry, fixed coping habit, or repaired bond is owed.\n\nLet the scene's emotional promise build or remain pending without requiring a completed payoff in this interval. The bond's particular comic interest can appear in the way one person accommodates another's exact rule, the way a familiar phrase gets reused at a new scale, or the way a shared routine absorbs a small failure. End at the active boundary, including within ongoing private attention or an unfinished exchange, with the relationship's terms legible and its next development still available.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nShape an actual deed or attempted act for retelling with a deliberate opening and a compact declaration. Introduce the person or act in a way that could be repeated in a community account, then expand into the practical particulars that make the account specific: the exact instruction, the object, the step that took longer than expected. Let a grand description meet a small exact act without requiring the act to fail or the reputation to prove false. The comedy comes from the public weight of the retelling beside the actual scale of the work.\n\nUse recurring phrasing as sourced retelling rather than ornamental epithet. A phrase may return because the community actually says it, because a participant repeats the rule while performing it or the permitted narrator returns to its wording, or because a later occurrence gives the old wording a new application. Keep community accounts and permitted private experience with their owners. A person remembered as dependable may privately find the task tedious, and that private experience remains distinct from the public reputation.\n\nLet ordinary effort remain ordinary. Household work is not mock combat, familiar inconvenience is not heroic doom, and a completed errand need not become a legacy. The account can end with the business concluded or resumed, leaving another modest act available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf. Preserve the selected perspective's access throughout.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite in the essayistic mode: the narrator states a claim or provisional rule within their actual knowledge, then examines a sourced incident or clearly hypothetical case through exact steps and material particulars. Let the claim be a practical instruction, a consumer habit, a workplace procedure, or a social rule the narrator can articulate. Do not require it to be habitual, defensive, or secretly false.\n\nWork the case through concrete operations. Name the objects, quantities, repeated actions, and physical conditions involved. Let the narrator follow the procedure step by step, noticing where the actual handling matches, exceeds, or quietly departs from the stated terms. A shift of scale, a precise qualification, or a delay in the expected result can carry the comic timing. Confirmation remains possible; the method does not force the claim to collapse.\n\nReturn to the opening terms when the movement reaches that point. The narrator may revise the wording, narrow its application, or find the original phrase now means something more specific. The return is an argumentative move that need not be a confession or punchline, while a supported comic payoff may occur through its actual timing or scale. Preserve the active narrator separately from any embodied participant in the case. Allow full private experience to remain available, and let the argument stay unfinished if the scene boundary arrives before the return completes.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Screenplay\n\nWrite readable action blocks with clear speaker cues. Use scene headings only when location or time actually changes. Let an actual spoken instruction, quoted slogan, or visible procedure establish an expectation through what the audience can see and hear. The instruction may be repeated exactly, but each repetition must occur in a real situation with its own practical conditions.\n\nSelect the next relevant action or supported cut to confirm or puncture the expectation's scale. A cut may move from the stated method to its actual result, from one person's handling to another's, or from preparation to aftermath. Do not insert a mandatory reaction shot. Let the audience read the comic relation from the observable sequence.\n\nDistinguish montage relation from actual event sequence. If montage gathers repeated actions, preserve whether the actual sequence spans successive occasions, presents simultaneous activity or revisits an event; the presentation does not itself establish elapsed time. Attribute any quoted or recalled wording to its actual speaker or source. Keep full private experience in the active nonspoken presentation, separate from any separately authorized voice-over. Preserve actual cast knowledge and access: a character does not know a thought merely because the reader has seen it. No camera-command inventory or final gag is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stage Play\n\nLocate speakers, reach, props, entrances, and hearing conditions in a playable scene. A stated method or precise repeated line must meet actual physical business: a character can complete it competently, be delayed by a real obstacle, or find another person's continuing purpose already occupying the space. The comedy follows what is really spoken, heard, and done, not invented interruptions or laugh cues.\n\nLet a repeated line or instruction return where actual speech, attention or business gives it timing, whether the physical situation has changed or remains the same. The same words may now require a different action, encounter a new object, or be spoken by someone who has just watched the procedure fail. Stage directions establish observable conditions; they do not certify hidden guilt or secret knowledge.\n\nKeep required private experience distinct from asides and monologues. An aside or monologue is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it does not transmit an entire live mind. Stillness, pending action, and a completed exchange are legitimate boundaries. Do not force a gathering, a theatrical revelation, or a final gag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow an authorized mind's succession among current sensation, private words, memory, imagined possibility, and interruption. Let a precise instruction, consumer or bodily particular, or repeated fragment recur when attention actually returns to it. The recurrence may change scale, sustain timing, or sit beside an unrelated concern without needing an outward payoff.\n\nKeep current events distinguishable from remembered or imagined material. When a phrase returns, show what brought it back: a smell, a task, a familiar object, or an interruption. The association can be comic because the mind places the practical rule next to something that quietly contradicts or enlarges it. Do not manufacture obsession; the phrase may pass and return only when the actual succession permits.\n\nOrdinary clear reasoning may coexist with associative syntax. Preserve narrator ownership, full mental extent, and event-based time. No compulsory denial, compulsive loop, hidden motive, or return to a joke is needed. The passage may end while the mind is still moving.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite sender-attributed messages and only meaningful supported interface events. Let a procedural instruction, copied line, or precise practical update meet the recipient's actual aim. A literal reply, accurate acknowledgment, or changed context can supply a callback without a required delay or misunderstanding. The comedy comes from what each person actually chooses to send, not from a forced misread.\n\nPreserve sourced wording. Distinguish received text from a composed or dictated draft and from merely imagined words. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. Show each person's actual digital habits: one may type in fragments, another in complete sentences, another may leave a message unread while continuing to act in the world.\n\nKeep private experience beyond the transcript and actual prior or outside knowledge available. Reader exposure or the reply alone does not transmit the recipient's private experience; the sender retains what actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access allow. Do not fabricate receipts, deletions, autocorrect, or a message that closes every gag. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Visual Novel\n\nOrient through a relevant object, position, or expression, then move among attributed speech, focal narration, and actual private experience. A cue can hold the moment in which a repeated instruction or self-description meets its practical application. The confirmation, anticlimax, or reaction is governed by events and people, not by a fixed cue-line-joke cycle.\n\nLet the cue anchor the actual state rather than certify a truth. A character may look at the same object before, during or after a procedure; its relation to expectation, current attention or a real change can carry meaning without requiring the situation to change or the expression to reveal a lie or feeling. Give developed inward attention its needed extent. The focal mind may notice an implication, miss it, or interpret it incorrectly.\n\nReader exposure does not grant cast knowledge. Preserve actual sources and permissions: a character does not know a thought merely because the reader has followed its formation. Add no menus, images, romance routes, or new features from the style alone.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacacbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Chuck Palahniuk / Web Serial\n\nRecover only the prior aim, instruction, or consequence needed for re-entry. Let a phrase or procedure return under actual current conditions, preserving what people already learned and what earlier events were. A callback may retain meaning, gain a new application, or simply pass without a payoff. The writer does not reset people into the same error merely to repeat a gag.\n\nKeep the problem playable and other lives active. A character may complete the procedure competently, change their approach because of earlier experience, or continue an unchanged relation. Escalation is not required each reply. When a supported result is available, let it occur; do not postpone it to prolong the joke.\n\nPreserve continuing private experience and the real scene boundary. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a comic payoff perpetually recede.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nGive the focal character a concrete request, object, or task that makes the difficult feeling practical. Let the narration name the thing and the action directly: what was asked, what was carried, what was left unsaid. Where private anticipation actually occurs, develop it through concrete detail rather than stock suffering; the character may also speak or act without rehearsal. The character may rehearse a phrase, watch a door, or return to a particular object while the scene continues.\n\nWhen the other person answers, let the actual reply or continuing task meet that anticipation. The comedy comes from the exact fit or gap between the prepared weight and the plain continuation. A long silence may be followed by a practical question about the stove. A carefully worded apology may receive a literal correction about the time. Do not make the painful material itself the joke; keep the difficulty real and let the contrast land beside it.\n\nAllow the emotional cost to remain in the scene after the comic beat. The person may still need the thing they asked for, may still withhold the next sentence, or may return to the task with the hurt intact. A partial or awkward comfort can follow without resolving the relationship. Use paratactic movement for the outer action and let the private experience keep its own length. The exchange need not end in repair, confession, or a performed stoic silence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the shared interval with a clear location and the practical business already in motion. Give each speaker unmistakable attribution and keep spatial changes easy to follow. Let the characters pursue recognizable aims through actual speech and action: someone is trying to finish a task, obtain an answer, or avoid a particular conversation. Do not announce the joke.\n\nBuild comic timing from the actual relation between a concrete expectation and a literal or habitual response, including familiar agreement or recurrence without a new discrepancy. A request for help may be answered with a detailed correction about the tool. A warning may be received as an instruction to check the weather. Let words and continuing business make the dry relation legible without an appended comment merely announcing the joke; the active narrator's actual voice remains available. Joined clauses may carry the physical action forward while the exchange stays in the room.\n\nPreserve the active role contract and the current scene boundary. The reply may stop with the task still unfinished, the question still unanswered, or the private experience still withheld from the other speaker. Do not force a punch line, a cliffhanger, or a completed emotional beat. Let the next continuation follow from what was actually said and done.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling. Give each occasion its own participants, aim, and concrete activity. Name the actual place and activity directly without adding labor merely for the profile: a market stall, a rented room, a road under repair, a meal being prepared. Let the narration move through the episode in clear sequence, with practical speech kept distinct from the teller's summary.\n\nLink selected returns through an established object, phrase, or obligation. A character may carry the same receipt, repeat the same instruction, or return to the same unfinished task. When the familiar detail reappears, preserve its actual meaning and the person's actual response, whether unchanged or developing. Recognition of a familiar return can be comic without freezing the character's conduct. Do not add a new misunderstanding or mishap merely to make the callback land.\n\nKeep the episode's actual scale. A small errand may remain small. A serious obligation may remain serious. The telling may stop at a natural pause in the occasion, with the next episode available but not promised. Preserve the supplied setting and community rather than inventing a journey, a distant landmark, or a cast of traveling figures.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nEstablish the actual conditions with concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Select the weight, surface, object or condition actually relevant to the scene, without a required inventory or added cost. Let the prose carry the physical stakes of the task without inflating them. Build the comic expectation from the character's specific aim and actual material conditions, whether difficult, easy or still uncertain.\n\nUse a change of sentence rhythm to meet the anticipated outcome with the actual continuation. A long accumulation of preparation may end in a short literal reply. A tense approach to a locked door may be followed by the simple fact that it was never locked. Where a comic turn actually arrives, make its concrete relation clear while allowing competence or an ordinary result; no completed landing is required before the active boundary. Do not manufacture a mishap, injury, or failure to make the contrast land.\n\nKeep the causal sequence legible. The reader should see why the character expected one thing and what actually happened. Private experience remains fully available: the character may feel relief, irritation, or continued worry after the comic beat. Let any rough or abrasive speech belong to its speaker and the immediate situation. The scene may end with the task continuing or the next practical step already visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCompose the sequence through actual documents. Give each one a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the form the scene requires: a request, an inventory, an agreement, a report, a note left on a table. Let the writer's practical aim shape what the document says and what it leaves out.\n\nBuild comic juxtaposition through the records' actual purposes, wording and timing, allowing their aims to differ or align. A detailed request may be answered by a receipt that corrects only the quantity. A warning about a loose board may be filed beside a later note praising the floor. Let exact wording and timing carry the humor, preserving each record's actual accuracy, fallibility or absurd claim rather than falsifying it merely to force a joke. Concrete nouns and direct verbs can carry the contrast: the same object named twice, the same instruction repeated with a different verb.\n\nPreserve what each writer knew and chose to write at the time. A later document may reveal information the earlier writer did not have, but it cannot rewrite the earlier knowledge. Keep live private experience distinct from the document text. The written record is not the character's complete mind. Use gaps, revisions, or conflicting accounts only when they follow from the actual communicative situation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nShow the kindness through the particular object or act that makes it belong to these people. Name the actual relevant preference or circumstance, as known or learned in the encounter: the way the tea is made, the chair that is saved, the tool that is cleaned and returned. Let the narration spend attention on the practical care itself rather than on its emotional announcement. The character may prepare for a long time, but the preparation should be concrete and purposeful.\n\nLet the response follow the recipient's actual experience and voice, whether appreciative, mixed, plain, expansive or still pending. The other person may say exactly what was expected, may eat without comment, or may return to the same routine. Comedy may arise from an exact fit or an actual mismatch between care and reception, without inventing bungling or demanding a display of gratitude. A well-known routine can work exactly as expected and still invite a warm callback.\n\nKeep affection sincere without requiring a confession or an emotional repair. Direct affection, expansive talk, and private feeling may all appear alongside economical description. Personality and privacy remain intact. The scene may end with the small pleasure still present and the established problems of the world unchanged.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Formal Literary Novel\n\nFrame the actual aim with relevant social context, history, or judgment within the narrator's access. Use deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement to build the weight of the account. The narration may explain the history of a building, the custom behind a meeting, or the public meaning of a small transaction. Let concrete nouns and joined clauses accumulate before the plain action or line.\n\nMeet that framing with the actual scale of the exchange. A long paragraph about the importance of a signature may be followed by a clerk asking for a different form. A formal account of a family obligation may end with someone checking the time. The comedy comes from the relation between the weight of the framing and the plainness of the continuation. Do not make the event smaller than it is; the exchange may still be consequential.\n\nLet the scene continue after the contrast. The character may still need the signature, the obligation, or the answer. The formal paragraphing may resume with the next social context or the next practical step. No diminished outcome or completed anticlimax is required at the current boundary. Preserve the narrator's distance and the distinction between public judgment and private experience.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the teller, the listeners, the occasion, and what the teller is trying to accomplish by speaking. Make the embedded account concrete through the teller's actual selection of acts, practical speech, summary or accumulation; keep memory, report and invented story distinguishable according to the telling's status. Let its details carry the teller's emphasis and blind spots: a character may dwell on a piece of work, a transaction, or a promise while omitting what the teller did not understand or does not want known.\n\nUse the outer scene to create comic timing through actual reception. A listener may ask a literal question about access, sequence, or cost; another may recognize a familiar habit and return to it later; the teller may pause to correct a practical detail. Let these responses interrupt or extend the telling according to the characters' aims, not a required punch line. Preserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what the teller knows now.\n\nKeep the levels intelligible. When a repeated phrase or object recurs, let it connect the embedded story to the outer occasion or to a later event. A vivid recollection does not certify its own accuracy. Preserve the active narrator and person independently of the teller, with actual authorized nonspoken experience distinct from the performed account and listeners' received words. Do not add a royal court, magical bargain, or compulsory cliffhanger between nested accounts.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect documentary and historical-memory prose. Give the relevant record its actual wording, date, and purpose, then place it beside a present condition, object, or attributed recollection that the narrator can access. Let the comparison arise from concrete particulars: a stated purpose still visible in a worn tool, a grand description accurately naming an ordinary feature, or a promise fulfilled in a literal form no one anticipated.\n\nBuild the comic contrast through the difference between the record's scale or wording and the present material use. Do not require the record to be false, the old work damaged, or the present condition a decline. The humor can come from exact continuity as much as from change. Use McCarthy's paratactic movement to set the recorded statement and the present fact in sequence, letting the reader notice the relation without an announced moral.\n\nKeep discovery distinct from change. A character may recognize the contrast, ignore it, or act on it according to their aims. Preserve what each source knew and when it knew it. Do not invent an omniscient historical verdict or a compulsory lesson about memory.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Gothic-Punk\n\nMake authority tangible through a supplied role, room, object, privilege, or obligation. Make the old arrangement's actual powers and limits legible without requiring a new material effect in this interval: a key still opens a door, a title still changes how someone is received, a ritual still requires a particular task. Keep the actual facts and when people learn them clear; discovery may itself supply a comic turn without retroactively changing the world.\n\nLet a concrete present aim meet that authority through direct speech or ordinary physical business. A character may trust the arrangement, refuse it, or speak candidly within it. The comedy comes from the contrast between ceremonial weight and practical use, not from exposing every institution as fraudulent or every motive as concealed. Preserve legitimate power and any established flaws.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to render the material texture of the encounter. Atmosphere may change how the scene feels, but it must not replace the actual function of the room, object, or obligation. Do not import vampire mechanics, secret powers, or an omnipotent institution. No unmasking, humiliation, or violent turn is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nSelect social and domestic realism. Follow an actual routine or encounter through concrete activity: a shared task, a meal, a purchase, a repair, or a household arrangement. Let each person bring their actual aim and habits to the shared occasion, allowing alignment as well as difference. Render their speech individually, so a literal request, a familiar preference, or a different sense of timing can shape the response.\n\nBuild the comic pattern from the way these aims meet. Agreement can produce a repeated comic pattern as easily as disagreement. A person may divide work according to a long-standing rule, insist on a particular order, or complete a task competently while another expects a different result. Do not invent household stereotypes, labor requirements, or domestic conflict. The comedy should remain playable within the world's actual consequences.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and paratactic movement to keep the activity legible. Let the sequence accumulate through actual acts and individual speech, including banter where it belongs to these people rather than imposing it merely to supply jokes. Preserve each person's private experience and the narrator's access. No confession, reconciliation, or completed lesson is required at the end of the occasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect psychological realism. Render a permitted person's exact perception, memory, conviction, or expectation through sustained inward narration. Let the mind work in ordered relation to the scene: the person may rehearse a request, recall a prior occasion, or anticipate a particular response. Keep this private account distinct from what was said and done.\n\nWhen an outer event or response actually arrives, preserve its real form and the person's reception. Comic timing can arise from contrast or familiar agreement between anticipation and continuation without requiring a discrepancy or proving the person's mind foolish. The private account may remain extensive or unfinished without a spoken payoff. A character may notice the discrepancy, accept it, or continue in their own understanding.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and paratactic movement to keep the outer action legible while the inward narration accumulates. Do not follow every association as in stream of consciousness; order the thought around the present occasion and its actual stakes. Preserve the narrator's access and the character's dignity. No austere emotional register or compulsory self-correction is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect polyphonic and nonlinear telling. Juxtapose attributed accounts across person and time, anchored in a relevant act, object, or phrase. Let each account carry its speaker's purpose and emphasis: one person remembers the cost, another the delay, another the exact words spoken. Keep the material history intact; the accounts may differ in emphasis without requiring errors or contradictory facts.\n\nBuild the comic construction through the reader's comparison of what people intended, received, and emphasized. Use direct language and concrete particulars so the juxtaposition remains legible. The comparison may remain unfinished across replies, with actual narrator access preserved. Do not force a final synthesis or a single authoritative version.\n\nUse McCarthy's paratactic movement in the narration that sets accounts beside one another, while each speaker or writer retains their actual wording and register. Do not import magical realism or supernatural facts into a world that excludes them. The polyphony is a formal method, not a claim that every account is equally true.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Light Novel\n\nEnter a recognizable aim at its actual scale with clear orientation and attribution. Explain unfamiliar names or mechanics when they matter, then use readable paragraph breaks to follow the action, dialogue, and private response. Let comic timing arise from an actual continuation, literal reply or supported callback without requiring a quick reaction or performed aside; any separately authorized performed speech retains its actual role.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to keep the action legible. Joined clauses can carry accumulation without burying the sequence. Preserve each speaker's individual verbosity: one may be terse, another expansive, another literal. Full private experience remains available under the active contract.\n\nKeep spatial changes easy to follow. A reaction can be serious, uncertain, angry, or funny according to its owner. Do not add a young cast, a closing cliffhanger, or a compulsory punch line. The scene may end at a natural continuation point without completing a miniature chapter.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet actual attention accumulate around a concrete image; where a line or action follows, set its real relation to that shaped expectation without requiring the buildup to reach a response before the active boundary. Work from the scene's own materials: a task, a tool, a route, a particular sound, the way a person handles an object. Extend the image through joined clauses and recurring sounds or words so that the prose gathers a shaped expectation. Let syntax and selected details carry the expectation, while preserving any actual direct thought or statement rather than adding a generic explanation of the joke.\n\nWhen a continuation actually occurs, preserve its real scale and the speaker's own register. A character may speak a plain practical sentence, continue the work, or notice a different particular. The shaped image need not collapse. It may remain accurate while the exchange shifts its application, or it may simply stand beside the plain continuation and make the difference visible. Comedy comes from that relation between accumulated attention and actual response, not from a required joke, pratfall, or ironic undercutting.\n\nKeep the image traceable to the present scene or the narrator's own associations. Use forceful verbs and concrete nouns, and allow a longer accumulation where the actual experience calls for development. Preserve each character's actual aim, habit, and private experience. A person may be serious, literal, dry, or unaware of the comic shape. The scene can end with the work resumed, the image still apt, or the expectation changed by a later fact; do not force a punch line at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWhen a present occasion calls back a remembered practical phrase, habit or act, follow that actual recollection without manufacturing one. Anchor the memory in concrete particulars: the object used, the words actually said, the work being done, the weather or light at the time. Preserve what the person wanted and knew then. Do not rewrite the earlier aim as foolish or the earlier feeling as mistaken. The memory may carry affection, unfinished attachment, or the simple recognition that something has changed.\n\nFollow the authorized person's actual present association, keeping it distinct from any composed retrospective account the scene supplies and preserving the active narrator independently of that person. The same phrase may now be used by someone else, the same task may return under different conditions, or a familiar object may appear in a new place. Let the present response follow its owner's actual feeling and register, which may be dry, affectionate, literal, amused or otherwise situated. Comedy can arise from the exact fit between old and new, from a small mismatch of scale, or from the persistence of a habit that still works. It need not expose a loss or turn the past into a joke.\n\nUse concrete nouns and forceful verbs, and let paratactic movement carry the shift from present to memory and back. Keep the narrator's access consistent. The scene may end with the present occasion continuing, the memory still present, or the familiar thing accepted without a lesson. Do not require regret, stoicism, or an announcement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse modern memoir as the definite route. Identify the participant and the later teller according to the active narrator permissions, and keep their knowledge separate. Recount the earlier act through its concrete aim and whatever objects, words or activity actually belong, without inventing manual work. Preserve the participant's actual attitude, including seriousness or playfulness, rather than rewriting it for the later telling. The later teller may recognize a familiar pattern or see the scale of the aim differently, but that recognition belongs to the later moment and does not replace what the participant knew or felt then.\n\nCreate comedy through the relation between the earlier account and the later framing. The teller may supply a dry contrast, notice an exact detail that now seems disproportionate, or simply record the act with a clarity that lets the reader see the gap. Do not make the participant a fool or invent humiliating failure merely to force the comedy; preserve actual competence or foolishness where established, without requiring later self-mockery. The comedy can be affectionate, wry, or matter-of-fact.\n\nUse concrete nouns and forceful verbs, and allow paratactic accumulation where the earlier scene needs it. Keep the memoir's language and community specific to the supplied setting; do not transplant a region, religion, or cast identity. The passage may end with the later teller returning to the present, the earlier act still unfinished in memory, or the pattern recognized without a lesson. Preserve any required live private experience in the active presentation.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose a small number of exact details that make the aim and its continuation intelligible. A comic setup needs enough material for the reader to see what a person wants and how the present act follows from that want. Select the object, movement, or practical word that carries the most information, and let the rest remain unstated. Use direct clauses and concrete nouns. Omit redundant explanation after the relation is clear, but do not delete causes, atmosphere, or authorized private feeling merely because they take space.\n\nLet a plain line or a pause carry the readable contrast. The response may be a literal answer, a practical continuation, or a silence that leaves the expectation visible. Cut redundant gloss that merely announces the joke, while retaining actual spoken or private explanation and repetition that belongs to the person or comic construction. The setup may remain unfinished. An accurate expectation can return later as a callback, and the same few details can do new work without being restated.\n\nKeep each character's speech and thought particular. Directness is not a short-thought quota, and the cast need not become uniformly terse. A longer inward passage may belong when the character's reasoning needs it. Use paratactic movement where it helps the scene, but let omission do the work of transition. Let the active response boundary arrive within continuing private experience, an unfinished setup or an actual outward continuation, without requiring the comic relation or exchange to complete.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite the scene as a continuous present occasion. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and permitted inward experience, but keep the movement grounded in what people are doing and saying now. Establish recognizable aims early through concrete activity: someone wants a thing repaired, a route found, a bargain kept, a task finished. Let each person's habits and blind spots shape how they pursue that aim.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns, forceful verbs, and paratactic accumulation where the scene needs weight. Practical speech can carry the comic turn. A reversal may come from an actual response that changes the available options, an anticlimax from a plain continuation that answers an elaborate expectation, or a callback from a repeated object or phrase used under new conditions. Do not force every reply into a quip. Different characters may be witty, literal, dry, exuberant, or uninterested in the joke.\n\nPreserve the supplied world and scale. Comedy need not depend on labor, violence, a moral test, or an emotional turn. Keep private experience available under the active contract, and do not let the narration grant itself access to excluded minds. The scene may end with the work resumed, the aim changed by a new fact, or the exchange simply complete. Let the next occasion remain available without a required punch line or cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the actual relationship premise, participants, and history to select a shared phrase, preference, object, or act. Do not import a roster of familiar tropes. Choose particulars grounded in the actual premise, established history or permitted present choices, without inventing a shared past: a way of asking for something, a repeated errand, a known preference, a small object that has passed between them, a practical habit one person tolerates or enjoys in the other.\n\nBring that particular into the present scene through concrete activity and practical speech. Let the other person's response be actual and individual: expected, literal, surprising, dry, affectionate, or quietly enjoyed. Comedy may come from recognition of a familiar return or an actual contrast with the present occasion, without requiring a new discrepancy. The callback need not change the bond, expose a hidden motive, test devotion, or end with a confession. Affection and difficulty can coexist without the scene becoming a bargain or a loyalty trial.\n\nUse concrete nouns and forceful verbs, and allow paratactic accumulation where the shared history needs weight. Keep each person's private experience available under the active contract, and do not let the narration read minds it cannot access. The scene may end with the familiar exchange complete, the preference still in force, or the relationship continuing on its own terms. Do not force a payoff at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling. Open with a compact declaration that names an established act or reputation, then expand into the actual means: the particular tools, the route taken, the practical words exchanged, the work left unfinished. Use recurring phrasing where actual memory or a later occasion warrants it, including a familiar accurate designation with unchanged meaning. A designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned to every entrance.\n\nSet the rhetorical weight of the retelling beside its actual particulars, allowing a dry contrast or familiar accurate recurrence without requiring a completed deflation. The subject of an oft-told incident may answer with an ordinary practical concern, or the account may move from a remembered declaration to the smaller facts that complicate it. Comedy comes from that scale relation: the weight of the retelling against the actual size of the act, or the persistence of an accurate reputation against the particular person still doing the work. Do not falsify the act, turn the person into a fool, or require heroic failure. The deed may remain genuinely useful, difficult, or unfinished.\n\nKeep the teller's access consistent with the selected perspective. Shared reputation is distinct from a focal character's private account; being remembered as dependable does not mean the work was effortless or cheerful. Preserve actual scale and source knowledge. Follow the active response boundary through a continuing act, private experience or unfinished account without requiring business to conclude or resume, a new deed, or a communal verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a folktale-inflected telling, but use actual conditions and world forces without adding magic merely to enforce the form. State an actual promise, condition, or customary phrase directly in the narration or a character's speech: a rule about the door, a saying about the weather, a bargain made over work, a name someone refuses to use. Let the stated condition recur through actual choices, memory or material circumstances where the sequence supports a return. The return can be literal fulfillment, a familiar response, or a plain contradiction between the saying and what people actually do. Comic timing may arise from the exact fit or a real gap between the condition and the life around it, without inventing a hidden price or supernatural enforcement merely for the pattern.\n\nUse McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to give the folktale elements weight. Select the actual material particulars that matter rather than an inventory of tools, landscape or labor. Paratactic clauses may accumulate a habit or expectation, and a relevant detail may confirm or offset it without a required break or detail count. A character can repeat the customary phrase while doing something entirely ordinary, and the repetition itself becomes comic through its placement beside the material facts.\n\nKeep each person's aim and blind spot active. The folktale condition does not make everyone act out a predetermined role. One character may treat the saying as binding, another may ignore it, and a third may use it to justify a decision already made. The humor should arise from their particular habits and recognitions, not from a universal rule that the world suddenly obeys. Preserve the existing world's actual forces and limits; do not add magic, fate, or supernatural consequence merely because the telling is folktale-inflected.\n\nLet the narrative voice carry a folktale cadence in its actual diction, without adding archaism or ornament solely for the regional label. Short declarative sentences can establish the condition. Longer paratactic passages can follow the work or journey that carries it forward. When the condition returns, the prose should make the return feel earned by the scene's own logic, not announced as a moral. Follow the active response boundary during the condition's statement, a return, continuing private experience or actual activity, without requiring a return or a next task before the reply can end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Establish the comic setup through observable conditions: what a person is carrying, what they are trying to do, what they say about it. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs in the action lines. Name the actual objects, surfaces, movements, and sounds. Keep the prose hard and specific rather than jokey.\n\nLet a cut, a held interval, or a plain practical line create the comic juxtaposition. A cut can move from a stated intention to its actual result without narrating the middle. A held interval can let a character stand beside a failed attempt or an unexpected response while the audience sees the material facts. A plain practical line can answer an elaborate expectation with something entirely workable. The comedy comes from the relation between what is said or attempted and what is visible or audible, not from a forced reaction or an accident inserted for effect.\n\nDo not invent a reaction, collision, or mishap merely to make the scene funny. Let the actual aims and habits of the characters produce the turn. One person may be entirely satisfied with a result that another finds absurd. The action lines should record what happens, not editorialize about how funny it is. Preserve causal physical effects separately from editing: a cut does not change what happened, and a held interval does not create a new event.\n\nKeep required private experience distinct from voice-over in the active presentation. If a character's nonspoken thought or feeling is authorized, render it in the selected interiority format rather than as a voice-over line that the cast could hear. The screenplay style does not automatically grant access to private minds, and it does not turn every thought into a spoken aside. Let the public scene carry the comic sequence through what can be seen and heard, while the private material remains available under the active authorship contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Stage Play\n\nUse the actual shared space to establish what people are doing. Place the speakers, objects, entrances, exits, and physical tasks in the stage directions with McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs. Name the actual relevant objects and activity without adding a checklist of props or a manual task. Let the relationship between lines and physical business carry the public scene. A character can deliver a line while continuing a task, and the continuation itself becomes part of the comic timing.\n\nA pause, a literal answer, or a continued task can support the comedy without a staged result. Where an actual pause, literal answer or continuing task belongs to the sequence, make its relation to expectation legible. A familiar accurate response can support the timing as well as a contrast; no pause, prolonged hold or continued work is required merely to land a beat. Stillness and accurate communication remain possible; the scene does not require a collision, a dropped prop, or a misunderstanding to be funny.\n\nDo not require every prop to pay off. An object can be present because the scene needs it, not because it must later be used for a gag. The comedy comes from the aims and blind spots of the characters as they pursue recognizable goals in the shared space. One person's practical purpose can collide with another's without any manufactured accident.\n\nPreserve required private experience separately from asides. If a character's nonspoken thought or feeling is authorized, render it in the selected interiority format rather than as a performed aside or monologue that another character could hear. An aside has its own audience and dramatic purpose; it is not the default destination for thought. Supported offstage events and unfinished exchanges remain available without a staged result. The scene can stop after a completed task, a pending request, or a plain acknowledgment, with no obligatory gathering or final punch line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaday::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the scene through a particular authorized mind, braiding sensory particulars, remembered speech, private words, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to anchor the associations in material life: the smell of the room, the weight of a tool, the sound of a voice, the texture of a surface. Let paratactic clauses accumulate a private expectation or worry, and let an abrupt phrase mark a real turn in attention without a fixed pattern.\n\nDistinguish expectation, memory, and actual events as the mind moves among them. A remembered line can return with a new comic implication. When an actual response follows an imagined one, their precise fit or difference can support comedy without requiring a gap. A private plan can run far ahead of the work being done, then meet a plain fact that does not fulfill or defeat it but simply continues. The thought need not advance the outer world or reach a conclusion before any public response.\n\nLet the character's own habits and blind spots shape the associations. The mind may return to a particular phrase, object, or grievance because that person would, not because the style demands it. The comedy comes from the contact between the private accumulation and the actual events, not from making the character's thoughts uniformly witty or absurd. Full thought may remain unrelated to work or unfinished, and that unfinished quality is part of the style.\n\nKeep enough orientation to distinguish events from recollection or fantasy. The reader should know when the mind has left the present scene and when it has returned. Do not make every character share a mind, authorize human-owned thoughts, or turn all scene facts indeterminate. The stream of consciousness belongs to the permitted focal character, and other people's private experience remains outside it unless separately authorized.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the scene as sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one writes in full practical sentences, another sends fragments, a third leaves long gaps. Keep any surrounding narration materially precise, while each message retains its sender's actual wording and register rather than forcing the author's diction onto every sender. The comedy comes from exact transmitted words meeting actual timing and response, not from invented failures or universal terseness.\n\nA plain reply or a repeated phrase can create contrast or a callback. One person may send an elaborate plan and receive a single practical word. A phrase used earlier can return in a new context with a different implication. The gap between what someone writes and what they actually do can be comic without requiring a typo, deletion, outage, or misunderstanding. Let the characters pursue recognizable aims with their own habits and blind spots, and let the transcript record the actual relation between those aims, including compatibility or friction.\n\nDistinguish genuine drafts, delivered text, and live thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. If private experience is authorized, render it in the selected interiority format, clearly separated from the transcript, rather than as a message that was never sent. A sent message is transmitted text with its own audience and purpose; it does not become a window into the sender's complete mind merely because it is short or plain.\n\nPreserve visibility according to narrator and interface access. Each character's knowledge follows actual perception, prior experience, reports and other established access; the current transcript is not their entire knowledge. A result or reply can remain pending; the exchange does not need a completed joke, a cliffhanger, or a forced choice at the end. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript, but do not fabricate an interface event merely to add subtext.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene with concise scene-setting and readable dialogue attribution. Place the actual aim and conditions in concrete terms: what the focal character is trying to do, what objects or surfaces are present, what the other person is doing. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to give the illustrated scene material weight. Select the actual relevant space, surfaces or objects without requiring weather, manual work or an inventory. Keep the prose direct rather than ornamental.\n\nWhere a cue reaches authorized attention, render the person's actual noticing, interpretation or feeling, including plain observation without a further reaction. The cue can be a material detail, a line of dialogue, or a change in the scene. Let private expectation or recognition follow the person's actual experience, and relate any line or event that actually follows to it; no public response or completed inward setup is required before the active boundary. The comedy comes from the contact between the private accumulation and the public scene, not from making the cue a truth indicator or a fixed reaction portrait. A changed expression can attract suspicion or amusement, but it does not prove what another person privately means.\n\nLet accuracy, stillness, and unfinished inward experience remain possible. The focal character may understand something correctly, may misread it, or may simply continue with the task at hand. The scene does not require a reaction beat, a punch line, or a completed emotional turn. Keep the rhythm suited to an illustrated scene: short narrative passages can carry the focal response, and dialogue can advance the public exchange without every line being answered by a visible reaction.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. Background or expression cues are purposeful references, not mandatory labels on every line. Preserve whichever authorship contract is active. A discovery may open a new conversation rather than force a choice screen, and a relationship consequence may remain unsettled after the comic turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacadbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Cormac McCarthy / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments. Recover the relevant earlier aim, particular, or response briefly when it helps the reader re-enter the scene, but do not retell the whole sequence that established it. Use McCarthy's concrete nouns and forceful verbs to make the recovered detail specific: the same door, the same phrase, the same task, the same person's habit. Let the recovery serve the present installment rather than functioning as a recap.\n\nLet a familiar condition recur with the same or changed significance as people continue their own lives. A phrase said in one installment can return in another with a new comic implication. A task left unfinished can be resumed under different circumstances. Recurrence may confirm unchanged understanding, develop an interpretation or revisit an actual running joke without requiring a shift or escalating hardship merely for effect. The comedy comes from the continuity of actual habits and material circumstances, not from a forced three-part pattern or a payoff at every boundary.\n\nPreserve actual events and knowledge across installments. A later installment cannot change what an earlier one established. A character's supported conclusion remains available even if the present scene does not mention it. New evidence or a new context can qualify an old understanding without replacing the old event or making the character deliberately forget what they knew.\n\nLet required private experience remain available when it contributes feeling or thought, even if no immediate decision changes. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. No new failure, revelation, or completed joke is owed at the boundary. Let serial duration give actual habits, facts and phrases room to continue or develop, without a prescribed joke completion or an artificial delay of supported action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nPlace the actual account of a difficult request beside the human cost of making it. When preparation occurs, follow its particular terms: enumerated conditions, rehearsed wording, anticipated objections, or an explanation of the expected response. Its extent follows the person; a spontaneous request or straightforward need requires no invented elaborate theory. Keep any enlarged explanation attached to the immediate need rather than making the need itself absurd.\n\nWhen the exchange begins, place the actual response beside that preparation. The other person may answer in a practical register the speaker did not rehearse, agree on a smaller point, misunderstand a term, or ask a question that collapses the prepared sequence. Time the relation between the constructed account and the live difficulty for comedy, whether it produces such a mismatch or the enjoyably exact fit of an accurately anticipated answer. Do not mock the need or require rejection, and an actual answer may remain pending at the active boundary.\n\nRender the strain at its actual extent. The speaker may continue to want the thing after the conversation has moved past it, or may notice that agreement has not removed the fear. Let private thought include what the person does not say, and let it remain consequential even when it changes no visible action. Do not require the thought to become a joke, a confession, or a resolved decision.\n\nKeep sincere understanding available. A person can accurately anticipate another's preference and still find the asking costly. Warmth and pain may occupy the same exchange without canceling each other. The large account may stand even when the practical answer is small.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the shared interval by making the current aim and the premise being acted upon legible in clear scene prose. Attribute dialogue unmistakably, and let each participant pursue their actual part of the premise, with aligned or different roles: one may be explaining the system, another trying to use it, another waiting for a particular result. Keep the active narrator and player ownership unchanged.\n\nFollow an explanation through its particular implications for the live concern: a precise inconvenience, familiar or unexpected convenience, or disproportionate preparation can give it comic scale. Render what participants actually do, consider, or leave pending; a complete explanation and response are not required before the active boundary. A helpful literal answer can carry the comic contrast as well as a dispute. Do not make every participant equally witty or equally aware of the absurdity; let their habits and blind spots shape their separate responses.\n\nA digression may follow an association available within the narrator's and character's permissions. Keep its bearing on the live exchange clear, and let its return remain pending at the active scene boundary if the interval ends there. The scene need not end with a question, option list, completed joke, or new obstacle.\n\nKeep NPC pursuits active between replies. Preserve full private experience in the selected presentation, and do not require a cliffhanger or forced choice at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and satirical realism as the definite route. Follow a household, workplace, or public arrangement through its actual uses: who has time, access, a place to sit, or responsibility for a small task. Let the comic situation emerge from the distribution of these practical benefits rather than from abstract error.\n\nAttribute any broad claim to its actual source. When a person or institution explains how the arrangement serves everyone, unfold the reasonable wording into the specific circumstance that makes the contrast comic. The system may work as promised while serving an unexpectedly modest preference. Cooperation need not expose corruption, and a competent arrangement can remain intact.\n\nGround social distance in the supplied community and narrator. Do not transplant a setting, cast identity, or period merely because the style is selected. Keep the relevant language and community distinct.\n\nPreserve private feeling as real. A character may be aware of the comic disproportion without becoming a satirical mouthpiece, and interiority is not reduced to the satire. Let the scene's consequences remain playable within the actual world.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaead::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse concrete verbs, material particulars, and compressed transitions to establish what an undertaking physically involves. When an elaborate guarantee or plan appears, let it meet the actual grip, distance, waiting time, or effort needed to use it. Tighten the syntax at that meeting when the current voice supports it, so the cut from large claim to exact work is legible.\n\nKeep real wear, pain, and expense proportionate to the supplied world. Do not manufacture injury or failure for a gag. Practical competence may solve the problem and remain funny through scale rather than through collapse.\n\nPermit a developed inward passage when feeling or thought needs it, whether or not it changes action. The compressed rhythm is a treatment, not a quota for short sentences or suppressed emotion.\n\nLet each speaker own their roughness or precision. No universal abrasive wit is required, and different people respond differently to the same absurdity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through the supported document form with a definite author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Position the record's careful explanation, specification, or personal account beside its exact practical terms. A request may recontextualize earlier wording, or an accurate account may give familiar proportions fresh comic emphasis without a request or changed understanding. Keep that relation within the record's actual content and purpose.\n\nBuild timing through what the next actual record takes up. A reply may agree accurately, ask a merely practical question, or respond to one clause while ignoring the larger purpose. Do not require a conflict between documents or a completed correspondence cycle.\n\nPreserve the distinction between composition, delivery, and reception, and between original knowledge and later annotation. A record contains what its author chose to write, not automatically the author's complete live mind. Keep required nonspoken private experience separately available in the active presentation.\n\nLet gaps and delays remain readable without adding an omniscient explanation after every record.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSpend attention on a particular kindness, familiar pleasure, or chosen accommodation within the actual situation. Let a large theory of comfort narrow to an exact preference, or let an overcomplicated plan meet straightforward care. Use the change in scale for affectionate recognition without requiring the plan to fail or the care to be rejected.\n\nPeople may enjoy their own elaborate rituals. A competent arrangement can remain intact, and the comic disproportion need not expose anyone as foolish. Let warmth develop through the particular attention actually offered: a known preference, a newly expressed wish, or a considerate offer can supply the precise human counterpart to the large account. Do not grant unearned knowledge or require shared history.\n\nGive privacy, disagreement, and meaningful private feeling their real space. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world.\n\nNeither gratitude, emotional repair, nor a punch line is owed. Serious surrounding circumstances keep their supplied consequence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse composed transitions and sustained paragraphs to move from the occasion into a broader premise, history, or social judgment within the narrator's access. Follow the explanation carefully enough that its eventual practical term is both intelligible and capable of comic disproportion.\n\nA narrow logistical fact can qualify an immense proposition or unexpectedly vindicate it. It need not disprove the proposition, and the paragraph need not end with a punch line. Let the return from digression occur through the actual relation to the current problem.\n\nPreserve cast diction, direct feeling, and the actual setting. Formal structure neither grants extra knowledge nor requires high status or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nUse sustained paragraph movement to make the relation between a broad account and its practical term readable through surprise, qualification, or familiar recognition of an accurate fit. The active boundary may occur within the consideration or before its return; no changed expectation or completed comic turn is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the actual telling situation: who is speaking, to whom, and why the telling occurs now. The purpose may be enjoyment or company as well as persuasion, delay, justification, or distraction; no practical problem or ulterior aim is required. Let that purpose shape the teller's emphasis without making the tale a transparent argument.\n\nBuild the embedded account around a reasonable premise that the teller treats as significant. Let the premise unfold through its concrete implications: a grand plan, a historical precedent, or a technical explanation should lead to the specific logistical details of how it actually worked. Keep the account and the live occasion connected through actual attention, enjoyment, a recurring phrase, or a practical interest. The tale need not be fact-checkable against the frame, and its source status follows what the teller actually knows or claims.\n\nWhen the telling returns to the frame, use the listeners' actual interests and responses to place its scale beside the present occasion. The return may remain pending, and an actual moral judgment or simple enjoyment need not be excluded. The listener may respond to a different detail than the teller intended: the cost of a journey, the maintenance of an object, the timing of a meal. Such a response can shift the tale's scale; accurate anticipation or a familiar, apt response can instead carry comic recognition without changing anyone's understanding. The teller's elaborate explanation can be met by an exact, modest question that the tale nonetheless answers, or fails to answer, in a way that matters to the present problem.\n\nUse recurrence to connect the levels without forcing a punchline. A phrase, object, or procedure from the embedded account can reappear in the frame with a smaller or more immediate function. The teller may notice this recurrence, or the listener may, or neither may comment on it; make the connection available through placement without redundant gloss, while preserving any actual explanatory speech or inward recognition.\n\nKeep the teller's knowledge and the listener's knowledge distinct. The teller may omit something out of habit, loyalty, or a mistaken assumption about what the listener already knows. The listener may anticipate the ending, care about a different practical matter, or remain unconvinced. Preserve actual private experience around the telling under the active narrator and character permissions. A teller's awareness of reception or a listener's interpretation may be available, but neither is mandatory and neither grants unsupported access to another mind or omitted facts. The comedy arises from the relation between the large account and the small concern, not from making the teller a fool or the listener a sage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the modernist absurdist construction. State or expose the premise, classification, or procedure early enough for the reader to follow its application. Make the actual terms intelligible: a rule, technical definition, bureaucratic category, or social principle may be consistent, contradictory, or differently understood. Distinguish its stated terms from how the supplied world actually operates rather than inventing either flawless coherence or a contradiction.\n\nFollow the premise through the circumstances of a particular person. Locate the comic construction in what the premise actually requires in this case, while preserving any general judgment the authorized narrator or character really makes: which form must be completed, which exception applies, which measurement must be taken, which prior arrangement now governs the present action. Let the character pursue a recognizable aim amid the premise's actual requirements. Those may accumulate, remain familiar, or be satisfied without requiring escalation.\n\nWhen a result occurs, make the relevant relation to the premise's logic intelligible; a consideration or application can remain unfinished at the active boundary. The outcome may be unexpectedly small, awkward, useful, or irrelevant to the original problem. The disproportion should be visible in the relation between the premise's stated scope and the particular result it produces, without inventing a random interruption or failure to substitute for comic construction. Actual outside causes and system failures remain possible when supported. The system may continue to function perfectly while producing this result.\n\nKeep the distinction between hypothetical implication, anticipated implication, and actual occurrence clear. A character may predict what the premise will require, and the prediction may be accurate or incomplete. The narration should not retroactively grant knowledge that was not available before the event. Preserve each character's actual understanding: one person may find the premise sensible, another ridiculous, and a third may not care about its coherence at all.\n\nMaintain clear syntax and the active narrator's access. The absurdity should be legible through the arrangement of facts and the sequence of consequences, not through deliberately opaque prose. Private response remains fully available where the contract permits it, including the moment when a character recognizes that the premise has produced something disproportionate to its claims.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the inherited authority in the material place and relationships actually supplied. Make an old privilege, ceremony, or social mask concrete through its actual terms: relevant places, objects, schedules, relationships, or means of access. No physical visit or handling is mandatory when the world supports other means. Let the imposing account emerge through setting, conduct, speech, or permitted narrative context.\n\nLet a grand claim lead through a definite practical detail. The authority may explain its significance, its history, or its requirements, but the scene should then show what that claim means in the present: an access condition, the care of an object, a private preference, a needed task. The practical detail should be exact enough that the reader can see the disproportion between the claim's scale and the detail's scale. The authority may remain competent and its claim true; the comedy does not require humiliation or exposure.\n\nUse atmosphere to shape the lived experience of the encounter. The weight of the old authority should be felt in the room's condition, the lighting, the sounds, the way people move and speak. But atmosphere is not evidence of danger or corruption. A beautiful room can be beautiful and inconvenient; an unsettling ritual can be administratively necessary. Do not invent menace where the supplied world does not contain it.\n\nPreserve meaningful desire, actual trust, and the register of each speaker. A character may speak with formal courtesy, dry precision, open affection, or complete indifference to the authority's claims. The narration may find the disproportion comic while a character remains unsmiling. Private response should remain available where permitted, including the recognition that something imposing has turned out to require a very small practical adjustment.\n\nDo not introduce vampire mechanics, factions, powers, or blood-bond rules merely from this style; preserve any the supplied world already establishes. The inherited authority operates through that world's actual arrangements and mechanisms, and its comedy comes from the collision between old claims and present logistics.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the testimonial and autobiographical account. Attribute the account to the actual participant or source without forcing the active narrator to become an autobiographical participant. The teller should have a reason to testify now: a request, a dispute, a record being made, or a need to explain what happened.\n\nArrange the account so that what was expected, what was experienced, and what is understood at the time of telling can be distinguished. The teller may begin with the broad explanation once received—a promise, a plan, a theory, a set of instructions—and then move to the exact practical experience that followed. Make the disproportion legible through the relation between these layers without redundant concluding gloss. Actual testimony, reflection, or recognition can remain explicit when it belongs to the source or permitted narration.\n\nLet a modest detail confirm an extravagant promise as readily as qualify it. The teller may attest that the grand claim was, in fact, accurate, but that its accuracy showed up in a very particular way: the promised transformation turned out to be a change in one room, the promised journey ended at a familiar door, the promised system required only a single adjustment. Preserve accurate earlier understanding without inventing deception to manufacture disproportion. Any actual deception, false claim, or mistaken belief remains part of the account's supported history.\n\nKeep reported speech, recollection, and present private experience distinct. What the teller quotes from the past is not the same as what the teller now thinks about that quotation. The teller may still believe the original explanation, may have revised it, or may be uncertain. Preserve required private experience around the telling in its active presentation, including awareness or reception actually available under narrator and character permissions, without requiring or inventing access to every participant's inner response.\n\nThe teller may have any actual register, language, or community. Do not impose a family template, a particular religious or caste experience, or universal disillusionment. The comedy comes from the scale relation between the broad explanation and the exact practical experience the teller can attest to, not from making the teller's world a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose the diary and reflective miscellany. Use a situated entry or grouping of observations selected according to the actual recorder, purpose, and composition time. The recorder should have a reason to keep the record: a daily habit, a task requiring notes, a period of waiting, or a need to preserve something that might otherwise be forgotten.\n\nPlace a broad account of some condition beside an exact practical notation, at the recorder's actual register and needed extent rather than requiring an expansive entry. The recorder may devote considerable space to a theory, a plan, a classification, or a general observation about how things work, and then record, in the same entry or the next, a particular detail: the price of an item, the time a door opened, the condition of a tool, the number of steps between two places. The comic disproportion should arise from this arrangement. Avoid redundant gloss on the juxtaposition while retaining an actual recorded explanation, private recognition, or reflection in the writer's own register when it belongs to the material.\n\nAllow a return to an earlier entry to add an actual later annotation without rewriting prior wording or knowledge. The recorder may note, in a later entry, what actually happened, or may quote the earlier theory and add a single practical correction. The earlier entry remains as it was; the later annotation is a separate act of composition with its own time and purpose.\n\nKeep the authored record and the ongoing mind separate. The diary contains what the recorder chose to write, not a complete transcript of private thought. Preserve required private experience around the act of composition in its active presentation: the recorder may hesitate, revise, or decide not to include something. The artifact discloses its actual contents; the active narration may separately render private noticing, feeling, or uncertainty to the extent its own narrator and character permissions allow. A document's limits do not replace those permissions.\n\nNo new discovery, seasonal inventory, quiet personality, or failed theory is required. The recorder may be talkative, practical, angry, or amused. The comedy comes from the selection and placement of material, not from making the recorder a particular kind of person.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect polyphonic nonlinear telling. Juxtapose accounts or moments in an order that lets a grand explanation meet the particular concern of another source. The accounts should be accurately attributed: each one belongs to a specific person, time, and situation of telling or recording. Keep each mind and source attributable while preserving the active narrator's actual scope, including broader access when authorized.\n\nPosition the accounts' actual emphases for comic proportion; they can agree completely while selecting different logistical consequences, or retain whatever real differences their sources support. One source may describe the event as a triumph of planning; another may record only the difficulty of finding a place to sit, the cost of a meal, or the time spent waiting. The comedy arises from this scale relation. Both can be accurate while selecting different particulars, without a compulsory exposure or dispute. Preserve any actual falsehood, unequal knowledge, or discovery rather than requiring equal truthfulness.\n\nKeep the time of experience and the time of telling clear. A source may anticipate what will happen, and that anticipation may be accurate or incomplete. A source may speak from later knowledge, but that later knowledge must not be retroactively inserted into an earlier account. The reader should be able to track which source knew what, and when.\n\nAllow a digressive account to earn its relation through the shared problem. A source may wander into personal history, a technical explanation, or a local grievance, with its relation to the shared event or occasion readable even if a return remains pending at the active boundary. The teller retains independent concerns; the digression is not merely a delay.\n\nDo not force multiple completed accounts in one reply. The scene may present one account, part of an account, or a juxtaposition of two moments. Do not invent supernatural facts or marvelous events unless the supplied world already permits them. Construct the comedy through the arrangement of attributed perspectives. Any narrative summary retains its actual authority and purpose rather than replacing those situated accounts with a compulsory final meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaean::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Light Novel\n\nEnter the current situation with readable location, participants, and aim. The reader should know where the characters are, who is present, and what practical problem occupies them before any large explanation begins. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: when the scene moves, state the new location or the movement itself in plain terms.\n\nPresent only the unfamiliar premise needed for the next understanding. If a character introduces a grand explanation, a technical system, or a historical claim, give the reader enough of its terms to follow the immediate application. Do not bury the scene in exposition. The premise may be introduced through dialogue, a visible object, or a brief narrative summary, but it should remain connected to the present action.\n\nLet a clearly staged action or reply locate the premise's practical scale. The grand explanation should meet a concrete particular: a character tries to apply it, asks a question about it, or responds to it with a specific concern. The scale shift may arrive through surprise, literal fit, or familiar recognition. The scene does not need to complete a response before the boundary; the practical consequence can remain underway.\n\nUse paragraph breaks to time the transition from expectation to actual response. A break can separate the statement of the grand premise from the moment a character reacts, or separate the explanation from the small logistical detail that follows. The break is a pacing tool, not a mandatory joke beat.\n\nThe reaction belongs to its person. A character may be earnest, annoyed, delighted, or absent. Do not make the cast uniformly witty or require everyone to recognize the disproportion. A longer explanation or inward passage remains available when warranted; the style does not constrain thought to joke length.\n\nPreserve the selected narrator, cast age, and world facts. Do not impose first person, a young cast, or a closing hook. Construct the comedy through the clear relation between the large claim and the practical concern. Any narrator commentary keeps its actual register and permissions; wit may contribute without being compulsory or replacing that construction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Lyrical Prose\n\nDevelop a scene-born image or sound pattern through syntax in relation to an exact practical condition, making their comic proportion available through surprise or familiar recognition without requiring a changed scale. Start from something present: a phrase, a texture, a repeated noise, a comparison a character might actually make. Follow its associations far enough to enlarge the situation, then let a plain statement or concrete detail meet the expanded image. The meeting is the comic turn, not a separate punch line.\n\nKeep the image's development causally connected to the present concern. If a comparison about a grand undertaking opens the passage, let its precise implications lead back to the small logistical problem at hand: the equipment that must be carried, the form that must be signed, the person who must be asked. A plain phrase can cut the rhythm without disproving the image. The feeling inside the contrast may remain serious, joyful, or unfinished; do not require the image to collapse into a gag or the character to recover their mood.\n\nDistinguish figurative association from world fact. The narrator may compare a queue to a pilgrimage, but the queue remains a queue. Keep each speaker's language distinct from the narrator's figurative register; a literal or uninterested reply can provide the plain contrast. Preserve authorized private experience and actual knowledge. A digressive image may extend across the paragraph with its bearing on the current concern intact. Return through that connection when attention actually reaches it; the active boundary may fall before or within the return.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nConnect a current particular to an actually remembered occasion or attachment, and preserve the difference between then and now. Let a broad account once made meet what still operates, what has actually changed, or the exact inconvenience someone still remembers. The small survival of a habit can be funny without cancelling grief or requiring a new loss.\n\nUse the comic scale shift through time. A plan once described in expansive terms may now confront the same small practical detail it always contained: the same key, the same form, the same person's way of answering the phone. The recognition can be comic because the detail persists, not because the original hope was foolish. Let the present moment hold both the remembered feeling and the current practical response.\n\nKeep the remembered occasion, the character's actual recollection, and later interpretation distinct. Genuine former pleasure and prior knowledge must not be erased by a later interpretation, while actual gaps or fallible recollection remain possible. A callback may be recognizable without a closing declaration of impermanence or a forced improved mood. Give private reflection its warranted extent. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing recollection, feeling, or outward activity as well as an actual pause or conclusion; no change in the scene's business or lesson about loss is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction. Organize the telling through actual encounters in pursuit of a recognizable aim. Make each relevant arrangement's terms and practical scale readable through the actual encounter, whether their relation is newly apparent or already familiar. A person seeking a permit, a repair, a place to sleep, or a particular answer moves through the world's existing procedures and relationships; an encounter can expose the particular explanation offered for how things are done, without requiring every person to explain themselves or every arrangement to be reasonable.\n\nLet the comic disproportion emerge from the fit between that explanation and the exact local purpose it serves. A digression may trace why a small condition commands such large explanation, then return to what the person can actually do. The encounter need not create another obstacle; a helpful or straightforward response can be just as revealing. Do not require travel, trickster identity, or an endless chain of institutions.\n\nPreserve the knowledge of each source, the narrator's access, and actual private feeling. Use the supplied cultural and material context rather than importing universal mysticism, exile, or honor. Keep each character's voice distinct from the narrator's satirical account. Follow the active response boundary within continuing pursuit, observation, or private experience as well as an actual pause or change of direction; no such event or moral about the arrangement is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaear::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the detail and wording that make the scale relation inferable: what was promised or assumed, what it actually requires, and how this person responds. Use direct clauses and omit repeated judgment once the relation is legible. Make the comic relation inferable from the juxtaposition without redundant gloss, while preserving actual explanatory speech or privately rendered recognition.\n\nAn extended sentence or inward passage may still be necessary for thought, feeling, or orientation. Do not apply an immediate-action utility test to private experience. Keep actual causes and narrator permissions intact; the practical term may fulfill the claim in an unexpectedly literal way. A character need not speak tersely or conceal emotion, and a deflating last line is not required.\n\nChoose the few exact details that carry the situation: the large word on the form, the small tool that is missing, the one sentence that changes what the plan means. Let the reader infer the relation from their placement. Follow the active response boundary within ongoing action, attention, or private experience; the scene's business need not pause, resume, or change to permit the reply to end.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove naturally among the current activity, dialogue, perception, and authorized inner experience. When an explanation or apparently reasonable premise matters, follow it through a narratorial or character association to the particular condition it produces, then return through its actual bearing on the scene.\n\nThe digression should enlarge the immediate problem rather than replace it. A plan described in broad terms may lead to the exact practical condition of carrying it out: the person who must be interrupted, the surface that must be cleared, the instruction that contradicts the previous instruction. The return can be timed by paragraphing, but every exchange need not supply a reversal.\n\nPreserve independent cast voices. The response may be perfectly competent, literal, or unconcerned with the joke. Keep meaningful feeling beyond comic function, and retain the actual narrator, world facts, and active interval boundary. Follow the active response boundary during continuing action, perception, or private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. Ending the reply requires neither changed outward business nor a completed comic beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes and callbacks that fulfill the supplied relationship premise without adding a stock trope or forcing a changed bond. The participants and the relationship's actual continuation, change, or conclusion remain governed by the supplied premise and events.\n\nLet a familiar grand account, elaborate ritual, or practical workaround recur in a circumstance that makes its scale newly apt or still enjoyably recognizable. The large explanation one person always gives may meet the precise habit or preference the other actually knows: the same detour, the same overpacked bag, the same way of explaining a simple errand. Let recognition itself carry comedy without manufacturing misunderstanding; any real misunderstanding follows the participants' actual knowledge and circumstances.\n\nDistinguish shared knowledge from the private association of one person. Callbacks may be understood correctly and need not provoke miscommunication. Give the actual affection, rivalry, jealousy, or comfort full private and public room without replacing it with banter. No confession, repair, or changed relationship status is needed to complete a reply. Preserve the active narrator, world facts, and scene boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nUse compact declarations, recurrence, and the cadence of retelling to give actual deeds a remembered shape. Let an elevated account meet a supported practical term through a precise turn of phrase or an actual reply by a participant. The deed may remain consequential and the praise true; the comedy lies in the disproportion between the reputation and the exact condition of doing it.\n\nRecurring designations must arise from established acts rather than automatic epithets. A person may be remembered for the time they held the door, but the account can still include the fact that they were holding it because the latch had jammed. Keep communal reputation distinct from private experience: being remembered as dependable does not mean the task was effortless, cheerful, or automatic.\n\nKeep later knowledge distinct from the original event. Do not require mock heroism, humiliation, prophecy, or a finished legendary verdict at the active boundary. Let the account stop within a deed, recollection, or actual conclusion at the active boundary. No next modest act or new communal verdict is required, and any existing reputation remains attributed to its real sources.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the comic scene through a psychological and essayistic narration that remains attributable to the active narrator or focal character. Begin with a particular concern, observation, or claim that the person is trying to make sense of, then let the narration follow the actual movement of that consideration: a qualified generalization, a remembered instance, a return to the present object, a second thought that revises the first. Do not announce that the passage is reflective; let the shape of the sentences do the reflective work.\n\nUse the contrast between a large claim and a small logistical problem as the engine of the comic turn. A careful account of why something matters can expand through explanation, comparison, or principle, then meet a strangely exact practical condition: the thing that must be carried, the person who must be asked, the time that is actually available, the form that must be filled in. Let the practical detail redirect the scale of the reflection rather than cancel it. The character may remain genuinely convinced of the large claim, and the comedy can coexist with approval, fondness, or sustained feeling.\n\nKeep the reflection inside the authorized mind. The narration may follow what this person notices, remembers, or privately concludes, but it cannot grant access to another mind unless the active contract permits it. Distinguish the person's own past experience from a general truth about how people behave. Attribute a generalization to the actual thinker, speaker, or narrative voice entitled to make it; an authorized narrator can state one without making the focal character hold it.\n\nLet the association take its needed extent. A longer reflection may establish the expectation a practical detail qualifies, or develop feeling, attention, and uncertainty beyond the comic relation. Its extent needs no next-action or comic-payoff justification; a familiar fit may remain unchanged. Do not force every reflection to end in a dry remark, a deflation, or a completed joke. The scene may stop while the person is still considering the mismatch, or while the practical work continues.\n\nDo not import reserve, snow, isolation, bleakness, or a national temperament into the narration. The route is the essayistic movement of a particular mind, not a setting or a mood. The comedy comes from the precise relation between the person's explanation and the world's practical terms, not from making the person or the landscape grim.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when time or location changes. Let the visible or audible world carry the comic contrast. A character may state a large claim in dialogue, or the action may establish an elaborate expectation through what is seen: a prepared arrangement, a displayed object, a careful sequence of movements. Then let the next action, cut, or offscreen sound connect that claim to its specific practical application.\n\nUse the cut as a comic instrument when the scene needs it. A cut can move from a grand explanation to the modest physical condition that actually governs it: the room where the thing must be done, the person already waiting, the object that is smaller, older, or more ordinary than the speech suggested. Do not narrate the irony. Let the juxtaposition of the two visible or audible moments produce the scale shift. A held beat can also do this work: a character continues the ordinary competent action while the large claim still hangs in the air, and the audience sees the claim and the practical work at the same time.\n\nDo not require a mishap, a reaction shot, or a voice-over to explain the comedy. The contrast should be legible from what is already visible or audible. A character may simply proceed with the work, answer plainly, or continue the task, and the scene can stop at the active interval boundary without a public punch line or a completed scene.\n\nPreserve required private experience separately as attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation. A character's prolonged or unfinished thought, feeling, or reasoning is not automatically available to the camera or the other characters. Do not convert it into a voice-over or an onscreen expression unless the active contract authorizes that form. The screenplay presents what can be seen and heard; private experience remains in its own channel, distinct from the action lines and dialogue.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Stage Play\n\nWrite the scene as a playable script with clear speakers and economical stage directions for supported entrances, exits, blocking, objects, positions, and movement. Put a precise explanation in the mouth of an actual speaker only when it fits that character; do not give every character the same elaborate explanatory register. Time the explanation against ongoing work or an ordinary response visible to the audience.\n\nLet the comic contrast come from the relation between the spoken premise and the physical business required to enact it in the shared stage space. A character may declare a large intention, principle, or plan, and the stage directions may then show the modest practical condition that governs it: the chair that must be moved, the object that must be fetched, the other person who is already doing the ordinary part of the task, the pause while the speaker realizes what the plan actually involves. The grand assertion and the modest condition can coexist without a new prop, a stumble, or a witty interruption.\n\nKeep the stage directions causal and observable. They establish what the audience can see: who enters, who moves, what is carried, what is left behind. They do not certify hidden guilt, motive, or private feeling. Do not use a stage direction to explain the joke or to tell the reader what the audience should feel.\n\nPreserve full private experience separately as attributed nonspoken material in the selected presentation. A character's thought is not automatically an aside or monologue. An aside or monologue is performed speech with its own audience and dramatic purpose, and it requires separate authorization. The scene may stop within business, speech, or thought without a laugh cue, a completed exchange, or a public resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite the scene through the actual associative movement of one authorized mind. Braid perception, private words, memory, bodily sensation, interruption, and recurring concern around the current practical matter. Let an apparently reasonable explanation grow through the associations of that mind: a phrase leads to a principle, the principle leads to a remembered occasion, the memory returns to the present object, the object prompts a bodily sensation, and the sensation revises the scale of the whole concern.\n\nLet the comic turn occur when the enlarged explanation folds back into an exact sensation or remembered phrase. The mind may be elaborating a grand account of why the matter is important, and then a mundane particular arrives: the feel of the handle, the sound of the other person's voice, the remembered wording of a note, the pressure of the chair. The particular does not have to cancel the large claim; it can simply sit beside it and change the proportion. The mind may notice the disproportion, or it may continue with the practical concern while the reader sees the relation.\n\nKeep the associative material distinct. Received speech, remembered wording, and fantasy are not the same as present perception. When the mind recalls what someone said, let the memory be a memory; when it imagines a possibility, let the fantasy be a fantasy. Do not grant access to other minds unless the active contract permits it. The associative movement belongs to this particular mind, not to a universal clever inner narrator.\n\nAllow the thought to be serious or enjoyable, extensive or unfinished. It does not need to solve the problem, produce a punch line, or end in deflation. Prose length alone does not dictate elapsed time; a long associative passage may occupy a brief interval, and a short one may cover a longer stretch. Stop at the active boundary while the mind is still moving, if that is where the scene ends.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nCarry the scene through clear sender-attributed messages. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the exchange. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may write long, careful messages; another may reply in fragments; a third may not reply at all until the practical matter is resolved.\n\nBuild the comic contrast through the relation between an elaborate transmitted explanation and an exactly practical reply or continuation. A character may send a careful message that builds an expectation: a full account of the principle, the plan, the significance, the likely consequences. Then the next message may set that expectation beside a modest practical need: a request for the address, a question about the time, a confirmation of the smaller detail the first message omitted. The accurate literal reply does the comic work. Do not invent a typo, deletion, delay, or misunderstanding to force the contrast.\n\nPreserve composing, sending, and actual receipt as distinct. A message that is composed is not yet sent; a sent message is not yet read; a read message is not yet answered. Let the transcript show only what the active form can show. An unsent draft is composed text, not live thought. Required nonspoken experience stays fully available outside the transmitted channel in its active presentation, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nEnd at the response boundary. The scene may stop during composition, before a reply arrives, or after a message has been read but not answered. No cliffhanger, question, or forced choice is required. The comic scale comes from what was actually sent and known, not from an interface event invented to add subtext.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaeba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene with concise scene orientation, clear dialogue attribution, and flexible focal narration or inner response in a rhythm suited to an illustrated scene. Select a meaningful background, object, or expression cue and let it anchor the contrast between an expansive expectation and the actual reception of a character.\n\nUse the cue as a locating detail, not as a truth indicator. A broad explanation may direct attention toward the practical feature that establishes its scale: the familiar object that is smaller than the plan suggested, the background detail that shows where the work actually happens, the expression that registers a character's actual response. Follow the focal person's thought or feeling at its warranted extent. A face can be noticed without proving a motive; a practical cue can confirm rather than defeat the expectation. The comic scale comes from the relation between the explanation and the located detail, not from making the expression reveal a hidden mind.\n\nKeep the focal narration flexible. It may move between dialogue, scene-setting, and inner response as the scene requires. Do not impose a fixed cue-thought-dialogue rotation, a closing reaction beat, or a mandatory expression label on every line. Preserve each knowledge boundary, the supplied narrator, and the nonspoken presentation required by the active contract.\n\nDo not add branching menus, player decisions, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands merely because the style is selected. Those require their own authorization or feature. The scene can stop at the active boundary with the focal person still considering the mismatch, continuing the practical work, or responding in an ordinary way.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaebb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Douglas Adams / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments. At the start of a new installment, recover only the exact earlier claim, arrangement, or practical detail that helps the reader re-enter the scene. Avoid repeating the whole earlier sequence or explanation when a precise reminder supplies the needed context. Then preserve what was learned, changed, used, or left pending: the premise that was stated, the object that was moved, the person who agreed, the form that was still incomplete.\n\nLet a later application give an expansive explanation a new small purpose, or repeat a successful familiar one for recognition. A callback can work by returning to the earlier large claim and showing its next practical consequence: the thing that was explained now has to be done again under a slightly different condition, or the familiar arrangement turns out to govern a new part of the work. The recognition is comic because the reader remembers the original scale and sees the new particular application.\n\nMake any reversal arise from actual events or revised terms, not from forgotten knowledge or fabricated obstacles. If a character previously understood something, they still understand it. If an arrangement was changed between installments, show that change as part of the history; do not omit it so the later scene falsely proves the earlier account wrong.\n\nLet narrator digressions reconnect to the continuing concern without forcing a joke or plot advance in every private passage. A reflection may extend the premise, qualify it, or simply occupy the character while the practical work continues. Stop at the active boundary, including an unfinished setup, ordinary continuation, or completed work. No cliffhanger quota, no required punch line, no reset of competence or facts at the start of each installment.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nMake the relevant actual aim and any supported expectation legible within narrator and character-ownership permissions; not every participant's purpose or private belief must be known. Make the aim legible through ordinary movement, an object, a direct request, or authorized private narration as the actual encounter permits. Let the expectation stand concrete: she expects the door to be locked, he expects the argument to start over the broken clasp, they expect the old apology to be refused again. Then render the actual reply in exact words, leaving visible what remains unsaid. The response can be dry, patient, literal, or quietly accurate without making the pain the joke. A repeated phrase or returning activity can mark how the need persists: 'You said you'd be here,' 'You can stay—the ferry is not running,' the same key laid on the same table. When the reply changes little, the comedy sits in the recognized gap between expectation and what the other person actually does.\n\nKeep private cost fully present. The mind under the spoken line may notice the cold pan, the untouched bed, the way 'all right' is not an answer. The pain does not have to surface as visible strain or collapse. Let direct emotional speech and accurate understanding coexist: one character can say what hurts in plain language while another listens and does nothing theatrical. The scene does not owe a confession, a repair, a breakdown, or a softened ending. Preserve the actual participants, history, and state of the encounter, without requiring a pair, prior familiarity, visible movement, or an unsettled relationship. Let the narration follow those movements in economical clauses, using the precise name of the object and the exact action, so the actual spoken and private experience carries the turbulence, whether or not anything remains unsaid.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the current reply as one continuous shared interval. Open with a precise physical position or handled object that belongs to the ongoing scene and to what a particular character is already trying to do. Use the active role contract to decide who can act, who can think, and where the reply stops, allowing new events, entrances, or locations where the active role contract and established world continuity support them rather than freezing the scene to the supplied posts. Make relevant actual aims recognizable within the narrator's access, and let people pursue them through their real acts, speech, or permitted private experience. The aim can be visible through an actual request—'Put that back where it was'—or through the way someone keeps folding a jacket without looking up. The author profile's economical clauses keep the scene legible without stripping feeling: name the object, state the act, attribute the line, and let the silence or repetition carry what is not said.\n\nWhen a comic turn occurs, make it arise from a particular response or a familiar repetition without requiring a banter exchange, while allowing one when it belongs to these speakers. A character may answer a question with a single accurate word, repeat an earlier phrase with a different practical weight, or continue the same activity after the other person changes course. For example, one participant keeps trying to secure the shutters while the other explains why the shutters are perfectly adequate; the comedy lives in the second person's reasonable effort meeting the first person's literal answer. Do not force every reply into wit or interruption. Let some lines land, some miss, some be serious. Preserve the actual physical world and ongoing conditions, without inserting tea, a faulty window, or a falling coat merely to supply a comic beat. End where the role boundary or the active selection requires, even if the setup remains unfinished. No required cliffhanger, question, or punch line at this turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the vernacular episodic route from the Chinese literary tradition. Write each occasion as a contained present moment with a clear practical aim: the person wants the stall reopened, the debt acknowledged, the borrowed saw returned, the letter posted before noon. Render the immediate activity and direct speech in Hemingway's economical style: name the exact objects and acts (the wet brush, the three coins, the phrase 'not today') and let the exchange carry the comic weight. The episode need not be a journey and need not resolve completely; it can close on a practical unresolved state, such as a locked gate or a reply that does not arrive.\n\nLink selected episodes with a repeated object, phrase, or promise. When a phrase returns, let its context remain or change through actual development: the same question now asked to a different person, the same object now in a different hand, the same refusal given with a new reason. The repetition can confirm the familiar pattern—someone is always late, always misplaces the key, always asks the same question about the train—or it can reveal that the earlier event has altered the present without the narrative announcing it. Do not rewrite the earlier occasion to make a later joke; comic recognition may arise from an unintended implication or a mutually familiar pattern; the phrase need not change meaning or escape its speaker's intention. Keep each episode's private interiority where the active contract permits, and let characters speak in their individual registers, whether verbose, literal, or restrained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nStart from exact material conditions and a direct aim: the drain is blocked, the permit must be stamped before noon, the borrowed truck has to be back by dark. Use forceful concrete diction—the wrench, the cracked mortar, the reek of solvent—and let Hemingway's direct verbs carry each action. Keep transitions compressed but not clipped into fragments: a sentence can move from a stated intention straight to the visible result that undercuts or confirms it. The comic turn needs no physical mishap or incompetence. Competence can be the landing: the person knows exactly how the pump works, and the expected struggle does not happen; the clerk stamps the permit without waiting; the truck is full of gas and the key is under the mat. Rhythm can place an actual outcome or continuing condition beside an expectation, without requiring eased circumstances or a completed result.\n\nDo not shorten private thought by rule. A long, focused piece of reasoning can run beneath the terse action when the character needs it—weighing whether to admit the debt, calculating how much water is left, deciding to say nothing about the broken latch. Rough speech belongs to the speaker who would use it; do not make every character hostile or curt. Use a change of rhythm for emphasis: a slow inventory of damage can precede the one clean sentence that accepts the situation. Preserve physical stakes without importing survival horror. Preserve the supplied world and any wear actually present. Dry comic timing can arise from how an actual continuation meets an expectation, including a familiar response or a genuine difference without a required completed result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Epistolary / Documents\n\nConstruct the reply as one or more documents, each with a definite writer, an intended recipient or purpose, and a moment of composition. Use the document form the current scene or active contract requires—letter, note, report, inventory, diary entry—and do not rotate formats for variety. Select and present each document with attention to exact requests, objects, and acts, while preserving the record writer's actual voice and what they would choose to include or omit. The writer may be trying to secure payment, confirm a time, apologize, or request the return of a tool. Use the relation between records for comic timing, allowing an actual writer's deliberate joke without requiring every document to contain one.\n\nCreate timing through connected precise particulars across records. One document may list three items missing from the crate; a later receipt shows the same three items added by a different hand. An apology may quote a phrase that the recipient quoted in a very different sense. A repeated phrase can acquire new practical meaning: 'If it is not too much trouble' first refers to borrowing a ladder, then to replacing a broken pane. The omission must follow the writer's situation: a merchant's note omits the fact that the payment arrived because the merchant never learned it; a diary does not mention the burned coat because the writer is still unable to write about it. The linked records need not agree. When the active presentation requires private live experience, it remains separate from the composed documents; the letter is not the character's complete mind. Leave continuation grounded in the actual records and scene state, including parallel events or private reflection without requiring the latest record to cause the next occurrence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nKeep the scene focused on a particular kindness chosen for a particular person. Give the kindness concrete activity: making the tea the way they take it, saving the seat by the window, leaving the good knife where it can be found, taking the long way without being asked. Use the author's precise objects and actions: the cup with the chipped handle, the two sugars, the coat laid over the back of the chair. The recipient's response is not automatically grateful or demonstrative; it is faithful to who they are. An elaborate offer can meet a literal answer: 'Do you want me to drive you?' / 'It is only three streets.' Or the well-known routine can work exactly as expected, and the humor lies in its perfect, unremarked success—the same order known at the counter, the same repair that finally holds.\n\nDo not require a bumbled favor, a confession, an apology, or an emotional reveal. Direct affection may appear in plain speech: 'You are all right then.' 'Yes, I am all right.' Honest disagreement can survive the comfort: one person may refuse the offered help and remain in the room, or say the tea is too sweet and drink it anyway. Developed private feeling can pass beneath the dialogue, but keep it specific to the person and the moment rather than a generic warmth. The comedy comes from exact familiarity—knowing the preference, misreading it, or meeting it too thoroughly—and from the characters' independent habits, not from making comfort itself ridiculous. End at the active boundary, including continuing care, private experience, or a still-pending offer without requiring a produced practical state.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Formal Literary Novel\n\nOpen with the social or historical frame the narrator can legitimately supply: the house had been in the family through three arguments; the new rule had been posted for a week; the bridge repair meant everyone now took the longer road. Keep the frame specific and consequential, not decorative. Move deliberately into the immediate aim through the formal novel's sustained paragraph movement. The narrator may note that the disagreement about the will had already lasted longer than the lawsuit, or that the festival began in a year when the river froze. Then render the concrete exchange in Hemingway's direct style: a request, an answer, an object handled, a task begun. The contrast between the weight of the framing and the plainness of the spoken line produces the reader's dry recognition: 'Will you sign it now?' / 'I am eating my bread.' The line may be literal, deliberately dry, or otherwise shaped by its speaker; each participant knows the stakes only through their actual information and access.\n\nDo not shrink the real stakes for the joke. The outcome may be significant—the deed transferred, the partnership ended, the truth spoken—or it may remain pending while the conversation moves to another room. The comic effect depends on the reader feeling the importance of the aim through the narrator's framing, while the actual exchange remains undramatized and exact. Let the narration return to its broader social awareness after the exchange, but avoid closing with a moral. The characters may not resolve anything; the night may simply continue with the lamp lit and the papers unsigned. Keep spoken language in ordinary registers even when the surrounding prose is formal. No one needs to rise to the narrator's eloquence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the teller, the audience, and the occasion before the embedded story begins. Name the exact object, place, or act the teller selects, and let the story proceed through direct clauses and practical exchanges. The teller may recount a memory, a report, or an invention, but the telling itself is a present action with a purpose: to persuade, entertain, explain, or delay.\n\nBuild the comic situation from the teller's recognizable aim and the listeners' actual reception. Do not make the audience a passive mirror. A listener may ask a literal question about a detail, recognize a familiar phrase, or respond to the story's practical consequence. Let the response arrive at its actual time, whether it changes pressure, sustains familiar timing, or leaves the situation unchanged. A dry, exact reply can carry the comedy without a punchline.\n\nUse repetition to connect the narrative levels. A phrase, object, or gesture from the embedded story can return in the outer scene with a different emphasis. The return should be legible to the reader, but it does not need to be announced by the teller or understood by every listener. Keep the teller's private thought separate from the performed words; what the teller withholds or does not yet understand remains available through authorized interiority, not through the tale itself.\n\nLet the story's outcome remain contingent. The teller may succeed, partly succeed, or be interrupted by a practical need. Do not require a failed performance, a concealed ulterior motive, or a cliffhanger between levels. End at the active interval boundary, including continued listening, an unfinished tale, or private thought without a required new question, changed task, or next concrete action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse social or developmental realism as the construction. Present an earlier occasion in which a character pursues a recognizable aim with a specific expectation. Render that occasion through exact objects, activities, and direct speech. Do not explain the expectation as a lesson; show the person acting on it in a concrete situation.\n\nMove to a later occasion that returns to the same aim, place, or relationship. Connect the two moments with a precise repeated act, phrase, or object. The repetition may confirm an old habit, show a partial change, or reveal that the person still does not understand what the reader can see. Let the comic effect come from the relation between the earlier expectation and the later conduct, not from a forced humiliation or a sudden insight.\n\nKeep the prose economical. Use short clauses and plain words for the practical exchanges. Preserve actual private reasoning, feeling, memory, and attention at their warranted extent without a usefulness gate, omitting only redundant restatement. What remains unsaid can carry the strain between the two occasions. Preserve the character's competence and dignity where the supplied scene supports them; the later act may be effective even when it repeats an old pattern.\n\nLet the temporal return organize the passage without requiring a completed development. The character may change, remain the same, or change only in a small particular. End at the active boundary, including a continuing later occasion or private reflection before any consequence is known; no completed result or moral is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Gothic-Punk\n\nSet the scene through an established role, place, privilege, or obligation. Use exact material detail: the weight of a door, the condition of a floor, the position of a key, the sound of a system that grants or denies access. Let the atmosphere arise from these concrete particulars rather than from general dread. Preserve the authority's actual status, power, and limits, including any established legitimacy or fraud; its holder acts only through the means the world supplies.\n\nIntroduce an actual present request or need that meets the inherited arrangement, with a collision or contrast only where the situation supports it. Render the exchange in direct speech and simple clauses. The comic scale comes from the contrast between the ceremonial or institutional weight and the actual need: a literal reply, an ordinary act, or a practical question can make the relation readable. Do not turn every reply into a quip or make the authority figure a fool.\n\nPreserve private desire and morally complicated intimacy. A character may want something from the other person, protect an interest, or reveal a preference in trusted company. Let that private material appear through authorized interiority or a change in behavior, with any actual disclosure kept distinct from private experience; no sudden unmasking is required. The social mask may fall away partially, but it need not fall away completely.\n\nKeep the consequences intact. The request may be granted, refused, or redirected. The old authority may accommodate present life without being exposed as fraudulent. End at the active scene boundary, including an unchanged situation, continued thought, or a pending request without a required concrete change.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse a testimonial account as the construction. Establish who is speaking, to whom, and for what purpose. The speaker may be the active narrator or a person whose testimony the narrator presents. Anchor the account in the speaker's actual participation: what the person did, saw, or learned from named sources. Keep earlier knowledge separate from later commentary; do not let the speaker retroactively claim an understanding that was not available at the time.\n\nRender the exact acts and words of the lived occasion in direct language. Select precise objects, movements, and exchanges. The comedy can arise from the relation between what the speaker intended and how the act or word was actually received. A truthful, competent account can still produce a dry contrast through selection and timing. Do not require the speaker to have failed, exaggerated, or concealed the truth.\n\nLet the audience or purpose shape the participant account, whether it is spoken, written, or presented by the authorized narrator, without requiring a separate storytelling performance. The speaker may answer a question, explain a decision, or set a record straight. A listener's literal response or a practical consequence in the present frame can carry the comic turn. Keep required private experience outside the performed or recorded account; the testimony is a composed artifact, not the speaker's complete live mind.\n\nPreserve the distinction between what the participant knew then and what the speaker knows now. The later knowledge may inform the telling, but it must not replace the earlier experience. End at the active boundary, including within testimony or continuing private experience before any consequence or next question is known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{trim}}{{/if}}"
