            "identifier": "nemo-init-recipes-comedy-02",
            "name": "🔧 Init · Comedy · 02/06",
            "content": "{{#if {{.NCGenreId == comedy}}}}{{// @category Variable-Init }}{{// @icon 🔧 }}{{// @badge INIT }}{{// @tooltip Comedy. Selected-partition data; keep enabled in supplied order after recipe selectors and before the Writing Recipe resolver. Part 2 of 6. }}{{// Source group: recipes-comedy; payload preserved. }}{{setvar::NPacafal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse early-modern comic/theatrical telling as a prose method, not a script format. Make relevant actual aims, positions, and utterances legible within the narrator's access without inventing excluded private intentions. Show where people stand, what they handle, and what they are trying to accomplish. Keep the physical business concrete: a task, an object, a movement that can be followed.\n\nLet the comedy arise from the relation between direct speech and the existing activity. A pause, a repeated line, or a literal response can meet the action at a moment that changes the situation. Do not impose a performative register on the cast. The speakers use their own diction; plain talk and stillness are available. No archaic setting, exaggerated speech, prop, or laugh is required.\n\nUse repetition as a theatrical device within the prose. A phrase or gesture can return with a different emphasis, making the earlier moment legible without a narrator's explanation. Keep the teller's private experience available through authorized interiority, separate from the public words. What a character withholds or does not understand can remain unsaid.\n\nLet the active boundary govern the ending, including ongoing speech, stillness, or private experience without a produced concrete continuation. The aim may be completed, interrupted, or redirected. Do not require a punchline, a completed joke, or a sudden reversal. The next action should follow from what has already been established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse social realism and chronicle as the construction. Follow an actual shared place, activity, or relation through ordered occasions. Each occasion should be concrete: a specific time, a specific set of people, and a specific task or exchange. Connect the occasions with precise repeated acts or phrases. The repetition can be a familiar accurate response that becomes funny through timing, not through a new failure.\n\nRender the exchanges in direct language with exact objects and movements. Keep the chronicle's sense of continuity: what has changed, what has stayed the same, and what the community knows. Attribute memories and reports to their sources. Community knowledge is not the same as every person's private mind; do not grant the narrator access to thoughts that belong to other characters unless the active contract permits it.\n\nLet the comic effect come from the relation between the routine and the actual occasion. A person may repeat a habit, adapt it, or be surprised by a change in the familiar situation. The routine need not collapse or humiliate anyone. Actual changes remain part of history and can affect the next occasion without being announced as a moral.\n\nEnd at the active interval boundary, including within an occasion before the next one is known; no new concrete consequence, cliffhanger, or completed arc is required. The passage may stop after a specific exchange that leaves the shared place or relation visibly changed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish the recognizable aim at its supplied scale, with clear attribution and orientation. If an unfamiliar name, object, or mechanic is needed to follow the encounter, explain it in plain terms at the point of use. Do not front-load a glossary or recap machinery.\n\nUse readable paragraph breaks around speech, activity, and private response. Keep spatial changes easy to follow. Render the exchanges in direct clauses and practical words. A literal answer, a familiar callback, or a concrete continuation can create comic timing without everyone being witty. Do not require a young cast, a completed joke, or a fixed reaction slot.\n\nLet private response remain available through authorized interiority. A character's thought may be long enough to make sense, but it should not be labeled as a reaction beat or forced to immediately change the visible action. What remains unsaid can carry the strain between the public exchange and the private experience.\n\nEnd at the active boundary, including continuing private experience or a situation whose next action is not yet determined. The aim may be advanced, interrupted, or redirected. Do not require a cliffhanger, a question, or a forced choice at the end. The next action should follow from the concrete situation already established.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Lyrical Prose\n\nBuild the comic situation around one exact object, activity, or habitual gesture that the scene can actually supply. Develop it through syntax and sound where actual attention takes it, before or within the exchange: repeat a phrase, extend a clause, or let a comparison unfold from the thing itself. The image may be graceful, absurd, or simply persistent, but keep it traceable to the actual scene or an association available to the authorized narrator, without requiring the character to share it. Comparisons may belong to the authorized narrator alone; keep their source in that voice or attention without making them shared character knowledge.\n\nPlace the actual line or continuation beside the developed image. Let the contrast come from a plain practical reply, a direct statement of feeling, or a continuation that ignores the image's weight. The line need not puncture the image. It can confirm it, answer it literally, or carry on with the work while the image stays present. Keep the register shift readable rather than announcing it: the syntax may shorten, the sentence may turn on a concrete verb, or the speaker may name the object without ornament.\n\nLet recurrence retain its meaning. If the image returns later, it can acquire a new emphasis from the changed situation, but it does not have to become a joke. Preserve the character's actual aim and private feeling. The comedy is in the relation between the shaped attention and the plain continuation, not in making the feeling false or the speaker foolish.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet the present scene recall one particular act, phrase, or object from an earlier occasion. Choose a particular that the character actually noticed or used then, not a general summary of the past. Present the memory through the same direct language: what was said, what was done, what the person was trying to do. Keep the earlier aim and pleasure intact. The memory may be fond, inconvenient, or simply persistent.\n\nBring the particular into the present through association rather than explanation. A familiar object, a repeated action, or a phrase spoken again can carry the memory. Let the present response meet it plainly: the character may repeat the phrase, recognize the old aim, or continue the current work while the memory stays. The comedy can come from the exact fit or mismatch between the remembered particular and the present situation. It does not require the earlier occasion to have been foolish or the present feeling to be regret.\n\nAllow the same meaning to remain intact or acquire a new emphasis. If the memory changes the character's understanding, show the change through a concrete detail or a direct statement. If it changes nothing, let that be the point. Keep the actual private feeling available even when it alters no outward response. No announcement of impermanence or lesson about the past is required merely by the style. Preserve any actual supported direct reflection in the person's own diction and within the narrator's access.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as a modern memoir with the participant and the later teller both identifiable under the active narrator permissions. The participant's part of the account uses the earlier knowledge and aims: exact acts, direct speech, and the practical purpose of the moment. The later teller's part may frame, compare, or recognize a pattern, but it must not replace the participant's live experience with a retrospective explanation.\n\nLet the later framing create the comic timing through contrast. The teller may note what the participant expected, what the other person actually did, or how the same phrase sounded later. Keep the contrast dry and specific. The earlier feeling remains real: the desire was actually experienced, while any judgment of it follows the real case and narrator's supported perspective rather than automatic approval, and the later teller need not mock them. The comedy comes from the distance between the two times, not from rewriting the earlier self as a fool.\n\nPreserve the actual outcome and knowledge at each time. The composed memoir is not the whole live mind of participant or teller; required actual nonspoken experience remains in its active presentation at either authorized time. The later teller may know what the participant did not, but that knowledge cannot be inserted into the earlier moment as a premonition or a hidden motive. If the memoir returns to the present, let the return be a continuation of the teller's actual situation, not a compulsory lesson or a final joke.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect the few particulars that make the aim and the continuation intelligible: the object in use, the action underway, the exact line spoken. Use direct clauses for the setup. Omit redundant explanation while allowing a repeated description or clarification where actual attention and context give it a purpose. The omitted material should remain inferable from what is kept, not delete the cause of the next action.\n\nLet the comic contrast come from the relation between the selected particulars. A plain response can follow a carefully placed detail without a transition that announces the joke. The sentence may shorten at the landing, but it need not become a fragment. A pause can be ordinary: the character may wait, look at the object, or say nothing. Do not make the silence carry a hidden meaning unless the scene supports one.\n\nPreserve full inward experience. A thought may be long when the character's reasoning needs it, and a direct emotional line may remain direct. The minimalist pressure selects what the reader needs to follow the scene; it does not make the character's mind shorter or less available. End at the active scene boundary, not at a forced payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nOpen inside the immediate scene with the character's recognizable aim and the exact activity that carries it. Move naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience without pausing to explain the scene's comic purpose. Make the actual aim legible through conduct, speech, or authorized private narration as appropriate; it need not receive an outward display.\n\nUse particular responses, repeated phrases, or plain continuations to create reversal, anticlimax, or callbacks where the scene supports them. A reversal can be a small practical change: the expected reply does not come, the task takes a different shape, or the other person responds to a different part of the request. An anticlimax can be the work continuing after the expected moment. A callback can be a phrase or object returning with its meaning intact or shifted.\n\nKeep private feeling and direct disclosure available. The character may think at length, state a feeling plainly, or leave it unsaid. The comedy does not require a hidden motive, a misunderstanding, or an emotional turn in every reply. Let the scene end at its natural boundary, with the aim still underway or the exchange concluded, rather than at a forced punch line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose a relevant shared preference, phrase, object, or routine from the actual supplied premise and participants. Establish it through a concrete use: who says the phrase, who reaches for the object, who follows the routine, and what each person is trying to do. Keep the particular tied to the relationship's actual history and present situation, not to a generic trope.\n\nLet the present use meet the response that actually occurs. The response may be familiar, literal, surprising, or quietly enjoyed. The comedy comes from the timing: the phrase arrives at the wrong moment, the object is used for a different purpose, the routine continues while the conversation changes, or the familiar act receives an unexpected but fitting reply. Do not force a misunderstanding or a test of devotion. The callback can confirm the bond, expose a small habit, or simply be shared.\n\nKeep private feeling and individual speech intact. Each person may think and speak in their own register; the relationship's private language does not make them interchangeable. A callback need not change the relationship or reveal a secret. End with the shared particular's actual state and meaning, including a pending or completed exchange; future use is possible where supported but not required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nOpen with a deliberate retelling gesture: a compact declaration of who did what, a phrase the community repeats, or an act already attributed to the person. Then expand into the actual means. Show the object used, the work done, the exact words spoken. Let the elevated opening meet the plain particulars without making the deed smaller than it was. The comedy comes from the scale of the retelling against the actual effort, not from falsifying the act.\n\nLet phrasing recur where actual history or attention brings it back, retaining the same application or developing a new one as the occasion warrants. A designation may return when the person does the same thing again, when someone else repeats it, or when the present scene recalls it. Keep the phrase tied to the established deed or way of speaking. Do not assign an ornamental epithet to every entrance.\n\nLet dialogue interrupt or agree with the chronicle. The subject of the remembered act may correct a detail, answer the elevated introduction with a practical concern, or confirm the account without ceremony. This need not become parody. Preserve the actual scale of the deed, the person's private experience, and any effort still underway. The account can end with the business concluded or resumed, leaving the act available to be remembered accurately rather than declared legend.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene as an essayistic account from the active narrator's position. Open with a situated observation the narrator can actually make from the present scene or from direct knowledge of the people involved. State it in plain, economical language without turning it into a universal lesson.\n\nBring that observation beside a precise case: an actual event, a remembered occasion, or a clearly hypothetical instance whose aims and reception are legible. Let the case contain exact objects, practical activities, and direct exchanges. The contrast between the general claim and the particular case can produce dry comic timing, but the claim need not be disproved or abandoned. The narrator may simply return to the claim's actual scope, noting where it held, where it did not apply, or what the case left unsettled.\n\nDo not force the case to end in a completed test, a moral, or a new proposition. The comedy can lie in the narrator's serious framing meeting an ordinary, specific continuation. Preserve each person's own habits and blind spots rather than making them all witty or all foolish. Required private experience remains available to the active narrator in its authorized presentation, but another character's mind is not automatically known.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Screenplay\n\nWrite the scene in concise action blocks with clear speaker cues and scene headings when location or time changes. Establish relevant public aims through visible or audible particulars, while authorized private aims may remain in the nonspoken channel: what a person is doing, what object they are handling, what they say they want, and what the other person actually does in response.\n\nUse a cut, a held interval, or a plain reply to place an expectation beside its continuation. The comic timing comes from the exact observable setup and the actual response, not from an invented accident, a missed line, or a forced reaction shot. A cut joins occasions in presentation; physical effects follow actual events and must not be attributed to editing alone.\n\nKeep required private experience in its active nonspoken presentation, separate from voice-over and from anything another character can hear. Do not turn private reception into a stage direction or a visible expression that proves what someone thinks. Let the active boundary govern the stop, including completed action, a pending request, stillness, or ongoing private experience without an obligatory acknowledgment or punch line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Stage Play\n\nUse the actual shared space to make the public exchange playable, with relevant aims established within narrator access and private purposes left nonspoken where appropriate. Give clear speakers, entrances, exits, blocking, and objects that can be handled on stage. Let a person continue a practical task while speaking, answer literally, or remain still. A pause or an accurately heard line can supply comic timing without requiring a prop to be used, a line to be missed, or a character to perform a reaction.\n\nKeep the relationship between lines and physical business carrying the public scene. The comedy can come from a direct answer meeting a recognizable intention, or from the visible continuation of work while someone waits for a different response. Do not turn every exchange into a quip or make random interruption stand in for comic construction.\n\nPreserve required private experience as nonspoken authoring material in its active presentation, distinct from asides and monologues. Another character does not hear it merely because the reader has access to it. Supported offstage events and unfinished exchanges may remain in progress; the scene need not end on a completed joke or a gathering of all speakers.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite from the particular authorized mind, braiding its sensory particulars, remembered phrases, private words, bodily sensation, and imagined responses through its actual attention. Let a concrete perception orient the comic buildup: the mind notices an exact object, hears a familiar phrase, or anticipates a specific reply, and the association moves from there.\n\nLet a real line or event meet the expectation only as the mind receives it. Unheard content remains unknown within narrator access. The comedy can come from the gap between the private buildup and the actual continuation, but the thought need not be useful, witty, or destined for speech. It may remain lengthy, unrelated, or unfinished, and its represented extent does not advance external time.\n\nKeep events distinct from recollection and fantasy. A remembered occasion is not the present scene, and an imagined response is not what the other person said. Preserve each person's own habits and blind spots rather than making the focal mind universally perceptive or everyone else a straight man.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange in sender-attributed messages with only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene. Let each person's actual digital habits shape their contribution: one may answer in full words, another in fragments, another after a long pause.\n\nUse exact transmitted words and actual channel timing to create comic timing. A literal reply, a resumed phrase, or a familiar pattern can meet an actual intention without an invented typo, deletion, outage, or misunderstanding. The comedy can come from what a person chooses to send, what they leave unsent, or the interval between messages.\n\nDistinguish a sent message from an unsent draft and private thought from transmitted text. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text, distinct from the writer's live private thought. Required actual private experience remains in its own active nonspoken presentation. Preserve narrator and interface visibility: another participant does not know a draft or a private reaction merely because the reader sees it. Context outside the exchange may appear when required, clearly separated from the transcript. The scene may end with a pending reply or a completed exchange without a forced cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Visual Novel\n\nWrite the scene with concise setting and readable dialogue attribution. Establish the actual aim and conditions through exact objects, practical activities, and direct speech. Let a background or expression cue orient the moment without making it an automatic truth indicator.\n\nWhere a cue draws authorized attention, render the focal character's full interpretation or feeling. Then let an actual line or continuation meet that private expectation at the scene's own pace. The comic timing can come from an accurate reading, a plain answer, or a still moment meeting the buildup. Do not turn the cast into fixed reaction portraits whose expression always reveals what they think or whether they lie.\n\nDo not transfer private thought automatically through reader access; preserve what other characters actually know through established sources or capabilities. The style does not activate branching menus, romance routes, images, or executable engine commands. The scene may stop on a completed exchange, a pending request, or an unfinished private response without a forced choice screen or a final punch line.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacafbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Ernest Hemingway / Web Serial\n\nWrite the installment with a readable through-line: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover a relevant earlier aim, phrase, or response briefly when needed, preserving the actual history and each person's knowledge. Do not retell the whole investigation or routine that first established it.\n\nLet repetition confirm a familiar pattern or alter its context through real events. A repeated exact particular can sustain comic continuity without requiring escalating mishaps or a changed meaning at every return. The same phrase may mean the same thing again, or a new condition may give it a different weight. Preserve earlier facts rather than replacing them for a joke.\n\nRequired private experience may continue without a decision or advance. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known. Serial duration should give selected facts room to acquire meaning, not make a payoff perpetually recede or force a correction, reveal, or finished joke at every boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene around a person who has a concrete, recognizable reason to ask for something, admit something, or return to someone, whose actual reasons, feeling or self-image give the matter personal weight; no completed private argument or rehearsal is required. Keep that account specific to the person's actual certainty or conflict: it can defend the person, accuse them, reverse their own conclusion, or entangle their self-image with the material circumstances they are actually in. Do not flatten this into a single anxious mood. Give the actual thought or direct feeling its needed extent, whether plain, sustained, settled or conflicted, but it must remain this character's thought, not a general essay on need.\n\nWhere another participant actually responds, follow that reception; the matter may also remain privately anticipated or await an answer. A difference of scale, kind or timing between private anticipation and actual reception can support a comic turn without requiring one in the current interval. The response can be accurate, generous, practical, or even aligned with the asker's hope; it does not need to be cruel or dismissive to be funny. Show the asker's private reaction to that response, including the possibility that the elaborate self-argument continues, stalls, or becomes irrelevant. Let the difficulty of needing remain real even when the answer is easy. The person may still feel the cost of exposure, the fear of being seen as weak, or the shame of having prepared so much for so little. Do not resolve this into a punch line that erases the feeling.\n\nKeep the comedy in the arrangement of expectation and reception, not in making the asker a fool. The form does not assign the other participant as a moral judge or comic foil; their actual role remains, and they have their own aims and habits. If their answer is generous, let it be generous in a way that fits them, not a generic comfort. If they misunderstand, let the misunderstanding come from their own actual attention, not from a forced plot device. The scene may end with the matter still privately anticipated, the request unspoken, or the admission made and received with awkward partial comfort. No confession, failed request, self-denunciation, or repaired mood is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable narrative paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution. Keep each speaker's actions, movements, and spoken lines clearly separated so the reader always knows who is doing what. Enter the scene cleanly: establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and what each one is pursuing in this moment. Comic construction can come from the relation among recognizable aims and actual reception, including shared aims, not from random interruption or a string of quips.\n\nWhere an actual character holds a personally important reason, assumption or self-description, let it meet the exchange without inventing a protective self-image for the comic role. This can be a defended moral position, a private theory about what the other person wants, or a self-image they are trying to protect. Render it in the person's actual spoken or nonspoken channel at its needed extent; it need not be expressed aloud. Follow any response from another participant's actual priority without requiring a new reply or completed comic turn. That priority can be practical, affectionate, annoyed, or simply different; it does not need to be a counter-argument. Comic recognition can arise when an actual account meets another person's concrete concern, preserving each person's own coherence or conflict rather than requiring elaborate reasoning.\n\nPreserve the active narrator, player ownership, and NPC pursuits. Follow the exact active ownership contract: leave human-owned material open where required, and render only the speech, actions or experience actually supplied or delegated. NPCs should continue their own practical lives and make choices that fit their established aims. Agreement and competent help are as available as dispute. The other person is not a comic foil or a moral judge by default.\n\nInclude the full selected nonspoken experience for the authorized viewpoint character. This is actual private reasoning, sensation, or feeling, not a summary. It may align with what the character says, or it may differ for a character-specific reason such as embarrassment, attention, or uncertainty. Do not make everyone perfectly perceptive or uniformly witty.\n\nEnd at the active boundary, including within continuing private experience or public activity, without requiring a pause or new direction. The reader should be able to see what is underway and what remains available to continue. No dilemma, question, or completed joke is required at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and satirical realism route. Ground the scene in an actual household, workplace, or community arrangement with concrete practical terms: who does what work, who receives what benefit, who owes what to whom, and how these terms are maintained. Do not invent a corrupt institution or a stereotyped social role to make the satire easy. The arrangement should be specific enough that the reader can see how these people actually live with it.\n\nUse a moral claim or self-description that actually belongs to a participant or the permitted account of the arrangement, rather than assigning a new justification to a comic role. The claim can be grand, modest or general, with its actual speaker, scope and grounds clear. A person may defend, accuse or reconsider through it where that belongs to their actual reasoning. Place it beside the arrangement's concrete distribution: action, a request, private consideration or a supported narrative comparison can show its relation to who does the work or receives the benefit. No new public use or personal justification is required.\n\nFollow any other participant's actual practical emphasis, with its accuracy or uncertainty grounded in their sources rather than assigned by plainness. The comic contrast comes from the difference in scale or emphasis between the grand justification and the small sincere preference it actually serves, or between two different accurate accounts of what is fair. The other character is not necessarily wrong, corrupt, or a moral judge. They may agree with the principle and still notice that the immediate question is who carries the box or who gets the extra portion.\n\nKeep all judgment attributed to a specific character or narrator. Do not grant a verdict automatic truth through narrative tone; preserve any broader judgment actually supported by the active narrator's knowledge and permissions. Preserve the supplied facts and the full private feeling of the viewpoint character. The private argument may be elaborate and serious even when the public exchange is funny. No hypocrisy, public exposure, or collapse of conviction is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Use concrete verbs, exact physical positions, and material details that show the actual task: the grip, the weight, the expense, the wear, the sound of the work. Do not pad the action with explanatory transitions. Let the physical stakes be real; if there is danger or cost, make its actual terms concrete without requiring a visible bodily effect.\n\nRender any actual private argument or direct feeling about what the person is doing or considering, without inventing a philosophical speech for the author label. This argument can be long enough to show the character defending, accusing, or reversing their own position. Its extent follows the person's actual reasoning or feeling rather than a requirement to be long or brief, and it may be serious even when the surrounding action is small. Where the actual sequence puts this account beside bodily work, a hard rhythmic transition can make their scale comic; preserve their actual order and allow either to remain unfinished. The comic effect comes from the rhythmic contrast: the elaborate moral self-account meets the grip, the effort, the expense, or the practical response of the task. A competent act may vindicate the claim in an unexpectedly literal way.\n\nDo not invent punishment or foolishness to force the joke; preserve actual mistakes, harm or competence when the scene supports them. The physical work is not there to humiliate the person or to prove that their conviction is empty. The character may complete the task well, badly, or with mixed results, but the outcome follows from the actual circumstances. Preserve real danger or wear without inventing either for the joke. Do not require universal profanity, humiliation, or a collapse of conviction. The private argument may persist, change, or become irrelevant after the physical turn; let the character's actual state determine that.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the document form the scene actually requires: a letter, a journal entry, a report, a note, a record, or another supported form. Do not rotate through all available formats for variety; each record exists because someone in the situation would compose it.\n\nUse a document's actual selection and emphasis to carry the account at a comic scale. Where its writer composes an argument about motives or a self-description, shape it for that actual recipient and purpose rather than requiring self-justification in an ordinary record. They may defend themselves, accuse someone, reverse their own position, or entangle their self-image with the material circumstances. The composition should show the writer's choices: what they include, what they omit, how they frame the event. This is a composed artifact, not the complete living mind of the writer.\n\nWhen a later actual document responds, follow what it takes up; a reply can remain pending rather than being manufactured to finish the pattern. The response can accept the account, ask a practical question, or simply use the information for its own purpose. The comic scale comes from what the next document does with the first one: an accurate practical concern can answer an elaborate moral account without being a counter-argument. The form does not assign the later writer as a moral judge or comic foil; their actual role remains, and they have their own aim and their own reason for writing.\n\nKeep original knowledge, delivery, and reception separate. What the first writer knew at the time of composition is not changed by what the second writer later says. A document may be delayed, misread, or answered in a way the first writer did not anticipate. Do not force a contradiction or an exposure. Preserve required nonspoken interiority separately at its warranted extent; it is not replaced by the documents. No completed exchange or confessional letter is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nBuild the comic occasion around an actual aim in offering, asking for or receiving care. Make the accommodation particular: a known preference, a place to rest, an ordinary task taken over or a small choice someone wants respected. An elaborate personal account of what care means can meet the exact practical thing offered, but a direct request and an accurate, considerate answer can also be funny through timing or scale.\n\nWhere a person actually holds a conviction about duty, independence or accepting help, give that private experience its needed extent. It can be settled, conflicted, affectionate or absent from attention; do not supply a defensive theory of deservingness simply to create the comic contrast. A specific act may confirm the person's account, bypass it or leave it pending without humiliation or compulsory self-correction.\n\nKeep the provider's sincere aim distinct from the recipient's actual response. Care can be welcomed, declined, partly comforting or met with indifference; no pleasure or practical benefit is guaranteed. Render actual nonspoken experience under the active narrator's permissions rather than requiring gratitude, confession or a spoken debate. The situation may continue with ordinary pleasure or serious concerns intact, and the active boundary can fall before a response or payoff without a repaired mood or forced bitter turn.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Compose the scene in broader paragraphs that can hold an attributed claim within its social and material context. Use the narrative distance the chosen narrator permits to frame the scene through social context, history, or judgment. This framing is not a neutral summary; it is the narrator's own way of placing the characters and their concerns. Distinguish the narrator's voice from the cast's spoken language. Do not impose ornate cast speech or a period setting; the characters speak as themselves.\n\nDevelop an actual attributed conviction or personal account within the active narrator's access, without assigning a new belief or required speech to a character. This account can defend, accuse, reverse, or entangle itself. It may be long enough to show the character's reasoning in full, and it may be interrupted, resumed, or left unfinished. The formal architecture allows the argument to occupy real space without turning every character into a philosopher. A plain speaker may unsettle an elaborate argument simply by answering it.\n\nWhere an actual reply or response arrives, make its relation to the argument legible, including confirmation of an unchanged account. The response need not wait for the argument to finish or produce a new interpretation. The reply can be a single sentence, a small action, or a concrete observation. It does not need to refute the argument or appoint a correct moral voice. The comic recognition comes from the relation between the sustained account and the practical response. The plain answer may confirm, bypass, or complicate the elaborate account; it is not automatically correct because it is plain. Preserve established truth and reliability. Do not require a moral verdict or a punch line per paragraph. Supported judgment remains available to the narrator, but the scene does not need to end by deciding who was right.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nLocate a teller, actual listeners and a present purpose for the account, including explanation, persuasion, company or entertainment. Let the embedded incident carry a conviction or self-image where it belongs to the teller's actual selection and emphasis. The account can be candid and accurate, or reveal an unintended emphasis within permitted knowledge; no self-accusation, decisive past choice or polished moral lesson must be supplied.\n\nKeep the source and narrative levels clear. Recount what earlier participants actually did, knew and felt according to the teller's sources, distinguishing later learning from earlier motives. The active narrator need not be the teller or past participant. A listener's actual practical concern can meet an elaborate interpretation with comic difference of timing or scale, while aligned understanding or sincere help may serve the sequence equally well. No audience response or changed relationship is required to justify the telling.\n\nPreserve actual private experience on both levels in the selected nonspoken channel at its needed extent. Participants retain prior knowledge, independent sources and permitted access rather than learning everything through the performed account or reader disclosure. The outer occasion can continue, pause or remain pending as events warrant; the active boundary may fall inside the telling or reception without an interruption, new obstacle, completed argument or forced comic result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modernist absurdist route for this scene, but ground it in the present situation rather than porting in any German-language national setting. Establish a precise principle, definition or rule actually available to a participant or the permitted narrator, without inventing a self-justifying belief for the role. Show it as attributed or genuinely known, not as narrator's mockery. Where the person actually defends or formulates the principle, render their own reasoning: they may anticipate objections, reverse positions, or entangle themselves because the stakes feel real.\n\nThen follow what actually applying that principle would mean here. Work out the concrete implication step by step: what follows from the premise here for this task and these people. Distinguish a predicted consequence, obligation or physical necessity, and do not make the premise itself compel a character to comply. Do not insert arbitrary obstruction, a failed system, or a bureaucratic trap. The comedy comes from the disproportion between the principle's moral weight and its unexpectedly modest, literal, or useful application. A character may discover that their elaborate definition requires them to do a small exact thing, or that their grand rule is satisfied by an ordinary act.\n\nLet other characters have their own practical concerns. They may accept the principle but notice a different next step, or apply it to their own aim. Their actual responses may support reversal, escalation, confirmation or continued setup without requiring a comic turn or making them debating abstractions. Preserve the actual world facts, setting, and character agency: no automatic absurdism that cancels causality or makes everyone a mouthpiece.\n\nFinally, distinguish anticipated implications from events that actually occur. A character's self-argument may predict disaster, but the scene may deliver a modest outcome. Keep the actual fit or mismatch legible and the world consequential, preserving effects when they occur as well as unchanged conditions or an untested premise. No new option is owed at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Gothic-Punk\n\nLet the material place carry Gothic-Punk texture without importing vampire rules or supernatural powers. Describe the actual place or arrangement in concrete detail, without requiring an urban location, room or decay: worn surfaces, old fixtures, markers of privilege or decay, the physical signs of inherited authority. Use this atmosphere to shape how the present encounter feels, not as evidence of hidden corruption.\n\nUse a moral account or self-description that actually belongs to the person's relation to the place, without assigning a new conviction for the style. Develop it through Dostoevsky's self-argument: they may defend a principle, accuse themselves of a past failure, reverse a judgment, or entangle their own motives. Preserve actual private experience in its selected nonspoken channel and any separately spoken account the person chooses to give. Sincerity, defensiveness or an alibi follows actual character and evidence rather than a requirement of the style.\n\nThen let that elaborate account meet the exact practical matter of the scene: who has access, what care is expected, what small preference or convenience is at stake. The inherited authority (an old rule, a respected tradition, a family expectation) may operate competently and benignly. The comic scale arises when the character's moral self-description encounters a literal, practical, or affectionate reality. For example, a person agonizing over protocol may be offered tea; a grand defense of duty may collapse into explaining why they are late.\n\nAllow private desire and social masks to remain intact. A mask need not fall; a secret need not be exposed. The contrast between public explanation and private practical life is enough. Preserve actual knowledge and motives: reader exposure to a private thought does not itself inform another character; actual sources and permitted access remain available. End at the active boundary, including while thought, setup or an exchange remains underway. Comic disproportion need not alter the interaction or produce a confession, unmasking or trap.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the testimonial and autobiographical route for this scene without relocating the cast or imposing a generic Indian family, religion, or community. Present an attributed account from an actual source: a character who can bear witness to the event, a found document, or a structured retelling. Fit this account to the active narrator: if the narrator is a witness, show their own participation and private experience; if the narrator is receiving a testimony, keep their access distinct from the teller's claimed memory.\n\nInside the testimony, separate what the person believed, did, and received at the earlier time from what they now say. They may defend, reconsider or plainly report an original motive or self-image according to their actual experience, but do not let present knowledge retroactively become earlier knowledge. They may honestly explain why a decision seemed necessary, while the narrated actions reveal a more modest or practical reality.\n\nWhere the account meets a present response, follow that actual reception; it may also await an answer or remain unfinished. A listener, reader, or the narrator themselves may respond with a practical qualification: an exact question, a plain answer, an offer of help, or a different emphasis. Comic scale can arise from the actual relation between moral explanation and reception, including two accurate accounts without requiring the recipient to be simpler or correct by default. The teller need not realize the full irony; the audience's understanding can create the comic effect.\n\nPreserve language, community, and private experience as supplied by the world, not as national scenery. Let the scene use the testimonial form for an actual purpose—such as answering what happened or choosing to set a record straight—without forcing a confession or a completed lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse social and psychological realism for this scene, without adding a diary format or forcing a Japanese setting, language pattern, or seasonal inventory. Keep the present encounter fully recoverable: show the actual people, location, and what is said or done. Ground the scene in ordinary social interaction—a request, an offer, a remark, a small task—so readers can track what literally happens.\n\nFocus on one permitted character's interpretation of the exchange. Render that person's private reasoning through Dostoevsky's self-argument: they may justify their own expectation, accuse themselves of assuming too much, reverse their reading of a phrase, or entangle old memories with current words. Let actual self-image, conviction or direct attention shape the interpretation where it belongs, without inventing a theory or universal overthinking. Show the specific reason why this particular exchange matters to them.\n\nFollow the actual public and private sequence as another person responds, without making a full inner argument a required prelude. They may be more practical, literal, or indifferent than the focal character expected—or they may be unexpectedly aligned. Comic scale can come from the actual relation between interpretation and reception: elaborate expectation may meet a simple answer, or accurate anticipation may meet precisely timed agreement. Neither response nor private account is automatically reasonable or required to change. Do not add an insult, hidden guilt or misunderstanding merely to force comedy; preserve what actual character and circumstances support; the other person can be entirely sincere.\n\nKeep private knowledge separate from interpersonal knowledge. Reader exposure does not itself communicate a thought to another participant; preserve actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access. Preserve chronology and the supplied setting. End at the active boundary, including during continued reception or private thought. Interpretation may remain stable or change when actually supported, without a completed payoff, confession or emotional collapse.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse polyphonic nonlinear telling for this scene. Do not add magical realism or a supernatural fact unless the world already permits it. Instead, arrange several permitted accounts or points of view around the same event or exchange, clearly marked by speaker, time, or source. These voices can appear as direct narrative, remembered speech, written notes, or shifts of focalization, as long as attribution is unmistakable.\n\nLet each voice carry its actual concern, with conviction, self-image or inner argument where they belong rather than a required debate in every mind. One character may defend a grand moral reason; another may recall only the practical inconvenience; a third may be genuinely indifferent. Make each account legible as its owner's actual experience, coherent or conflicted according to the person rather than a strawman or a forced contradiction. Do not merge minds or grant one voice authority by placing it last; all accounts can be accurate but differently scaled.\n\nJuxtapose these accounts nonlinearly if it serves the scene: a later practical result can appear before the elaborate motive that preceded it, or two simultaneous reactions can interrupt each other. Preserve established facts and any known correct interpretation. The comedy arises from the deliberate contrast of scales—the same event is a crisis in one mind and an errand in another—while each account remains legitimate.\n\nMaintain full private experience under the actual permissions of the fiction. If the active contract prohibits access to a mind, use only an attributed external account. Let the arrangement span replies, with the active boundary able to fall within one account before every voice or comic relation is complete. Preserve source and event orientation without a required consensus or verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Light Novel\n\nOrient the actual people, location and aim with the context needed to follow the exchange. A personal conviction or self-description may establish an expectation, but it must belong to the person or permitted narrative account rather than being assigned as a philosophical role. Introduce an unfamiliar circumstance through actual observation, dialogue or authorized explanation without requiring a character to ask for information they already know.\n\nUse paragraph breaks where speaker, action or focal response needs separation. A developed moral account can meet a literal answer, competent act or ordinary preference with comic scale; accurate agreement can be as apt as a supported difference. Let placement make the relation readable without a prescribed break immediately after a reply or a reaction paragraph owed next. The actual public and private sequence may put thought before, during or after speech.\n\nGive authorized reasoning, feeling and sensation their needed extent in the selected nonspoken channel, without a limited block or immediate action payoff. Speakers retain their own registers and knowledge, and the active narrator's person and role remain unchanged. The scene can stop within setup, continuing thought or an unanswered exchange, as well as after a result. No new practical change, stable pause, joke, corrective reaction or cliffhanger is required before the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Lyrical Prose\n\nDevelop a scene-born image, sound or association through syntax and recurrence within the active narrator's permissions. It may carry a character's actual moral self-description or the permitted narrator's comparison, with the source clear: a duty remembered as a weight, a debt imaged as a thread, a name that gathers accusation or affection. Let repetition and variation shape the sentence, with relevant explanation available where the actual account needs it, so the image becomes the argument's body. Place the image beside a plain practical detail from the actual world when the sequence supports it—a shelf that needs straightening, a receipt, a faint smell from the next room—and let the prose carry the juxtaposition through implication or a direct connection the permitted account can make. The detail need not refute the image; it may merely occupy a different scale, and their juxtaposition can carry comic tension whether the participant notices it or not; preserve the actual source, attention and degree of conviction without requiring equal weight in one mind.\n\nKeep the character's actual moral voice present: the image may accuse, defend, entangle, or reverse itself in the rhythm of phrases. If the person argues with himself, let the syntax turn on itself rather than summarizing the argument. A plain utterance from another character can meet the shaped expectation without having to deliver a witty deflation or a moral verdict. The other speaker's diction remains their own; do not absorb them into the narrator's figurative register. Preserve full private experience: associations actually experienced by a character remain their own, not shared with others merely through reader exposure. Their actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access remain available.\n\nDo not require the image to fail or the person to be mocked. The practical encounter may produce laughter, mild discrepancy, or an intensified sense of the image's importance; the ending remains contingent. If warmth or fellow-feeling appears, it can coexist with the image's pressure. Avoid manufacturing guilt for ornament and avoid forcing a final deflation. A comparison may unfold when it changes how the narrator understands the situation, but it does not have to resolve into a lesson.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nConnect an actual present detail to an earlier conviction, exchange, or unresolved attachment with clear temporal orientation. Show the now-familiar item, phrase, or gesture as it appears in the present scene, then let its remembered weight surface through association: the character may recall the earlier argument, the occasion when the conviction felt urgent, or the unfinished feeling that still accompanies it. Use direct reflection or let the detail carry the memory without announcing its significance. Keep the boundary clear between what was understood then and what is known now; later knowledge may add resonance but must not retroactively grant innocence or erase prior understanding.\n\nAllow the former grand argument to recur through a modest habit or ordinary use. The comic tension emerges when the once-weighty justification attaches to a small current aim—returning a borrowed object, choosing a meal, waiting for a reply—without requiring the character to renounce the original attachment. Humor and longing may coexist; the weight need not be debunked, and the present use need not diminish it. The character may notice the disproportion himself or remain absorbed in the feeling; either is valid.\n\nPreserve the accuracy actually present in the memory. Locate any actual error at the time the person held it; mark later recognition by its actual sources rather than inventing a past or present mistake for comic effect. If the memory was partial or uncertain, keep that uncertainty. Do not manufacture loss, moral conversion or a lesson on impermanence merely for the boundary; actual developments remain governed by the people and circumstances. Private reflection may be direct, extensive, or unfinished; it does not have to prove its worth through action, and no final statement of what the memory means is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction: follow a concrete aim through a sequence of supported encounters, each arising from the character's actual situation and world conditions, without requiring resistance. The aim may be material, social, or personal—returning something, making a claim, finishing an errand—but keep it recognizable as it is pursued, paused or legitimately changed according to the person. In each encounter, let the character bring whatever convictions or self-justifications actually belong to them: an elaborate moral argument, a defensive reason, a habit of self-accusation. Do not announce the satire; let the disproportion between the justification and the practical concerns of the encounter appear through action and dialogue.\n\nStage the meeting of perspectives: another person may have a different practical concern, agree sincerely, understand accurately, misunderstand for an actual reason, or offer straightforward help. The comic effect comes from the coexistence of scales, not from everyone being a fool or a cheat. When the character explains themselves, show what the listener actually does with the explanation—asks a practical question, offers a small kindness, continues their work—following any actual effect on the person's course without requiring a changed trajectory or moral duel. Keep authorized private experience at its full needed extent without entering an excluded mind: their reasons, doubts, and self-argument can be rendered at their actual place before, during or between exchanges at their needed extent.\n\nPreserve local circumstances and knowledge exactly as supplied. A plain speaker may unsettle an elaborate argument without becoming the story's moral authority. Do not impose travel, a trickster identity, universal deceit, endless obstruction, or a new moral dilemma at every encounter. The active boundary can fall within continued thought, an encounter or a pending aim, as well as after completion. No natural pause, fresh practical step or confrontation is owed.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect exact details and use direct clauses with economical transitions to make an actual claim and practical circumstance legible. Preserve their actual order and any relevant direct explanation rather than requiring inference alone or a completed claim-then-response pair. Where the selected form permits summary, condense redundant framing rather than actual speech or private experience whose full extent matters. Keep what the person actually expresses and the causal connection to any response intact. A meaningful private argument or a genuine feeling may take its required length—do not cut it to a slogan.\n\nKeep the actual claim and response close enough for their comic relation to be readable without duplicated commentary. Preserve whether the claim is spoken or private and when the response actually occurs; a practical particular can provide the other scale: closing a drawer, handing back exact change, repeating a practical question. The narration need not repeat that the scales differ, while relevant explanation remains available; the juxtaposition does the work. The other character may not understand the claim, may understand it fully, or may simply address a different need; plain speech has no automatic moral authority. Do not make them a mute foil.\n\nDo not erase causes, actual expression, or character privacy. If the character retreats into thought, show that thought's particular turn rather than a summary. Do not impose clipped cast speech, a silence quota, or a deflating last line. The ending can remain unresolved; the comic recognition may occur for the reader while the character continues to hold the claim sincerely.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove naturally among activity, speech, perception, recollection, and authorized inward experience while the character pursues a current aim. Introduce the conviction as a thought that belongs to the moment—an actual reason, conviction or direct feeling that may be plain or self-justifying in the character's mind as they look for something, wait, or begin to explain. Let it be present in the scene's texture: the character may rehearse the argument while handling an object, or address a line to themselves in the middle of an exchange. Do not insert a detached lecture merely to display the author label; actual extended speech or inward argument retains its needed extent; let the argument emerge in relation to the immediate material circumstance.\n\nWhere another person actually responds, let that reception meet the argument; a response may remain pending and private anticipation need not become spoken. The other character may have their own practical concern, agree in a way that scales the argument differently, or simply offer help that bypasses the self-definition. The comic disproportion arises because the character's inner architecture faces a plain actuality—not because one of them is wrong. A plain answer may confirm, qualify, or bypass the argument without deciding its moral worth. The other person's diction and purpose remain their own; they are not a device for judging the protagonist.\n\nKeep the reasoning available after speech if the character continues to think. Private thought may extend the argument, notice the disproportion, or remain untouched by it; do not force a reversal, confession, or lesson. Preserve individual diction, meaningful feeling, and established facts. The scene can end at the active boundary, including within continued thought, speech or activity, without owing a joke, a changed conviction, or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse the supplied relationship premise as the source of scene selection and callbacks. Choose an occasion and situation that can make the actual participants' particular bond legible: what they do together, how they address each other, what they return to. A familiar justification—a repeated moral argument, a habit of self-description, a shared practical accommodation—can recur in a new circumstance. The comedy comes from the specific reception this time, not from a generic misunderstanding: one may answer with affectionate accuracy, the other may offer a serious reply along a different line, or both may attach different supported meanings to the same phrase.\n\nPreserve each character's independent understanding through the sources and private access the active contract actually permits, without opening an excluded mind for symmetry. Do not turn the bond into a mutual commentary machine or force banter. When a callback occurs, follow its actual reception or non-reception within those permissions: one may feel the obligation, the other may remember the earlier occasion differently, and both may be right. Give affection, rivalry, jealousy, or comfort full space where it belongs to the supplied premise—these are not interruptions of the comedy but its material. If the bond is new or the history thin, do not invent a shared shorthand; let a first-time act or response matter in the present without pretending to a longer past.\n\nPreserve private knowledge and established history: what each character knows, what they feel, and what they have actually done together must remain consistent. Do not impose a trope roster, confession, repaired bond or status change merely from the style; any actual supported development can stand. The active boundary can fall during thought or an unfinished exchange, as well as after a conclusion or when a practical step is available. No emotional payoff is owed here.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nUse deliberate cadence, compact declarations, and grounded recurrence to give actual deeds a retold shape. Give a deed a compact place in the retold account: who did it and how an actual source remembers or judges it. An attributed declaration can establish moral scale without inventing a reputation or requiring a fixed opening. Keep the declaration attributed—someone says this, or the chronicle voice distinguishes between the teller's claim and the known event. Then expand into the practical circumstances: the exact room, the object handled, the unremarkable effort. The comic contrast arises because the remembered moral statement and the practical act occupy different scales, but the act remains consequential in the community's account. Do not impose mockery or empty bombast merely to display the style; preserve actual speech and judgment where supported; the person may have acted competently and the praise may be deserved.\n\nA recurring designation should come from an established deed or way of speaking, not from an ornamental epithet assigned at every entrance. Return to a phrase for actual remembered emphasis, familiar repetition or a supported new application, without requiring changed meaning. If a reputation is offered, show the person living with it in private or in dialogue: their own justification may be more elaborate, more self-doubting, or simpler than the public verdict. Distinguish original motive, later telling, and private experience according to the active access. The subject of an oft-told incident may object to a detail or answer an elevated introduction with a practical concern; this need not become parody.\n\nDo not assign a collective verdict on the story's behalf or require a fixed opening formula. The active boundary can fall within ongoing telling, thought or business, as well as after conclusion or resumption. No next act must become available to be remembered. No completed moral judgment is owed at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite social and domestic realism: let the practical terms of an arrangement appear through concrete work, shared space, care, or time. When someone gives an elaborate moral account of fairness or independence, place it beside the exact preference or task that these people actually follow. Comic scale can come from the account meeting the practical life around it, whether their relation is a mismatch or accurate correspondence; agreement and competent cooperation may remain. Keep private reasoning and feeling separate from public explanation: the narrator may show a character's inward defense or self-accusation alongside another person's actual activity or reception, without requiring usefulness or opening an excluded mind. Reader exposure does not itself communicate thought; actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access remain. Use the specific setting, language, and community as supplied. Do not shrink the situation, relocate the cast, or invent hypocrisy to force a joke. The arrangement can stay unchanged, with recognition present, absent or still developing at the active boundary; the comedy lies in how people live with it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Screenplay\n\nWrite in screenplay form: brief action lines, speaker-attributed dialogue, and scene headings only when location or time changes. Place an actual moral account, spoken or privately experienced, beside the relevant practical circumstance in the selected channels; do not assign a justification where none belongs. A cut, held beat or action within the same scene can make their scale readable: an actual principle may meet an ordinary task, accurate agreement or pending response. Preserve actual timing and location rather than requiring a new scene. Let the juxtaposition support comedy without inventing a mishap or corrective line to force it; actual mistakes and corrections remain available. Preserve spatial and knowledge continuity; keep voices individual and truth intact. Write required private argument as full attributed nonspoken material in the active presentation; do not turn it automatically into voice-over. No public payoff, confession, or completed scene is required at the response boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stage Play\n\nWrite a playable script with clear speakers and economical stage directions. Block real entrances, props, and tasks. Where an actual attributed moral argument develops, place it in the playable space with the other participants' own activities and responses. Simultaneous or sequential scales can support comedy through accurate agreement, literal concern or a supported difference, without assigning a philosophical speaker and a practical helper or forcing contradiction. Keep each voice distinct and do not let gestures read as proof of hidden motive. Place full nonspoken argument and feeling as separate attributed material under the active presentation, never forced into monologues or asides. The scene can end mid-speech, mid-task, or mid-thought without a laugh cue or final verdict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Stream of Consciousness\n\nWrite inside one authorized mind. Braid perception, memory, bodily sensation, and private argument as they actually arise. Let a defended self-image lead by association to a practical detail or let an ordinary feeling interrupt the argument; the comedy comes from the disproportion between the mental claim and the concrete sensation or memory, not from a witty reversal. Distinguish memory, imagination, and current reception clearly. Observe separately authorized access to other minds. Thought can be long, unresolved, directly affectionate, or cut short; there is no guilt quota and no fictional-time charge based on prose length.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nWrite the exchange as sender-attributed messages with only the interface events that matter. Follow the actual scale and purpose of a composed or sent account, including an elaborate explanation where it belongs; if another message arrives, follow what it takes up: an accurate agreement, a simple practical need, or a question about a detail. Sequencing and the relation between an account and its reception can carry comic scale, including accurate alignment or a still-pending answer. Do not invent misunderstanding or a hidden-motive revelation to force it. Keep composition, transmission, and receipt distinct. An unsent draft is composed text, not live private thought; write any required private experience separately as full attributed nonspoken material. Do not manufacture deletions, delays or confessions for the format, while preserving actual supported events; the exchange can stop before a message is sent or received.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Visual Novel\n\nWrite concise scene-setting, attributed dialogue, and focal narration around a specific illustrated cue: a background detail, object, or expression. Use the cue to place an actual expectation or private account beside the relevant visible circumstance. The account may be simple, elaborate or absent from attention; comic scale can lie in timing, accurate fulfillment or a supported difference without a required pivot or response. The active boundary may fall within cue reception or continuing thought. Render inward material at its needed length and actual position, without a fixed cue-thought-dialogue rhythm. A face can attract attention but does not prove shame, deception, or agreement; keep supported prior knowledge available. Preserve narrator and ownership. Do not add images, branching menus, romance routes, or a forced final reaction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacagbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Fyodor Dostoevsky / Web Serial\n\nWrite each installment as part of a continuous story. When a familiar moral argument or practical condition returns, recover just enough of the earlier scene to keep continuity clear. Let any callback carry actual emphasis, an unchanged concern, a pending attempt, a successful response or a supported new scale, but do not forget what the characters previously learned or accepted. Private feeling matters beyond the joke; keep narrator access unchanged. Do not manufacture a fresh disagreement or force escalating self-accusation. The installment may end during continuing thought, ordinary work, or an unfinished setup; no cliffhanger or confession is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nLet a difficult exchange begin inside a recognizable practical aim: someone needs a favor, an answer, an apology, or a return to a shared task. Bring a remembered phrase, family saying, or public account into that exchange through an actual source, and show what this person expects it to do. Arrange the relation between the phrase's gathered history and its precise present use for comic recognition, whether their fit is exact or their scale differs. An accurate literal reply, a familiar domestic habit, or a practical objection can make the repetition funny while the need behind it remains costly.\n\nDevelop the actual private experience of the exchange at its needed extent, independently of the amount spoken. A character may repeat the phrase to buy time, to avoid naming what they want, or because the phrase is the only shared language left for the difficulty. Give the cost of admitting or withholding its particular spoken, nonspoken or practical expression where it actually occurs; an unchanged action or unexpressed feeling can carry it. Direct feeling is welcome in the person's own diction without a generic vocabulary standing in for experience. Warmth and pain may coexist without one canceling the other.\n\nPreserve what was understood at the earlier time. The phrase does not need to be exposed as false, newly reinterpreted, or made to mean the opposite of what it once meant. A shared understanding can remain intact and still be funny in its new application. Do not require a confession, a misunderstanding, an emotional repair, or a complete resolution at the boundary. The scene may end while the difficult return is still underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Chat Roleplay\n\nStage the interval through clear paragraphs and unmistakable dialogue attribution. Let each participant pursue a recognizable current aim. When a familiar name, phrase, or account belongs in the interval, bring it through an actual source: someone heard it from a relative, repeats it as a family rule, or applies it to the task at hand. The callback may become comic in its current use or another person's actual reception, with the original occasion still meaningful; delivery and reception need not occur before the active boundary. An interval can also continue without introducing a recollection or callback. Agreement, competent help, or a practical correction can carry the comic relation as well as resistance can.\n\nUse a gathering sentence to connect the present to earlier occasions only within the narrator's access. It may recall the phrase's origin or the family habit that kept it alive without becoming a flashback or making public rumor shared knowledge. Keep what each person knows distinct from what the narration knows. The inherited formulation need not be false or newly exposed; its comic effect can come from an unexpectedly literal or practical application.\n\nPreserve player ownership, NPC activity, and full private prose under the active contract. An NPC may continue a task, offer help, or respond to the callback while pursuing their own life. The active boundary may stop while the exchange is still developing; it does not need to finish the callback, resolve the aim, or pose a question.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse vernacular episodic telling. Open directly on a particular occasion: who is present, what they want, and what practical business or social obligation brings them together. Develop the episode through direct narration, attributed speech, and the participants' actual conduct. Let a supported phrase, family designation, or remembered account connect this occasion with another known one when the connection is relevant, not as a decorative refrain.\n\nThe comic pattern comes from the recurrence itself. A familiar account may meet an unexpectedly literal application, a household saying may be used to settle a modest dispute, or a name may attach to someone who now performs a different role. The humor does not require a failed expectation or a revealed falsehood. Attribute the language to its source and preserve what happened each time; do not infer a collective narrator or communal verdict from the regional label, and preserve the active narrator's actual scope.\n\nKeep the actual setting, speech, and private experience intact. An episode may end while the practical matter is still underway. No completed episode, forced lesson, or single-reply resolution is required. A recurrence may deepen the sense of the people and their history or sustain a familiar joke; preserve its actual prior uses without requiring a new meaning.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nUse forceful verbs and compressed transitions to show the contact, effort, or material condition involved in a supported task. A circulating claim about that task can gather relevant earlier occasions before meeting a precise present detail: the weight of a box, a worn tool, a specific stain, or the actual number of trips required. The relation can create comic contrast or exact recognition without inventing a mismatch or forcing mockery; actual character mockery retains its supported place.\n\nPreserve actual wear or danger without inventing either to earn the contrast. If the work is difficult, show the difficulty; if the claim is accurate, show its accuracy. Keep direct experience, remembered speech, and rumor distinct. A character may recall who first said the phrase and what it meant then, without the narrator treating the recollection as proof of the present claim.\n\nAllow full inward feeling to develop beyond any practical use. Meaningful reasoning, feeling or association can develop at its actual extent without a length quota or action payoff. Do not require cast-wide profanity, injury, humiliation, or failure. The rhythm may shift between compressed physical action and a gathered public account, but the scene remains grounded in what people actually do.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Epistolary / Documents\n\nCarry the sequence through documents with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Trace a phrase, name, or account only through its actual circulation: a letter quotes a relative, a report records a public claim, a note applies an old family rule to a modest practical concern. Each document knows and conceals only what its writer would know or choose to preserve at that time.\n\nArrange the relation between the phrase's gathered weight and its later use for comic recognition; an exact fit or a difference of scale may carry it. A later record may accurately continue a familiar joke, apply a grand formulation to a small household matter, or copy an earlier phrase for a new purpose. Keep the copied formulation attributed to its original source rather than treating its circulation as a new firsthand experience. Preserve original wording and knowledge when later annotations appear; do not require contradiction, exposure, or a parade of document types.\n\nComposed records remain distinct from live nonspoken experience. A letter or journal contains what its author records, not automatically their complete mind. Keep required private experience in its actual extent under Hidden or Visible presentation, without replacing it with a document.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nGive chosen care and small pleasures room within the actual scene. Let a remembered phrase gather occasions of generosity or familiar inconvenience, then meet the present preference that makes the repetition apt, amusing, or simply recognizable. The callback can be affectionate without requiring a confession or an emotional repair. A person may appreciate the joke, miss it, or not care without losing the kindness of the exchange.\n\nShow the care through practical detail: a familiar food, a remembered route, a task done before being asked, or a private joke returned at the right moment. Attribute the phrase's history to the actual family memory, shared experience or other supported source. Represent the account faithfully, including any established errors or limits, without inventing them for a joke. Affection may be sincere while the surrounding problems remain real. Do not make everyone agreeable or remove established difficulty from the world.\n\nPreserve private feeling beyond the public story. The narration may show what a person enjoys or withholds without making the other characters know it. Individual habits, disagreement, and privacy stay intact. The scene may end in ease or in partial comfort; no improved mood or completed kindness is required at the boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraph movement and deliberate temporal transitions to connect the occasion with family memory, public accounts, or prior uses of a formulation, only within narrator access. A specific current act, reply, or private association can establish the comic relation without an added explanatory joke. The paragraph may gather the phrase's public history and then land on its precise present use, letting the scale of the two make the comedy recognizable.\n\nDistinguish inherited judgment from fact and private feeling from public reputation. Large praise may remain deserved; a phrase may be grand in origin and still apt in its new application. The comic effect does not require the phrase to be exposed as empty or its meaning reversed. Keep ordinary cast diction and the actual social world. Do not import ornate speech, an ancestry survey, or a completed historical verdict merely because the style is formal.\n\nThe active boundary may occur while the gathering continues, before any return outward. The scene can end inside the composed reflection or the present exchange, without a completed resolution. Preserve the narrator's distance and the characters' own voices.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nAnchor the scene in a specific occasion where someone tells or retells an account to listeners with their own situated interests, including enjoyment of the telling. Establish why this audience is hearing it now: to secure cooperation, explain a delay, defend a reputation, or make a request legible. Keep the outer scene's narrator and perspective intact; the teller's performance is an event within that scene, not a replacement for the active narrator.\n\nBuild the comic movement through selection and return. Let the teller's actual selection of a remembered phrase, family story or public version relate to their present purpose, whether deliberate or habitual. Give that embedded account enough concrete detail to create a recognizable expectation about a person, place, or outcome. Where the sequence returns to the outer scene, let any actual response follow the listeners' own concerns; the active boundary may also arrive during the telling or ongoing private experience. They may accept the story, apply it to a different practical matter, ask about a detail the teller considered unimportant, or simply continue what they were doing. Comic timing may arise from an exact fit or a gap between intended effect and actual reception. Preserve actual competence, foolishness or honesty without inventing a trait to supply a joke.\n\nKeep the levels of knowledge separate. The teller may omit something out of habit, loyalty, or incomplete understanding; the listeners may already know part of the account or interpret it accurately. A recurring phrase can connect the embedded story to the present without proving the story true or forcing a single meaning. Follow the active boundary during an unfinished tale, thought, listener response or ordinary business without requiring one to follow or finish another. Do not add a royal court, magical bargain, or compulsory interruption. The frame-tale form organizes the sequence; it does not require every embedded account to end with a cliffhanger or a laugh.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse documentary and historical-memory prose as the organizing route. When a record is actually available in the scene—a notice, registry entry, official letter, published account, or institutional file—present its wording with attention to its authorship, date, and purpose. Do not turn the record into the story's only voice; place it beside what an authorized character remembers or observes.\n\nUse the situated relation between a record's wording and remembered particulars for comic scale; agreement can carry it as readily as an actual contradiction. An official formulation may describe a public event, a family's standing, or a practical arrangement in broad or ceremonial terms. A character's memory can supply the precise domestic or bodily circumstance that accompanied it: the heat in the room, the errand that was interrupted, the relative who kept repeating the wrong name. Both accounts can be true. Their juxtaposition can carry humor without a discovery of error; preserve any actual errors or discoveries rather than requiring or excluding them.\n\nKeep the temporal and evidentiary layers distinct. A character's present knowledge may include what the record later made clear, and current recollection may carry that interpretation; distinguish it from what the participant actually knew or felt at the earlier time. Rumor, record, memory, and current thought remain separate sources. Preserve the active narrator and all authorized nonspoken experience beyond the document. The record may prompt reflection, but it does not replace a permitted character's live interiority or make the narrator its recorder. No investigation, secret, or compulsory contradiction is required. Let the scene follow the actual aim or routine that brought the record and the memory together.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Gothic-Punk\n\nGround the inherited story in the actual material setting, using urban detail where it belongs to the supplied place. If a place, family, title, or institution carries a remembered public meaning, show the physical details that carry that meaning now: a door that still sticks, a portrait hung above a copying machine, a rule written in old language on a laminated notice. Let a gathering sentence, actual exchange or developed atmospheric passage connect the historical formulation to this particular place and its occupants.\n\nBring that inherited story into contact with a present task, private preference, or practical access condition. A character may need a key, a signature, a room, or an exception. The comedy comes from the collision between the grand formulation and the ordinary need: the ceremony that delays a lunch, the privilege that complicates a simple errand, the reputation that must be worked around. Preserve the authority's actual competence or limitations and the claim's established truth or uncertainty. Do not invent humiliation, corruption, or unmasking as the required comic beat.\n\nLet atmosphere shape how these particular people experience the meeting. The room's weight, the smell of old paper, the echo of a corridor can make the contrast felt without supplying new supernatural rules or danger. Preserve the actual sources of the public story and the meaningful private desires that persist beyond it. A social presentation may change in trusted company or another supported voluntary or involuntary context; no such change is required here. Keep the exchange consequential, preserving what actually persists, changes or ends without requiring a particular result or a completed exchange at the active boundary.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nUse testimonial and autobiographical narration as the definite route. Keep the account situated in an actual participant or known source without forcing the active narrator into that role. If the narrator is not the testifying person, make the source's identity and relation to the events clear through the scene's own terms.\n\nArrange what circulated, what was experienced, and what the teller knows now with clear attribution and time. A widely repeated version may describe a public occasion, a family achievement, or a local scandal in broad terms. The teller's concrete experience can supply a smaller practical concern: who had to pay for the chairs, which relative arrived too early, what was said while the official speech was being prepared. Neither account has to be false. Arrange the relation between circulated language and experienced particulars for comic recognition. Accurate differences of emphasis can carry it without an exposure, while any actual error or discovery keeps its supported place.\n\nPreserve prior knowledge and present private feeling. The teller may already understand the comic gap or may recount it without amusement. Later knowledge must not rewrite what the teller knew at the earlier time. Keep the language, community, and period supplied by the scene; do not impose a generic family structure, confession, or lesson. The testimonial form organizes the telling, but the scene's actual characters and consequences remain the story.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nChoose social and psychological realism. Stay close to the actual occasion, habits, and authorized private attention of the person encountering a familiar saying, name, or account. When an earlier association actually enters attention, show its particular source: a family usage, a neighbor's explanation, a schoolroom memory, or a public event they witnessed. Keep the remembered association distinct from the current reception.\n\nA current perception, private association or next actual action or reply can give the phrase a different scale or confirm an enjoyable pattern. Earlier association and a next public response are not required before the active boundary. The person may hear the familiar words while doing something ordinary—paying for a ticket, waiting for a call, measuring a doorway—and the practical context may make the phrase's public weight comically disproportionate. Or the person may use the phrase deliberately and receive an accurate, unimpressed response. The comedy comes from the particular reception, not from a universal public verdict or a compulsory misunderstanding.\n\nKeep the person's real feeling intact beyond the comic use of the recurrence. The phrase may carry affection, irritation, grief, or pride, and that weight can coexist with the scene's humor. No diary artifact, quietness, or seasonal scenery is imposed. Preserve the active narrator and actual world facts, with full nonspoken experience for each mind to which access is authorized. The realism lies in the precise relation between the phrase's known history and the person's actual present experience, including unchanged or unfinished feeling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaham::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect polyphonic nonlinear telling. Move among permitted accounts and times through clear source markers: a remembered conversation, a public announcement, a family explanation, a later private reflection. Let a phrase, name, or concern connect them without making the connection explicit in every section. The reader should recognize the recurrence through the material itself.\n\nRender each account's actual scale and source faithfully, including established errors or uncertainty without inventing either. One may treat the occasion as a public event, another as a practical errand, and a third as a private memory. Their facts and interpretations may agree. The comedy comes from the presentation order and the difference of scale: the grand version followed by the small practical detail, or the private memory preceding the public formulation it quietly corrects. Do not force a contradiction or a completed comparison. The juxtaposition itself is the comic operation.\n\nKeep chronology and knowledge intact. A later account may know what an earlier participant did not, but that later knowledge must not be written into the earlier account. Preserve each voice's distinct access and the active narrator, with meaningful private experience for all minds permitted by the active perspective. No fixed number of voices is required. The polyphonic form organizes the telling; it does not grant shared consciousness or add supernatural facts to a world that excludes them.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly. Establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and the recognizable aim that organizes the scene. Keep spatial changes easy to follow: a move from one room to another, a shift from a public space to a private one, or a change in who is present should be legible in the paragraphing.\n\nWhen an unfamiliar name, public account, or recurring phrase enters the scene, give enough context to understand its use. A character may explain it, the narration may supply a brief gathered history, or the dialogue may show how the phrase functions for these people. Give explanation the development needed for actual understanding or preparation, including before action when that is the causal order. Cut redundant exposition without making its prose length consume fictional time or excluding meaningful private association.\n\nUse paragraph rhythm to connect expectation and reception. A familiar phrase may be introduced in one paragraph and answered in the next, or a public account may be recalled while a character performs an ordinary task. Let the comic relation emerge from the actual causal order of action and private association. Each person may be serious, amused, accurate, or uninterested. Do not require a fixed reaction rotation or a response before the active boundary. Preserve the active narrator, world and cast age; do not impose first person, universal quips, new mechanics or a compulsory closing hook. The scene's pace carries the comedy, not a checklist of beats.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Lyrical Prose\n\nLet a sound, phrase, or image arise from the present scene and develop through sentence rhythm and shifts of attention. When an earlier occasion or public account enters authorized attention, give enough actual source and time cues to orient it; a current repeater, first source or specific trigger need not be available. Do not let the recurrence become a general communal memory without a source.\n\nBuild the comic turn by meeting the gathered resonance with a plain statement or practical detail. A change of register can carry the joke without invalidating the image; an actual correction is also available when supported. A phrase remembered as solemn may return beside the sound of a gate being propped open, or a family saying may arrive just as someone asks for the salt. Preserve the actual feeling and the image's supported significance; a plain detail may alter their apparent scale or confirm a familiar relation without requiring either change.\n\nKeep figurative association distinct from literal world facts. If a house 'remembered' its former occupants, distinguish figurative association from any literal memory the supplied world actually grants the house; the lyrical wording alone establishes no new power. Let each speaker's diction remain their own; the narrator's figurative register does not become the cast's speech. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The response may end while the association or feeling continues; no collapse of the image, magical event, or punch line is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nLet an actual present detail call up a phrase, joke, or account with a known personal history. Show what remains recognizable and what has really changed. A once-public joke may return through a modest habit: the same nickname used for a different reason, the same toast offered at a smaller table, the same complaint repeated by someone who no longer needs the original remedy.\n\nKeep later recollection distinct from prior experience. The narrator may know now what no one knew then, but do not place that later knowledge inside the earlier scene. Preserve genuine former pleasure and accurate memories. The humor and the longing coexist; neither cancels the other, and no one is required to announce impermanence or discover that the past was better or worse than it was.\n\nAllow full private reflection to linger without turning into action, an explanation of the joke, or a verdict on time. The response may end while the character is still holding the present detail and its earlier occasion together. Do not invent loss, innocence, or new understanding to make time visible.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse satire and picaresque construction as the definite route. Follow the actual person as they pursue a supplied aim through supported encounters, carrying only the accounts or formulations they really know. Do not invent a journey, a trickster role, a deceptive institution, or an obstruction the scene does not supply.\n\nLet a saying or account circulate through attributed sources. One person may take up a phrase for an unexpectedly practical purpose; another may use it exactly as intended in a new situation. Arrange the relation for comic recognition: the same formula can organize a marriage negotiation and a dispute over a broken shutter, and the comedy lies in the exact use each speaker makes of it. Attribute circulation rather than treating a rumor as common memory. If the narrator reports what 'everyone said,' show who said it and who heard it.\n\nKeep the actual cultural setting, language, and community distinct. Do not import a generic antiquity, mysticism, exile, or honor code. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and full private response. The response may end with the aim still underway or with the phrase now attached to a new occasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose the wording, source cue, and present particular that make the recurrence understandable. Use direct clauses to place the public account beside its actual present use at the scale the situation warrants. Comic recognition can arise from an exact or familiar fit as well as a supported difference of scale, without requiring deflation or change. A family saying may return when someone asks for change for the bus, or a remembered public promise may be quoted while someone measures a doorway. Let the relation remain inferable once sufficient context exists. Omit a redundant comic gloss while preserving any actual spoken explanation or meaningful private recognition.\n\nOmit redundant judgment, never actual causality or meaningful private feeling. An inward passage can be long enough to make sense and need not prove its worth through action or a joke. Preserve the difference between a remembered remark, rumor, and current fact. If the narrator recalls what someone said, keep it as that person's speech; if the town repeated it, show the repetition rather than turning it into direct knowledge.\n\nDo not require clipped cast voices, concealment of actual feelings, or a deflating final line. Each person's expression and privacy remain their own. The response may end with the relation established and nothing further explained.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nMove naturally through activity, dialogue, perception, and authorized inner experience. When an actual phrase or concern recalls earlier occasions, let the sentence gather them through clear source and time anchors before returning to the present use. A present argument over a borrowed ladder may recall the day the ladder was first lent, who was there, and what was said about it; then the present speaker asks a practical question and the gathered history meets the current need.\n\nThe response may be literal, helpful, amused, or indifferent. Comedy need not expose an inaccurate account. Someone may answer the elevated recollection by pointing out that the ladder is still in the yard, and that is enough. Keep rumor from becoming direct memory: if the narrator reports what people said, keep it attributed. Retain individual private meaning beyond public reputation; the person remembered for a public act may be thinking about something else entirely.\n\nNeither flashback, family history, nor punch line is required on every turn. The active boundary may fall within continuing thought or action.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nChoose scenes and callbacks from the actual bond and its premise. Let an established formulation return through a new or familiar practical occasion, and make its reception specific to the relationship rather than inserting generic banter. A private name, a shared joke, or a remembered promise may surface while the two people are doing something ordinary, and the comedy lies in how each of them now uses it.\n\nBoth people may understand the formulation accurately, or attach different supported meanings. Neither misunderstanding nor confession is compulsory. One may quote the old phrase to get the other to laugh, while the other hears it as a reminder of an unfinished argument; preserve each person's actual meaning, presenting nonspoken experience only where perspective access permits and leaving unavailable motives unclaimed, without forcing a resolution. Give real affection, rivalry, jealousy, or comfort its warranted private and public space.\n\nPreserve past facts and knowledge, including knowledge the pair already shared. Do not have one character forget what they both knew in order to create a reveal. No changed status, repaired mood, or roster of tropes belongs to every installment. The response may end with the callback received and the relationship still in motion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive actual deeds a retold shape through compact declarations, deliberate cadence, and grounded recurring phrasing. Let a reputation gather through its actual available sources, which may identify a deed, witness or later teller without requiring an invented chain. Place particular conduct or private experience beside the formulation where the sequence supports that relation; the active boundary need not wait for a next act or reply. A person remembered for a great public gesture may be found doing the small practical follow-up that made the gesture possible, and the chronicle's elevated phrasing meets the actual work.\n\nPraise can be accurate, the deed important, and the person competent. No mock battle, humiliation, or invented legend is required. The relation between remembered shape and exact present deed can carry comedy whether the reputation is accurate or actually mistaken; an exposure is not required. Keep sources, later additions, and original knowledge distinct. If the community added a detail over time, show the addition as a later account rather than as the original event.\n\nPreserve private experience beyond public memory. The person remembered for the deed may be thinking about the cost of it, or about something unrelated. No fixed opening, ceremonial cast dialogue, or completed verdict at the response boundary is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the social and domestic realism route. Let a familiar phrase, family account, or public version of events enter an actual shared arrangement of time, care, work, or space. The comedy comes from the precise way particular people live with it, not from everyone suddenly agreeing or from a compulsory contradiction.\n\nAnchor the phrase in its source. If it is a family saying, mark its actual source and present use within the shared arrangement; a former repeater, task or change of habit belongs only where supported. If it is a public account, show the household or workplace receiving it through ordinary obligations: someone is cooking, repairing, waiting, filing, or minding another person while the account circulates. A broad statement about how things are done can meet the concrete preference that sustains or qualifies it. The recognition may be comic even when the characters agree.\n\nUse the present arrangement to make recurrence's comic scale legible, whether its meaning changes or remains familiar. A phrase once used for a serious decision may return beside a small domestic choice, or a minor private habit may be repeated with the weight of a public occasion. Do not force the new use to contradict the old one. The humor can lie in the disproportionate care, the exact timing, or the way one person's blind spot fits another person's practical need.\n\nKeep historical references sourced. Distinguish what a character remembers from what is reported, and keep private feeling distinct from public habit. A person may comply outwardly while privately carrying a different association, or may privately accept a phrase that they refuse to repeat aloud. Preserve the active narrator, perspective, and world facts. The method works in the supplied community and situation without relocating the cast or shrinking a consequential event into a cozy vignette. Do not import reserve, snow, bleakness, or an obligatory lesson as regional atmosphere.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Screenplay\n\nEstablish a recurring phrase or account through what the scene can actually show or hear: spoken dialogue, an audible object, a visible note, a broadcast, or a physical arrangement. Do not import communal narration into action lines. Make the phrase's actual use concrete and recognizable, supplying later orienting context where needed without rewriting its earlier occasion.\n\nUse concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings when location or time changes. A cut may juxtapose the earlier use of the phrase with a present practical circumstance, letting the audience recognize the changed scale. A held beat may do the same work within a single scene: a character repeats the phrase while continuing an ordinary task, and the pause or the next action reveals its new proportion.\n\nPreserve actual chronology and who heard what. A character can answer only through their actual access, which may include hearing, reading, prior knowledge or a received report; a later scene cannot retroactively make an earlier remark public. Keep the limits of any visible cue: a face, object, or gesture may attract attention, but it does not prove a motive or make a private association available to the rest of the cast.\n\nDo not require a new mishap, camera command, or voice-over for the joke. The comic return can be accurate, literal, or enjoyably apt. Full required nonspoken experience remains separately attributed in its active presentation, including unfinished thought. Stop by the active interval rather than a public payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Stage Play\n\nUse clear speaker cues and economical stage directions for real positions, objects, entrances, exits, and actions. Let a supported familiar formulation meet ongoing work or a reply whose timing makes its public history newly apt or disproportionate. Make the relation between line and physical business legible for comic timing, without an added redundant gloss; an actual explanatory speech or private understanding keeps its place.\n\nKeep performers in their own registers. One character may repeat the phrase with ceremony, another may answer it literally while occupied, and a third may not notice it at all. Do not turn the cast into a chorus of communal judgment, and do not add a new prop or laugh cue merely to signal the joke. What a person knows follows actual hearing and access, not audience knowledge.\n\nLet recurrence be playable. A phrase first spoken while arranging a table may return during a different task, and the blocking can show the change in scale: the same words now accompany a smaller or larger action. Preserve the actual chronology of who said what and who was present.\n\nPreserve required private experience as separate nonspoken material rather than mandatory monologue or aside. An aside is performed speech with its own audience and permissions; it is not the default destination for thought. End within speech, business, or inward continuation when the active boundary requires it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Stream of Consciousness\n\nFollow the actual movement of one permitted mind among sensation, private wording, memory, and received accounts. A familiar expression may gather several supported occasions before a present sound or practical concern shifts its scale. The amusement is possible but not compulsory; the character may already understand the connection without announcing it.\n\nGive enough source and temporal orientation to distinguish remembered speech, rumor, fantasy, and current reception. When a public phrase enters the mind, use enough available source and time cues to orient its actual association; the first hearing, chain of repeaters or bodily trigger need not be known or reconstructed. The braid can move from a remembered voice to the pressure of a task to a private joke, but the reader must be able to tell which is which.\n\nLet the comic timing arise inside the mind's own movement. A phrase remembered with ceremonial weight may collide with the smell of a meal or the effort of opening a door. A private wording may return at a different scale than its public use. Do not make the mind a community: unavailable words and minds stay unavailable unless separately authorized.\n\nLet private feeling develop at its real extent without a decision, public joke, or fictional-time charge based on prose length. The character may remain inside an unfinished association, and the scene may stop there.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nKeep sender-attributed messages, actual recipients, and relevant interface events clear. Let a familiar formulation return through what someone really composes or sends. Its next actual reception may be accurate, literal, or delightfully apt without a typo or misunderstanding. The comedy can come from the exact practical use the new sender gives the phrase, or from the recipient's reply to it.\n\nA forwarded account remains sourced material, not firsthand memory or proof of receipt by everyone. Distinguish what a sender knows from what they have only forwarded. If a phrase circulates through a group, show who actually received it and who responds; do not make the whole channel share one reaction.\n\nSeparate drafts, transmission, and private thought. An unsent draft is a composed artifact, not automatically the writer's live private thought. A sent message is not proof of the sender's feeling. Retain full Hidden or Visible nonspoken experience beyond the channel, clearly separated from the transcript.\n\nUse only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the sequence. Do not invent a delay, deletion, response quota, or cliffhanger. The active boundary can occur before sending or receiving.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the illustrated scene through concise setting and clear dialogue attribution. Let a supported visual detail attract attention to an actual remembered formulation: a familiar object, a change in the room, or an expression that belongs to a particular moment. Then follow the particular thought, feeling, or exchange through which the phrase returns at a different or enjoyably familiar scale.\n\nA face is an observation, not proof of a motive. The association belongs to its source and need not be shared. Focal narration may recognize a public phrase and privately connect it to a personal occasion, while the dialogue continues on another track. Do not make the illustrated expression a truth indicator or require every cue to produce a matching thought and line.\n\nLet the rhythm move among scene-setting, speech, and focal response without a fixed cue-thought-dialogue cycle. A background detail may anchor the return of a phrase without any character commenting on it. A spoken line may recall an earlier scene without a visual cue. Preserve narrator, world, and full nonspoken presentation in the active form.\n\nDo not activate images, branching menus, romance routes, or a compulsory closing reaction. The scene can stop after a partial recognition or an unfinished exchange.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacahbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / García Márquez / Web Serial\n\nBriefly recover the relevant earlier phrase or occasion when needed for re-entry, preserving what was really said, learned, changed, or left pending. A later use can reveal another practical scale or repeat a familiar successful joke. Novelty and escalation are not owed; the comic significance can accumulate through accurate recurrence.\n\nKeep remembered, reported, and current knowledge distinct. A character may remember a phrase from an earlier installment, hear it reported by someone who was not present, or use it now without knowing its full history. Allow actual previous understanding to persist. Do not reset competence or retroactively alter an earlier event to make the callback land.\n\nLet independent lives continue around the callback. The recurring formulation may enter while someone is working, caring for another person, or pursuing a separate aim. Meaningful private experience continues beyond the joke. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or an unfinished association.\n\nDo not require a reset of competence, a retroactive altered event, or a closing hook. An installment may end during ordinary work or a partial recognition.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWhen a character must admit, ask, apologize, or return to something difficult, find the exact word, gesture, or practical fact on which that request turns. Write the moment around that particular rather than around a general emotional state: the specific chair they do not sit in, the precise amount they cannot say, the one sentence they repeat because it is safer than the sentence they need. Let the interval before, during, and after the request hold actual strain, anticipation, or direct warmth. A plainly apt reply can be funny because it lands against that held weight, not because the need is mocked or deflated.\n\nUse brief declarative movement to place the selected particular so the reader sees the relation between what was risked and what actually arrived. A meaningful gap can do comic work: the other person answers the practical half of the request and leaves the emotional half standing in the silence. Do not add redundant gloss to the interval; preserve actual explanation, direct admission, or private recognition when it belongs to the scene. Let the character's private feeling remain full and available under the active narration contract, including spontaneous asking, known motives, and an unanswered request at the boundary. The comedy belongs to the timing and the particular, not to a required resolution or a compulsory deflation of the need.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Chat Roleplay\n\nOpen the shared interval by making the present aim and the active speakers immediately legible. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably, and keep the surrounding world active: other participants continue their practical lives, pursue their own aims, and respond according to their own habits rather than waiting for the scene to finish. Build comic situations from these owned contributions, with any narrator commentary retaining its actual voice and permissions rather than replacing the situated sequence.\n\nSelect the concrete detail that makes an actual contribution legible beside a preceding expectation. A character sets down the wrong object, answers the question before it is asked, or performs the exact helpful act that reveals they misunderstood the request. Let that particular arrive through brief declarative movement, then leave room for the other participants' real responses: aligned habits, contrasting assumptions, or simple continuation. Do not make every speaker terse, withhold known thought, take player control, or require a response, reversal, question, or new obstacle before the boundary. The interval itself carries the comic sequence; the next contribution can follow naturally from what is already underway.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse classical or vernacular episodic telling. Give each occasion an intelligible aim and let the episode move through that aim without requiring a completed arc, a new encounter, or a changed outcome. Select the particular phrase, object, or action on which the occasion's comic timing turns: the same bowl returned to the wrong shelf, the formal greeting delivered to the wrong person, the repeated instruction that now means something different because the circumstances have changed.\n\nWhen an earlier occasion exists, make the relevant continuity readable through that detail's present use. An object may appear in a new context, a phrase pass to another speaker, or a practical act produce a different result; an unchanged familiar use can also be funny through precise recurrence. Make that relation readable without redundant gloss while preserving actual narrative explanation or inward recognition. A first occasion needs no invented prior episode; it simply establishes the detail that may return later. Keep the real community and narrator intact, with no required martial plot, revelation, or forced closure. Known private experience remains available beyond what is publicly said, under the active narration contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nPut the relevant weight, grip, distance, sound, or cost in concrete verbs beside what someone actually expected or attempted. Write the object's resistance, the body's effort, the material fact of the task: the lid that will not turn, the step that is farther than it looked, the package heavier than the sender claimed. Let that practical particular arrive cleanly for comic recognition, including an accurately anticipated familiar fit when the expectation is met exactly. Compress repeated transitions so the selected fact lands with force rather than being buried in connective explanation.\n\nLet sentence length and roughness follow the speaker's voice and the moment's actual pressure. A developed inward passage remains available for actual feeling, attention, uncertainty, or reasoning without needing to change a decision or serve the joke. A short clause can carry the comic relation when it is already legible; thought extent is not a brevity quota. Do not invent injury, failure, violence, or a compulsory hard-bitten attitude. Real effort and pain keep their actual weight, but the comedy comes from the precise material fact and its relation to expectation, not from making the world uniformly punishing.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Epistolary / Documents\n\nUse the document form actually supplied by the scene, with its writer, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition made clear. Let the document's own register carry the telling: a report's official phrasing, a letter's careful politeness, a journal's private shorthand. Place an exact phrase or notation where its relation to the record's broader context or an actual later reply becomes readable. The comic gap may be simple precision rather than concealment: the writer records the wrong date, the recipient answers the only question that was not asked, the marginal note contradicts the formal paragraph above it.\n\nPreserve each writer's register and needed extent. A terse letter is not required, and a document may be as long as its actual purpose demands. Keep original knowledge separate from later annotation, and keep full live private experience separate from what the document records. No second record, contradiction, or revelation is required; the comedy can rest in the selected detail and the actual timing of its uptake.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nAttend to the detail that makes care particular: a known or expressed preference, a chosen accommodation, an available pleasure. Write the helper's actual approach beside that detail. The friend brings the wrong tea because they remembered a preference from years ago; the colleague reserves the quiet desk because they noticed without being told; the sibling sets out the good blanket before the guest arrives. Let the interval of reception carry enjoyment, familiar recognition, or quiet amusement. The comic timing lives in the relation between the offered particular and the person receiving it, not in a joke at anyone's expense.\n\nKeep real preference knowledge and independent voices intact. The recipient may notice the care, miss it, accept it, or simply enjoy it without comment. Help need not be secretly planned, accepted, thanked, or transformative. Private feeling can be long, direct, or unfinished under the active narration contract. Warmth and amusement coexist without requiring a confession, a payoff, or a change in the relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraph movement and deliberate transitions to establish the occasion's permitted social or historical context. Let the narration frame the scene through the chosen narrator's distance: the room's arrangement, the history of the household, the public expectation that has gathered around this meeting. Within that ample frame, place a distilled phrase or factual detail against the context. The formal invitation contains a request for the wrong service; the carefully prepared speech turns on a single word that means something different to the audience; the ceremonial object is set down in the wrong order.\n\nLet the surrounding interval and subsequent supported attention make the comic relation legible. Gu's precise selection need not fragment the formal paragraph, force terse speech, change the reader's expectation, or culminate in a deflating last line. The comedy comes from the proportion between the composed context and the exact particular placed within it. Preserve direct feeling and all authorized explanation under the active narration contract.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nEstablish the telling as a present occasion: who is listening, why the teller is speaking now, and what the listeners want from the account. Let precise selection give the embedded story its shape while the teller keeps their actual register, purposes, and needed extent. A detail can matter through enjoyment, a private association, or the teller's own concern as well as the listeners' immediate interests; no universally terse telling is required. A listener's interruption, glance, question, or silence can create a pause in the frame without stopping the inner account; the interval between what the teller says and what the audience does with it is part of the comic construction.\n\nSelect a phrase, object, or event in the embedded account for its relation to the frame. Its recurrence may carry changed weight or familiar recognition without requiring a return or visible audience effect before the boundary. Comic recognition can come from a listener taking the tale more literally than the teller intended, from the teller's own blind spot about which detail matters, or from the audience already knowing the ending and waiting for the teller to reach it. Do not require the tale to change anyone's opinion or produce a practical result.\n\nKeep each level's knowledge distinct. The teller can omit what the teller did not understand at the time, and a listener can notice an implication the teller never intended. Private experience remains available to the authorized focal character in the frame even when the spoken tale moves forward; an unsaid reaction can sit beside the telling without becoming public knowledge. Follow the active response boundary within ongoing telling, listening, or permitted private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. Keep the levels intelligible without requiring the telling to stop inside the fiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nWork in the social or developmental realist route. Open with a concrete encounter in which a participant holds a recognizable expectation about belonging, competence, or responsibility: a person expects to be included, to manage a task, or to be taken seriously. Render the expectation through the participant's actual preparation, speech, or private reasoning rather than announcing it as a theme.\n\nSelect one exact action or phrase that places the expectation beside its actual reception. Use brief declarative movement to deliver the selected particular, then let a meaningful gap follow it. The gap may contain the participant's uncertainty, another character's ordinary response, or the simple continuation of the encounter. Comic recognition can arise from the expectation being met in an unexpected proportion, from the participant's blind spot about what the situation actually requires, or from the encounter proceeding without the acknowledgment the participant assumed would come. Do not require a lesson, revised self-image, concealed motive, failed test, or completed encounter.\n\nIf the scene spans repeated occasions, give a returning understanding its actual concrete form, familiar or changed, without requiring a novel instance. The same expectation may be confirmed, qualified, or simply outlast its original occasion. Preserve the narrator's access and the full nonspoken experience of the focal character: confidence, doubt, embarrassment, or relief can remain private while the visible encounter moves forward. Follow the active response boundary during continuing exchange, consideration, or other private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. The expectation may remain, change, or be satisfied according to events; no in-world pause is needed to end the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Gothic-Punk\n\nPlace an actual privilege, social mask, or old claim in a present-life encounter with concrete urban or domestic material detail. Let the inherited gravity of the claim be real: the authority may be competent, the mask may be worn for good reason, and the intimacy may be sincere. Then select one exact contemporary term that matters to this particular exchange: a rent payment, a bus schedule, a household rule, a borrowed object, a text message, a shift at work. Use brief declarative movement to put the old claim and the present term side by side, and let a meaningful pause hold them together.\n\nThe comic proportion comes from the relation between the two, not from proving the authority hollow. A person may insist on an old form of respect while also needing a practical favor. A private desire may survive unchanged inside an ordinary errand. A social mask may stay in place while the encounter's practical stakes make its maintenance visibly effortful. Let atmosphere arise from lived experience: the feel of a room, the weight of a coat, the sound of a door, the specific inconvenience of the present term. Do not use mood as evidence of menace or sincerity.\n\nTrust and intimacy may be freely offered, incautious, or long-held without requiring a mask to fall. Private feeling remains available to the authorized focal character even when the public exchange stays formal. Preserve supplied mechanisms and world rules; do not add or delete powers to force the collision. Follow the active response boundary within continuing public or private experience, an actual pause, or a supported conclusion. The relation between old claim and present term stays intelligible without requiring the encounter to stop or resolve.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nWork in the lyric or philosophical inwardness route. Begin with an authorized focal character already considering an actual claim, question, or obligation that belongs to their supplied situation. Let the consideration proceed through direct argument, remembered instances, and felt response rather than through a linear report of events. Keep the language and community supplied by the scene; do not impose spiritual content, a particular family structure, or a generic regional temperament.\n\nSelect one phrase, perception, or remembered instance and let it recur through the character's actual thought. The recurrence may be a phrase the character repeats to test its meaning, a memory that returns at a different angle, or a perception that keeps drawing attention. Comic proportion can emerge inside the mind: the character may notice that a grand claim depends on a very small practical fact, that a familiar duty has outlasted the situation that produced it, or that two sincerely held beliefs cannot both fit the same afternoon. The recognition need not change the claim, produce an answer, or return the character to outward action.\n\nUse precise declarative placement and meaningful intervals to shape the inward movement without forbidding actual sustained rumination, fluent argument, or expansive feeling. A distilled image can interrupt or focus the consideration. Keep the focal person's knowledge and uncertainty attributable; any broader narrative context follows the active narrator's actual permissions and does not become the person's knowledge merely by being rendered. Preserve the full extent of permitted private experience, including uncertainty that stays unresolved. Follow the active response boundary during ongoing consideration, sensation, or feeling as well as a real resting point or conclusion. Neither a pause nor a still-unanswered question is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaial::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the early-modern comic/theatrical telling route as narrative construction, not as a script format. Establish who is present and what each person can actually see or hear. Then select one exact action, entrance, or line and place it in relation to an expectation the scene has already made legible: a person expects a particular arrival, a particular answer, or a particular performance of a familiar role.\n\nLet clear access and placement determine what the reader can notice. A character may see the action coming before another character does; the reader may know what the public scene does not yet expose. Use brief declarative movement to deliver the selected particular, then hold a meaningful gap before or after it. Comic recognition can come from the expected fitting act arriving exactly as anticipated, from the interval being shorter or longer than the participants assumed, or from a repeated wording whose meaning shifts through placement alone. Do not require a stock fool, obligatory trick, closed gag, new misunderstanding, or terse cast.\n\nKeep the active prose form and narrator distance. The telling may borrow theatrical timing without becoming dialogue blocks or stage directions. Actual private knowledge remains available to the authorized focal character and distinct from what the public scene exposes; a character may understand more, less, or differently than the visible exchange suggests. Follow the active response boundary before, during, or after an actual action, line, or private passage. No delivery of the selected detail, in-world pause, or still-pending expectation is required to end the reply.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nUse the polyphonic or nonlinear telling route. Attribute each account or moment to its source and time: a character's earlier recollection, a later report, a bystander's version, or a document's record. The order of presentation may follow the needs of the comic construction rather than chronology, but each segment must remain legible as belonging to a particular person and moment.\n\nSelect one recurring concrete particular: an object, a phrase, a gesture, a sum of money, a shared room, a repeated errand. Place it in differently situated accounts so the reader can compare what each source emphasizes. One account may treat the particular as trivial while another treats it as decisive; one may remember it accurately while another's emphasis reveals a blind spot; two compatible accounts may simply disagree about its size. Comic recognition can come from the comparison itself, without requiring agreement, a liar, a new revelation, or a completed convergence.\n\nUse brief declarative movement and meaningful gaps within each segment so the polyphony does not become a pile of competing summaries. Preserve the distinction between what a source knew at the original time and what the same source knows later. Full authorized minds remain available; private experience belongs to its owner and does not become public knowledge merely because another account appears. Keep the supplied world's rules intact; do not add supernatural facts to force a marvelous reading. Follow the active response boundary within an ongoing account, juxtaposition, or authorized private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. No completed comparison or changed weight across accounts is required.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaian::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Light Novel\n\nEnter the immediate situation cleanly: name the location, the participants, and the current aim in the opening lines. Keep spatial changes easy to follow by anchoring each new position to a visible or practical detail. If a name or mechanism is unfamiliar, introduce it through actual explanation, preparation, observation, or use rather than through a glossary pause. Orient the reader to actual positions and the immediate activity within narrator access, distinguishing an apparent aim from a privately known or still-unknown one; clarity does not require disclosing unavailable motives.\n\nUse flexible paragraph breaks to locate the salient action or phrase beside an expectation. A character may expect a particular answer, a particular result, or a particular reaction; the selected detail or reply arrives in its own brief declarative movement. Let a meaningful gap follow it, with the gap filled by the focal character's private response, another character's ordinary continuation, or the simple next step of the task. Comic recognition can come from a familiar precise response arriving exactly as expected, from the expectation being met in a different proportion, or from the character's blind spot about what the situation actually requires. Do not require a surprise, a completed demonstration, or a closing hook.\n\nPrivate reaction keeps its owner and full extent. A character's uncertainty, embarrassment, relief, or amusement can remain nonspoken while the visible scene moves forward. The form does not automatically supply a young cast, first person, or a cliffhanger; use the narrator and cast already established by the scene. Follow the active response boundary during continuing exchange, task, or private experience as well as an actual pause or conclusion. Keep the situation legible without requiring delivery of the selected detail or a pause inside the fiction.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose an image, sound, or repeated phrase already present in the scene or available to the authorized mind. Let its recurrence develop through syntax, echo, and shifts of attention across the paragraph, so the reader feels the association before the comic relation is stated. Keep the image traceable to the actual moment or the teller's memory; do not let metaphor introduce a new object, person, or event into the world.\n\nPlace a direct phrase or concrete particular beside that developed image at the point where expectation and actuality meet. The particular may be a flat practical answer, an ordinary object, or a simple refusal that shows the image's proportion. It should not automatically puncture the feeling. The serious or pleasurable quality of the image can remain intact while the plain particular makes the gap between it and the immediate situation legible.\n\nUse Gu Long's selective movement within the lyrical passage. A brief declarative sentence can hold the paragraph's turn, and a meaningful gap can let the reader complete the connection. What the focal character notices but does not explain may carry the comic recognition. Do not require the character to arrive at a new understanding, laugh at the contrast, or return the image to a finished meaning. The scene may continue with the image still present and the practical situation unchanged.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nSelect a remembered phrase, object, or interval whose comic exactness still belongs to the present person. Let the recollection arrive through a concrete detail: the way someone said a word, the placement of an object, or the timing of an old reply. Render the memory as actual recollection, including its gaps and the private feeling that accompanies it. Do not smooth the past into a lesson or a completed joke.\n\nDistinguish the earlier occasion from the present through the actual temporal relation: a real irreversible change may sit beside the remembered detail, or a continuing habit may retain its familiar comic exactness without a newly invented loss. The same habit may still be funny in its exactness even though the occasion is no longer available. The pleasure of the memory and the fact of its absence can coexist without one canceling the other. Do not manufacture loss where the world supplies none, and do not make the past foolish merely because it is past.\n\nUse Gu Long's brief declarative movement to hold the turn between then and now. A single sentence may mark the distance; a meaningful gap may let the reader feel what the narrator does not explain. What the person notices now, or declines to say, can carry the attachment. The passage may end with the memory still present and the present still unfinished, without a changed interpretation, an improved mood, or a final statement of impermanence.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the passage as modern memoir: a participant's composed recollection of an actual occasion, told through the permitted narrator. Do not invent narrator participation in events the narrator did not attend. Select a direct phrase or concrete detail that locates the original expectation and experience: the exact wording of a request, the position of an object, or the particular habit that shaped the moment. Let that detail carry the comic relation rather than explaining it in general terms.\n\nPosition later commentary at its own time. The participant may now notice a proportion, a gap, or a familiar exactness that was not the whole of the original experience. Keep the earlier moment's actual feeling distinct from the later selection. Retrospective knowledge need not become a revelation, confession, regret, or revised verdict; preserve any such development when the actual account supports it. The memoir may simply record what the participant now chooses to place beside the earlier detail.\n\nUse Gu Long's selective movement within the authored account. A brief declarative sentence can mark the shift between the remembered occasion and the later perspective. A meaningful gap can let the reader hold both times at once. The composed account is separate from full live private experience; it records what its author records, not an omniscient explanation of every mind involved. The passage may end with the earlier detail still exact and the later commentary still partial.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Minimalist Prose\n\nChoose the fact or phrase that makes the comic relation inferable and place it cleanly beside the relevant expectation or established context. One exact detail can do the work of several explanatory sentences: the wrong object on the table, the unchanged reply, or the particular word that shows the gap between aim and result. Keep the causal connection legible. Omission should leave something inferable rather than delete why the moment matters.\n\nCut repeated explanation, not needed cause, voice, or private recognition. A long thought, uncertainty, or feeling may remain because it belongs to the experience, even if it does not prove immediately useful. Render the focal mind's actual state directly where permitted, or let an inference arise from selected context where appropriate; neither explicit feeling nor private recognition needs to be concealed. Gu Long's gaps are intervals of meaning rather than concealed focal knowledge; the reader may complete the connection, but the character's own awareness remains available under the active contract.\n\nUse brief declarative sentences to carry the turn. A direct clause may set the expectation, and another may show its particular realization. Do not require clipped dialogue, a witty retort, or a final deflation. The comic relation can be exact and quiet: the expectation still present, the actual fact still itself, and the scene continuing without a punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaias::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nFollow the actual activity through speech, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Let the scene's ordinary movement establish the aim, the habit, or the blind spot before the comic relation becomes readable. Avoid redundant gloss on the joke while preserving actual explanation or private recognition; explanatory prose alone does not require a fictional pause. The telling particular may be a concrete detail, a direct line, or a small action whose relation to the current aim emerges across a meaningful interval.\n\nSelect that particular with Gu Long's exactness. A brief declarative sentence can hold the moment where expectation and actuality meet. A meaningful interval can make the fit or surprise recognizable without redundant commentary, while any actual explicit narrative judgment keeps its own permissions and purpose. The comic effect may be a familiar exactness, an anticlimax, or a supported reversal, but it need not arrive as a finished revelation. What the focal character notices or declines to explain can carry the recognition.\n\nKeep the world and mind continuing according to their actual circumstances. Needed connective explanation and independent voices remain available. Do not make every interval cryptic, every sentence short, or every ending a turning point. The scene may end with the situation still in motion, the aim still pursued, and the comic relation present without a completed outcome.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nSelect scenes and recurrent particulars that fulfill the supplied bond's specific promise, whether affection, rivalry, comfort, or another established relation. Use the actual premise rather than a roster of familiar tropes. A repeated phrase, object, or small act should belong to these particular people: something one of them says, something they both recognize, or a private reference with a history the scene has established or can make legible.\n\nPlace that familiar particular at a meaningful interval where its real shared or private significance can be funny. The callback may confirm an old joke, expose a habit, or land differently because the present occasion has changed its proportion. Do not require a misunderstanding to create the comedy. The humor can come from exact recognition: both people know what the phrase means, and the reader sees the gap between that meaning and the current moment.\n\nUse Gu Long's selective movement within the relationship scene. A brief declarative sentence can deliver the callback; a meaningful gap can let its weight settle before the next exchange. Keep actual knowledge, direct feeling, and independent motives available. Keep private language attributable to its actual participants while allowing any explanation or context the narrator is authorized to provide; presentation alone grants no extra shared knowledge. The scene may end with the callback still echoing and the relationship unchanged, without a confession, a repaired misunderstanding, or a new promise.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the account a sense of retelling through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing. Establish the act and the actual source of its retelling: who did it, what was said, and which features the teller or established community remembers. Do not invent communal familiarity or reputation merely to supply a retold cadence. Then place the exact practical detail beside that remembered form. The precise term can confirm the praise while making its proportion funny: a smaller, stranger, or more ordinary practical term may alter the account's proportion, or a precisely fitting familiar detail may make an accurate telling funny without diminishing the deed.\n\nKeep reputation attached to its real source. The original person's unspoken effort, pleasure, or private awareness remains distinct from the communal account. Being remembered as dependable or bold does not mean the act felt effortless, cheerful, or automatic. Let a brief declarative sentence hold the gap between the retold declaration and the actual occasion. A meaningful gap can let the reader feel the difference without the narrator mocking either side.\n\nDo not require an oath, a mock hero, an earned epithet, a humiliating reversal, or a completed legendary verdict before the active boundary. The active boundary may fall within ongoing telling, original or retrospective private experience, or an actual conclusion. Preserve the source's real knowledge and any established communal account without a mandatory new legend, dismissal, or continuing public telling.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nWrite the scene inside the actual social and domestic arrangement already supplied. Establish who has time, who uses a space, who makes a request, and who enjoys a routine through concrete movement: a person sets down a bag, waits for the kettle, reads a note left on the table, or holds a door for someone carrying too much. Let these ordinary allocations of space and attention carry the situation rather than importing isolation, snow, or a family history the world does not supply.\n\nSelect one precise word, object, or small action that belongs to this arrangement. Place the particular clearly through precise declarative selection. Its actual use or recurrence can carry comic recognition, without requiring a first-appearance/later-use pair in the current reply or inventing a prior occasion. The same mug may be the wrong one for the guest, the same chair may be the one nobody sits in, the same phrase may be repeated with a different practical meaning. Use the actual interval or placement to make the relation readable without redundant gloss, while preserving explicit recognition or explanation in the person's or narrator's actual voice.\n\nKeep selection compact, using a direct clause or a meaningful interval where fuller explanation would only repeat the detail. Sentence length and cast register remain flexible; developed feeling, attention, or thought need no comic or next-action justification. A person can be competent and cooperative while still missing the particular implication. Their private feeling remains fully available: uncertainty, irritation, affection, or amusement may be rendered directly without being hidden for a later reveal. No domestic labor needs to surround every attempt, and no hidden grievance or changed relationship is required to make the scene comic. The ordinary interval can remain ordinary; the comedy comes from the selected particular, not from a forced reversal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Screenplay\n\nWrite concise action blocks with clear speaker cues. Mark scene changes only when location or time actually changes. Establish an expectation through visible or audible detail: a person checks a drawer, listens for a car, reads a label, or waits beside a door. Keep the action block brief and declarative so the selected detail is legible without camera commands.\n\nPlace a supported sound, action, or object against that expectation. The held competent action can be as funny as an actual cut or new arrival: a person continues wiping the same clean glass, opens the wrong cupboard without comment, or hears the expected knock from the wrong direction. Let the interval between the setup and the placed detail carry the comedy. Do not add a reaction shot, a witty interruption, or a completed gag unless the scene itself supplies one.\n\nKeep the visible sequence distinct from required nonspoken interiority. If the active contract requires private thought or feeling, render it in its permitted attributed form rather than converting it to voice-over or making another character hear it. The action blocks and dialogue remain observable; the private material remains separate. No cinematic command quota, offscreen sound, or cut is required. The scene can stop on a held action, an ordinary continuation, or a new arrival without forcing a punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Stage Play\n\nStage clear speakers, positions, and supported movement or stillness. Mark entrances and exits when they occur. Give each character an actual physical relation to the shared space: one stands at the table, one folds a cloth, one checks the time, one remains seated with a cup. Keep the directions observable and playable; do not add film-camera instructions or unstageable business.\n\nPlace a precise line beside the object, task, or response that gives it particular weight. The line may be a simple request, a repeated instruction, or a flat statement that lands differently because of what the speaker is doing while saying it. Let ongoing competent business or an ordinary pause carry the comic recognition: a person keeps sorting papers while answering, holds a tray steady through a long silence, or continues polishing a shoe after the other has left. No stumble, witty interruption, or public confession is required.\n\nKeep actual private experience separately attributed in its required presentation. A monologue or aside is performed speech with its own audience and purpose, not the default destination for thought. Another character does not hear a private conclusion merely because the reader has seen it. The scene can stop after a held action, an exit, or an ordinary continuation without a finished exchange, new prop, or completed gag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid the authorized mind's perception, remembered wording, bodily sensation, and current concern in the order they actually arrive. A person hears a phrase, feels a sleeve catch on a drawer handle, remembers the same phrase from a different room, and returns to the present task. Keep events, memory, and fantasy distinguishable: a remembered conversation is not happening now, and a feared outcome is not yet an event. Enough orientation remains for the reader to tell them apart.\n\nLet a distilled particular recur where its actual association changes or confirms the proportion of a thought. The same word may return with a different inflection, the same sensation may attach to a different object, the same remembered line may become funnier or more irritating as the mind moves around it. Do not force the recurrence to resolve into a lesson or a completed joke. The comic proportion can coexist with unfinished private movement.\n\nKeep known thought directly available rather than hidden for an eventual reveal. Long feeling or uncertainty needs no useful conclusion. Prose length alone does not determine fictional elapsed time; actual attention, waiting, and events still have their real duration. The response can stop inside continuing thought, an unfinished association, or a present sensation without requiring the mind to change direction or produce a result.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nPresent clear sender-attributed messages in each person's actual digital register. One sender may write in complete sentences with careful punctuation; another may use short fragments, dropped capitals, or practical abbreviations. Expansive messages are permitted when they belong to that person's habit. Use only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene.\n\nSelect the phrase whose literal fit or relation to a real reply carries the comedy. The humor may come from a message that answers the wrong question precisely, a correction that arrives after the other person has already acted, or a shared word used with two different meanings. Let the actual timing of composition, sending, receipt, and reading remain separate. A person may compose a long explanation, then send only the last line; the unsent draft is authored text, not live thought.\n\nKeep full private experience in its own permitted channel rather than pretending the transcript reveals it. A delay, typo, deletion, misunderstanding, or completed reply is not required. The exchange can stop after a sent message, a read receipt, or an ordinary continuation without a forced punchline or a fabricated interface event.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaiba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Visual Novel\n\nOrient the illustrated-style scene with concise scene-setting and clear dialogue attribution. Give the reader enough spatial and visual context to place the characters: where they stand, what they are holding, what the room or street looks like. Keep the narration flexible; it may move between action, dialogue, perception, and authorized inner response as the scene requires.\n\nChoose a meaningful background, object, or expression cue that locates a direct line or action in relation to an expectation. The cue may be a familiar confirmation: the same poster on the wall, the same cup on the desk, the same expression a person always makes before asking for something. It does not prove hidden motive or transmit thought to the cast. Let the interval between the cue and the line or action carry the comedy.\n\nKeep private feeling and reflection fully available in the active permitted form. No fixed cue-thought-dialogue cycle is required, and no changed response, new menu, or closing reaction is needed. The scene can stop after a familiar confirmation, an ordinary continuation, or an unfinished exchange without forcing a punchline or a branching choice.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacaibb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Gu Long / Web Serial\n\nRecover only the prior detail or wording needed to re-enter the current activity. If an earlier installment established that a person always checks the same drawer, returns the same borrowed tool, or repeats the same instruction, bring back that particular in a brief declarative sentence. Avoid repeating the whole earlier sequence when the exact remembered particular supplies the needed context.\n\nPreserve the detail's real use, known consequences, and unresolved aspects. A later encounter may confirm a familiar comic relation: the drawer is still empty, the tool is still borrowed, the instruction is still being repeated. It may also support a new one: the same object now appears in a different context with a different practical meaning. Do not hide known causes or make a character forget a supported conclusion to force a fresh surprise.\n\nKeep the through-line readable without recap machinery. No per-reply advance, revelation, changed relationship, completed setup/payoff, or cliffhanger is required. Full private passages need no plot utility. The installment can close on completed work, a pending request, or plain acknowledgment of what is now known, leaving the continuing interval available without forcing a punchline.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nBuild the scene around a character pursuing a recognizable aim that matters to them, such as returning an item, asking for a small accommodation, or carrying out a familiar task. Let the aim create the comic pressure through timing and the gap between what the person expects and what the situation actually supplies. The expectation need not be absurd or humiliating; it can be a reasonable hope that meets an unhurried practical response, a literal answer, or a continuation that ignores the emotional weight the character has attached to it.\n\nRender the character's private concern at full length through the Murakami profile's method: place the concern beside ordinary activity and exact surfaces. Where an activity is actually present, describe its particulars alongside the inward experience; no cup-washing, schedule-checking, or other task must be invented, and the passage may remain in thought. Let the association between an ordinary thing and a private worry develop without announcing its meaning. The character may notice the texture of a cloth, the sound of a door, or the particular order of objects on a table whether the harder thought is communicated, partly expressed, or left unspoken. This sustained inward attention is not a symptom to be cured or a secret to be exposed; it is the actual experience accompanying the comic sequence.\n\nUse the angsty style to make the cost of the aim legible without forcing a visible lapse. The character may need something from someone they have hurt, may be preparing to apologize, or may be trying not to ask for help. Render actual strain fully, showing its effects on conversation only where they occur and allowing unchanged outward conduct: a question arrives late, a helpful gesture is accepted with too much care, an ordinary phrase lands with private weight. The other person need not know what is happening. The comedy can come from the mismatch between the character's turbulent interior and the even, practical surface of the exchange.\n\nDo not require anxiety to produce a mistake, a confession, or a repaired relationship. The scene may end with the practical aim completed while the private concern remains unresolved. A dry response can be funny because it does not address the emotional stakes, not because it humiliates the person. Keep the world consequential enough that the aim remains playable, and let warmth or partial comfort coexist with pain when the scene supports it.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Chat Roleplay\n\nWrite the shared interval in readable paragraphs with unmistakable dialogue attribution, preserving the active role contract and narrator access. Establish the actual place and activity, including conversation or stillness without an invented task. Activity may continue or pause according to the people's actual choices, including a deliberate joke. The comic timing comes from recognizable aims: participants pursue actual aims at their supplied scale, with private purposes known only within narrator access, and their habits or blind spots shape how they pursue it.\n\nUse the Murakami profile's unhurried practical attention inside the scene. A character may take time to pour a drink, straighten a stack of papers, or check the contents of a bag while the conversation moves around them. Describe the action evenly and let it occupy real narrative space. The focal character may follow a private association during this activity without the others sharing it. The narration can render that association in full, but other characters know what their actual senses, prior information, communication, and established capabilities make available, not automatically the private association.\n\nLet particular responses create the comedy rather than random interruption or a compulsory quip. A literal answer to a figurative question, a familiar routine completed at the wrong speed, or two people pursuing different aims with the same object can all generate the scene's humor. One participant may be knowingly comic while another remains uninterested in the joke. Preserve the difference between what the narrator knows and what each character perceives.\n\nEnd the interval at a natural continuation point that follows from the scene. The next reply can pick up the same practical task, a new arrival, or the unresolved difference between what the characters wanted. Do not force a question, cliffhanger, or choice screen. Accurate understanding, stillness, and unfinished thought remain possible; the scene can close with the participants simply continuing their ordinary activity.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Ground the scene in one lived arrangement: a household, a workplace, a neighborhood shop, or another shared setting where people have established routines and actual preferences. Do not invent estrangement or generational conflict to generate interest. The comedy comes from the familiar operation of this arrangement and the particular aims people bring to it on this occasion.\n\nFollow an actual shared routine in detail. Show who usually does what, which objects belong where, and how the space accommodates the people moving through it. Let an ordinary repeated detail carry the comic construction: a particular cup that is always in the wrong place, a door that sticks, a meal prepared in a fixed order. When the detail returns, give it a concrete place in the actual encounter, including a private or symbolic association without requiring practical usefulness or shared meaning. A literal answer, a familiar agreement, or a private association can all serve as the callback where the established history supports it.\n\nRender the focal character's private experience through the Murakami profile's method. Place an association beside the practical activity: while the person chops vegetables, waits for a kettle, or folds laundry, their mind moves toward a concern that the others need not share. Describe the ordinary surface evenly and let the private thought develop at its own length. Other characters know what their actual sources and permitted access support, rather than automatically receiving the private association, and their responses may be accurate, partial, or concerned with something else entirely.\n\nPreserve genuine ease and competence. The routine can succeed, the meal can be good, the errand can be completed. The comic effect may come from the gap between the character's private association and the smooth continuation of the shared routine, not from a mistake or a failure. Keep the supplied household and community distinct and let the scene end at a natural point in the routine.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nOpen with concrete physical texture and forceful transitions grounded in the established conditions of the scene. Name the worn surface, the weight of the object, the sound of the floor, the practical stakes of the task. Use compressed clauses to move through action and setting without softening the material world. The roughness belongs to the actual environment and the actual people in it; do not manufacture danger, violence, or survival pressure to justify the style.\n\nAllow a longer inward passage where the character's attention actually needs it. The Murakami profile's unhurried association does not have to be shortened to match terse action. After a compressed description of the room or the task, the narration can slow into the character's private thought: a particular object reminds them of a past arrangement, a repeated sound carries an old concern, an ordinary routine opens into a memory they have not finished examining. Describe the association evenly and let it occupy the space it requires.\n\nUse the change of rhythm as the comic mechanism. Let the private passage have its actual extent. Where a dry response, literal answer, or familiar act occurs, compressed concrete prose can meet the inward rhythm; no long buildup or outward answer is required in the current interval. The contrast between the extended inward attention and the abrupt practical continuation creates the comic effect without requiring the character to be humiliated or the task to fail. The physical action can complete cleanly: the door closes, the payment goes through, the repair holds.\n\nPreserve real scale and stakes. If the scene involves difficulty, keep it credible and specific rather than inflating it. The comedy can coexist with roughness, but it does not require harm or an outward reaction. The character's private concern may remain unexpressed while the practical situation continues.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through actual documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Choose the specific document form the scene requires: a note left on a counter, a work email, a repair request, a message to a landlord, a list with an added comment. Do not rotate through formats mechanically. What each document knows or conceals follows from who wrote it, why, and what they believed at that time.\n\nBuild the comedy through reception across documents. Let an established phrase or item recur where a later or parallel record gives it a concrete relation. One writer may describe an ordinary object with careful practical attention; another may respond to it as an inconvenience, a request, or a piece of news. The gap between the writers' purposes creates the comic effect. Agreement, unchanged routine, or a literal answer can all serve as the recurrence; no hidden meaning or escalating misunderstanding is required.\n\nSelect and present precise ordinary details while preserving each document writer's actual wording and register; the author profile does not make every source writer use one literary voice. A writer may record the exact condition of a hallway, the time a delivery arrived, or the order in which a task was completed. This attention can carry a supported oddity without certifying that the writer is troubled or that the detail is a clue. A recorded oddity may remain unexplained or receive an actual explanation from the writer, without a required mystery or an invented implication.\n\nPreserve required live private experience outside the recorded words. A document contains what its author chose to record; it is not automatically their complete mind. When the active contract requires visible or hidden interiority, render it as nonspoken fictional private experience in the active presentation, clearly distinct from the document text. The character may write a polite note while their private association runs in a different direction, and the narration can show that difference without making the letter dishonest.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nDevelop a feasible kindness or shared pleasure around actual preferences the scene has established. The care can be practical: preparing a favorite food, remembering how someone takes their tea, returning a borrowed item in better condition, or keeping a small promise. Spend narrative attention on the chosen kindness itself rather than rushing toward a punchline. Let the action occupy real time and show the particular steps involved.\n\nUse the Murakami profile's unhurried practical attention to render the care. Describe the exact surface of the task: the temperature of the water, the arrangement of food on a plate, the sound of a familiar object being set down. The focal character may follow a private association during this activity without the other person sharing it. That association can accompany uncomplicated enjoyment or a separate preoccupation; it does not need to be resolved by the comfort or turned into a joke.\n\nLet the comedy come from familiar timing and actual preferences. A repeated sensory detail, a literal reply to a warm remark, or a well-known routine completed with unexpected precision can supply a gentle callback where the scene supports it. The humor may belong to one character, to the shared history, or to the gap between the giver's expectation and the recipient's actual response. The recipient need not perform gratitude or emotional repair. They may accept the care with genuine pleasure, with distraction, or with a dry comment that the giver understands.\n\nKeep personality, privacy, and disagreement intact. Comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. The scene can end with the practical care completed and the private concern still present, or with a small pleasure enjoyed without commentary. Do not require a confession, a lesson, or a resolution.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse sustained paragraphs and deliberate transitions to set an actual encounter within the social context the narrator can access. The frame may include the history of the arrangement, the public meaning of the occasion, or the serious expectation attached to an ordinary aim. Let the frame establish proportion: the character has prepared for this meeting, the institution has its own procedures, the neighborhood has its own rhythms. The comedy comes from placing the concrete continuation beside that frame, not from shrinking the person into a caricature.\n\nDescribe the encounter with the Murakami profile's even attention to practical activity. Show the character moving through the actual steps: waiting in a particular chair, checking a document, walking a familiar route, performing a task with unhurried care. Let ordinary surfaces carry the scene, with any commentary remaining within the actual narrator's voice and access rather than a compulsory explanation. The narrator's distance can observe the scene, but the character's private associations remain their own. When the contract permits, render that private experience directly; when it does not, let outward behavior and accessible knowledge support only the interpretation they actually warrant, leaving excluded experience unknown without inventing a hidden gap.\n\nPlace the concrete continuation beside the frame with even description. A dry contrast may occur without a forced small result: the serious expectation meets a literal answer, the elaborate preparation meets a routine procedure, the formal occasion continues while the character's attention moves elsewhere. The comic effect can come from the sustained narrative distance itself, which holds the serious frame and the ordinary continuation in the same paragraph without resolving which one matters more.\n\nKeep ordinary speech and full private feeling in their own registers. The dialogue need not match the narrator's formal prose, and a character's private concern need not be announced in public language. The arc can remain unfinished. The scene may close with the encounter completed and the larger question still open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajah::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Framed Oral Storytelling (Arabian Nights-inspired)\n\nWrite the scene as a situated telling: someone recounts a memory, report, or invention to a listener or listeners whose actual reason for hearing it may include company, interest, or enjoyment as well as a practical purpose. Give the occasion a concrete purpose—persuasion, explanation, entertainment, delay, or warning—but do not require the tale to succeed or fail.\n\nLet the teller build the account from unhurried practical details and private associations. A remembered errand, a room, a meal, or a repeated phrase can carry the teller's actual concern while remaining ordinary. Keep the telling even in tone: the strangeness, if any, is described with the same attention as the familiar.\n\nMake comic timing arise from the relation between the tale's expected shape and the listener's actual attention. The listener may interrupt with a literal question, remember a different version, ask about a practical consequence, or simply wait through a pause. The teller may continue, adjust, or miss the point. Let recognition occur at its real time rather than as a forced reversal or punchline.\n\nUse a repeated phrase or object to link the outer occasion and the embedded account, but let its meaning remain, change, or stay open through actual recurrence without requiring growth or certifying a hidden pattern. Preserve the difference between what the teller knew then and what the teller understands now, and between what the listener hears and what the teller privately feels. The outer scene can return to a concrete question or action without requiring a cliffhanger or a changed relationship.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajai::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / German-Language Literary Traditions\n\nUse Romantic and uncanny narration where an actual entry, return, or familiar boundary matters to the focal character, without inventing one to demonstrate the form. Write the approach through unhurried practical activity—arriving, waiting, checking a room, preparing to leave—rather than through an announced supernatural event.\n\nHold a concrete detail beside the character's sense of it. The threshold may feel larger, stranger, or more significant than its plain appearance; describe both the physical fact and the private association in the same even tone. Keep the feeling authorized by the character's history or present concern, not by an invented cause.\n\nLet the real encounter proceed at its own pace. The person on the other side may answer literally, offer a practical accommodation, repeat a familiar habit, or simply continue what they were doing. The contrast between the felt expectation and the actual continuation can produce dry comic proportion without requiring mockery, embarrassment, or a sudden revelation.\n\nPreserve the boundary's actual consequences. It may be comfortable, inconvenient, or understood; it need not become uncanny or dissolve. If the scene remains pending, let that be a supported pause in the character's aim rather than a compulsory unresolved mystery. Do not import impossible events or make the character's private sense of the threshold a fact others must share.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaj::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Gothic-Punk\n\nWrite the scene around a supplied role, room, privilege, or obligation that carries inherited authority into present life. Use unhurried material detail—keys, furniture, procedures, clothing, the way a door is opened—to make that authority concrete and embodied.\n\nLet the character's private associations attach to the ordinary operation of the authority. A rule, title, or inherited arrangement may feel heavy, strange, or personally significant without becoming a secret power. Keep the authority's legitimacy and flaws both available: it can be respected, inconvenient, absurd, or genuinely consequential.\n\nMake comic weight arise from a concrete need meeting the actual operation. A literal reply, a practical accommodation, a repeated habit, or a delayed permission can answer the character's expectation at its real scale. The character may notice the disproportion privately, speak about it indirectly, or remain unaware. Do not force a quip or a confession.\n\nPreserve morally complicated intimacy without requiring an unmasking. Private desire may remain unspoken, be communicated awkwardly, or be understood by someone trusted. A change in social presentation follows the person's actual circumstances, voluntary or involuntary: trust, fatigue, anger, surprise, or a public event may matter. No mask-fall is required. Keep the actual arrangement and its human costs or comforts intelligible, including already-known terms or an unresolved question without requiring improved understanding.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajak::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Indian Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the modern urban or popular narration route: the comic sequence moves across linked encounters in the actual supplied setting. Give each encounter a concrete occasion—a shop, a workplace, a meal, a transit stop, a visit—and let the same phrase, request, or familiar activity recur where the character's history supports it.\n\nWrite each encounter with unhurried practical detail. The character may be buying something, asking a question, waiting, or carrying out a routine. Describe the ordinary surface evenly, including the particular sounds, objects, and movements of the place, without turning the setting into a generic backdrop.\n\nLet recurrence work through actual reception. The same request may be answered differently by different people, repeated by the same person, or met with a practical correction. The character's private association with the recurring phrase or activity can deepen, shift, or remain private. Comic timing comes from the relation between what the character expects and what each encounter actually gives.\n\nPreserve what each person receives. One audience may take the phrase seriously, another may miss it, another may respond with an unrelated concern. Do not require a new misunderstanding, added complication, or completed gag at every stop. The sequence can end with a supported pause, a small change, or a return to an earlier occasion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajal::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Japanese Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the diary and reflective miscellany route. Write the sequence as a continuing recorder's or authorized reflective voice's selected observations, with continuity supplied by that recorder's selection; the record may be private or addressed to an actual recipient without becoming a rotation of unrelated communications. Give the recorder a situated reason to keep the record—habit, memory, work, or private interest—but do not require every entry to serve a single purpose.\n\nLet each entry preserve actual acts, phrases, and impressions. The recorder may note a meal, a walk, a conversation, a repeated phrase, or an ordinary object. Describe the surface evenly, with the same attention to familiar and strange details. Keep the recorder's private association attached to the particular observation rather than announced as a general lesson.\n\nMake comic juxtaposition arise from selection and arrangement. A serious expectation may be placed beside a plain detail; a familiar observation may return unchanged; an entry may stop before reflection is complete. Let juxtaposition make the relation available, while the recorder may explain or comment where that belongs to their actual purpose and voice. Do not force a hidden pattern or a concluding insight.\n\nPreserve the difference between event time, composition time, and live private experience. The recorder may look back, misremember, or write around what was felt then. Unconnected entries and unfinished reflection are allowed. The sequence can end with an ordinary observation that remains open.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajam::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Latin American Literary Traditions\n\nSelect the fantastic or metaphysical construction route within the supplied world. Begin with a real detail—an object, a phrase, a repeated event, a place—that prompts a permitted character to consider another reading. Keep the detail concrete and faithful to its actual nature, ordinary or extraordinary as established by the supplied world.\n\nLet the character develop an explicitly conditional possibility: what might be true, what the detail could mean, how the day would go if that reading held. Write the imagined continuation with the same unhurried attention as the actual scene, but mark its status clearly through the character's own awareness, syntax, or return to the present fact. Do not let the possibility silently become an event.\n\nUse even treatment to make the relation comic. The character may take the possibility seriously while continuing to eat, wait, or answer a practical question. The actual conduct may match, ignore, or quietly contradict the imagined version. The disproportion between the private construction and the ordinary continuation can produce dry comic proportion without requiring a false theory, multiple alternatives, or a revelation.\n\nPreserve the world's actual rules. Preserve extraordinary events already established without adding new powers. An imagined continuation is not itself an event, but a proposed ordinary or extraordinary reading may gain actual support according to the supplied world. The scene can end with the character returning to the concrete detail, leaving the relation open without turning it into a fact.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajan::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Light Novel\n\nEnter the scene with a recognizable aim at its actual scale. Orient the reader quickly: who is present, where they are, and what the focal character is trying to do. Explain unfamiliar names, objects, or mechanics clearly when they matter, without pausing for a glossary.\n\nKeep spatial changes and dialogue attribution easy to follow. Use readable paragraph breaks around action, speech, and private response. A break may hold a long inward passage as well as a quick line; the rhythm follows the character's actual attention, not a fixed alternation between talk and reaction.\n\nLet comic timing arise from a literal reply, private association, or concrete continuation. The character may expect a particular outcome, and the scene may meet it with something plainer, slower, or more practical. The response can be serious, uncertain, dry, or uninterested. Do not require every speaker to be witty or every beat to end with a punchline.\n\nUse unhurried attention where the actual sequence permits, without slowing events or requiring a practical task. The character may continue preparing food, walking, or waiting while the exchange unfolds. The strangeness, if any, is described with the same even attention as the familiar. The scene can end at a supported pause or small change without a cliffhanger or a completed gag.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajao::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Lyrical Prose\n\nChoose a concrete scene detail or an association available to the active narrator or permitted focal character, and let the prose return to it through sound and syntax rather than explanation. The detail may be an object, a repeated phrase, a quality of light, or a small habitual action. Develop it in longer rhythmic sentences when the character's attention lingers, then place a plain statement or an ordinary continuation against that shaped expectation. The contrast can be comic without being announced: a practical reply may arrive in a short direct clause, a familiar object may remain exactly what it was, or the expected significance may simply fail to appear.\n\nKeep the image attributed to its actual narrator or experiencing person; it may remain unspoken or be communicated through an actual exchange without automatic transfer of private knowledge. It may color how the character experiences the moment, but it does not change material facts, grant knowledge to other characters, or become a universal symbol the scene must honor. Other people may pursue their own aims without noticing the association. The image can remain apt, shift register when the situation changes, or stay unresolved; do not force a moment where the character explains its meaning or where the world confirms it.\n\nBuild comic situations from recognizable aims and blind spots. A person may be preparing for something small with disproportionate care, waiting for a reply that does not match the imagined tone, or returning to a familiar task while a private association gives it particular felt weight without declaring that significance unjustified. Let the actual response—literal, patient, indifferent, or unexpectedly kind—meet that preparation. A supported gap, shared expectation, or familiar repeated response can carry the comedy without requiring mockery or an unrelated interruption. Keep narration even where the voice supports it, preserving actual activity and timing without inventing a task; a strange detail may be reported calmly while the character's inner rhythm gives it comic proportion.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajap::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Melancholic / Reflective\n\nWhen a memory belongs to the scene, keep the earlier person's actual aim and understanding intact. Do not correct the past from the present. Show what the character expected then through a particular phrase, sensation, habit, or small plan, and let that particular return beside the present occasion. The return may be dry, affectionate, or simply present: the same phrase said by someone else, the same task done under different conditions, the same preference now met without ceremony.\n\nLet the present association organize the return rather than a composed later telling. The character may notice the repetition without announcing its full history. A present pleasure can remain pleasurable even when it echoes something no longer available. The comedy can come from the persistence of the old expectation—a person still waiting for the same response, still arranging things the same way—or from the mild mismatch between the remembered weight and the present ordinariness. Do not require the character to realize a mistake, apologize to the past, or convert the memory into loss.\n\nUse unhurried, even narration where appropriate without slowing actual events or inventing a practical task. Other people continue their own concerns while the focal character carries the association. A callback may land for the reader through placement and repetition, not through a character's explanation. The remembered detail does not have to change the present outcome; it may simply coexist with it. Preserve the possibility of unfinished attachment and unchanged feeling. No regret, resolution, or emotional closure is owed by the end of the passage.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaq::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Middle Eastern Literary Traditions\n\nUse the modern memoir route: a composed first-person or otherwise authorized retrospective account that distinguishes the participant then from the teller now. Identify both positions through the narration itself—what the earlier person noticed, wanted, or misunderstood, and what the later teller now frames, qualifies, or leaves open. Recount a specific earlier encounter through even practical description: the errand, the waiting, the preparation, the ordinary object that seemed important. Let the later framing confirm the earlier feeling, qualify it, or simply note that it persisted without explaining everything.\n\nA small detail may carry dry contrast without shrinking the actual aim. The comedy can arise from the gap between the participant's expectation and the teller's present knowledge, or from the teller's calm refusal to make the past more dramatic than it was. Do not require foolishness in the earlier person or automatic superiority in the later teller; any actual judgment follows their real perspective and the supported case. Both positions may be sincere.\n\nKeep composed recollection distinct from required live private experience at either authorized time. The memoir text is an artifact with its own occasion and purpose; it is not automatically the teller's present unspoken thought, and it does not replace required interiority for the active scene. Use even retrospective narration where the voice supports it, preserving the earlier events' actual timing and the person's particular associations without requiring a task. The retrospective frame may remain unresolved, but the scene facts it reports stay stable.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajar::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Minimalist Prose\n\nSelect a small number of exact details that establish the actual aim and its continuation. Use direct clauses and concrete particulars for the actual activity, expectation, and any received or still-pending response, without requiring a result to arrive. Omit redundant explanation when context already supports the inference. A literal reply, a familiar repeated detail, or an object that remains ordinary can provide dry timing without commentary.\n\nLet an unhurried association be the central inward connection when the character's attention warrants it. The private passage may be longer than the surrounding action and need not produce a useful conclusion. Preserve actual private feeling, memory, and association at their warranted extent, including unresolved or unchanged experience. Do not shorten the mind to match terse action, and do not withhold emotion as a stylistic rule.\n\nBuild the comic situation from an actual recognizable aim, habits, and perceptions, with a blind spot only where established. The character may be overpreparing for a small task, expecting a particular response, or returning to a familiar routine with an unspoken hope. Let the actual continuation meet that expectation plainly. The comedy can come from the exactness of the mismatch, not from mockery or a random interruption. Use even, unhurried narration where it fits without altering actual event timing or requiring a practical task; a strange detail may be reported in the same plain register as everything else.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajas::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Modern Literature / Contemporary Novel\n\nWrite sustained scene-first prose that moves naturally among action, dialogue, perception, recollection, and authorized inner experience. Establish relevant actual aims through activity, speech, or authorized private narration: what the character is trying to do, what they expect, and what habit or blind spot shapes the attempt. Then let a concrete response, a repeated detail, or an even description of a supported oddity carry the contrast or callback.\n\nKeep the setting and events grounded. The world need not become surreal, and a strange detail may be described calmly without demanding explanation. Expectations may be accurate; a person can anticipate exactly what will happen and still be caught by its timing, its tone, or its ordinariness. The comic sequence follows actual responses rather than a required reversal. A reply can stop with a setup or a thought unfinished, leaving the next exchange to continue the situation.\n\nLet narration attend unhurriedly to actual activity and private associations where appropriate, without slowing events or inventing a task for the method. Private experience may run beside the visible scene without being announced. Other characters pursue their own aims and may not notice the focal character's expectation. Do not turn every reply into a quip or force a completed gag. The comedy lives in recognizable pursuit and reception, not in mockery or random interruption.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajat::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Relationship-Centric Fanfiction (AO3-inspired)\n\nUse an actual shared phrase, preference, or activity from the supplied relationship premise to establish what one person expects. The expectation should be legible through scene selection and private language: a familiar order, a habitual question, a small ritual one person assumes will proceed as before. Then let another person's real response meet it. The response may match the expectation exactly, offer literal help without noticing the private weight, or follow a different concern entirely. Misunderstanding is not required.\n\nLet recognition carry a callback where it occurs, while actual explanation may belong to the speakers or narrator without becoming compulsory. A repeated moment may gain comic weight because the reader and the focal character both know its history, while the other participant treats it as ordinary. The callback need not change the bond, expose a motive, or restore closeness. Even description can hold different personal resonances for different participants without declaring a hidden truth. Preserve the full supplied premise: rivalry, difficulty, or unresolved strain may remain present, but the author profile does not require estrangement.\n\nDescribe actual activity evenly where the chosen voice supports it, preserving its real timing and any interval without a task. Private associations remain character-owned and are not automatically known to the other person. The comedy can come from the persistence of a familiar routine, the gap between one person's expectation and another's actual attention, or the calm continuation of a shared habit that means different things to each of them. Do not force a confession, a relationship repair, or a completed emotional payoff.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajau::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Saga / Oral Epic\n\nGive the passage the sense of an account being retold through deliberate openings, compact declarations, and recurring phrasing around an established deed, promise, or way of being remembered. Preserve the remembered particular's actual scale; an ordinary example may be a person who always arrives early, a task done the same way for years, a small promise kept or nearly kept. Expand from the declaration into actual activity and authorized private resonance, so the account stays particular to the person, whether the reputation is accurate for its purpose or incomplete.\n\nLet a plain reply or familiar designation carry comic timing. Dialogue may agree with the account, continue it without comment, or quietly correct one detail. The subject of the retelling need not object, and the teller need not be wrong. Actual efforts and meanings can remain unchanged or unfinished. Do not turn household work into mock combat or familiar inconvenience into heroic doom. The comedy comes from the persistence and proportion of the remembered act, not from diminishing its real scale.\n\nKeep public remembrance distinct from the focal character's private account. Being remembered as dependable, strange, or particular does not mean the experience was effortless, cheerful, or automatic. Let the retelling take an unhurried cadence where the voice permits, without altering the deed's actual pace or requiring practical work to carry private resonance. The account may end with business concluded or resumed, leaving an actual act or unfinished effort available to be remembered rather than declaring it legend on the community's behalf.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajav::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Scandinavian Literary Traditions\n\nUse the essayistic route as a way of holding a serious observation beside an unhurried concrete case. Open with a situated claim in the authorized narrator's terms—something the narrator believes or has noticed about the present world, relationship, or practical situation. Keep the claim specific enough to be tested by the scene rather than a general proverb about life.\n\nThen bring the claim beside an actual, remembered, or clearly hypothetical encounter. Let the encounter proceed through recognizable aims and ordinary activity: someone is trying to cook, wait, explain, return an item, or finish a task. Describe the concrete details evenly, without pushing them toward a punchline. The comic effect can come from the gap between the claim's seriousness and the case's plain continuation, or from the way the case quietly confirms the claim in an unexpected register.\n\nReturn to the claim's scope after the encounter. The narrator may notice that the claim still holds, holds only in part, or applies differently than expected. The return may confirm, qualify, or refute the claim where the actual case supports it, without requiring refutation or a new proposition. It may simply acknowledge what the case did and did not show. Allow the construction to remain unfinished; the narrator can set the matter down without concluding it.\n\nKeep private associations attributed to the narrator or focal character; they may inform an argument where relevant but need not justify themselves as evidence for it. A remembered phrase, an object, or a repeated gesture may carry private concern while the public case continues. Do not turn the essayistic frame into a lecture or make every character speak in the narrator's reflective register. The claim organizes the telling; the people in the case keep their own habits, blind spots, and actual responses.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaw::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Screenplay\n\nUse concise action blocks, clear speaker cues, and scene headings to establish the relevant aims and conditions. Make relevant actual aims legible within narrator access, including conversation, private consideration, or a practical task where present rather than an invented chore. Describe the visible action in plain, specific terms. Distinguish observable action from inferred meaning, using only context and interpretation the active presentation actually permits.\n\nBuild comic timing through cuts and held intervals. A cut may move from an expectation to its actual continuation in another place or time. A held interval may show the same room, the same object, or the same person continuing an ordinary task while the expected response does not arrive. Let the juxtaposition carry the comedy. Do not invent a reaction, mishap, or supernatural event to complete the beat. The actual continuation can be quieter, slower, or more literal than the expectation.\n\nPreserve required private experience in its active presentation, distinct from voice-over. A character's associations, remembered phrases, or private concern may be extensive even when the visible action remains unchanged. Keep required inward material nonspoken, without automatically transferring knowledge to other characters. Separately authorized speech or voice-over may communicate selected words but does not replace actual private experience. The screenplay's public surface and the character's private resonance can move at different speeds.\n\nKeep physical causes separate from editing. A cut does not cause an event; it connects two actual occasions. A held interval does not stop time; it may contain stillness or continuing visible activity according to actual events. Let the active boundary fall in completed action, a pending task, stillness, or ongoing private experience without a required acknowledgment or reveal.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajax::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Stage Play\n\nUse the shared stage space to make the public exchange legible. Establish who is present, where they are, and what objects or tasks are available. Where physical business is actually present, let it help make the public exchange legible; an aim may also be stated in speech or known only through permitted private experience without an invented task. The business should be stageable and specific, not a vague gesture.\n\nBuild comic timing through the relationship between lines and physical business. A plain line may land while someone continues a task. A pause may be filled by the sound of a drawer closing or a kettle beginning to boil. A character may answer a question while looking for a missing object. Let the continuation of ordinary activity meet the expectation without a compulsory interruption. No prop use, entrance, or laugh is owed.\n\nPreserve required nonspoken experience distinct from asides. A character's private associations, remembered phrases, or concern may continue while the public scene proceeds. Keep required inward material in its active nonspoken presentation. Separately authorized asides or monologues have performed words and audiences and do not replace that channel. Reader access does not automatically give the cast the same knowledge; preserve what each actually knows through established sources or capabilities.\n\nLet the active boundary govern the stop, including ongoing talk or thought without a required result or acknowledgment. Track actual positions, exits, and changes of scene without assuming an indoor room or forbidding a legible change of place or time. Use exits and entrances only when they follow from the scene's actual conditions, not to manufacture a beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajay::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Stream of Consciousness\n\nBraid actual sensation, remembered phrases, private words, bodily sensation, and imagined continuations through a single permitted mind. Let the movement follow association rather than a linear report. A smell, a sound, or the feel of a surface may lead to a memory, which may lead to a private phrase, which may return to the present room. Keep the orientation clear enough that the reader can distinguish what is happening from what is remembered or imagined.\n\nLet an actual line or event enter only as received. Render only what the focal person actually receives within narrator access: a voice, hand movement, or placed object may register, while another event or word may pass unnoticed. Do not grant new knowledge merely through an association; preserve what the mind actually knows about another intention or an event elsewhere through established sources and capabilities. Keep unknown content and imagined continuations distinct. The mind may imagine what the other person will say next; that imagined line is not the actual line.\n\nBuild comic expectation through the associative movement. The mind may prepare for a response, rehearse a phrase, or notice the gap between what it expected and what the senses actually received. The comedy can come from a dry continuation, a literal reply, or the persistence of an expectation that no public event settles. Do not require a joke, a mishap, or a public response to complete the beat.\n\nLet thought length follow the mind's actual state. A long association may accompany a brief external event. A short perception may interrupt an extended memory. The private movement does not advance external events; the room, the task, and the other people continue at their own pace. The scene may end with the association unfinished.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajaz::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Text Messages / Chat Transcript\n\nUse sender-attributed messages with supported channel details. Each person's digital habits shape their contribution: one may write in full sentences, another in fragments; one may reply immediately, another after a long interval; one may edit a message, another may send a second message to correct the first. Keep the channel mechanics readable. Include only the timestamps, delays, edits, receipts, attachments, or channel changes that matter to the scene.\n\nBuild comic timing through actual message content and receipt. A person may send a careful message and receive a literal reply. A resumed topic may return after an unrelated exchange. A familiar timing may be met or missed. Let the comedy come from the gap between intention and received text, or from the way a plain phrase lands in a particular sequence. Do not invent a typo, outage, or misdirected message to create the beat.\n\nDistinguish genuine drafts from delivered words and live thought. An unsent draft is genuinely composed interface text, distinct from the writer's live private thought, with visibility governed by the actual interface and narrator access. A delivered message is what the other person actually received. Private interpretation may accompany the exchange, but it remains separate from transmitted text without automatically becoming another participant's knowledge; actual disclosure or prior knowledge follows the scene's real sources. No later message needs to settle that interpretation.\n\nLet the exchange continue through the people's actual concerns or ordinary interests, without requiring a practical agenda. The people may be arranging a meeting, returning an item, or asking about a task. The channel's distance does not prove estrangement, and a warm message does not prove closeness. The scene may end on a delivered message, a pending reply, or a plain acknowledgment without a cliffhanger.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajba::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Visual Novel\n\nUse concise scene-setting and readable speaker attribution to place the actual aim and conditions. Establish where the characters are, what they are doing, and what actual concern or activity is present, without requiring a practical goal. Keep the setting description brief enough to leave room for the illustrated scene's focal rhythm. Do not use background or expression cues as automatic truth indicators.\n\nBuild comic timing through the focal expectation and its actual meeting. The focal character may wait for a response, prepare a phrase, or notice a familiar detail. The scene may then meet that expectation with an actual cue or line: a plain answer, a continued task, a familiar object, or a silence. Let the meeting be accurate without requiring a changed understanding. The comedy can come from the expectation's persistence or from the gap between the prepared phrase and the received line.\n\nRender private association separately from observable fact and inferred meaning. When attention turns to a cue, the focal character may recall a phrase, connect the cue to an earlier occasion, or imagine a continuation. Keep inward material in its active presentation without automatically supplying it to other characters; their actual sources and permitted capabilities determine what they know. Do not let an inferred meaning become an observed fact. An accurate interpretation, stillness, or unfinished inner passage can support the comic timing without a fixed reaction portrait, a menu, or an embarrassed face.\n\nEnd at the active boundary, including continuing private experience, action, or talk without a required acknowledgment or completed event. The illustrated rhythm can hold a moment without forcing a new event. Do not add branching menus, player decisions, or romance merely because the style is selected.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacajbb::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Haruki Murakami / Web Serial\n\nMaintain a readable through-line across installments: what is underway, what changes locally, and what remains available to continue. Recover a relevant earlier aim, phrase, or response briefly when needed. Use the exact particular that matters—the borrowed item, the unfinished task, the repeated question, the familiar reply—rather than retelling the whole earlier exchange.\n\nLet the familiar pattern remain accurate, change context, or recede according to the people involved. A recurring phrase may return in a new situation and mean something different. A familiar aim may be completed, abandoned, or quietly replaced. A private concern may persist or settle without advancing the pattern. Do not promise a hidden explanation for the recurrence, and do not manufacture an escalating series of mishaps merely to prolong the serial; earned escalation may follow actual choices and consequences.\n\nKeep the comic continuity grounded in ordinary practical life. The characters may be returning to the same task, the same place, or the same relationship across installments. Let actual habits and any relevant established blind spots support recurrence without requiring a flaw. The interval may close at its active boundary, including ongoing private experience or ordinary activity without a required change or acknowledgment. Uncertainty need not remain unresolved for serial duration; a question may be answered, a pattern may end, and the story may continue through a different ordinary concern.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakaa::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Angsty / Emotionally Turbulent\n\nWrite the scene around a specific need that someone finds difficult to voice, withhold, or receive. Keep the difficulty concrete: what the person hopes the offer will mean, what they fear it will expose, and what they actually say or do instead. Do not make the asker foolish for having the need. The comic timing comes from the exact fit or mismatch between the offered wording and the private concern it touches, without requiring a mistake or failed expectation; either remains possible when actually supported.\n\nLet the offer's social shape carry more than one meaning. A considerate answer can be unexpectedly exact: it may relieve the person, embarrass them, or make the fear harder to dismiss. Show that effect in the person's actual response and subsequent behavior, while keeping their full private strain available even if no request is voiced. If the narration permits free indirect judgment, keep its owner clear and attribute the judgment to its actual participant or narrator, preserving any supported narrative irony without creating an unlicensed observing voice.\n\nAllow warmth and pain to coexist without forcing a repair. A partial comfort, an awkward apology, or a moment of genuine ease may occur and still leave the underlying difficulty present. End the response wherever the active scene boundary falls; do not require the person to change their interpretation, recover their mood, or resolve the relationship before stopping.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakab::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Chat Roleplay\n\nOrient the present occasion in clear narrative paragraphs: who is present, what practical aim each person is pursuing, and what has just been said or done. Resolve the submitted material according to the active authorship contract, preserving human-owned openings without deciding them. Attribute every line of dialogue unmistakably, and keep each participant's response owned by that participant.\n\nBuild the comic interval from the social meaning of actual offers and their reception. One participant may accept an offer literally, recognize its intended kindness, or attend to a different term of it. Show that actual relation, including accurate alignment, through placement and attributed interpretation rather than making everyone exchange quips. A character may be witty, literal, dry, or uninterested in the joke according to their own habits.\n\nKeep authorized private experience available alongside public activity. A character's inward strain, pleasure, or calculation may accompany what they say without being known to the others. Do not make the narrator read minds the active contract excludes. End at the active boundary, including during ongoing private experience or public activity, without adding a question, cliffhanger, or forced change in social position.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakac::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Chinese Literary Traditions\n\nUse the intimate urban or domestic narrative route. Follow one concrete shared occasion through the actual arrangement of nearby lives: available space, objects, proximity, and the small courtesies or preferences that pass between people. Keep the setting specific to the supplied world rather than transplanting a location or cast identity.\n\nPlace what a gesture means to its maker beside how it is received within permitted knowledge. An accurately known preference can make the timing funny without exposing a hypocrite or forcing a misunderstanding. Show how the physical arrangement lets people notice or overlook one another: a doorway, a shared table, a borrowed object, or a room's layout can carry the social meaning of an offer.\n\nLet each person's estimate of generosity, convenience, or propriety remain attributable to that person. Render authorized private judgment, direct feeling and sensation at their needed extent; reader exposure does not itself inform another participant, whose actual prior knowledge, sources and permitted access remain available. Keep the occasion intimate and spatially concrete without requiring an urban setting, a family structure, or a new conflict.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakad::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Compressed Gritty Prose\n\nWrite with forceful diction and compressed transitions. Use concrete handling, actual expense, weight, wear, or physical effort where the supplied world provides them. Do not add rough speech or danger merely to signal grit; ground any abrasive voice in its actual speaker.\n\nPut an attributed estimate of generosity, convenience, or propriety beside what carrying out the act really entails. The comic scale emerges from that juxtaposition: a grand courtesy may require a small awkward labor, or a plain task may carry unexpected social weight. Let rhythm and concrete detail make the relation readable, with direct explanation available where it contributes rather than duplicates the account.\n\nPreserve thought at its actual needed extent. A character may reason at length when the situation requires it, and prose length alone does not determine fictional duration or cancel reflection; actual elapsed time follows the events, attention and any supported temporal transitions. Keep public and private chronology intact, and do not require a mishap, humiliation, or changed plan to complete the comic beat.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakae::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Epistolary / Documents\n\nTell the sequence through documents, each with a clear author, intended recipient or purpose, and time of composition. Use the specific document form the scene requires—letter, journal, report, note, or record—rather than rotating through available formats.\n\nAttend to an offer, omission, or chosen wording as an actual writer frames it for someone. A courteous phrase may be carefully composed for one recipient and read differently by another. Later records may address the practical terms of an earlier offer or recognize its intention accurately. The comic scale comes from the exact matter each record takes up: what one writer treats as a small detail, another may treat as a large obligation or a pointed slight.\n\nDistinguish composition, delivery, and subsequent knowledge. A document contains what its author records, not the author's complete live mind. Keep full nonspoken experience in its separate authorized channel. Let the sequence remain pending without forcing a reply or a contradictory account.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakaf::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Fluffy / Comforting\n\nSelect a real kindness or small pleasure that someone offers, requests, or privately considers. Spend attention on the specific details of accommodation: the familiar preference, the practical care, the particular ease being arranged. Make those details concrete enough that an elaborate courtesy and a plain preference can fit amusingly well.\n\nKeep the recipient free to welcome, decline, partly enjoy, or ignore the care according to actual character. Sincere intent does not guarantee comfort, and comfort does not make everyone agreeable or remove established problems from the world. Let personality, privacy, and disagreement remain intact.\n\nPreserve actually accurate social understanding without inventing either a mistake or infallibility. A character may know exactly what another person likes without that knowledge requiring a confession or emotional repair. Full private pleasure or other feeling may continue alongside the public occasion. End without forcing a payoff, a changed relationship, or a completed emotional arc.}}\n{{trim}}\n{{setvar::NPacakag::♢ * [STYLE] Comedy / Jane Austen / Formal Literary Novel\n\nUse deliberate transitions and sustained paragraph movement. Establish the relevant setting, history, or social context within the narrator's permitted access, then return to the small exchange at hand. The formal architecture frames the scene; it does not require an aristocratic setting, archaic vocabulary, or uniformly elaborate speech.\n\nAttribute a participant or narrator estimate of an offer, reputation, or omission, then relate it to the actual words or circumstances. The estimate need not be wrong to be comic: its proportion may simply be larger or smalle